  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 24d13becfb3100d4d8970a3eb64c00bc, type: 3}
  m_Name: PlaytestTexts
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Clover.Runtime::Clover.UI.PlaytestTexts
  Version: Combat Playtest 0.5 (unreleased)
  WhatsNew: "<color=#E8C77A>What this playtest is</color>\n\nA testbed for the combat. It grows with each update as the fighting is reworked. Everything is open: try anything, and tell us what felt good, what felt wrong and what you did not understand.\n\n<color=#E8C77A>New in 0.5 (phase 4: keystones, pairs and shapes)</color>\n\n\u2022  <b>Six new keystones.</b> A keystone is a big mark at the top of a tree. It changes how the whole tree plays.\n\u2022  <b>Into the Fire.</b> Dodge toward the enemy you face and you cannot be hurt for the whole roll. You roll through bodies and set them burning. A dodge away costs twice the stamina.\n\u2022  <b>The Blade Drinks Fire.</b> Your Fire weapon no longer lays Burn, and burns do not hurt. Instead, each blow on a burning enemy drinks its Burn and hits harder for it.\n\u2022  <b>High Water, Low Water.</b> A tide turns every 6 seconds. At high water your spells hit harder and your weapon hits softer. At low water it is the other way round. A perfect guard or a perfect dodge turns the tide at once.\n\u2022  <b>Drawn Back to Break.</b> Each dodge away stores a wave, up to three. Your next blow breaks them all in a cone. The cone soaks the enemy and throws it 2 metres back. With no wave stored, your weapon hits 20% softer.\n\u2022  <b>Shared Roots.</b> A third of each wound you deal spreads to Seeded enemies within 8 metres. Blows on Seeded enemies no longer heal you.\n\u2022  <b>In Its Season.</b> Leave a seed alone for 6 seconds and it ripens. A ripe seed bursts three times as hard and grows a thornbush. An unripe one bursts at half strength.\n\u2022  <b>Four old keystones are back.</b> The Closed Ring, All Blades Home, Two Suns and Plague Bearer stand at the top of their trees again. Two Suns now makes your spells wait a fifth longer instead of costing mana.\n\u2022  <b>A branch for every pair.</b> Carry two elements and a small diamond of marks opens in the middle of the chart. It is named for the pair and its reaction. Every pair of elements has one now. Its marks work only while you carry both elements.\n\u2022  <b>Pair marks change their reaction.</b> Many pair marks change the numbers of their own reaction: bigger, longer or harder. The guide shows the new numbers when the mark is yours.\n\u2022  <b>A surge for every pair.</b> Each pair has a surge of its own. Choose it at a rest fire while you carry both elements. It goes in the Hand's surge slot and uses its own mana and time.\n\u2022  <b>Devotion.</b> Carry one element twice (all in) and you can buy its Devotion, a strong mark no pair can have. The deep strike (L2 with R2, all in) now needs the Devotion. Until you buy it, R2 all in is the plain infused strike and costs no mana.\n\u2022  <b>Shapes at the rest fire.</b> A new Shapes entry, after the Hand, lists each spell and reaction that has a Shape mark. Pick which shape it wears.\n\u2022  <b>Spells that move you.</b> Some pair spells now take more than one press of their button. Three Weasels rushes in and stops just in front of the enemy, then the next press cuts straight through. Under the Black Water swaps places with the enemy. Elites and gods stay put, and you come up behind them. Its pool curses, and your next blow hits half again as hard.\n\u2022  <b>Dodges remember the enemy.</b> A run of dodges keeps counting \"toward\" and \"away\" against the first enemy. It lets go when that enemy dies, when you get 16 metres away, or after 3 seconds with no dodge.\n\u2022  <b>New effects.</b> 32 reactions are redrawn under their game names. 13 element looks are new, among them Glaze's frost and Bedrock's earth armour on your body. Gust and Stone Rise are now looks. Nothing is drawn on your blade any more: the slashes and their colours stay. Bright white flashes are gone. A finished enemy gets a pale gold cross, and the ring that grows as an enemy staggers is gold too. A Burn set off and an ice shatter each burst in their own colour. A soldier's guard arc hides while its guard is held open.\n\u2022  <b>Ice swings like ice.</b> Ice's swing trail is now pale glacier ice with a steel-blue edge. It no longer looks like Water or Metal.\n\u2022  <b>Not yet:</b> some new marks still use a stand-in look or none (the tide, the waves behind you, a ripe seed, the blade drinking fire, Shared Roots' roots, Into the Fire's lunge). Pair surges wear their first element's look. A few pair spells still wait for their extra presses, and the guide says which under \"Not yet\".\n\n<color=#E8C77A>New in 0.4 (phase 3: reactions)</color>\n\n\u2022  <b>Reactions, all 55.</b> When an affliction lands on an enemy that already has another element's affliction, the two react, and every pair of elements does something different: <b>Flashover</b> (Fire and Lightning: the Static goes off through the flame), <b>Steam</b> (Fire and Water: a scalding puff), <b>Thermal Shock</b> (Fire and Ice: the enemy cracks, and your next heavy blow splits it), <b>Capsize</b> (Wind and Water: the enemy falls down), <b>Smoulder</b> (Dark and Fire: the fire sinks in, grows stronger, then flares up), <b>Fish Poison</b> (Poison and Water: enemies with lots of poison float up, stunned), and 49 more. The guide to effects has an entry for each one, with its numbers.\n\u2022  <b>Many need your weapon.</b> A reaction often leaves something on the enemy for a few seconds: a crust of ice that your heavy blow blows out of its far side, a crack your heavy blow splits, a stone that rocks until your third blow knocks it down, or coils of roots your blows pull tighter. Some go off when the enemy dies (Smoulder flares up, Shut in the Dark's jar breaks, Drowned Weight bursts).\n\u2022  <b>Where you stand matters.</b> Several reactions throw things away from you, past the enemy (Carried Embers, Rime, Red Hot, Golden Feathers, Thunder Gust). Get the enemy between you and the others.\n\u2022  <b>How they work.</b> A reaction happens once, then has to wait a few seconds before it can happen again on that enemy (most wait 4 seconds, the pairs three apart on the ring wait 6, and a few have their own wait: Steam 3 seconds, Shut in the Dark and The Grudge 8 seconds). Afflictions that a reaction adds don't start a reaction of their own. Anything can set one off: your weapon, spells, marks, the charged blade, strikes, even an enemy wet from the river. The order never matters, but Seized (Lightning and Water) and The Grudge (Dark and Lightning) need two or more Static on the enemy.\n\u2022  <b>Big enemies resist.</b> Anything that holds or slows an enemy works half as well on an elite and not at all on a god. Some reactions give elites and gods their own numbers instead (each entry says).\n\u2022  <b>You see the name.</b> A reaction's name rises over the enemy for a moment. Each reaction's own look comes later. For now, there is a puff in its two colours, plus whatever it does (a burst of lightning, a burst, fires, a slick).\n\u2022  <b>Switch between weapon and spells to react.</b> Carry two elements. Your weapon adds the first element's affliction, and your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike add the second's.\n\u2022  <b>Not yet:</b> the duo branches' shapes that change a reaction will come in a later update. Many reactions are simpler than their full plan, and each entry in the guide says what is still to come under \"Not yet\": the side a blow comes from (Rime, Mock Suns), things to pick up (Shining Herb, Firebloom), standing stones and tree trunks (Anvil Stone, Frost Heave, Girdled), The Grudge's cloud answering the enemy's blow, Black Chain's shared wounds, and others.\n\n<color=#E8C77A>New in 0.3 (phase 2: two elements and the Hand)</color>\n\n\u2022  <b>Two elements at once.</b> Carry a first and a second element (carrying the same one twice is called all in). Each spell is cast in its own element, adds its own affliction, and looks like its own element, in its own colour.\n\u2022  <b>The altars set both.</b> If you carry one element, use another altar to make its element your second. Use the altar of the element you carry to go all in. If you carry two (or are all in), an altar starts you over with only its element. Before you press, the altar's prompt tells you what it will do (\"Carry Lightning as second\", \"Carry Fire all in\", \"Carry Ice alone\"). You arrive carrying Fire.\n\u2022  <b>Your weapon only adds the first element.</b> Weapon blows add the first element's affliction, even if you've learned the second element's root mark. A mixed branch's own marks are the exception: Biting Wind, Wet Seed and the rest add both of theirs. The second element's affliction comes from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike, so two afflictions meet on an enemy when you switch between weapon and spells.\n\u2022  <b>The charged blade takes the element of the spell that landed.</b> Flame Wave charges it with Fire, and Thunderclap with Lightning. Only the first thing that cast hits counts. A duo spell charges it with the first element of its pair.\n\u2022  <b>Strikes for each element.</b> L2 with R1 (RB) is the first element's strike, and L2 with R2 (RT) is the second's. Neither costs mana. If you carry one element, or are all in, R2 is the deep strike for 20 mana, like before.\n\u2022  <b>The rest menu.</b> A rest fire now opens a menu: Set the Hand, The chart, and Leave the fire. Closing the chart brings you back to it.\n\u2022  <b>The Hand.</b> Your four spells in a diamond laid out like the buttons, with the surge in the middle. Choose a slot to see only what can go there, grouped by element, with each spell's role and a short description. The weapon skill button shows the numbers. Pick the surge from either element. Suggested Hand fills it for you. Three saved Hands keep a Hand for each kind of fight (use puts one on, and heavy attack saves the one you have on there). Once you set a Hand, the slots stop filling themselves. The Hand never changes which elements you carry.\n\u2022  <b>The spell diamond</b> colours each slot by its own spell's element, and names the R2 strike for the second element.\n\u2022  <b>Duo branches</b> (Plasma and the other mixes) only work while you carry both of their elements. A duo spell sits in a slot like any other.\n\u2022  <b>Slots open as you go, outside the playtest.</b> In the full game, you start with two slots (West and North) and the surge. East opens when the Champion of the Gate falls, and South when the Fire god falls, and they stay open from then on. The Hand shows a closed slot and tells you what opens it. In the playtest, every slot is open.\n\u2022  <b>Not yet:</b> in the full game, there's still nowhere to choose the second element (the hub's braziers only give one element). That comes when the hub's choice is reworked. Duo surges, shapes, and the planned starting Hands for each element come with the reshaped trees.\n\n<color=#E8C77A>New in 0.2 (phase 1: spells on cooldowns, mana for big moves, the charged blade, infused strikes and the spell diamond)</color>\n\n\u2022  <b>The charged blade.</b> A spell from one of your four slots that lands charges your weapon with its element. Your next three blows that land carry it: each hits 20% harder and adds the element's affliction, and the third does one extra thing (Fire leaves the ground burning, Light shoots a ray, Poison makes the poison bite right away, and so on). You can see it on the weapon: flame and embers along the edge for Fire, lightning crackling along it, frost for Ice. Three dots next to the spell diamond count the blows left. A new spell that lands gives you three fresh blows.\n\u2022  <b>Infused strikes.</b> Hold L2 (LT) and press R1 (RB): one of your weapon's own moves, with your element on it, shaped by the weapon (the Katana's drawing cut sends Burning Edge along its swing, the Hammer's drop puts it in a circle, the Spear's thrust in a line). L2 with R2 (RT) is the deep strike, a heavier version, for 20 mana. Each button has an 8-second wait, and fighting with your weapon shortens the wait, like a spell's. A strike counts as a weapon blow (it gives mana and shortens spell waits) but never charges the blade. On the keyboard, hold Left Ctrl and click the left or right mouse button. Every element has its own pair of strikes; the guide to effects lists them.\n\u2022  <b>The spell diamond</b> in the bottom-right corner replaces the old line of spells. It shows your four slots laid out like the face buttons, each with its picture, its button, and shading that sweeps back as its wait runs out, plus the surge in the middle and the two strikes under it. It is always small, and grows while L2 is held, showing each spell's name. The buttons are named for the controller or keys you used last, and follow any buttons you've changed.\n\u2022  <b>Hunger</b> (Dark's surge, paid for with health) now has a 25-second wait after it ends before you can light it again.\n\n\u2022  <b>Four spell slots on the L2 button.</b> Hold L2 (LT) and press Square, Triangle, Circle or Cross (X, Y, B, A) to cast the spell in that slot. On the keyboard, 1, 2, 3 and 4 cast them directly. While L2 is held, the face buttons only cast spells: no flask, skill, dodge or use.\n\u2022  <b>The surge is a tap of L2.</b> Tap it and let go quickly. On the keyboard, it stays on F.\n\u2022  <b>The weapon skill has its own button:</b> Triangle (Y), or Q. Holding the heavy button now only gives the charged heavy blow.\n\u2022  <b>Basic spells cost no mana.</b> Instead, each slot has a 6- to 12-second wait after a cast. Fighting with your weapon brings spells back sooner: a light blow that lands takes 0.3 seconds off every waiting slot, a heavy blow 0.6 seconds, a perfect guard 2 seconds, a perfect dodge 1.5 seconds, and a finisher 3 seconds.\n\u2022  <b>Mana is for the big moves:</b> the surge, the weapon skill, and each element's one larger spell (Meteor Leap, Seven Flashes, Sky Dance, Winter Wall, Poured Blade, Elder Tree, Grave of Swords, Daybreak, One by One; the duo spells built so far too). You enter each area with half your mana. Out of a fight, mana rises to half and stops there; anything above half is kept. A fight always starts with at least half.\n\u2022  <b>Filling the slots.</b> Your element's own spell starts in West, and each spell you learn fills the next empty slot. At a rest fire, choose a learned spell on the chart and press use to put it in the next slot. The Hand screen comes in the next update.\n\u2022  <b>Controls:</b> the pause menu's controls page has rows for the spell button and the four slots, for both the controller and the keyboard.\n\u2022  <b>Not yet:</b> marks that only made the basic spell cheaper do nothing for now, and some spell details on the chart still describe the old way of casting; the trees will be reshaped later. Two elements at once and the Hand screen come in the next update.\n\n<color=#E8C77A>New in 0.1</color>\n\n\u2022  <b>The Proving Ground</b>, where the playtest begins: a rest fire, a rack with every weapon (the Storm Katana and the Sun-Spear among them), altars for every element, training dummies (one that stands still, one that fights back with red and pale blows, and one with each element's affliction on it), pens where you can let out the castle's soldiers and the Fire land's people, gates to Narukami and Khepharu, and doors to the Castle, the Summit, Sun City and the Sun Court.\n\u2022  <b>Playtest rules.</b> Resting gives you unlimited Shards, so you can buy every mark, the gods' gifts included. You can walk the chart into every tree and branch. Falling in battle puts you back at the last fire with nothing lost.\n\u2022  <b>Back to the Proving Ground</b> from any level: pause, then choose Return to the Proving Ground.\n\u2022  <b>The guide to effects</b>, from the pause menu and from the chart: every affliction, state and resource, with its numbers, what sets it off, how to use it, and how it looks.\n\u2022  <b>Details for the high-level marks</b> on the chart: what a mark does, when, the numbers, what it works with, and an example. Written in full for the Fire and Lightning trees and their mixes; the other trees show what their data says for now.\n\u2022  <b>Not built yet</b>: empty slots on the rack and on the chart show where the gods' weapons and boughs still to come will go.\n\u2022  This title screen, with the version and what's new."
  Effects:
  - Id: burn
    Title: Burn
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Fire''s affliction. A burning enemy keeps taking damage until the fire goes out. More stacks make it hurt more each second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 damage a second. Each stack adds 30%, up to 5 stacks (12.5 damage a second at full). Adding it again keeps whichever time is longer, but never more than 8 seconds. When it goes out, all its stacks go too. When something sets it off, it deals 10 damage plus 10 for each stack, all at once.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Flame Wave sets whatever it touches on fire for 5 seconds, and its burning ground does too. Each blow during Kindle sets the enemy on fire for 3 seconds. Once you learn Fire''s root mark, every weapon blow sets the enemy on fire for 4 seconds and adds a stack. Some marks set off a burn.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Burn keeps working while you guard and dodge. Set the enemy on fire, then fight safely. Build up stacks before a mark sets it off, so the burst hits harder.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Flames lick up the enemy''s body. An orange diamond sits under its health bars, bigger with more stacks.'
  - Id: static
    Title: Static
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Lightning''s affliction. Electric charge builds up on an enemy and waits to be set off. When it goes off, lightning jumps into the enemy and everyone near it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 5 stacks, lasting 6 seconds. Each new stack restarts the timer. When it goes off, it deals 7 damage and 5 stagger for each stack to everyone within 4 metres (35 damage and 25 stagger when full).


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Once you learn Lightning''s root mark, each light blow adds a stack. A heavy blow or a finisher on a charged enemy sets it off. Some marks set it off in other ways. Static put on a Soaked enemy also spreads to everyone within 4 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> String together light blows up to five stacks, then finish with a heavy blow. Do it in a crowd: the burst hits all of them, and its stagger sets them up for finishers.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Small bolts jump over the enemy''s body. A pale blue diamond sits under its health bars. When it goes off, a bolt hits the enemy.'
  - Id: unsteady
    Title: Unsteady
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Wind''s affliction. An Unsteady enemy loses its footing. It is easier to stagger, flies farther when hit, and turns slowly.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 5 stacks, lasting 6 seconds. Each stack means the enemy takes 6% more stagger, is shoved 15% farther, and turns 8% slower. At full, that''s 30% more stagger, 75% farther shoves, and 40% slower turning.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Gust adds two stacks. Once you learn Wind''s root mark, every weapon blow adds a stack. During Tailwind, every weapon blow adds one more.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Slower turning lets you circle behind an enemy and hit its back. Longer shoves help you throw enemies into danger zones and walls. The extra stagger works with every heavy blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Streaks of pale air circle the enemy''s feet. A pale green diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: chill
    Title: Chill and Frozen
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Ice''s affliction. Each stack of Chill slows an enemy down. When it is full, the enemy is Frozen: stuck in ice until it thaws or a heavy blow shatters it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 5 stacks, lasting 6 seconds. Each stack slows the enemy by 7%. At 5 stacks, it freezes for 2.5 seconds. Elites thaw after 1 second. A god thaws even sooner: in its human form after 0.75 seconds, and in its god form after 0.3 seconds. For a god it''s just a short pause, and then it carries on with whatever it was doing. After a god thaws, it can''t be frozen or held again for 3 seconds (in human form) or 5 seconds (in god form). Its Chill still builds up, and the next cold after that freezes it. A heavy blow on a frozen enemy shatters it for 45 more damage. A thaw or a shatter ends the Chill. A frozen enemy can''t be chilled any more.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Hoarfrost adds two stacks, and a weapon blow among its crystals breaks them for one more. Once you learn Ice''s root mark, every weapon blow adds a stack. During Glaze, every weapon blow adds one more.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Freeze a dangerous enemy to buy time, then shatter it with a heavy blow. Soaked spreads Chill to everyone close by.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Frost crystals grow at the enemy''s feet. When Frozen, it glows a steady pale blue inside a cage of tall white crystals, and its diamond turns white and gets bigger. A god in its god form glows the same blue for that moment, and the crystals burst where it was last hit.'
  - Id: soaked
    Title: Soaked
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Water''s affliction. A Soaked enemy takes more damage from spells, and passes Static and Chill on to everyone close to it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> It is either on or off, and lasts 8 seconds. Spells deal 25% more damage to it. Static or Chill put on it is also put on everyone within 4 metres.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Riptide soaks everything its wave carries. Once you learn Water''s root mark, every weapon blow soaks. During Wellspring, every weapon blow soaks too.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Soak a group, then charge or chill one of them, and it reaches all of them. Cast your spells on Soaked enemies.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Water drips off the enemy, and rings of water spread on the ground at its feet. A deep blue diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: seeded
    Title: Seeded
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Life''s affliction. A seed grows inside the enemy. Each of your weapon blows on it heals you, and when it dies, it bursts into thorns.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 3 stacks, lasting 8 seconds. Each weapon blow on it heals you 1 for each stack. When it dies, it deals 12 damage for each stack to everyone within 3 metres (36 when full).


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Briar Patch Seeds whatever stands in it. Once you learn Life''s root mark, every weapon blow adds a stack. During Regrowth, every weapon blow adds one more.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Fast weapons get the most out of it, since every blow heals. Kill a fully Seeded enemy in the middle of a group, so its thorns hit the rest.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Vines grow at the enemy''s feet, and pink flowers open on it. A green diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: tremor
    Title: Tremor
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Earth''s affliction. The ground shakes under the enemy. It is slowed, and its balance doesn''t come back while the shaking lasts.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 3 stacks, lasting 6 seconds. Each stack slows the enemy by 10%. While it lasts, the enemy''s stagger meter doesn''t go down.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Stone Rise adds a stack. Once you learn Earth''s root mark, every heavy blow adds a stack, and a finisher adds all three. During Bedrock, every weapon blow adds one more.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Tremor keeps the stagger from fading, so you can step back, guard or heal, and the meter waits for you. Use it on enemies with a big stagger meter.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Pebbles hop around the enemy, and the ground cracks under it. A brown diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: sundered
    Title: Sundered
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Metal''s affliction. Notches in the enemy''s armour let your weapon cut deeper.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 3 notches, lasting 8 seconds. Each notch means 8% more damage from weapon blows (24% when full). It doesn''t affect spells.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Flung Blade adds a notch when your next weapon blow drives the blade in. Once you learn Metal''s root mark, every heavy blow and every perfect guard adds a notch, and a finisher adds all three. During Temper, every weapon blow adds one more.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Notch the armour first, then use your hardest blows. Sundered and a critical hit multiply each other.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Splinters of armour fall off the enemy. A steel-grey diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: marked
    Title: Marked
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Light''s affliction. A mark of light makes your weapon blows on the enemy critical hits, until the Mark is used up or fades.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Only one stack. It lasts 8 seconds or three weapon blows, whichever ends first. Each weapon blow on it is a critical hit (one and a half times the damage) and gives 2 more mana. Marking the enemy again gives you three fresh blows.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Glare Marks whatever its ray hits. Once you learn Light''s root mark, the last blow of a chain and every perfect guard add a Mark. During Clarity, every weapon blow Marks.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Save the Mark for your three hardest blows. A heavy blow or a finisher on a Marked enemy hits hardest. The extra mana pays for your next spell.


      <b>How it looks.</b> A beam of white light shines over the enemy''s head. A pale gold diamond sits under its health bars. Each critical hit shows a gold star.'
  - Id: cursed
    Title: Cursed
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Dark''s affliction. A Cursed enemy hits more weakly, and killing it heals you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Only one stack, lasting 8 seconds. The enemy deals 15% less damage. Killing it heals you 6.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Grasp Curses whatever it reaches. Once you learn Dark''s root mark, every weapon blow Curses. During Hunger, every weapon blow Curses too.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Curse the enemy that hits hardest, and its blows take less of your health. Curse enemies that are almost dead to heal as you kill them. This works well with Hunger, which costs health.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Dark violet smoke sinks over the enemy. A violet diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: poisoned
    Title: Poisoned
    Group: Afflictions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Poison''s affliction. Doses of poison build up, and on a slow, steady beat, the poison bites for all of them at once.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 8 doses, lasting 6 seconds. Each new dose restarts the timer. Every 2 seconds, it bites for 2 damage for each dose (16 when full). The first bite comes 2 seconds after the first dose. When something sets it off, it deals 6 damage for each dose all at once.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Miasma gives a dose to whatever stands in its cloud once a second. Once you learn Poison''s root mark, every weapon blow adds a dose, a heavy blow adds one more, and a finisher adds two more. During Fever, every weapon blow adds one more. Some marks set it off.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Keep adding doses so the timer never runs out. The more doses, the harder each bite. Then dodge, guard, and let the poison do its work.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Yellow-green bubbles rise off the enemy, bigger with more doses. A yellow-green diamond sits under its health bars.'
  - Id: staggered
    Title: Staggered and the finisher
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Every enemy has a balance meter. Blows fill it up. When it''s full, the enemy is staggered: it kneels, helpless, for a few seconds. A heavy blow on it then is a finisher.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Normal enemies stay staggered for 3.2 seconds, elites for 3 seconds, and gods for 2.8 seconds. The meter starts to go down 2 seconds after the last blow, at 25 a second. While an enemy is staggered, light blows deal one and a half times the damage. A finisher reaches up to 5 metres, deals three times the damage, costs no stamina, gives 15 mana, and nothing can hit you while it lasts. On a god, it takes at least a fifth of its health, and on an elite at least a third.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Every blow fills it. Heavy blows, perfect guards and some spells fill it the most. Unsteady makes everything fill it more. Tremor stops it from going down.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Watch the balance bar under the enemy''s health. When the enemy flickers, one or two more blows will break its balance. Save your heavy blow for the finisher.


      <b>How it looks.</b> At 70%, the enemy flickers amber. When staggered, it pulses gold and has a broken gold ring over it.'
  - Id: grey-health
    Title: Grey health
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A blocked blow still takes a little health, but that part turns grey and you can win it back. Deal damage soon, and it comes back. Wait too long, and it fades.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 35% of a blocked blow''s damage turns grey. Each point of damage you deal wins back 0.6 of grey health. The grey starts to fade 2.5 seconds after your last block or win-back, at 10 a second. A blow you don''t block removes all of it. Grey health can never take your last point of health, so guarding alone can''t kill you.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Blocking with the guard button. Regrowth and Clarity turn all grey health back into real health at once.


      <b>How to use it.</b> After a block, hit back right away to win the grey health back. Don''t take an unblocked blow while you have grey health, or you lose it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> A grey stretch at the end of your health bar, between your health and the empty part.'
  - Id: guard-broken
    Title: Guard broken
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Holding your guard costs stamina for every blow it blocks. If you don''t have enough stamina for a blow, your guard breaks and the blow hits you in full.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A block costs 0.9 stamina for each point of damage, with most weapons. When your guard breaks, the blow deals full damage, your stamina empties, and you are stuck for 0.8 seconds (a normal flinch is 0.35 seconds).


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A blocked blow that costs more stamina than you have. If you have a ward, it takes the blow instead.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Watch your stamina while you guard. Against long strings of blows, dodge some of them or perfect-guard them, since a perfect guard costs nothing. Stop attacking and let your stamina refill before you block a big blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The guard arc pulses red, a warning flash shows, and the words "Guard broken" appear.'
  - Id: perfect-guard
    Title: Perfect guard
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Press the guard button just before a blow lands. The blow is pushed aside and does nothing, and the attacker loses its balance.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The blow must land within 0.18 seconds of your press, with most weapons. Pressing guard again within 0.4 seconds of your last press doesn''t give a perfect guard, so mashing the button doesn''t work. A perfect guard costs no stamina and no health, gives 15 mana, and adds a lot of stagger to the attacker (40 to 55 on the castle''s enemies). Clarity makes the timing window 50% longer.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A fresh press of the guard button, timed to the blow. It also blocks red attacks, which holding your guard can''t stop.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Learn each enemy''s rhythm and press at the moment the blow hits, not when it winds up. It is the best way to get mana and stagger in the game, and many marks reward it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The guard arc flashes gold, a ring and a pale flash burst from it, the world freezes for an instant, and the words "Perfect guard" appear.'
  - Id: perfect-dodge
    Title: Perfect dodge
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Roll through a blow at the last moment, and time slows down for a moment, giving you mana and an opening.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A roll goes 4.5 metres in 0.35 seconds and costs 25 stamina. Blows pass through you from 0.03 seconds to 0.25 seconds into the roll. A blow that passes through you in the first 0.13 seconds is a perfect dodge. It gives 12 mana and slows time to 0.3 speed for 0.35 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A dodge started right as a blow arrives. You can dodge red attacks this way like any other.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Roll into or across the attack, not away from it, so you come out close enough to hit back. Tailwind halves the dodge''s cost, sends it 30% farther and keeps you safe 25% longer.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The world slows for a moment, and the words "Perfect dodge" appear.'
  - Id: red-attacks
    Title: Red attacks
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Some enemy attacks can''t be guarded. Holding your guard does nothing against them: the blow hits you in full, and you flinch.


      <b>The numbers.</b> They hit as hard as any other attack, and often harder. The lunges, charges, leaps, bolts and roars of the castle''s enemies and gods are among them.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> The enemy chooses them, often from a distance or as the last blow of a string.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Dodge them, or perfect-guard them, since a perfect guard blocks even these. A ward also takes one. Many of them leave the attacker open for a long time afterward, so hit back.


      <b>How it looks.</b> As the enemy commits to the attack, a red flash shows over its head instead of the white glint of a blow you can guard. The warning shape on the ground is drawn in red.'
  - Id: critical
    Title: Critical blows
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A critical hit is a weapon blow that lands for one and a half times its damage.


      <b>The numbers.</b> One and a half times the damage. You start with no chance of a critical hit. Some marks add a chance, some make certain blows always critical, and Marked makes every weapon blow critical for three blows.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Weapon blows on a Marked enemy, the chance that marks give, and blows that marks promise (for example, a counter cut on an enemy that is starting its attack).


      <b>How to use it.</b> Put your hardest blows on a critical hit: a heavy blow or a finisher on a Marked enemy. Critical hits stack with Sundered and with the surge.


      <b>How it looks.</b> A gold star with long points, and a spray of gold sparks where the blow lands.'
  - Id: wards
    Title: Wards
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A ward blocks one blow that would have hurt you. It is used up instead of your health.


      <b>The numbers.</b> You can hold up to 3 wards. Each one takes a blow you didn''t guard, or a blow that would have broken your guard. Wards stay until they are used up, until you rest, or until you change element.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Glaze (Ice''s surge, 40 mana, lasts 12 seconds) gives one ward when you light it. Some marks give wards too.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Light Glaze before a big attack you can''t avoid, or when you want to trade blows. A ward also takes a red attack, which holding your guard can''t.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The status line next to your bars reads "Ward" or "Wards 2". When one is used up, a pale blue ring and sparks burst in front of you with a clang, and the word "Warded" appears.'
  - Id: hurt
    Title: Hurt (the flinch)
    Group: States
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A blow that lands makes you flinch. You are pushed back and can''t act for a moment.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A flinch lasts 0.35 seconds and pushes you back 1.1 metres. A broken guard holds you for 0.8 seconds. A blow that arrives while you''re flinching is ignored, so two enemies can''t keep you down.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Any blow that isn''t blocked, dodged or stopped by a ward. The flinch cuts off your attack, spell, dodge or drink, and drops your guard. Some heavy moves are steady: while you wind them up and swing, a small blow still hurts you but doesn''t stop them.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Don''t start a slow drink or a long string of blows next to an enemy that is about to swing. Enemies flinch too: most at every blow, some only after a few light blows.


      <b>How it looks.</b> A red flash at the edges of the screen, a spray from the wound, and a shake of the camera.'
  - Id: health
    Title: Health
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Your life. It drops when blows land, partly turns grey when you block, and comes back from flasks, from some marks and from resting.


      <b>The numbers.</b> You start with 100. Some marks raise it. Below 30%, the edges of the screen turn dark red, and they get darker below 15%.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Unblocked blows take it away for good. Blocked blows turn some of it grey (see Grey health). Flasks, Seeded enemies, Cursed kills, Regrowth, Hunger and Fever give it back. A rest fire fills it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Win back grey health by attacking, and only drink when you''re safe. In the playtest, falling in battle sends you back to your last rest fire with nothing lost, so feel free to try risky things.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Your health bar in the corner of the screen. What a blow just took stays pale for a moment before it drains, so you can see how big the blow was.'
  - Id: stamina
    Title: Stamina
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> What you spend to attack, dodge and block. When it runs out, you can''t dodge, and the next blocked blow breaks your guard.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 100. It refills at 30 a second, starting 0.8 seconds after you last spent any. A dodge costs 25. Attacks cost about 12 each, more for the heavier weapons. A block costs 0.9 for each point of damage, with most weapons. A broken guard empties it.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Attacking, dodging and blocking use it up. Waiting refills it. A rest fire fills it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Never empty it completely in front of an enemy. Keep enough for one dodge. Short pauses between strings of blows let it refill.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The bar under your health bar.'
  - Id: mana
    Title: Mana
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> The power behind your big moves: the surge, each element''s one larger spell, the duo spells that are built so far, and your weapon skill. Basic spells never cost mana. They have waits instead (see Spell cooldowns).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to 100. You enter every area with half (50). Out of a fight, mana below half goes up by 5 a second until it reaches half; mana above half stays where it is. A fight always starts with at least half: if you have less when an enemy notices you, it is raised to 50 right away. A fight ends when no enemy within 12 metres has noticed you for 8 seconds. Weapon blows that land give 3 to 16 mana, depending on the move. A perfect guard gives 15, a perfect dodge 12, and a finisher 15. A Marked enemy gives 2 more for each blow. Blocked blows give nothing. Surges cost 30 to 40 (Hunger costs health instead, and has a 25-second wait after it ends). Weapon skills cost 25 to 35, the deep strike 20, and the larger spells 30 to 50 (the built duo spells 40 to 60).


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Landing blows and timing guards and dodges well earn it. An infused strike''s blow earns it like any weapon blow. The surge, the weapon skill, the deep strike and a larger spell spend it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Basic spells are free, so cast them whenever they''re ready. Save mana for the surge or your element''s larger spell. Whatever you earn above half in one fight is still there in the next. Perfect guards and perfect dodges fill it fastest.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The bar under your stamina, in the colour of the element you have (grey with none). On the spell diamond, a price is written in blue under the slot that costs mana, and turns red, with the slot dimmed, when you don''t have enough.'
  - Id: spell-slots
    Title: Spell slots and the spell layer
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> You carry four spells, one in each slot: West, North, East and South, laid out like the controller''s face buttons. Hold L2 (LT) and press a face button to cast that slot''s spell. On the keyboard, 1, 2, 3 and 4 cast them directly. While L2 is held, the face buttons only cast spells: the flask, the weapon skill, the dodge and use wait until you let go. A quick tap of L2 on its own lights the surge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A tap of L2 counts if you let go within 0.25 seconds and didn''t cast a spell while holding it. The surge lights when you let go. If you hold it longer, letting go does nothing. On the keyboard, the surge stays on F, and Left Ctrl is the button for the infused strikes (Left Ctrl with a mouse button). While L2 is held, R1 and R2 are the infused strikes, not attacks.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> At a rest fire, choose Set the Hand, and pick what goes in each slot from the spells of the elements you carry (see The Hand). Until you first set a Hand, the slots fill themselves: each element''s own spell first, then each spell you learn in the next empty slot. Picking a learned spell on the chart and pressing use still puts it in the next slot.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Keep spells with different jobs in your four slots: one to start a fight, one to break a guard, one to finish, and one to escape. You can change the buttons for each spell slot in the pause menu''s controls, for both the controller and the keyboard.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The spell diamond in the bottom-right corner: four round slots laid out like the face buttons, each with its spell''s picture (or its first letters) and its button, the surge in the middle, and the two infused strikes under it. It is always small, and grows while L2 is held, showing each spell''s name. The buttons are named for the controller or keys you use, and follow any buttons you''ve changed.'
  - Id: the-hand
    Title: The Hand and the rest menu
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Your build: the four spells in your slots, plus the surge a tap of L2 lights, chosen from the elements you carry. Resting at a fire opens the rest menu: Set the Hand, The chart (where you spend Shards) and Leave the fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Four slots and one surge. A slot can hold any learned spell of an element you carry, that element''s own spell, or a duo spell while you carry both of its elements. Nothing can go in two slots at once. The surge can be either element''s. You can save three Hands. In the full game, the slots open as you go: West and North at the start, East when the Champion of the Gate falls, and South when the Fire god falls. Each stays open from then on. A closed slot holds nothing, shows as closed on the Hand along with what opens it, and is never filled. In the playtest, every slot is open.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Use a rest fire, then choose Set the Hand. Choose a slot and press use to see its list, grouped by element, with each spell''s role and a short description. Press use on one to put it in the slot right away. The weapon skill button shows the numbers. On a saved Hand''s row, use puts on that Hand, and heavy attack (or a right click) saves the Hand you have on now there. Suggested Hand fills the slots with each element''s own spell, then your learned spells in order. The stick, the d-pad, W and S, the mouse wheel and the mouse all choose; dodge (or Esc) goes back. The world is paused while you do this.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Read your Hand like a hand of cards: an opener, a breaker, a detonator, a counter, and a way to move. Keep a saved Hand for each kind of fight, and swap at the fire. The Hand never changes which elements you carry; you choose those in the hub (in the playtest, at the altars).


      <b>How it looks.</b> The four slots in a diamond laid out like the face buttons, named with your own buttons, each ringed in its element''s colour; the surge in the middle; the elements you carry along the top; and the suggested and saved Hands on the right.'
  - Id: two-elements
    Title: Two elements
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> You carry two elements at once, the first and the second. Carrying the same one twice is called all in. Each spell is cast in its own element and adds its own affliction, in its own colour. Your weapon''s blows only add the first element''s affliction. The second one''s affliction comes from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike, so mixing comes from switching between weapon and spells.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The weapon''s blows only add the first element''s affliction, even if you''ve learned the second element''s root mark. The exception is a mixed branch''s own marks, which add both of their afflictions. Lightning''s Static only builds from your weapon while Lightning is first, but a heavy blow still sets off Static added another way. Marks from both elements work. A duo branch''s marks need you to carry both elements, and a duo spell also needs a slot. The surge you have picked makes your weapon stronger while it is lit. If it''s the second element''s surge, your blows still only add the first element''s affliction. R1 strikes with the first element, and R2 with the second. With one element, or the same one twice, R2 is the deep strike for 20 mana once you''ve bought that element''s Devotion. Before that, R2 is the plain strike again. The mana bar takes the first element''s colour.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> You pick the elements you carry in the hub. In the full game, the hub''s braziers still only give the first. In the playtest, the altars around the rest fire set both. If you carry one element, another altar makes its element your second, and the altar of the element you carry makes you all in. If you carry two (or are all in), an altar starts you over with only its element. You arrive carrying Fire, and each altar''s prompt tells you what it will do before you press. Any spells in your Hand from an element you no longer carry leave their slots, and a surge from that element switches back to the first element''s.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Put the element you want on every blow first. Use the second element''s spells and strike to add its affliction next to the first one. A charged blade carries a spell''s element on your next three blows, alongside your own. Going all in trades the mix for more power in one element.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Each spell, patch of ground and burst looks like its own element, in its own colour. An enemy with two afflictions shows both diamonds under its health bars.'
  - Id: cooldowns
    Title: Spell cooldowns
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Basic spells cost no mana. After you cast a spell, its slot has to wait a few seconds before you can cast it again. Fighting with your weapon shortens the wait for every slot that is waiting.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Basic spells wait 6 to 12 seconds (the tree spells 7 to 12 seconds, each element''s own spell 6 to 9 seconds, and the extra mixed spells 12 seconds). Each element''s one larger spell costs mana instead and has no wait: Meteor Leap 45, Seven Flashes 45, Sky Dance 45, Winter Wall 35, Poured Blade 30, Elder Tree 40, Grave of Swords 50, Daybreak 45, One by One 50. Pawned Heart is paid for with health and waits 40 seconds. Earth''s larger spell (Cairn) isn''t built yet. The duo spells built so far (Ball Lightning, Eye of the Storm and the others) cost 40 to 60 mana. Fighting with your weapon takes time off every waiting slot: 0.3 seconds for a light blow that lands, 0.6 seconds for a heavy blow (once per move, no matter how many enemies it hits), 2 seconds for a perfect guard, 1.5 seconds for a perfect dodge, and 3 seconds for a finisher. The two infused strikes each wait 8 seconds, and weapon play shortens their wait the same way (a strike''s own blow shortens every other wait, but not its own). Hunger, Dark''s surge, waits 25 seconds from the moment it ends. Resting at a fire ends every wait.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Casting a spell starts its slot''s wait. Landing blows, perfect guards, perfect dodges and finishers shorten it. If you press a slot while it is waiting, it says "Not ready".


      <b>How to use it.</b> Switch back and forth: cast, fight with your weapon until the spell is back, then cast again. Perfect guards and dodges bring spells back fastest. Marks that only made a basic spell cheaper do nothing now; the trees will be reshaped in a later phase.


      <b>How it looks.</b> On the spell diamond, a waiting slot is shaded, and the shading sweeps back like a clock hand as the wait runs out, with the seconds left written in it. The slot''s ring swells for a moment when it''s ready again.'
  - Id: charged-blade
    Title: The charged blade
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Magic powers up your weapon. When one of your four spells lands on an enemy, it charges your weapon with its element. Your weapon''s next three moves that land carry the element: each one hits 20% harder and adds the element''s affliction, and the third one does one extra thing, special to its element.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Three moves, each dealing 1.2 times the damage. Each one adds one stack of the element''s affliction (two for Poison), on top of what the blow already adds, or alongside it if that''s a different affliction. A move counts once, no matter how many enemies it hits, and carries the charge to all of them. A blow that hits an enemy''s guard uses nothing up. A new spell that lands replaces the charge with a fresh three. Resting ends it. What each element adds: Fire''s third blow leaves the ground under the enemy burning for 3 seconds. Lightning''s blows each jump to one more enemy within 4 metres for half the damage, with Static. Wind''s blows each shove the enemy 1.5 metres in the direction of the swing, and the third one 4 metres. Ice''s blows each shatter an enemy that is already Frozen, light blow or heavy. Water''s blows each splash everything within 1.5 metres for a fifth of the blow''s damage, and soak it. Life''s third blow throws thorns around the enemy (2 metres, Seeded). Earth''s blows each throw a 2-metre spray of gravel. Metal''s blows go through any guard. Light''s third blow shoots a ray 5 metres in the direction of the swing that Marks. Dark''s third blow adds two more Curses. Poison''s third blow makes the poison bite right away.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A spell from one of your four slots that lands on an enemy within 8 seconds of being cast (the first thing that cast hits). It charges your weapon with that spell''s own element. With Fire and Lightning carried, Flame Wave charges it with Fire, and Thunderclap with Lightning. A duo spell charges it with the first element of its pair. Ground left burning by an earlier cast, the surge, an infused strike and marks'' effects never charge it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Switch back and forth: cast, then hit three times, then cast again while your weapon brings the spell back. A spell''s wait and a charged blade are two sides of the same fight.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The element runs along the weapon''s edge: flames shedding embers for Fire, bolts that jump along it for Lightning, frost feathers and cold mist for Ice, and each other element''s own look and colour for the rest. There is less of it as the blows are used up. Three dots next to the spell diamond, lit in the element''s colour, go out one at a time with each charged blow.'
  - Id: infused-strikes
    Title: Infused strikes
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Hold L2 (LT) and press R1 (RB) for an infused strike: one of your weapon''s own moves, done with your element on it. L2 with R2 (RT) is the deep strike, a heavier version that also costs mana. You only get the deep strike after you buy your element''s Devotion. On the keyboard, hold Left Ctrl and press the left or right mouse button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Each strike button has its own 8-second wait, which fighting with your weapon shortens like a spell''s (0.3 seconds for a light blow, 0.6 for a heavy blow, 2 for a perfect guard, 1.5 for a perfect dodge, 3 for a finisher). The deep strike costs 20 mana. A strike counts as a weapon blow: it gives mana and takes time off your spells, but it never charges the blade and doesn''t use up a charge. The move each weapon uses: the Katana''s drawing cut (done right away, 22 damage, 170 degrees wide), the Massive Sword''s rising cut, the Scythe''s full spin, the Great Spear''s first thrust, the Great Hammer''s drop, the Twin Knives'' crossing cut, the Great Bow''s shot, the Chain Sickle''s snap, and the Fists'' and the Armament''s kick. Each element''s strike, then its deep strike: Fire, Burning Edge (a sheet of flame along the swing and 3 metres past it, 2 Burns on everything it reaches, and the ground burns for 3 seconds), then Burnt Offering (a pillar of flame on the enemy hit: 30 damage, and its burn is set off). Lightning, Flash Cut (a 5-metre lunge through enemies as a line of lightning, 50% more than the blow''s damage, 2 Static; nothing can hit you from the wind-up to the end of the lunge), then Down the Channel (bolts come down along the whole line 0.4 seconds later). Wind, Cutting Wind (a crescent out to 7 metres, 60% of the blow''s damage, 2 Unsteady, a 1-metre push), then Whirl Cut (the first enemy is held in the air for 1 second). Ice, Frost Bite (a spike of ice through each enemy hit: 2 Chill, held for 0.8 seconds), then Ice Through (frozen right away if it has three Chill). Water, Lash (a whip that reaches twice as far as the weapon, 80% of the blow''s damage, Soaked, pulled 1.5 metres in), then Thrown on the Reef (thrown 5 metres away). Life, Grown Into It (2 Seeded and a green shoot), then Briar Root (rooted for 1 second). Earth, Broken Ground (four slabs of rock heaved up in a 4-metre line: 14 damage, 25 stagger, Tremor), then Mountain Cleaver (6.5 metres, 55 damage, 70 stagger, 2 Tremor). Metal, Struck Plate (a ripple 3 metres around the enemy hit, 10 damage, Sundered), then Red Iron (50% of the blow''s damage again and two Sundered on it). Light, Ray Along the Edge (a ray 6 metres out: 10 damage, 15 stagger, Marked), then The Sun on the Blade (the same cut at half strength on every Marked enemy within 8 metres). Dark, Taken (everything hit is dragged 2 metres toward you: 12 damage, 20 stagger, Cursed), then Yama''s Noose (40 damage and 50 stagger to the enemy you face within 8 metres). Poison, Venom Flick (a string of venom 5 metres ahead and a puddle that gives a dose once a second for 3 seconds), then Nine Times Refined (six doses on what the cut hit).


      <b>What sets it off.</b> L2 with R1 or R2 (Left Ctrl with a mouse button). If you press a strike while its button is waiting, it says "Not ready". A deep strike without 20 mana says "No mana".


      <b>How to use it.</b> The strike is your element at your weapon''s reach and in your weapon''s shape: a hammer''s drop puts it in a circle, a spear''s thrust in a line. Use it to start a fight or to add your affliction to a crowd. With two elements, R1 (RB) is the first element''s strike and R2 (RT) is the second''s, and neither costs mana: the strike is how your weapon adds the second element. If you carry one element, or the same one twice (all in), R2 is that element''s deep strike for 20 mana, but only once you''ve bought its Devotion. Until then, R2 does the plain strike and costs nothing.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The weapon''s swing in the element''s colour, then the element along what it covered: flame, lightning, wind, ice, water, vines, stone, ripples, a ray, shadow or venom. Under the spell diamond, there are two small rings, one for each button, showing their waits.'
  - Id: spell-diamond
    Title: The spell diamond
    Group: Spells and cooldowns
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Your spells on the screen, in the bottom-right corner: the four slots laid out like the face buttons that cast them, the surge in the middle, the two infused strikes under them, and the charged blade''s dots next to them.


      <b>The numbers.</b> It is always small, so you can read the waits during a fight. It grows to full size while L2 (or Left Ctrl) is held, showing each spell''s name, and shrinks back when you let go. Each slot shows its spell''s picture or first letters, its button, its wait and its mana cost.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> It is always shown while you carry an element. The buttons are named for whatever you used last: the controller''s (Xbox or PlayStation) or the keys (1 to 4, and Ctrl with the mouse for the strikes). Change a slot''s button in the controls, and its new button shows right away.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Glance at it between blows: a clear slot is ready, and a shaded one is waiting. Hold L2 to read the names before you cast.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Round dark buttons, each ringed in the colour of its spell''s element (with two elements carried, there are slots of each colour). The surge is ringed in the colour of the surge you have picked. The R2 strike is named and coloured for the second element, or for the deep strike. A waiting slot is shaded, with the shading sweeping back like a clock hand. A ready slot''s ring swells for a moment. Costs are shown in blue, and in red when you don''t have enough. The surge''s ring brightens while it is lit, and is shaded while it waits.'
  - Id: flasks
    Title: Flasks
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Your healing drinks. Drinking takes a moment, so drink when you''re safe.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 flasks, each healing 45. A drink takes 0.8 seconds and heals at 0.4 seconds. If a blow or a roll stops the drink before then, the flask isn''t used up. At full health, the button does nothing, so you never waste a flask. With none left, dealing 120 damage earns one back. Wellspring makes flasks heal 50% more.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> The flask button. A rest fire refills all of them.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Drink after a dodge has put some distance between you and the enemy, or while it is staggered. With every flask gone, keep fighting to earn one back. Some enemies come at you when they see you drink.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Flasks drawn next to your bars, one for each you carry. With none left, the first one slowly fills up as you earn it back, and the words "Flask earned" appear when it is full.'
  - Id: shards
    Title: Shards
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> The money for the upgrade chart. Spend them at a rest fire to learn marks on your element''s tree.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Most enemies give 1 the first time you beat them. The stronger gilded ones give up to 3, an elite gives 3, and Narukami gives 5. Marks cost 1 (minor), 2 (root), 3 (major), 4 (spell), 6 (keystone), and 3 to 5 on a mixed branch.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Killing enemies. In the full game, you lose your unspent Shards when you fall. In the lands, the enemies you killed since your last rest carry them, glowing, and killing them gets the Shards back. If you fall again first, they''re gone for good.


      <b>How to use it.</b> In the playtest, resting gives you unlimited Shards and falling loses nothing, so learn anything you want and try every tree.


      <b>How it looks.</b> "Shards" and a count on the screen. Little lights fly from each body to you, and the count grows as they land.'
  - Id: memories
    Title: Memories
    Group: Resources
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> The second kind of money, earned by beating people. It will buy the weapon tree (the Way) and the Body branch, which covers health, stamina, flasks and steadiness. Memories don''t exist yet.


      <b>The numbers.</b> None yet. Memories will arrive in a later phase of the playtest (phase 5), along with the Way.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> The plan is for Memories to come from beating each kind of enemy for the first time, and from elites, named fighters and gods, so you can''t farm them.


      <b>How to use it.</b> In the playtest, Memories will be unlimited, like Shards, so you can try every technique. In the full game, you keep them when you fall.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Not shown yet.'
  - Id: reactions
    Title: Reactions
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> When an affliction lands on an enemy that already has another element''s affliction, the two react. Each of the 55 pairs of elements does something different. The entries below go around the ring of elements: neighbours first, then the pairs two apart, and so on, to the pairs farthest apart.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A reaction happens once, then has to wait before it can happen again on that enemy: 4 seconds for most, and 6 seconds for the pairs three apart on the ring. Some have their own wait (Steam 3 seconds; Cold Short, Red Hot and The Bolt Runs True 5 seconds; Shut in the Dark and The Grudge 8 seconds). Each entry gives its own. The wait is kept separately for each enemy and each pair, so a crowd can react one after another. Afflictions that a reaction adds don''t start a reaction of their own.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> The second affliction landing, added by anything: a weapon blow, a spell, a mark, the charged blade, a strike, or the world (an enemy wet from the river reacts to your fire). The order doesn''t matter, except that Seized and The Grudge need two or more Static on the enemy.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Carry two elements. Your weapon adds the first element''s affliction, and your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike add the second''s, so switch between blade and spells on the same enemy. Many reactions then leave something on the enemy for a few seconds (a crust, a crack, coils) that your next blow, the end of its time, or the enemy''s death sets off: a heavy blow to break or split it, or a few quick blows to rock it or tighten the coils. Wait for the reaction to be ready again, or turn to another enemy.


      <b>How it looks.</b> The reaction''s name rises over the enemy for a moment. Twenty-four reactions have their own look, drawn using both elements on the enemy and on the ground at its feet for about a second. The rest show each element''s own burst together, with a mark on the ground, until their own look is made.'
  - Id: reaction-flashover
    Title: Flashover (Fire and Lightning)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Static meets Burn. Flame carries electricity: the fire on the enemy becomes electrified, and its Static goes off through it right away.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The Static is used up, dealing 7 damage per stack to everyone within 3 metres, and 4 stagger per stack (20 when full). The enemy flinches for 0.4 seconds, which breaks a wind-up (but not a steadfast enemy''s). The Burn isn''t used up and keeps burning. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Burn landing on a charged enemy, or Static landing on a burning one.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Set the enemy on fire with your blade first, then charge it with a spell, or the other way around. The Static goes off without a heavy blow and leaves the fire burning. Use it to catch an enemy in the middle of a wind-up.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Plasma''s Shape mark (Flashover: Corona or Fulgurite, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Fire and Lightning, Flashover uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Corona: nothing is used up. Violet flame crawls over the enemy for 4 seconds, and every 0.7 seconds an arc jumps to the nearest enemy within 5 metres for 4 damage, a Burn and a Static. Fulgurite: a scorched patch 2.5 metres across appears under the enemy for 4 seconds, dealing 4 damage, a Burn and a Static every second.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a burst of lightning where it stands. Not built yet: the electrified flame pulling in other bursts of Static nearby.'
  - Id: reaction-thunder-gust
    Title: Thunder Gust (Lightning and Wind)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Static meets Unsteady. A crack of thunder and wind throws the enemy away from you, and its charge goes off where it lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is thrown 3 metres straight away from you (big enemies less, and enemies that can''t be shoved stay put). 0.4 seconds later, its Static goes off, the same way a heavy blow sets it off: 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack to everyone within 4 metres. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Static landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a charged one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Tempest''s Shape mark (Thunder Gust: Thunderhead or Bolt from the Blue, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Lightning and Wind, Thunder Gust uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Thunderhead: the enemy is thrown up for 1 second, and half a second in, its Static goes off over the heads of the rest, for 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack within 4 metres. Bolt from the Blue: nothing is thrown. Its Static goes off where it stands, and an arc runs through up to three enemies, for 14 damage and a Static each.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Charge the enemy up a lot first, then stand so it is thrown into the crowd. The Static goes off among them.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a burst of lightning where it lands. Not built yet: enemies it hits on the way taking the burst first.'
  - Id: reaction-rime
    Title: Rime (Wind and Ice)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Unsteady. A crust of ice grows on the enemy, and your heavy blow blows the ice out of its far side.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The crust lasts 5 seconds. Your next heavy blow on the enemy breaks it: a fan of ice about 4 metres long past the enemy, away from you, deals 10 damage, 2 Chill and 1 Unsteady to everything in it. A light blow doesn''t break it. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Blizzard''s Shape mark (Rime: Rime Tree or Spindrift, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Wind and Ice, Rime uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Rime Tree: the crust never breaks, and for 5 seconds every blow on the enemy adds a Chill. Spindrift: the ice blows out of the enemy''s far side the moment Rime goes off, at half damage (5 damage, 2 Chill and 1 Unsteady), with no heavy blow needed.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Get the enemy between you and the others, then land a heavy blow. The ice lands on them.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst of ice past it. Not built yet: the crust growing only on the side facing you, so a heavy blow from behind wastes it.'
  - Id: reaction-supercooled
    Title: Supercooled (Ice and Water)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Soaked. The water on the enemy goes still, colder than freezing, and the next blow freezes it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy freezes it solid, no matter how much Chill it has, and uses up the Soaked. An elite gets 3 Chill instead, and a god 2. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on a Soaked enemy (from your Water, or from the river), or Water landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Glacier''s Shape mark (Supercooled: Sea Ice or Hard Water, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Ice and Water, Supercooled uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Sea Ice: the blow that freezes the enemy also puts 2 Chill on every Soaked enemy within 4 metres. This is on purpose: it may start another Supercooled. Hard Water: only a heavy blow freezes it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> One touch of cold on a wet enemy, then any blow: it freezes, ready for a heavy blow to shatter it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the freeze.'
  - Id: reaction-quickening
    Title: Quickening (Water and Life)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Seeded. The soaked seed splits open and flowers right away.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Its Seeded fills all the way (3). For 4 seconds a flower is open on it, and a heavy blow bursts it: you heal 10, and everything within 3 metres is Soaked and Seeded. If you leave it alone, the flower wilts. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Water landing on a Seeded enemy, or a seed landing on a Soaked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Bloom''s Shape mark (Quickening: Floating Flower or Rootlets, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Water and Life, Quickening uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Floating Flower: the flower drops to the ground as a lily 2 metres across that lasts 5 seconds and soaks whatever stands on it, and you heal 8 as it opens. Rootlets: every Soaked enemy within 5 metres has its Seeded filled all the way, and a heavy blow on the flowering enemy within 4 seconds still heals you 10.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Soak a seeded enemy in a crowd, then hit it with a heavy blow. You heal, and the whole crowd is wet and Seeded.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the white flower on its back.'
  - Id: reaction-split-rock
    Title: Split Rock (Life and Earth)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Seeded meets Tremor. Roots dig into the shaking ground and hold the enemy in place, and a heavy blow splits the floor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is held for 2 seconds (an elite for 0.8 seconds; a god for 0.6 seconds in human form, 0.24 seconds in god form, and not at all while its guard is up). A heavy blow while it is held splits the floor, dealing 10 damage, a Tremor and a Seeded to everything within 3 metres. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A seed landing on a shaken enemy, or Tremor landing on a Seeded one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Overgrowth''s Shape mark (Split Rock: Taking Root or Root Web, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Life and Earth, Split Rock uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Taking Root: the enemy is only held for 1 second, and every other enemy within 2.5 metres gets a Tremor right away. Root Web: every Seeded enemy within 6 metres is held for 0.5 seconds and gets a Tremor.


      <b>How to use it.</b> The hold is your chance: land the heavy blow before it ends.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst of earth when the floor splits.'
  - Id: reaction-anvil-stone
    Title: Anvil Stone (Earth and Metal)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Sundered. An anvil stone heaves up behind the enemy, and your heavy blow smashes the enemy into it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 5 seconds, your next heavy blow on the enemy breaks its armour all the way (three notches) and sends out a ring of sparks, dealing 10 damage, 15 stagger and a notch to everything within 3 metres. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a notched enemy, or a notch landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Forge''s Shape mark (Anvil Stone: Ringing Iron or Hammer Scale, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Earth and Metal, Anvil Stone uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Ringing Iron: no stone, and no heavy blow needed. The enemy''s Sundered fills all the way right away, and sparks ring out within 3 metres, dealing 10 damage, 15 stagger and a notch to each. Hammer Scale: no ring. Instead, up to four enemies within 6 metres take 8 damage, a notch and a Tremor.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Shake a notched enemy, then hit it with a heavy blow. The whole crowd gets notched, ready for your Metal.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a ring of sparks. Not built yet: the stone itself standing behind the enemy.'
  - Id: reaction-glint
    Title: Glint (Metal and Light)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Sundered. The light catches in the broken armour and bounces off at the next enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A ray hits the nearest other enemy within 6 metres for 10 damage, 15 stagger, a Mark and a notch. It never hits the first enemy. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A notch landing on a Marked enemy, or a Mark landing on a notched one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Halo''s Shape mark (Glint: Mirror to Mirror or Burning Mirror, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Metal and Light, Glint uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Mirror to Mirror: the ray keeps going through up to four enemies, each within 6 metres of the last, for 10 damage, 15 stagger and a Mark each. Burning Mirror: no ray. Instead, for 5 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy adds two notches and 10 more damage.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Mark a notched enemy to Mark the one next to it too, ready for your critical hits.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a hit where the ray lands.'
  - Id: reaction-diamond-ring
    Title: Diamond Ring (Light and Dark)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Cursed. Darkness covers the enemy, like an eclipse, and a bead of light breaks out at the edge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Half a second later, the enemy takes 16 damage and 30 stagger. For 4 seconds, your blows don''t use up its Mark, so every blow during that time is a critical hit. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Mark landing on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on a Marked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Eclipse''s Shape mark (Diamond Ring: Shadow Bands or Through the Throat, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Light and Dark, Diamond Ring uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Shadow Bands: the bead of light like the plain one, plus a Curse on everything within 5 metres. Through the Throat: the light slips out of the enemy''s far side as a line 6 metres long, dealing 12 damage and a Mark to everything on it. Both shapes keep its Mark from being used up for 4 seconds.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Curse a Marked enemy, then hit it again and again while the ring lasts.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and for a moment, a black disc ringed in gold with one burning bead of light at its edge. Not built yet: the disc staying for the 4 seconds.'
  - Id: reaction-shut-in-the-dark
    Title: Shut in the Dark (Dark and Poison)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Cursed meets Poisoned. A black ring seals the enemy inside like a jar.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 8 seconds, its doses stop fading. When the 8 seconds end, or when it dies, the jar breaks. It deals 5 damage for each dose to everyone within 3 metres, and half its doses (rounded up) spill onto everything it reaches. Then it waits 8 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Curse landing on a poisoned enemy, or poison landing on a Cursed one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Blight''s Shape mark (Shut in the Dark: Swarm or Rot to the Bone, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Dark and Poison, the jar uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both shapes seal and break like the plain jar. Swarm: as it breaks, 2 doses and a Curse jump to the nearest enemy within 8 metres, which may seal a new jar there. Rot to the Bone: 9 stagger every second while the jar is sealed.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give the enemy lots of doses before you Curse it. The more doses, the bigger the break. Killing it breaks the jar right away.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst when the jar breaks. Not built yet: the black ring on the floor, and poisoned enemies that die inside crawling to it to feed it.'
  - Id: reaction-fire-medicine
    Title: Fire Medicine (Poison and Fire)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Burn meets Poisoned. The poison catches fire like gunpowder and goes bang.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A 0.6-second fuse, then the bang. It deals 4 damage for each dose and 6 for each Burn stack to everyone within 2.5 metres, plus 30 stagger, and everyone it reaches is set on fire. Half its doses (rounded up) are used up. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on a burning enemy, or Burn landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Pyre''s Shape mark (Fire Medicine: Fire Lance or Flying Fire, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both Poison and Fire, Fire Medicine uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Fire Lance: after the fuse, a path 5 metres long past the enemy takes 4 damage per dose and 6 per Burn stack, plus 30 stagger, a dose and a Burn. Flying Fire: five small fires land in a ring 3 metres out and last 3 seconds, burning and poisoning anything that walks in. Both use up half the enemy''s doses.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Poison it first, then set it on fire. Lots of poison makes the biggest bang.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the bang a moment later.'
  - Id: reaction-carried-embers
    Title: Carried Embers (Fire and Wind)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Burn meets Unsteady. The wind lifts the fire off the enemy as a spinning column and carries it onward, away from you, setting fire to whatever it reaches.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The column travels 5 metres past the enemy, in a straight line from you through the enemy, in about two thirds of a second. Anything within 1 metre of it catches fire (1 Burn) the moment the column reaches it. Nothing it doesn''t reach catches fire. Where it blows itself out, it drops its embers as three small fires along the last metre or so of its path. They burn for 3 seconds, are 1 metre across, and deal 3 damage and 1 Burn per bite, two bites a second. The fire they add doesn''t start anything else. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Burn landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a burning one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Fire and Wind''s duo branch (Carried Embers: Fire Whirl or Hot Wind, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Carried Embers uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Fire Whirl: for 3 seconds, once a second, every enemy within 3 metres is pulled 1 metre toward the enemy, and everything within 2 metres gets a Burn. Hot Wind: a path 8 metres long, blowing with the wind, puts a Burn on everything in it, and the enemy is shoved 2 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> You aim it by where you stand. Get the enemy between you and the ones behind it, and the fire lands among them.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a column of fire spins up off it and runs away from you down a scorched path, setting fire to anything it passes, until it spins apart.'
  - Id: reaction-sleet-charge
    Title: Sleet Charge (Lightning and Ice)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Static meets Chill. The frost on the enemy charges itself up until a bolt of lightning falls.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 3 seconds, it gains one Static every half second (until full). Then a bolt sets it all off, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger for each stack to everyone within 4 metres, and everything it reaches gets a Chill. A heavy blow sets the Static off early, like always. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Static landing on a chilled enemy, or Chill landing on a charged one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Ice and Lightning''s duo branch (Sleet Charge: Thundersnow or Glass Spear, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Sleet Charge uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both charge up the same way. Thundersnow: when the charge goes off, a bolt jumps on to every chilled enemy within 6 metres, for 7 damage and a Static each. Glass Spear: when the charge goes off, the enemy is Frozen right away for 1.5 seconds, no matter how much Chill it has (an elite gets 3 Chill, a god 2).


      <b>How to use it.</b> Wait the 3 seconds for a full charge, or set it off sooner with a heavy blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the burst of lightning. Not built yet: the bolt falling right away if the enemy freezes.'
  - Id: reaction-capsize
    Title: Capsize (Wind and Water)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Unsteady. Wet and off balance, the enemy falls down.


      <b>The numbers.</b> It falls down for 1.2 seconds and can''t do anything. An elite only staggers (25 stagger), and a god takes 15 stagger. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Water landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a Soaked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Wind and Water''s duo branch (Capsize: Waterspout or Breakers, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Capsize uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Waterspout: the enemy is lifted up in spray for 1.2 seconds instead of falling down. Breakers: it falls down like before, and spray thrown 4 metres behind it Soaks whatever stands there and gives it 2 Unsteady. This is on purpose: those enemies may capsize too.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Free hits: use your heaviest blows.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and it falls flat on its back, lies there, and gets up when its time is over.'
  - Id: reaction-winter-seed
    Title: Winter Seed (Ice and Life)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Seeded. The seed sleeps under the frost and wakes up as thorns when it thaws.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While the Chill lasts, its Seeded stops fading. When the Chill ends (a frozen enemy thawing or shattering also ends it), thorns burst out of it. They deal 12 damage for each Seeded stack (up to 36) to everyone within 2.5 metres and Seed them, and you heal 3 for each stack. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on a Seeded enemy, or a seed landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Ice and Life''s duo branch (Winter Seed: Hard Spring or Heart Under Ice, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Winter Seed uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both sleep like before. Hard Spring: it wakes up twice as hard, dealing 24 damage per seed within 2.5 metres, and fills the Seeded of everything it reaches all the way. Heart Under Ice: waking up heals instead, dealing 6 damage per seed to enemies, healing you 5 per seed, and giving you a ward.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give it lots of seeds, chill it, then shatter the freeze for the fastest wake-up.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst of thorns when it thaws.'
  - Id: reaction-the-ground-gives
    Title: The Ground Gives (Water and Earth)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Soaked. Shaking wet ground turns to sand, and the enemy sinks to its knees.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 4 seconds, it moves and acts at half speed (an elite at three-quarters speed; a god doesn''t sink). A heavy blow while it is sunk holds it in place for 0.5 seconds. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a Soaked enemy, or Water landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Water and Earth''s duo branch (The Ground Gives: Quicksand or Sand Boil, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, The Ground Gives uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Quicksand: the enemy sinks for 4 seconds, and once a second everything within 1.5 metres is pulled 1 metre toward it. Sand Boil: the enemy sinks for 1.2 seconds, then is lifted for 1 second, and everything within 3 metres takes 12 damage, a Soaked and a Tremor.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Sink it, then come in with heavy blows. Each one pins it for a moment. Not built yet: a sunken enemy should be unable to dodge, leap or step back, and other enemies wading in should be slowed too.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and for a moment the floor sinks into a pit around it, wet at the edge. Not built yet: the ring of sand staying around its feet.'
  - Id: reaction-iron-thorns
    Title: Iron Thorns (Life and Metal)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Seeded meets Sundered. Iron thorns grow out through the broken armour and fly off with each blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 6 seconds, each of your weapon blows on the enemy snaps a thorn off at the nearest other enemy within 6 metres, for 6 damage, a Seeded and a notch. There are three thorns. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A seed landing on a notched enemy, or a notch landing on a Seeded one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Graft, the Shape mark of Life and Metal''s duo branch (Iron Thorns: Pruning Hook or Iron Harvest, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Iron Thorns uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Pruning Hook: your first three weapon blows on the enemy each cut off a thorn and heal you 4. Iron Harvest: no thorns are thrown. When the enemy dies or the 6 seconds end, a ring of blades bursts out to 3 metres, dealing 8 damage and a notch to each enemy.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Fight the enemy with the thorns. The others around it take the thorns.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a hit where each thorn lands. Not built yet: one thorn for each notch the enemy had.'
  - Id: reaction-earthlight
    Title: Earthlight (Earth and Light)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Marked. Light breaks up through cracks in the floor under the enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Its Mark is refreshed (three more critical hits). Half a second later, everyone within 3 metres takes 14 damage, 25 stagger and a Mark. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a Marked enemy, or a Mark landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Lit Stone, the Shape mark of Earth and Light''s duo branch (Earthlight: Down the Passage or Lit Seam, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Earthlight uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both refresh its Mark. Down the Passage: a line 8 metres long past the enemy deals 12 damage and a Mark to everything on it. Lit Seam: the light breaks out like the plain one, and for 5 seconds each heavy blow on the enemy flashes for 10 damage and 15 stagger on everything within 3 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Shake a Marked enemy in a crowd. They are all Marked for your critical hits, and the first one keeps its three.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then light bursting up around it. Not built yet: only enemies standing on the star-shaped cracks should be hit (for now, it is a circle).'
  - Id: reaction-black-chain
    Title: Black Chain (Metal and Dark)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Cursed meets Sundered. A black chain ties the enemy to the nearest other enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The nearest other enemy within 6 metres is chained to it for 5 seconds. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A notch landing on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on a notched one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Hidden Chain, the Shape mark of Metal and Dark''s duo branch (Black Chain: Drawn Tight or Shared Wound, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Black Chain uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Drawn Tight: the chain pulls tight right away, dealing 12 damage and 25 stagger to the enemy and to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres, then falls off. Shared Wound: no leash, but the chain lasts 8 seconds, and half of every blow is shared.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Not built yet: what the chain does. It will pass 30% of your weapon damage on either enemy to the other, and keep the two within 4 metres of each other.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the chain between them.'
  - Id: reaction-burning-sap
    Title: Burning Sap (Light and Poison)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Poisoned. The sun wakes up the poisoned sap inside the enemy, and your critical hits raise blisters that burst.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 3 seconds, each of your weapon blows on the enemy (each one a critical hit, since it is Marked) raises a blister. Its poison doesn''t bite during those 3 seconds; it saves up its bites. At the end, they all land at once, doubled, and each blister bursts, putting two doses on everyone within 2.5 metres. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Mark landing on a poisoned enemy, or poison landing on a Marked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Lit Veins, the Shape mark of Light and Poison''s duo branch (Burning Sap: The Light Finds Them or Blistered, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Burning Sap uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. The Light Finds Them: works like the plain one, and every poisoned enemy within 8 metres is Marked too. Blistered: for 3 seconds, each weapon blow on the enemy splashes two doses on everything within 2.5 metres right away. At the end, the saved-up bites land at once, doubled.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Land the Mark''s three blows within the 3 seconds for three blisters.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst of poison at the end.'
  - Id: reaction-smoulder
    Title: Smoulder (Dark and Fire)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Cursed meets Burn. The fire sinks out of sight inside the enemy and grows stronger, then flares up.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While the Curse lasts, its Burn keeps hurting, stops fading, and gains one stack every 2 seconds (every 3 seconds on a god), until full. When the Curse ends, or the enemy dies, it flares up, dealing 10 damage for each Burn stack to everyone within 3 metres and setting them on fire. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Curse landing on a burning enemy, or Burn landing on a Cursed one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Banked Coals, the Shape mark of Dark and Fire''s duo branch (Smoulder: Night Sun or Coal Seam, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Smoulder uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Night Sun: works like the plain one, and each of your blows on the enemy heals you 1 for each Burn on it. Coal Seam: the fire goes into the floor as a patch 2.5 metres across that lasts 8 seconds and Curses whatever stands on it, so burning enemies smoulder.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Set it on fire, Curse it, and leave it. The longer the Curse lasts, the bigger the flare. Killing it makes it flare right away.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the flare. Not built yet: the fire hidden as a dark glow until it flares up.'
  - Id: reaction-nerve-fire
    Title: Nerve Fire (Poison and Lightning)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Poisoned meets Static. Each bite of poison gives the enemy an electric shock.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While both last, each bite of poison uses up one Static and shocks the enemy, for 7 damage, 5 stagger and a 0.3-second flinch that breaks a wind-up (but not a steadfast enemy''s). An elite only flinches once every 4 seconds, and each elite keeps its own wait. A god never flinches and takes 11 stagger instead. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on a charged enemy, or Static landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have Struck Nerve, the Shape mark of Poison and Lightning''s duo branch (Nerve Fire: Ozone or Lockjaw, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart), and carry both, Nerve Fire uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both shock like the plain one. Ozone: each shock also adds a dose and a Static to everything within 1 metre. Lockjaw: the third shock holds the enemy for 1.2 seconds.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give a poisoned enemy lots of Static. Five Static means five shocks, one at each bite.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a shock at each bite.'
  - Id: reaction-thermal-shock
    Title: Thermal Shock (Fire and Ice)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Burn meets Chill. The enemy''s body cracks, and the next heavy blow splits it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 14 damage right away, and the enemy is cracked for 5 seconds. Your next heavy blow splits it for 30 damage and 40 stagger. Both afflictions stay. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Burn landing on a chilled enemy, or Chill landing on a burning one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Fire and Ice''s duo branch (Thermal Shock: Hot Shards or Crazing, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Thermal Shock uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Hot Shards: the split also throws shards 3 metres around, dealing 8 damage, a Burn and a Chill. This is on purpose: those enemies may crack too. Crazing: no heavy blow needed. While the enemy has both afflictions, it splits every 1.5 seconds for 10 damage and 12 stagger.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Cold on a burning enemy, or fire on a chilled one, then a heavy blow within the 5 seconds.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, a crack, then the split.'
  - Id: reaction-seized
    Title: Seized (Lightning and Water)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A Soaked enemy with two or more Static. The electricity locks up its muscles, and the water runs off it, electrified.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is held for 1 second (an elite for 0.4 seconds; a god for 0.3 seconds in human form, 0.12 seconds in god form, and not at all while its guard is up). The water forms an electrified film 2.5 metres around its feet for 3 seconds, giving one Static every half second to every other enemy standing in it. The locked-up enemy itself isn''t charged by it. Its own Static isn''t used up. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A second Static on a Soaked enemy, or Water landing on an enemy that already has two. One Static isn''t enough, and the wait doesn''t start until there are two.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Lightning and Water''s duo branch (Seized: Can''t Let Go or Running Water, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Seized uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Can''t Let Go: the enemy is held for 1 second, there is no film, and for 3 seconds each weapon blow holds it 0.3 seconds longer. Running Water: no hold. Instead, everything within 7 metres is locked up for 0.5 seconds and gets a Static.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Charge a wet enemy twice in a crowd. It is held, and the film charges everyone nearby for your Lightning.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the film on the floor.'
  - Id: reaction-windthrow
    Title: Windthrow (Wind and Life)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Unsteady meets Seeded. The seed roots the enemy to the ground, and your next heavy blow tears it out.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is rooted for 2 seconds (an elite for 1 second; a god not at all). It can''t walk or be shoved, but it can still turn and swing. Your heavy blow while it is rooted tears it out: 14 damage and 35 stagger, it is thrown 6 metres away from you, and a torn-up pit 1.5 metres across stays for 5 seconds where it stood, Seeding anything that walks in. The pit counts as something you grew. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Unsteady landing on a Seeded enemy, or a seed landing on an Unsteady one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Wind and Life''s duo branch (Windthrow: Blown Seed or Deep Roots, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Windthrow uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Blown Seed: the enemy is rooted for 2 seconds, and your heavy blow blows its seeds 6 metres with the wind, giving two Seeded to everything they reach. This is on purpose: it may root them too. Deep Roots: the enemy is rooted for 4 seconds, each blow heals you 2, and tearing it out deals 60 stagger.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Root it, step around so it gets thrown where you want, then land a heavy blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a burst of earth as it is torn out. Not built yet: any shove (a Gust, a charge) tearing it out, not only a heavy blow.'
  - Id: reaction-frost-heave
    Title: Frost Heave (Ice and Earth)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Tremor. The cold gets into the ground and heaves the enemy up.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 0.4 seconds later, the floor heaves up under the enemy, dealing 12 damage and 25 stagger, and it is thrown into the air for 1 second (an elite for less; a god only staggers). Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on a shaken enemy, or Tremor landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Ice and Earth''s duo branch (Frost Heave: Sorted Stones or Pingo, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Frost Heave uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both heave the enemy like the plain one. Sorted Stones: every other enemy within 3 metres is pushed 3 metres away. Pingo: 2 seconds later, the mound bursts, dealing 24 damage and two Chill within 2.5 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Hit the enemy while it hangs in the air.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the heave. Not built yet: the frosty mound staying for 6 seconds.'
  - Id: reaction-rust
    Title: Rust (Water and Metal)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Sundered. The wet cuts rust, and the rust spreads.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While the enemy is Soaked, its notches don''t fade, and the rust adds a notch every 2 seconds. While it rusts, its notches can go past three, up to five. Once it is rusted all the way through (five notches), a plate of armour falls off. For 6 seconds, it takes 16% more damage from your weapon, wet or dry. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Water landing on a notched enemy, or a notch landing on a Soaked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Water and Metal''s duo branch (Rust: Seized Joints or Flaking, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Rust uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both rust like the plain one. Seized Joints: the enemy moves a fifth slower while it rusts. Flaking: each heavy blow uses up its notches, dealing 6 damage for each one to everything within 3 metres, plus a notch and a Soaked.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Keep the enemy wet and let the rust work, then come in with your weapon.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: seeing the plate of armour fall off.'
  - Id: reaction-shining-herb
    Title: Shining Herb (Life and Light)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Seeded meets Marked. A shining herb springs up next to the enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The herb stays for 6 seconds. If you leave it alone, it flowers at the end and Marks and Seeds everyone within 3 metres of the enemy. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A seed landing on a Marked enemy, or a Mark landing on a Seeded one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Life and Light''s duo branch (Shining Herb: Bitter Leaf or Seed of Light, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Shining Herb uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Bitter Leaf: for 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy picks the herb and heals you 8. Seed of Light: for 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy bursts a flash of light that Marks and Seeds everything within 4 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Let it flower in a crowd. Not built yet: walking over the herb to pick it up, for 8 health and a critical hit on your next blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then a puff when it flowers. Not built yet: the herb on the floor.'
  - Id: reaction-turned-to-stone
    Title: Turned to Stone (Earth and Dark)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Cursed. Stone climbs up the enemy from its feet.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Over 2 seconds, the enemy slows down (30%, then 60%), then it stands as stone for 1.5 seconds. While it is stone, it can''t walk or start an attack, takes double stagger, and takes half damage from spells. A heavy blow breaks it out right away for 50 stagger. An elite is slowed half as much and stays stone half as long. A god isn''t turned to stone. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Earth and Dark''s duo branch (Turned to Stone: The King''s Men or Grave Marker, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Turned to Stone uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both turn the enemy to stone like the plain one. The King''s Men: when your heavy blow breaks it out, a Curse and a Tremor jump to the nearest enemy within 5 metres. Grave Marker: if it dies while it is stone, a patch stays for 10 seconds and Curses whatever stands in it every 2 seconds.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Wait for the stone, then hit it with a heavy blow. That''s often enough to break its balance.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the stone climbing up its legs.'
  - Id: reaction-quicksilver
    Title: Quicksilver (Metal and Poison)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Sundered meets Poisoned. Liquid mercury runs into the cracks in the armour, and every blow makes the poison bite.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While the enemy has both, each of your weapon blows makes its poison bite right away, for all its doses, on top of its normal beat (at most once every half second on each enemy). The doses aren''t used up. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A notch landing on a poisoned enemy, or poison landing on a notched one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Metal and Poison''s duo branch (Quicksilver: Heavy Silver or Running Silver, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Quicksilver uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both keep the bites on each blow. Heavy Silver: the enemy moves 30% slower, and a silver puddle 1.5 metres across stays for 6 seconds, poisoning and notching anything that steps in. Running Silver: when the enemy dies, four doses and two notches run to the nearest enemy within 6 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give it lots of doses and keep hitting. Every blow is a bite.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a bite with each blow.'
  - Id: reaction-kindling-point
    Title: Kindling Point (Light and Fire)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Burn. The light gathers into one burning point, like sunlight through a magnifying glass.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Each of your weapon blows on the enemy (a critical hit, since it is Marked) adds one Burn stack. The third one sets it on fire: all its Burn bursts out at once, dealing 10 damage for each stack, and it keeps burning with two stacks. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Mark landing on a burning enemy, or Burn landing on a Marked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Light and Fire''s duo branch (Kindling Point: Burning Glass or The Fire Is Hungry, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Kindling Point uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Burning Glass: after the burst, the point stays for 4 seconds, adding a Burn stack each second. The Fire Is Hungry: the third blow''s flame jumps to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres, dealing 10 damage per Burn, Marking it, and giving it two Burn stacks.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Three quick blows: the third one is the payoff.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the flame bursting out on the third blow.'
  - Id: reaction-the-grudge
    Title: The Grudge (Dark and Lightning)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> A Cursed enemy with two or more Static. A black storm cloud gathers over its head.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The cloud waits over the enemy for 8 seconds. The enemy''s next blow at you, whether it lands, is guarded, or is blocked by a ward, calls the cloud down. All its Static goes off at double strength, for 14 damage per stack within 4 metres, and the enemy is held for 0.6 seconds. Then it waits 8 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A second Static on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on an enemy that already has two. One Static isn''t enough, and the wait doesn''t start until there are two.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Dark and Lightning''s duo branch (The Grudge: First Strike or Ash Plume, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, The Grudge uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. First Strike: your first heavy blow on the enemy calls the cloud down, setting off its Static at double strength and holding it for 0.6 seconds. Ash Plume: when the enemy next attacks you, or its 6 seconds run out, one bolt falls around it for each weapon blow you landed, for 10 damage and a Static each, and its Static goes off within 4 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Charge a Cursed enemy, then let it swing. Guard the blow, or take it, and the cloud strikes back. Not built yet: the cloud drifting to the next enemy if the enemy under it dies first.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the cloud itself.'
  - Id: reaction-ill-wind
    Title: Ill Wind (Poison and Wind)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Poisoned meets Unsteady. The enemy''s poison comes loose like dust.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While the enemy has both, your heavy blow on it shoves it, and all its poison comes off at once in a cloud behind it, away from you. The cloud is poisoned grit 1.2 metres wide that stays for 3 seconds and gives a dose once a second to everyone in it. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Poison and Wind''s duo branch (Ill Wind: Dust on the Blade or Dust Bank, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Ill Wind uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Dust on the Blade: the heavy blow doesn''t make a cloud. Instead, your next three weapon blows on the enemy each add a dose and an Unsteady. Dust Bank: a cloud like the plain one, plus a line of dust 8 metres long blowing with the wind, which stays for 4 seconds and makes whatever stands in it Unsteady.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Stand so the cloud lands on the other enemies, then land a heavy blow.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the cloud on the floor. Not built yet: every shove and throw knocking the poison loose, the cloud spreading along the enemy''s path, and the doses shared among the enemies it settles on.'
  - Id: reaction-steam
    Title: Steam (Fire and Water)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Burn meets Soaked. The water on the burning body turns to steam in a scalding puff.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The Soaked is used up, along with half the Burn (rounded up). The puff deals 8 damage, plus 4 more for each Burn it took, and 15 stagger, to everyone within 2.5 metres. Then it waits 3 seconds, the shortest wait of all.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Fire landing on a Soaked enemy (from your Water, or water in the world), or Water landing on a burning one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Fire and Water''s duo branch (Two Sisters: Blind Steam or Scald, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Steam uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Blind Steam: the cloud of steam stays low, 3 metres around the enemy, for 4 seconds, stinging for 3 damage a second. Enemies in it when it forms can only see 2 metres and swing a fifth slower. Scald: no cloud, and the Burn stays. A burst deals 14 damage plus 6 for each Burn, and 30 stagger, and throws everything within 2.5 metres 1.5 metres away.


      <b>How to use it.</b> More Burn makes a bigger puff, but Steam takes half of it. Soak a crowd and make them steam one by one. Its short wait means it comes back often.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a burst of water where it stands.'
  - Id: reaction-lightning-flower
    Title: Lightning Flower (Lightning and Life)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Static meets Seeded. A red, fern-shaped pattern of lightning blooms on the enemy''s skin.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The fern throws a bolt to the nearest other Seeded enemy within 8 metres, for 8 damage, 10 stagger and a Static. The enemy keeps both afflictions. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Static landing on a Seeded enemy, or a seed landing on a charged one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Lightning and Life''s duo branch (Field or Oak: The Rice Wakes or Struck Oak, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Lightning Flower uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. The Rice Wakes: the fern throws its bolt like the plain one, and a field of shoots grows 3 metres around the enemy for 6 seconds, adding a Static every second to whatever stands in it. Struck Oak: no fern. Instead, a burst within 3 metres deals 16 damage, plus 5 for each Static and Seed the enemy had, and uses both up.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Seed two enemies, then charge one of them.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a hit where the bolt lands. Not built yet: the things you grew (briars, saplings, lilies) throwing bolts into it.'
  - Id: reaction-logan-stone
    Title: Logan Stone (Wind and Earth)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Unsteady meets Tremor. The enemy rocks back and forth on its feet like a balanced boulder, and your third blow knocks it down.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 3 seconds, it rocks. Your blows break its balance faster (the first 1.25 times as fast, the next 1.5 times). The third weapon blow knocks it down hard for 1.8 seconds, and its fall deals 20 damage, 30 stagger and a Tremor within 3 metres. An elite rocks and then staggers (40 stagger). A god only takes the extra stagger. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Unsteady landing on a shaken enemy, or Tremor landing on an Unsteady one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Wind and Earth''s duo branch (Grit or Drift: Sandblast or Drifted Over, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Logan Stone uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both make the enemy rock and knock it down on your third blow, like the plain one. Sandblast: the dust from the fall blows on, away from you, dealing 6 damage, 15 stagger and an Unsteady to the enemies behind it, which may set them rocking. Drifted Over: the enemy stays down 1 second longer, and enemies within 2 metres have to wade through sand at 60% speed.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Three blows within 3 seconds, and your weapon knocks it down.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the fall: it lies flat on its back, and gets up when its time is over.'
  - Id: reaction-cold-short
    Title: Cold Short (Ice and Metal)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Sundered. Frost fills the notches, and the armour turns brittle.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is brittle for 5 seconds, and its notches don''t fade. Your next heavy blow cracks the plates of armour off. For 6 seconds, it takes 25% more damage from your weapon, and its notches stay (for two minutes, for now). Then it waits 5 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on a notched enemy, or a notch landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Ice and Metal''s duo branch (Plate or Snap: Shed Plate or Snapped Blade, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Cold Short uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Shed Plate: brittle and cracked like the plain one, and the cracked plate falls as black ice 2 metres across for 4 seconds. Enemies within 1.2 metres slip and fall flat for 0.6 seconds. Snapped Blade: the armour doesn''t crack. Instead, for 5 seconds, its notches stay and its own blows deal half damage.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Chill a notched enemy, then hit it with a heavy blow, then keep fighting it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the plates of armour falling off.'
  - Id: reaction-caught-light
    Title: Caught Light (Water and Light)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Marked. A net of light falls on the wet floor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 4 seconds, the net covers 3 metres around the enemy. Everyone in it is Marked, and everyone standing in it when it falls moves a quarter slower for the 4 seconds (gods aren''t slowed). The Mark that made it isn''t used up. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Water landing on a Marked enemy, or a Mark landing on a Soaked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Water and Light''s duo branch (Net or Eye: Thrown Wide or The Fish''s Eye, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Caught Light uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Thrown Wide: the net is 5 metres across for 4 seconds, and when it ends, everything within 2.5 metres is pulled 2 metres toward its enemy. The Fish''s Eye: no net. Instead, your next weapon blow on the enemy within 6 seconds is a critical hit for three times the damage, uses up the Mark, and hits for 12 behind it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Soak the crowd, then Mark one of them. All of them are Marked for your critical hits, and slowed.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the net on the floor. Not built yet: enemies who walk onto the net later being slowed too.'
  - Id: reaction-girdled
    Title: Girdled (Life and Dark)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Seeded meets Cursed. Black fig roots wrap around the enemy and squeeze tighter with each blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> One coil of roots wraps it right away. It moves 25% slower (gods aren''t slowed) and takes 6% more damage from your weapon. Each of your weapon blows pulls the roots one coil tighter (6% more damage each time) and heals you 1. At four coils (your third blow), it is girdled: held for 1.5 seconds while the roots crush it (an elite for 0.6 seconds; a god for 0.45 seconds in human form, 0.18 seconds in god form, and not at all while its guard is up). It takes 20 damage and 40 stagger, and you heal 4. The roots wait 8 seconds for your blows. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A seed landing on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on a Seeded one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Life and Dark''s duo branch (Flowers or Fig: Corpse Flowers or Fig in the Crown, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Girdled uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both wrap and crush like the plain one. Corpse Flowers: when the roots let go, three pale flowers grow where the enemy stood for 8 seconds, and count as things you grew. Fig in the Crown: an enemy that dies in the roots Seeds and Curses the nearest enemy within 5 metres, which may start getting wrapped up too.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Three blows: each one weakens it more and heals you, and the third one crushes it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the crush. Not built yet: the enemy being unable to dodge or leap while it''s wrapped up, and the hollow tree trunk left where it dies.'
  - Id: reaction-chokedamp
    Title: Chokedamp (Earth and Poison)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Poisoned. The cracked ground breathes out a heavy poison gas that flows to the other shaken enemies.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The gas pools under the enemy and flows to up to three other shaken enemies within 7 metres. For 5 seconds, each one in it gets a dose every second and moves 20% slower (gods aren''t slowed). Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a poisoned enemy, or poison landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Earth and Poison''s duo branch (Damp or Ore: Blackdamp or Red Ore, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Chokedamp uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Blackdamp: the gas doesn''t flow. Instead, a pool 3.5 metres wide stays for 7 seconds, giving a dose each second to whatever stands in it, and slowing enemies in it when it forms. Red Ore: no gas. Instead, your next heavy blow on the enemy within 4 seconds bursts the ore within 3 metres, dealing 14 damage plus 3 for each dose, three doses and 30 stagger.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Shake the crowd first, then poison one of them. The gas finds them all.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the pools of gas staying on the floor where they settle.'
  - Id: reaction-red-hot
    Title: Red Hot (Metal and Fire)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Sundered meets Burn. The notched armour heats up until it glows red.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 5 seconds, each heavy blow on the enemy throws sparks out of its far side, away from you, in a cone about 3 metres long. Each enemy in it takes 6 damage and a Burn. While it glows, its own Burn bites twice as hard. Then it waits 5 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A notch landing on a burning enemy, or Burn landing on a notched one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Metal and Fire''s duo branch (Slag or Shirt: Slag or The Burning Shirt, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Red Hot uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Slag: for 5 seconds, the enemy drips a pool of melted metal each second. Each pool is 0.8 metres across and burns for 3 seconds, dealing 3 damage and a Burn each second to everything in it except the enemy dripping it. The Burning Shirt: no sparks. Instead, for 5 seconds, the enemy can''t guard, and it gets a Burn every 1.5 seconds. In both shapes, its Burn bites twice as hard for those 5 seconds.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Get it between you and the others and hit it with heavy blows. Each heavy blow sets them on fire.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then sparks past it with each heavy blow.'
  - Id: reaction-the-bolt-runs-true
    Title: The Bolt Runs True (Light and Lightning)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Static. A thread of light rises from the enemy, then a straight bolt of lightning runs from it along a path straight away from you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Half a second later comes the path: 8 metres long and 1.2 metres wide, starting at the enemy. The enemy and every enemy on the path take 20 damage, 35 stagger and a Static, each hit once. Then the enemy''s own Static goes off at full strength (7 damage and 5 stagger for each stack to everyone within 4 metres). The Mark isn''t used up. Then it waits 5 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Mark landing on a charged enemy, or Static landing on a Marked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Light and Lightning''s duo branch (Flat or Again: Laid Flat or Struck Again, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, The Bolt Runs True uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Laid Flat: 0.5 seconds later, it deals 18 damage and two Static to the enemy and to the nearest other Marked enemy within 10 metres, then the enemy''s Static goes off. Struck Again: no big bolt. Instead, while its Mark lasts, each weapon blow on the enemy calls down a bolt for 9 damage, 15 stagger and a Static.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Charge it up a lot before you Mark it, or Mark it before you charge it. The bolt sets off the charge for you. Stand so the others are behind it, and the bolt runs through them.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the thread and the bolt on it. Then the bolt comes down again and again as it runs away from you down the path, followed by the burst of Static.'
  - Id: reaction-breath-taken
    Title: Breath Taken (Dark and Wind)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Cursed meets Unsteady. A black gust pulls the breath out of the enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The enemy is winded for 1.5 seconds (an elite for 0.6 seconds). It can''t start an attack (but it finishes one it has already swung), and it takes 30% more stagger. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Curse landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a Cursed one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Dark and Wind''s duo branch (Storm or Breath: Black Storm or Second Wind, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Breath Taken uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both wind the enemy like the plain one. Black Storm: the two nearest other enemies within 6 metres are winded too, and get a Curse and an Unsteady. Second Wind: the breath comes to you, giving you 25 stamina and 8 mana, and your dodges cost no stamina for 3 seconds.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Wind the enemy, then break its balance while it can''t fight back.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the breath being carried away on the wind.'
  - Id: reaction-venom-rime
    Title: Venom Rime (Poison and Ice)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Poisoned meets Chill. The poison won''t fade, and each bite chills the enemy closer to freezing.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While both are on the enemy, its poison doesn''t fade, and each bite adds a Chill, until it freezes. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on a chilled enemy, or Chill landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Poison and Ice''s duo branch (Floor or Bone: Frost Over Frost or Cold Damage, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Venom Rime uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both keep the poison and add a Chill with each bite, like the plain one. Frost Over Frost: once the enemy freezes, frost covers 3 metres around it for 5 seconds, adding a Chill and a dose each second. Cold Damage: while frozen, each bite deals twice as much, and when the enemy is shattered, every dose goes off at once, for 6 damage each.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Poison the enemy and chill it once. The poison freezes it for you.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a Chill with each bite.'
  - Id: reaction-firebloom
    Title: Firebloom (Fire and Life)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Burn meets Seeded. The heat splits the seeds open, and they grow into lilies made of flame.


      <b>The numbers.</b> The Seeded is used up. One lily for each stack (one to three) springs up 2.5 metres around the enemy and stays for 5 seconds, burning anything within 1 metre (3 damage and a Burn every half second). They count as things you grew. The enemy''s Burn stays. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Burn landing on a Seeded enemy, or a seed landing on a burning one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Fire and Life''s duo branch (Firebloom: Between Two Fires or Ash Bed, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Firebloom uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Between Two Fires: the Seeded is used up, and a pair of lilies grows for each seed, 3 metres apart on either side of the enemy. Ash Bed: no lilies, and nothing is used up. While the enemy is Seeded, its Burn doesn''t fade, and when it dies, a bed of shoots 3 metres across stays for 6 seconds, Seeding anything that steps in.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give it lots of seeds before you set it on fire. More seeds mean more lilies.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the lilies around it. Not built yet: walking past a lily to pick up its fire.'
  - Id: reaction-earthed
    Title: Earthed (Lightning and Earth)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Static meets Tremor. The charge runs into the ground, leaving a fern-shaped burn mark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> All its Static runs into the floor at once, dealing 5 damage and 3 stagger for each stack to every other enemy within 4 metres, plus a Tremor. The Earthed enemy itself isn''t hit by it. The enemy is rooted in place for 1 second (an elite for half that; a god not at all). Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Static landing on a shaken enemy, or Tremor landing on a charged one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Lightning and Earth''s duo branch (Earthed: Thunderstone or Step Current, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Earthed uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Thunderstone: nothing is used up. The enemy''s Static is held for 6 seconds, and your heavy blow on it throws all of it at everything within 6 metres, for 7 damage per stack and a Static each. Step Current: the burn mark spreads to 6 metres and stays live for 3 seconds, dealing 4 damage and 6 stagger every half second.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Charge it up a lot in a crowd, then shake it. The charge goes to everyone nearby.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a burst around it. Not built yet: the fern-shaped mark on the floor.'
  - Id: reaction-sickle-wind
    Title: Sickle Wind (Wind and Metal)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Unsteady meets Sundered. The wind tears off the enemy''s loose armour plates and whirls them around it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 3 seconds, the plates whirl 1.5 metres around the enemy, cutting everything they pass, including the enemy. They deal 3 damage for each shard (one for each notch, plus one) and 4 stagger every 0.4 seconds. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Unsteady landing on a notched enemy, or a notch landing on an Unsteady one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Wind and Metal''s duo branch (Sickle Wind: Sent Downwind or Cut Unfelt, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Sickle Wind uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Sent Downwind: the whirlwind lands as three patches in a line past the enemy, cutting for 3 damage and 4 stagger every 0.4 seconds for 3 seconds, and notching each enemy there once. Cut Unfelt: nothing happens for 3 seconds. Then it deals 32 damage and 28 stagger within 1.5 metres, plus 6 more for each weapon blow you landed on the enemy during that time.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give it lots of notches first. More notches mean more blades. Stay out of the whirlwind, or fight inside it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the whirlwind around it.'
  - Id: reaction-mock-suns
    Title: Mock Suns (Ice and Light)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Chill meets Marked. Two fake suns appear in the sky next to the enemy, the way they sometimes do on icy days.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 4 seconds, your weapon blows on the enemy add two Chill and don''t use up its Mark, so they stay critical hits. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Chill landing on a Marked enemy, or a Mark landing on a chilled one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Ice and Light''s duo branch (Mock Suns: Prism or Lens of Ice, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Mock Suns uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Prism: no suns. Instead, for 4 seconds, its Mark isn''t used up, and your next weapon blow on it sends three hits in a line past it, each dealing 8 damage, a Mark and a Chill. Lens of Ice: while the enemy is chilled and Marked, its Mark isn''t used up, and a heavy blow on it at full Chill deals 18 more damage and Marks every enemy within 4 metres.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Hit it as often as you can within the 4 seconds. Each blow is a critical hit and chills it.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the suns turning with the enemy, and only blows from its sides counting.'
  - Id: reaction-drowned-weight
    Title: Drowned Weight (Water and Dark)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Cursed. The Curse turns the water black and heavy, and the enemy swells up.


      <b>The numbers.</b> It is swollen for 5 seconds. It moves 20% slower (gods aren''t slowed) and takes 15% more damage from your weapon. When it dies, or when a heavy blow lands while it is swollen, it bursts in black water, dealing 14 damage, 20 stagger, Soaked and Cursed to everyone within 4 metres. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Water landing on a Cursed enemy, or a Curse landing on a Soaked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Water and Dark''s duo branch (Drowned Weight: Drawn Under or The Pull of Night, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Drowned Weight uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Both swell the enemy for 5 seconds, like the plain one. Drawn Under: the enemy is held for 1.5 seconds, everything within 3 metres is pulled 2 metres toward it, and it bursts like the plain one. The Pull of Night: no burst. Instead, each of your weapon blows on it pulls every other Soaked enemy within 5 metres 1 metre toward it.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Kill it in a crowd, or hit it with a heavy blow there. The black water spreads both afflictions to the rest.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the burst. Not built yet: only a finisher should burst it (for now, any heavy blow does).'
  - Id: reaction-rot-roots
    Title: Rot Roots (Life and Poison)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Seeded meets Poisoned. Roots spread from the enemy''s feet to the enemies near it, and its poison runs down them.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Up to three other enemies within 5 metres (the nearest ones) are joined to it for 6 seconds, and each one is Seeded once when the roots reach it. Every bite of poison on the first enemy also bites each joined enemy for half. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on a Seeded enemy, or Seeded landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Life and Poison''s duo branch (Rot Roots: Gathered In or Summer Grass, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Rot Roots uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Gathered In: roots reach three enemies within 5 metres for 6 seconds, and each is Seeded. Each bite on one of them takes a dose and passes it to the nearest other enemy, usually the one the roots grow from. Summer Grass: no roots. If the enemy dies within 20 seconds, a puff of spores 4 metres across follows 1.5 seconds later, giving three doses and a Seed to everything in it, and healing you 6.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give one enemy lots of poison, then Seed it in a crowd. One enemy''s poison hurts four. The joined enemies are Seeded too, ready for Life''s own marks.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a puff of poison on each joined enemy at every bite. Not built yet: the pale roots on the floor.'
  - Id: reaction-burnt-mound
    Title: Burnt Mound (Earth and Fire)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Tremor meets Burn. The shaking floor throws up three stones, and the enemy''s fire heats them red-hot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Three hot stones stay 1.5 metres around the enemy for 6 seconds and burn anything that touches them (3 damage and a Burn every half second). Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Tremor landing on a burning enemy, or Burn landing on a shaken one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Earth and Fire''s duo branch (Burnt Mound: Heaped Stones or Fire-Cracked, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Burnt Mound uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Heaped Stones: one red-hot boulder appears next to the enemy for 6 seconds, and your heavy blow on the enemy rolls it 8 metres away from you, dealing 20 damage, two Burns and two Tremors to each enemy it rolls over. Fire-Cracked: the three stones stay for 6 seconds, and once a second each deals 6 damage and a Burn to whatever stands within 1 metre.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Fight the enemy among its stones. Anything that steps on them burns.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the stones around it. Not built yet: hitting the stones with your weapon to knock them into the crowd.'
  - Id: reaction-live-iron
    Title: Live Iron (Metal and Lightning)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Sundered meets Static. The enemy''s broken armour becomes electrified, and a blow on it sends lightning only from iron to iron.


      <b>The numbers.</b> For 6 seconds, each of your weapon blows on the enemy sends an arc of lightning to the nearest other Sundered enemy within 8 metres (never to one without notches), for 35% of the blow''s damage, adding a notch of Sundered where it lands. From there, it jumps once more to another notched enemy. At most one arc every 0.3 seconds. Spells don''t make arcs. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Static landing on a Sundered enemy, or Sundered landing on a charged one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Metal and Lightning''s duo branch (Live Iron: Drawn Iron or Forged Bolt, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Live Iron uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Drawn Iron: arcs like the plain one, and each blow also pulls everything within 8 metres 1.5 metres toward the enemy you hit. Forged Bolt: no arcs. Instead, the enemy''s Static stays for 6 seconds, and your heavy blow takes up to three of them and throws a bolt 10 metres onward, dealing 12 damage for each, 20 stagger and two Static to everything on the line.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Notch the crowd first, then electrify one of them and fight it. Every blow reaches the others. The arc''s notches set up the next arc.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and a hit where each arc lands.'
  - Id: reaction-golden-feathers
    Title: Golden Feathers (Light and Wind)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Marked meets Unsteady. The enemy''s Mark breaks into golden feathers that ride the wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Three feathers fly 6 metres past the enemy, away from you. Each one Marks whatever it touches and makes it Unsteady. The first enemy keeps its Mark. Then it waits 4 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Mark landing on an Unsteady enemy, or Unsteady landing on a Marked one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Light and Wind''s duo branch (Golden Feathers: Eagle''s Sight or Wingbeat, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Golden Feathers uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Eagle''s Sight: no feathers. Instead, for 4 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy is a critical hit and deals 10 more damage. Wingbeat: no feathers fly. Instead, everything within 3 metres of the enemy is thrown 3 metres away from it and Marked.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Stand so the others are behind it. They get Marked for your critical hits.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, then the feathers landing past it. Not built yet: the feathers spreading out across 40 degrees, each stopping at the first enemy it touches.'
  - Id: reaction-stolen-warmth
    Title: Stolen Warmth (Dark and Ice)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Cursed meets Chill. The Curse steals the enemy''s warmth, and each blow draws it into you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> While both last, its Chill doesn''t fade, and each of your weapon blows adds a Chill and draws 2 health into you (up to 8 a second from each enemy; a god gives half). Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> A Curse landing on a chilled enemy, or Chill landing on a Cursed one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Dark and Ice''s duo branch (Stolen Warmth: Cold Shade or The Long Night, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Stolen Warmth uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Cold Shade: the warmth is drawn like the plain one, and when the enemy dies, a patch stays for 4 seconds where it fell, chilling everything within 2.5 metres once a second. The Long Night: you don''t draw any health. Its Chill stays and your blows chill it, and while it is frozen, everything within 3 metres gets a Chill each second.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Keep hitting. Every blow heals you and chills the enemy closer to freezing.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy. Not built yet: the last of its warmth being torn out when it freezes.'
  - Id: reaction-fish-poison
    Title: Fish Poison (Poison and Water)
    Group: Reactions
    Body: '<b>What it is.</b> Soaked meets Poisoned. The water runs off the enemy poisoned, and enemies with lots of doses float up, stunned, like poisoned fish.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A poisoned slick 3 metres across stays for 5 seconds and Soaks anyone standing in it. If the enemy has four or more doses when this happens, it floats up stunned: lifted and held for 1.5 seconds (an elite for 0.5 seconds; a god takes 30 stagger instead). Your next weapon blow on it is a critical hit and uses up two doses for 6 damage each. Then it waits 6 seconds.


      <b>What sets it off.</b> Poison landing on a Soaked enemy, or Water landing on a poisoned one.


      <b>Its shapes.</b> If you have the Shape mark of Poison and Water''s duo branch (Fish Poison: Rock Pool or The Ebb Takes It, 3 Shards, in the middle of the chart) and carry both, Fish Poison uses the shape you picked at a rest fire instead. Rock Pool: the slick is 1.5 metres across and floats the enemy up at two doses. The Ebb Takes It: the slick runs 6 metres away from you as four patches, each 1.5 metres across, and floats the enemy up at four doses, like the plain one.


      <b>How to use it.</b> Give it four doses before you soak it, then be ready with your blade.


      <b>How it looks.</b> Its name over the enemy, and the slick on the floor. Not built yet: other enemies in the slick floating up when they reach four doses.'
  Marks:
  - Id: blackchain.shape
    Title: 'Black Chain: Drawn Tight or Shared Wound'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hidden Chain unlocks two new shapes for Black Chain. At a rest fire, you pick which shape to use. From then on, your Black Chain uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Drawn Tight</b>: the chain pulls tight right away. The two enemies are dragged together and smash into each other. Then the chain falls off. <b>Shared Wound</b>: there is no leash. Instead, the chain lasts longer, and half of every blow you land on one enemy also hits the other.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Black Chain goes off while you carry both Metal and Dark. Black Chain goes off when a Curse lands on a notched enemy, or when Sundered lands on a cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Black Chain. The plain one chains the enemy to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres for 5 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Drawn Tight: the enemy takes 12 damage and 25 stagger, and so does the nearest other enemy within 6 metres. Shared Wound: the enemy is chained to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres for 8 seconds, and half of every blow is shared. After Black Chain goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grasp and Flung Blade to set it up, Dead Weight, One Noose for Two, The Smith''s Net.


      <b>Example.</b> Two shield bearers stand side by side. Drawn Tight smashes them together. A big brute stands next to a small enemy. Use Shared Wound, then cut down the small one, and half of each blow goes to the brute.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Drawn Tight hits both enemies where they stand instead of dragging them together. Shared Wound''s chain does not yet pass a Curse from one enemy to the other.'
  - Id: blackchain.deadweight
    Title: Dead Weight
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When one of two chained enemies dies, the chain drags the other one to where it fell. Then it holds that enemy in place and Curses it again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies while it has both a Curse and Sundered on it, while you carry both Metal and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: every enemy within 6 metres of where it fell is pulled 3 metres toward that spot. Any enemy within 2 metres of the spot is held for 1 second and Cursed. This can happen at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Black Chain, One Noose for Two, Never Sated.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a chained soldier. Its partner is dragged to the body, held, and Cursed, ready for your next blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now it goes off on any death of an enemy that is both Cursed and notched, and it pulls every enemy nearby, not only the chained partner.'
  - Id: blackchain.rust
    Title: Rust in the Curse
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Notches on a Cursed enemy do not fade for as long as the Curse lasts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Metal and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Once the Curse is gone, the notches start to fade as usual.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flung Blade and Sunder (for notches), Grasp (for Curses), Black Chain.


      <b>Example.</b> Curse a brute, and its broken armour stays broken.'
  - Id: blackchain.noose
    Title: One Noose for Two
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When you land a finisher on a chained enemy, it also hits the enemy it is chained to. That second hit does half the damage and half the stagger.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time a finisher lands on an enemy that has both a Curse and Sundered on it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: half the finisher''s damage, plus 30 stagger, jumps to the nearest other enemy within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Black Chain, Dead Weight, The Smith''s Net, Sunder.


      <b>Example.</b> Finish off a chained captain, and its guard staggers from the same blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It jumps to the nearest other enemy, not to the chained partner. Its stagger is always 30, not half of the finisher''s.'
  - Id: blackchain.net
    Title: The Smith's Net
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw a net of thin black chain. A moment later it opens and falls. You only see a shimmer and its shadow growing on the floor. Every enemy under it is Cursed, notched, and held in place, so Black Chain binds them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Metal and Dark. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 16 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands up to 7 metres ahead of you. It falls 0.4 seconds after you cast it and covers a circle with a 2-metre radius. Each enemy under it takes 6 damage and 10 stagger, gets a notch and a Curse, and is held for 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Black Chain and its shapes, One Noose for Two, Dead Weight.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw the net over a pack, then cut through them while they stand chained.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It does not yet chain every enemy under it to every other one for 6 seconds. It does not yet keep them inside the circle for 3 seconds. It does not yet pass a quarter of every blow to the rest.'
  - Id: blight.shape
    Title: 'Shut in the Dark: Swarm or Rot to the Bone'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Shut in the Dark, the jar. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your jar uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Swarm</b>: when the jar breaks, a swarm crawls out to the nearest enemy and seals a new jar on it. <b>Rot to the Bone</b>: while the jar is sealed, the rot wears down the enemy''s balance.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Shut in the Dark goes off while you carry both Dark and Poison. It goes off when Poison lands on a Cursed enemy, or when a Curse lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain jar.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: the enemy is sealed for 8 seconds, and its poison doses don''t fade during that time. When the 8 seconds end, or when the enemy dies, the jar breaks. It deals 5 damage for each dose to everything within 3 metres, and gives each of them half its doses. Swarm: as the jar breaks, 2 doses and a Curse jump to the nearest enemy within 8 metres, which may seal a new jar there. Rot to the Bone: 9 stagger every second while the jar is sealed. After Shut in the Dark goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Miasma and Grasp to set it up, Wither, Feeding Rot, One Death Feeds, The Reaping, Wasting Grip.


      <b>Example.</b> Against a pack, Swarm moves the jar from one enemy to the next. Against a brute, use Rot to the Bone, then land a finisher when its balance breaks.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The swarm jumps across as a streak of poison and Curse instead of crawling. Rot to the Bone''s stagger comes on a steady beat, not with each bite of poison. The balance meter still refills.'
  - Id: blight.wither
    Title: Wither
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Poison hits a quarter harder on a Cursed enemy. It also doesn''t fade from that enemy for as long as the Curse lasts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Dark and Poison. It works whenever poison bites a Cursed enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Poison on a Cursed enemy bites 25% harder and keeps all its doses for as long as the Curse lasts. This mark was changed in Phase 4: your heavy blows no longer add a Curse and poison (the jar does that job now).


      <b>Works with.</b> Curse or Venom, Feeding Rot, Shut in the Dark, The Reaping, Grasp and Miasma, and the infused strikes (R2 adds the second element).


      <b>Example.</b> Poison first: put eight doses on a soldier with your blade, then Curse it with Grasp or your second element''s strike. While it stays Cursed, the poison never runs out, and it bites for 20 each time.'
  - Id: blight.feedingrot
    Title: Feeding Rot
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each time poison bites a Cursed enemy, you get a little of it back as health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every bite of poison on a Cursed enemy. Poison bites on a slow, steady beat.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. You heal half a point for each dose that bit. At eight doses, that is 4 health every 2 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wither (the poison doesn''t fade while the Curse lasts), Curse, Dread, One Death Feeds, Wasting Grip.


      <b>Example.</b> Three Cursed soldiers each have eight doses. Every two seconds you heal 12, even while you guard.'
  - Id: blight.wastinggrip
    Title: Wasting Grip
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You brace yourself and pour a short stream of rot at whatever is in front of you. It bites again and again and adds a Curse. While you have this mark, any of your spells that hits a poisoned enemy also heals you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While it pours, you can turn it slowly with the stick or the cursor. Dodging stops it, and getting hit breaks it. The healing happens whenever one of your spells hits a poisoned enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, 5 Shards, 40 mana. If the enemy is already Cursed and poisoned, the stream seals the jar on it right away, even if the jar normally would have to wait (at most once every 2.5 seconds). The stream is 2.5 metres long and 1.2 metres wide. You brace for 0.3 seconds, then it pours for 2.5 seconds. It bites every half second for 8 damage, 6 stagger and a Curse. An enemy that stays in it takes five bites. A spell that hits a poisoned enemy heals you 5, at most once a second. For now, it doesn''t yet grab an enemy, drink its poison, or throw it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wither, Feeding Rot, Blood Price (pay with health when you run low on mana), Miasma and Grasp (both heal you when they hit a poisoned enemy), and the charged blade (it charges your blade with Dark).


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned brute stands in front of you. Pour the rot over it. The bites Curse it, and every spell you land on it heals you 5.'
  - Id: blight.onedeathfeeds
    Title: One Death Feeds
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a Cursed enemy dies, the poison in every other enemy near it bites right away, without waiting for its next beat. You heal for each enemy bitten.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The moment a Cursed enemy dies, no matter what killed it. At most twice a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. Every poisoned enemy within 12 metres of where it fell gets bitten right away, for its doses. You heal 4 for each one bitten. After that, each poison goes back to its own beat.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wither, Feeding Rot (each extra bite on a Cursed enemy heals you again), Shut in the Dark''s Swarm shape, Curse.


      <b>Example.</b> Five soldiers each have eight doses. Kill one that is Cursed, and the other four each take 16 at once while you heal 16.'
  - Id: blight.reaping
    Title: The Reaping
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your finisher on an enemy that is both Cursed and poisoned sweeps the whole area. A huge blade swings once around it, and time slows down. All the poison in every enemy nearby hits at once. You get half of all that damage back as health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every finisher (a heavy blow on a staggered enemy) that lands on an enemy that has both a Curse and poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. The sweep takes 0.42 seconds and reaches 9 metres around the finished enemy. Every poisoned enemy it reaches has all its poison set off and used up: 9 damage and 4 stagger for each dose (72 damage at eight doses). You heal half the total damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wither, Shut in the Dark (spread the doses first), Devouring Maw, Miasma, Grasp, Never Sated.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers, each with eight doses, stand around a Cursed brute. Finish the brute, and each soldier takes 72 while you heal half of it.'
  - Id: blizzard.shape
    Title: 'Rime: Rime Tree or Spindrift'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Rime. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Rime uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Rime Tree</b>: the ice crust never breaks, and every blow on the enemy adds Chill, so it ends up frozen inside its crust. <b>Spindrift</b>: the wind tears the crust off right away, and the ice blows out of the enemy''s far side the moment Rime goes off. You don''t need a heavy blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Rime goes off while you carry both Wind and Ice. Rime goes off when Chill lands on an Unsteady enemy, or when Unsteady lands on a chilled one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Rime.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Rime Tree: for 5 seconds, every blow that lands on the enemy, from a weapon or a spell, adds one Chill. Spindrift: two bursts land in a line behind the enemy, from 0.7 to 4.5 metres past it. Each burst deals 5 damage, 2 Chill and 1 Unsteady to everything in it. After Rime goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windchill, Sliding Ice, Diamond Dust (Rime Tree freezes enemies so you can shatter them), Cold Front, Gust and Hoarfrost.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute you need to freeze: use Rime Tree, land three quick blows, and it stands frozen. A pack behind one soldier: Spindrift sprays the ice into them the moment the two afflictions meet.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Rime Tree''s freeze does not yet last a second longer, and it is not yet harder to shatter.'
  - Id: blizzard.windchill
    Title: Windchill
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If the enemy you are facing is both Unsteady and chilled, it gets colder on its own, one stack at a time, until it freezes. For now, only the enemy you face gets colder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every 2 seconds during a fight (when an enemy is within 12 metres), on the enemy you face, if it has both Unsteady and Chill.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. It adds one stack of Chill every 2 seconds. At five stacks, the enemy is Frozen for 2.5 seconds (1 second for elites and gods).


      <b>Works with.</b> Rime''s shapes, Cold Front, Sliding Ice, Hoarfrost and Gust, Diamond Dust (gives you a frozen enemy to shatter).


      <b>Example.</b> Hit a soldier with Gust and then Hoarfrost. Keep facing it while you guard. Eight seconds later it freezes, without you landing a blow.'
  - Id: blizzard.front
    Title: Cold Front
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge leaves a blast of ice behind you. It hits the attacker in a cone and adds two stacks of Chill. At the same time, every enemy around you gets two stacks of Unsteady and is thrown back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect dodge, which is when a blow passes through you at the very start of your roll. The cone aims at whoever hit at you. If the blow came from far away, it aims at the enemy you are facing.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The cone is 4 metres long and 90 degrees wide. It deals 10 damage, 20 stagger and two stacks of Chill. Every enemy within 4 metres of you gets two stacks of Unsteady and is pushed 3 metres away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windchill (this adds both afflictions at once), Rime''s Spindrift shape, Gust, Tailwind.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll through a brute''s swing at the last moment. It now has two stacks of both afflictions, and it is pushed away from you before it can recover.'
  - Id: blizzard.sliding
    Title: Sliding Ice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A light blow on a chilled enemy throws back every enemy close to you. Half a second later, ice bursts where the enemy you hit was standing. For now, it doesn''t yet send a Frozen enemy sliding into a wall to shatter; the push and the burst take its place.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When a light blow lands on an enemy that has Chill. At most once every 1.5 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Every enemy within 3.5 metres of you is pushed 5 metres away. Half a second later, the burst hits a circle with a 2.5-metre radius for 20 damage and 20 stagger. It adds no affliction.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windchill, which it comes after on the chart, Hoarfrost, Avalanche.


      <b>Example.</b> You are surrounded. Hit a chilled soldier, and the whole ring is thrown 5 metres back. Now you have room to raise your guard.'
  - Id: blizzard.avalanche
    Title: Avalanche
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A huge wave of snow shaped like a dragon rushes down a path in front of you. It hits everything it passes and carries it along. Where it stops, its jaws close and bite hard. For now, the enemies it carries are not buried and Frozen where it stops.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 50 mana. The path is 9 metres long and 2 metres wide on each side. The wave takes about 1.3 seconds to reach the end. Each enemy it passes takes 12.5 damage, 15 stagger and four stacks of Unsteady, once each, and is shoved along. The bite at the end hits a circle with a 3-metre radius for 25 damage, 30 stagger, a heavy blow and four stacks of Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windchill or Cold Front (it comes after either on the chart), Rising Cut (the bite leaves enemies with five stacks), Sliding Ice, the charged blade (Wind''s shoves).


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soldiers stands on a bridge. Send it down the bridge, and the whole line is piled up at the end and bitten.'
  - Id: blizzard.dust
    Title: Diamond Dust
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When you shatter an enemy, its ice shards don''t fall. They float, spinning around you. Your next dodge throws all of them along your path.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you shatter a frozen enemy, four shards join the ring around you. Your next dodge throws them.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. You get four shards for each shatter, up to twelve. They stay for up to 40 seconds after your last shatter. The dodge throws them along a path 9 metres long and 1.1 metres wide on each side, four at a time. Each group of four deals 24 damage, 16 stagger and one stack of Chill to everything in the path.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windchill, Cold Front, Rime Tree, a heavy blow on a frozen enemy to shatter it, Ice''s charged blade (its blows shatter), Long Stride.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter three frozen soldiers, then roll toward the archers. Twelve shards go with you, and each archer is hit three times.'
  - Id: bloom.shape
    Title: 'Quickening: Floating Flower or Rootlets'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Quickening. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Quickening uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Floating Flower</b>: the flower drops to the ground under the enemy as a lily. The lily heals you and soaks enemies standing on it. <b>Rootlets</b>: as the flower opens, small roots spread through the water and fill every nearby enemy with Seeded. A heavy blow still bursts the flower to heal you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Quickening goes off while you carry both Water and Life. Quickening goes off when Seeded lands on a soaked enemy, or when Soaked lands on a seeded one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Quickening.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes fill the enemy''s Seeded all the way up. Floating Flower: you heal 8 as it opens. The lily is 2 metres across and lasts 5 seconds. It soaks anything standing on it once a second. Rootlets: every Soaked enemy within 5 metres gets Seeded all the way up. A heavy blow on the flowering enemy within 4 seconds heals you 10. After Quickening goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Briar Patch to add the water and the seed, Drowned Roots, Strangling Vine, and the burst of thorns Seeded makes when a seeded enemy dies.


      <b>Example.</b> A wet pack has one seeded soldier. Rootlets fills them all with Seeded, and each one that dies bursts into thorns.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The lily heals you once as it opens, instead of 3 every second while you stand on it.'
  - Id: bloom.roots
    Title: Drowned Roots
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against an enemy that is both Soaked and Seeded wraps it in roots. It is held in place for a moment.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has both Soaked and Seeded.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The enemy is held still for 1.2 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Briar Patch (each adds one of the afflictions), Quickening''s shapes, Great Lotus, and every Water and Life mark that works on a perfect guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Soak and seed a duellist. Then guard its next thrust perfectly. It stands stuck in roots while you land two cuts.'
  - Id: bloom.dew
    Title: Morning Dew
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Drinking a flask gives you a ward that blocks one blow. Also, your blows sometimes throw a short blade of water toward the enemy you hit, soaking whatever it passes. For now, the dew doesn''t yet gather in your health bar.


      <b>When it happens.</b> You get a ward each time you drink a flask. The blade of water comes when a weapon blow lands, at most once every 0.8 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One ward for each flask; you can hold up to three. The water blade flies 3.5 metres at 14 metres a second and is 0.8 metres wide. It deals 4 damage, no stagger, and adds Soaked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wellspring, Deep Well, Drowned Roots.


      <b>Example.</b> Drink right before the brute''s red overhead swing. The ward takes the hit, and your cuts back soak the brute.'
  - Id: bloom.vine
    Title: Strangling Vine
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A huge vine bursts out of the ground under the enemy nearest to you. It hits hard and throws that enemy, and anything next to it, into the air for a moment. If the enemy it lifts is soaked and seeded, the vine drinks up the water (the Soaked is used up) and the seed blooms right away. That sets off Quickening, even if Quickening would normally have to wait. For now, the vine comes up under the nearest enemy, not the nearest soaked one. The double heal from a heavy blow on a lifted enemy is not built yet.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It marks the spot under the enemy and follows the enemy until the vine comes up.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 45 mana. It targets the nearest enemy within 5 metres. If there is none, it lands 5 metres ahead of you. It comes up 0.5 seconds after you cast it and hits a circle with a 3-metre radius. It deals 20 damage and 50 stagger, counts as a heavy blow, and adds Soaked. Anything it hits is thrown into the air for 0.8 seconds. If it lands, it charges your blade with Water.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quickening (a soaked and seeded enemy it lifts blooms right away, even if Quickening would normally have to wait), Drowned Roots or Morning Dew (it comes after either on the chart), Riptide.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute and two soldiers close in. The vine throws all three into the air, and you cut the brute as it falls.'
  - Id: bloom.great
    Title: Great Lotus
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you drink a flask while your surge is lit, a huge lotus opens under you. Inside it you heal, and enemies are Soaked and Seeded. Each perfect guard you make inside it closes a petal around the attacker and holds it in place. When the last petal closes, or time runs out, the lotus bursts. It hits harder for every petal that closed.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Water and Life. Light it with the surge button. Drink a flask while it is lit to open the lotus.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. The lotus has a 3-metre radius and lasts 10 seconds. Every second, each enemy in it gets Soaked and a stack of Seeded, and you heal 2 while you stand in it. There are five petals; each holds its enemy for 3 seconds. The burst has a radius of about 5 metres. It deals 30 damage, plus 30% more for each petal closed (75 with all five), 40 stagger, and a stack of Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Morning Dew (the flask gives a ward), Drowned Roots.


      <b>Example.</b> Light the Great Lotus, drink a flask in the middle of a pack, then perfect-guard five blows inside the lotus. It bursts for 75 on every enemy it holds.'
  - Id: breathtaken.shape
    Title: 'Breath Taken: Black Storm or Second Wind'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Storm or Breath unlocks two new shapes for Breath Taken. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Breath Taken uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still leave the enemy winded, like before. <b>Black Storm</b>: the stolen breath doesn''t scatter. It keeps going as a streak of black dust and winds the next enemies too. <b>Second Wind</b>: the breath blows over to you, and you breathe it in. You get stamina, mana and free dodges.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Breath Taken goes off while you carry both Dark and Wind. Breath Taken goes off when a Curse lands on an Unsteady enemy, or when Unsteady lands on a cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Breath Taken.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes, same as the plain one: the enemy is winded for 1.5 seconds (0.6 for an elite). While winded, it can''t start an attack, and it takes 30% more stagger. Black Storm: the two nearest other enemies within 6 metres are also winded for 1.5 seconds, and each gets a Curse and an Unsteady. Second Wind: you get 25 stamina and 8 mana, and your dodges cost no stamina for 3 seconds. After Breath Taken goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Omen to set it up, Short of Breath, The Last Breath, Blown Through, Wind From the Grave.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack is about to swing. Use Black Storm, and three of them double over. A long fight on your own: use Second Wind, and dodge as much as you like.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Black Storm''s streak doesn''t yet travel with the wind from one enemy to the next. For now, the two nearest enemies are winded at the same time. Second Wind makes every dodge free for its 3 seconds, instead of only the next one.'
  - Id: breathtaken.illomen
    Title: Ill Omen
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge Curses the enemy you dodged and gives it two stacks of Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge, while you carry both Dark and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A Curse and two stacks of Unsteady, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath Taken (one perfect dodge adds both afflictions), Short of Breath, Blown Through.


      <b>Example.</b> Slip past the swing at the last moment, and the enemy''s breath is taken.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it hits every enemy within 2.5 metres of you, not only the one you dodged.'
  - Id: breathtaken.short
    Title: Short of Breath
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows on a winded enemy hit 50% harder. Each one also takes 1 second off the wait on every spell that is waiting to be cast again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While an enemy is winded by Breath Taken (1.5 seconds; 0.6 for an elite), while you carry both Dark and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Your weapon does 1.5 times its damage on the winded enemy. Each heavy blow takes 1 second off every spell''s wait, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath Taken and its shapes, Ill Omen.


      <b>Example.</b> Wind the enemy, then hit it with a heavy blow while it gasps for air.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it keeps its own timer for how long each Breath Taken leaves the enemy winded. Every weapon blow hits harder during that time, not only heavy blows.'
  - Id: breathtaken.lastbreath
    Title: The Last Breath
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a winded enemy dies, its last breath bursts out as a black gust. Every enemy within 4 metres is pushed 3 metres and Cursed.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When an enemy dies while it is still winded by Breath Taken (1.5 seconds; 0.6 for an elite), while you carry both Dark and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A 3-metre push and a Curse on every enemy within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Omen, Short of Breath, Breath Taken.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one enemy while it gasps. Its pack is thrown back and Cursed, ready to be winded next.'
  - Id: breathtaken.blownthrough
    Title: Blown Through
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You stand inside a black gust for 1 second. The first blow that comes at you hits nothing. The wind carries you through the attacker and sets you down 2 metres behind it, and its breath is taken. If you hit its back within a second, that first blow is a critical hit and gives back half the wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Dark and Wind. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, counter, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. When a blow comes, the attacker takes 6 damage, 15 stagger, a Curse and an Unsteady, so Breath Taken goes off. You step to 2 metres behind it. Your first weapon blow on it within 1 second is a critical hit and takes 4.5 seconds off every spell''s wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath Taken and its shapes, Short of Breath, Ill Omen.


      <b>Example.</b> Wait for the brute''s swing, pass through it, and cut its back.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The wind doesn''t yet carry you through untouchable and set you down facing the enemy; for now, you just step past it. The blow to its back takes 4.5 seconds off every spell''s wait, instead of giving back half of this spell''s wait. If no blow comes, the gust doesn''t yet end in a small push.'
  - Id: breathtaken.grave
    Title: Wind From the Grave
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, a black wind blows out from you in the direction you face. It is 9 metres long and 100 degrees wide. Enemies in it are pushed away half a metre each second and made Unsteady each second. A Curse put on any enemy in the wind is carried along with the wind to the enemies behind it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Dark and Wind. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 35 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: once a second, a cone 9 metres long and 100 degrees wide adds an Unsteady to every enemy in it, and every enemy within 6 metres of you is pushed half a metre away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath Taken, Ill Omen, Blown Through.


      <b>Example.</b> Light your surge, face the pack, and Curse one of them. The wind does the rest.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> There is no flowing wind that turns with you yet. The push goes all around you, and a Curse is not yet carried along by the wind.'
  - Id: burningsap.shape
    Title: 'Burning Sap: The Light Finds Them or Blistered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Lit Veins unlocks two new shapes for Burning Sap. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Burning Sap uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>The Light Finds Them</b>: when it goes off, enemies all across the room are Marked too, and their veins light up. <b>Blistered</b>: the blisters don''t wait until the end. Each one bursts on the blow that raises it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Burning Sap goes off while you carry both Light and Poison. Burning Sap goes off when poison lands on a Marked enemy, or when a Mark lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Burning Sap.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes, like the plain one: for 3 seconds, the enemy''s poison doesn''t bite. It saves up its bites, and at the end they all land at once, doubled. The Light Finds Them: every poisoned enemy within 8 metres is Marked. At the end, each blister also bursts, putting two doses on everything within 2.5 metres. Blistered: for 3 seconds, each weapon blow you land on the enemy splashes two doses on everything within 2.5 metres. After Burning Sap goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glare and Miasma to set it up, Seen Through, Nectar After Poison, Bared to the Sun.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned pack: The Light Finds Them Marks them all at once, ready for your critical hits. A one-on-one fight: use Blistered, and three quick blows splash poison onto any enemy that comes to help.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Blistered''s blisters come with every weapon blow, not only critical hits.'
  - Id: burningsap.nectar
    Title: Nectar After Poison
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies with both a Mark and poison on it gives you health and mana.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy with both dies, while you carry both Light and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. You heal 6 and gain 8 mana.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burning Sap, Bared to the Sun, and Daybreak and One by One to spend the mana.


      <b>Example.</b> Clear out a Marked, poisoned pack, and walk away with more health and mana.'
  - Id: burningsap.seenthrough
    Title: Seen Through
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a poisoned enemy Marks it and makes all its poison bite at once.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against a poisoned enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. It adds a Mark, and every dose the enemy has bites right away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burning Sap (a Mark on a poisoned enemy starts it), Bright Venom, Five Needles.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it is Marked, bitten, and its sap starts to wake up.'
  - Id: burningsap.brightvenom
    Title: Bright Venom
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The saved-up poison bites hit 15% harder when they land.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Burning Sap goes off, while you carry both Light and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The bites Burning Sap held back land 15% harder at its end.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seen Through, which it comes after on the chart, Burning Sap.


      <b>Example.</b> Each time the sap wakes up, the bites it saved up land a little harder.'
  - Id: burningsap.bared
    Title: Bared to the Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You tear the shade away from a spot. A sharp beam of sunlight shines down where you aim for 5 seconds. Every enemy in it is Marked. Poisoned enemies start Burning Sap right away, even if Burning Sap would normally have to wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Light and Poison. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 13 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands up to 8 metres ahead of you, covers a circle with a 2.5-metre radius, and lasts 5 seconds. Once a second, it deals 2 damage and adds a Mark to every enemy in it. A poisoned enemy starts Burning Sap once in each cast. Each enemy keeps its own wait, so every poisoned enemy in the beam gets its turn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burning Sap and its shapes, Miasma (poison them first), Nectar After Poison.


      <b>Example.</b> Poison a pack, then shine the sun on it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your weapon blows inside the beam don''t raise more blisters yet.'
  - Id: burntmound.shape
    Title: 'Burnt Mound: Heaped Stones or Fire-Cracked'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fired Hard unlocks two new shapes for Burnt Mound. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Burnt Mound uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Heaped Stones</b>: the three hot stones melt together into one boulder. Your heavy blow rolls it through a line of enemies. <b>Fire-Cracked</b>: you don''t hit the stones. Instead, each stone cracks on its own when an enemy steps near it, so you can drive enemies across a field of them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Burnt Mound goes off while you carry both Earth and Fire. Burnt Mound goes off when a Burn lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Burnt Mound.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Heaped Stones: one red-hot boulder appears 1.5 metres from the enemy and stays for 6 seconds. It burns anything that touches it (3 damage and a Burn every half second). Your first heavy blow on the enemy sends the boulder rolling 8 metres away from you. Each enemy it rolls over takes 20 damage, 20 stagger, two Burns and two Tremors. Fire-Cracked: three stones appear 1.5 metres from the enemy and stay for 6 seconds. Once a second, each one deals 6 damage and a Burn to anything within 1 metre of it. After Burnt Mound goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Timber-Laced, Hot Stones, Brick and Fire (which keeps both afflictions coming), Vitrified Wall.


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soldiers: use Heaped Stones, and hit the first one toward the rest with a heavy blow. A crowd rushing at you: use Fire-Cracked, and fight from behind the stones.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Heaped Stones'' boulder doesn''t yet roll from where it lies. For now, your heavy blow on the enemy sends the roll out from the enemy itself. Fire-Cracked''s stones bite every second, instead of each cracking once when an enemy steps up, for 12 damage and a Burn within 2 metres.'
  - Id: burntmound.timberlaced
    Title: Timber-Laced
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Rock you raise catches fire from a burning enemy within 2 metres. It burns anything that touches it for 4 damage and a Burn every half second. When it ends, it bursts into shards of glass that deal 12 damage within 2.5 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, each time one of your spells hits a burning enemy, while you carry both Earth and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The ground under the enemy burns in a patch 2 metres across for 3 seconds (4 damage and a Burn every half second). Then it bursts into glass shards for 12 damage and 10 stagger within 2.5 metres. At most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burnt Mound, Earth''s raised stones, Brick and Fire, Vitrified Wall.


      <b>Example.</b> Raise stone under a burning brute, and the ground catches fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your raised rock doesn''t catch fire itself yet. For now, any spell that hits a burning enemy puts the burning ground and the glass shards under it.'
  - Id: burntmound.hotstones
    Title: Hot Stones
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Burnt Mound''s stones stay 2 seconds longer and roll 2 metres farther.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Burnt Mound goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The three stones stay for 8 seconds instead of 6. With Heaped Stones, the boulder stays for 8 seconds and rolls 10 metres instead of 8. With Fire-Cracked, the stones stay for 8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burnt Mound and its shapes, Timber-Laced.


      <b>Example.</b> The stones are still glowing when the pack comes back around.'
  - Id: burntmound.brickfire
    Title: Brick and Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows on a burning enemy add Tremor. Light blows on a shaken enemy add Burn. Mixing light and heavy blows keeps both coming.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each heavy blow on a burning enemy, and each light blow on a shaken one, while you carry both Earth and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. One Tremor or one Burn per blow. For your first element''s affliction, it adds nothing beyond what that element''s root mark already adds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burnt Mound (each affliction brings on the other, so the stones keep coming), Timber-Laced, Vitrified Wall.


      <b>Example.</b> Hit one enemy light, light, heavy, and it shakes and burns, back and forth.'
  - Id: burntmound.wall
    Title: Vitrified Wall
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A wall of stone woven with burning wood rises 4 metres ahead of you. It is 6 metres wide and blocks for 5 seconds. A heavy blow on it, or the end of its time, melts it: the stone turns to black glass and bursts outward. The burst deals 30 damage, 60 stagger, two Burns and two Tremors within 3 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Earth and Fire. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It rises after 0.4 seconds and stands for 5 seconds. The burst deals 30 damage, 60 stagger, two Tremors and two Burns to everything within 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burnt Mound (everything in the burst gets both afflictions), Brick and Fire, Timber-Laced.


      <b>Example.</b> Block the pack with the wall, keep them pinned against it, and let it burst in their faces.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Enemies standing where it rises are not thrown aside yet. The wall doesn''t burn anything that touches it yet. A heavy blow on it doesn''t make it burst early yet; for now, it bursts when its time ends.'
  - Id: capsize.shape
    Title: 'Capsize: Waterspout or Breakers'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Capsize. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Capsize uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Waterspout</b>: instead of falling down, the enemy is lifted up in a column of spray. It stays in the air while your blows keep it up. <b>Breakers</b>: the enemy falls down like before. As it falls, it throws a sheet of spray behind it, so a line of wet, Unsteady enemies falls over like dominoes.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Capsize goes off while you carry both Wind and Water. Capsize goes off when Unsteady lands on a soaked enemy, or when Soaked lands on an Unsteady one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Capsize. The plain one knocks the enemy down for 1.2 seconds. Big enemies are only staggered.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Waterspout: the enemy is lifted for 1.2 seconds. An enemy too heavy to lift staggers back instead. Breakers: the enemy is knocked down for 1.2 seconds (an elite takes 25 stagger instead, a god 15). Two bursts of spray land in a line behind it, from 0.9 to 4 metres past it. They Soak whatever stands there and give it 2 Unsteady. This is on purpose: it can make those enemies capsize too. After Capsize goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Gust to set it up, Rolled In, Swamped, Ride the Squall, and Updraft''s juggles (with Waterspout).


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soaked soldiers stands in a hallway. Use Breakers on the first, and they fall over one after another.'
  - Id: capsize.wake
    Title: Wake
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge leaves a line of spray behind you for 3 seconds. Anything that crosses it is Soaked and made Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge (roll right as a blow would land).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Four patches, each 0.8 metres across, in a line 3 metres long, for 3 seconds. An enemy standing in them gets Soaked every half second and Unsteady every second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Capsize (crossing it twice is enough to set it off), Rolled In, Ride the Squall.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a charge. The charging enemy runs through your spray and falls down.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The line lands in front of where your dodge ends, not along the path of the dodge.'
  - Id: capsize.rolledin
    Title: Rolled In
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that capsizes is dragged 2 metres toward you as it falls, so it''s in reach.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Capsize goes off on an enemy, plain or with a shape.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Pulled 2 metres toward you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Swamped (the enemy falls where you can hit it), Capsize''s Breakers shape.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer capsizes at the edge of the fight and slides right to your feet.'
  - Id: capsize.swamped
    Title: Swamped
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Blows on a capsized enemy fill its stagger meter twice as fast.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Wind and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: each blow that lands on an enemy that is both Soaked and Unsteady adds 10 stagger, at most five times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Capsize, Rolled In, the finisher.


      <b>Example.</b> Knock a brute down, and two blows leave it open for a finisher.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it adds a flat 10 stagger to blows on any Soaked, Unsteady enemy, instead of doubling the stagger of blows on an enemy that is down.'
  - Id: capsize.squall
    Title: Ride the Squall
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You ride 7 metres in the direction of the stick on a low, fast sheet of spray. Every enemy near your path takes 6 damage, is Soaked, and gets 2 more stacks of Unsteady. So if it was already Unsteady, it capsizes.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Wind and Water. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. You travel 7 metres. Every enemy within 1.5 metres of your path takes 6 damage, 6 stagger, 2 Unsteady and a Soaked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Capsize, Wake, Open the Gourd.


      <b>Example.</b> Ride through a soaked pack, and leave them on their backs behind you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You can still be hit during the ride. Casting it right after a perfect dodge doesn''t yet skip its wait or make your next blow a critical hit.'
  - Id: capsize.gourd
    Title: Open the Gourd
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, a gourd full of wind hangs at your hip. Each dodge lets out one gust along the path of the dodge. The gust Soaks enemies, gives them two stacks of Unsteady, and shoves everything nearby. Each time an enemy capsizes, you get mana.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Wind and Water. Light it with the surge button. Each dodge while it is lit lets out a gust.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. Each capsize while it is lit adds 1 second, up to 15 seconds. The gust covers a path 6 metres long and 2 metres wide and gives 2 Unsteady and a Soaked. Every enemy within 3 metres of you is shoved 2 metres. You get 6 mana for each capsize.


      <b>Works with.</b> Capsize, Ride the Squall, Wake.


      <b>Example.</b> Light your surge and roll through the fight. Every roll knocks over a line of enemies.'
  - Id: carriedembers.shape
    Title: 'Carried Embers: Fire Whirl or Hot Wind'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Carried Embers. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Carried Embers uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Fire Whirl</b>: the flame rises up on the enemy as a spinning column. It pulls nearby enemies into it and sets them on fire. <b>Hot Wind</b>: instead of embers, one long gust blows away from you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Carried Embers goes off while you carry both Fire and Wind. Carried Embers goes off when Unsteady lands on a burning enemy, or when Burn lands on an Unsteady one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Carried Embers. The plain one sends a column of fire 5 metres with the wind, setting fire to what it reaches.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Fire Whirl: lasts 3 seconds. Once a second, every enemy within 3 metres is pulled 1 metre toward it, and every enemy within 2 metres gets a Burn. Hot Wind: a path 8 metres long and 2 metres wide past the enemy puts a Burn on everything in it, and the enemy is shoved 2 metres. After Carried Embers goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flame Wave and Gust to set it up, Fan the Flame, The Fire Breathes In.


      <b>Example.</b> A ring of soldiers: Fire Whirl pulls them in around the burning one. A hallway: stand at one end, and Hot Wind burns all the way down it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The whirl stays on the enemy, instead of moving around and pulling as a danger of its own. Hot Wind only shoves the enemy, not everything in the path.'
  - Id: carriedembers.fan
    Title: Fan the Flame
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge fans the flames on the attacker. It gets one more stack of Burn. If it is also Unsteady, Carried Embers goes off again right away, even if it would normally have to wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge (roll right as a blow would land) against a burning enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Burn on the enemy you face. Carried Embers goes off without waiting its usual 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Gust and Updraft (they add Unsteady), Long Embers, The Wind Carries It.


      <b>Example.</b> A burning, Unsteady brute swings. Dodge it perfectly, and its embers fly again into the pack behind it.'
  - Id: carriedembers.long
    Title: Long Embers
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When Carried Embers goes off, one extra ember flies 2 metres farther than the rest.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Carried Embers goes off, plain or with a shape, while you carry both Fire and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Four embers fly instead of three, and they land 2 metres farther with the wind. The farthest lands about 8.5 metres away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carried Embers, The Wind Carries It.


      <b>Example.</b> Embers land on the archers at the back, as well as the soldiers in front.'
  - Id: carriedembers.carries
    Title: The Wind Carries It
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows on a burning enemy throw its embers with the wind, as if Carried Embers had gone off. You don''t need Unsteady, and there''s no wait. Each throw uses up one of its Burn stacks. Where you stand when you hit decides where the fire goes, so with every heavy blow you choose where it lands.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on a burning enemy, while you carry both Fire and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Three small fires land 5 metres past the enemy, 1 metre apart. Each is 1 metre across and lasts 3 seconds, dealing 3 damage and a Burn every half second. One of the enemy''s Burn stacks is used up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder (gives you Burns to use up), Flame Wave, Long Embers, Fan the Flame.


      <b>Example.</b> A burning soldier stands between you and a pack. Three heavy blows later, the pack is standing in fire.'
  - Id: carriedembers.breathesin
    Title: The Fire Breathes In
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A ring of flame appears where you aim. The fire sucks in its own wind. Every nearby enemy is pulled toward it and made Unsteady as it comes, and then the fire bursts over them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Fire and Wind. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 16 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands up to 6 metres ahead of you. For 3 seconds, it pulls in everything within 6 metres. Then it bursts for 20 damage, 20 stagger, two Burns and an Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carried Embers (enemies come out burning and Unsteady), The Wind Carries It, Fan the Flame.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it into a spread-out fight, then fight the bunch of enemies it pulls together.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ring doesn''t yet stay for 6 seconds burning what''s inside. The pull doesn''t yet reach 8 metres or make enemies Unsteady every 2 seconds. A heavy blow doesn''t yet throw an enemy toward it.'
  - Id: caughtlight.shape
    Title: 'Caught Light: Thrown Wide or The Fish''s Eye'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Net or Eye unlocks two new shapes for Caught Light. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Caught Light uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Thrown Wide</b>: the net is thrown wider. When its time ends, it is pulled in, dragging everything on it toward the enemy it started on. <b>The Fish''s Eye</b>: there is no net. Instead, the water on the enemy''s body gathers the light into one white eye. Your next weapon blow on that enemy is a critical hit for three times the damage, and it goes through to the enemy behind.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Caught Light goes off while you carry both Water and Light. Caught Light goes off when Water lands on a Marked enemy, or when a Mark lands on a Soaked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Caught Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Thrown Wide: a net 5 metres across that lasts 4 seconds. It Marks anything standing in it twice a second. Enemies inside it when it is thrown move a quarter slower. When it ends, everything within 2.5 metres of its enemy is dragged 2 metres toward that enemy. The Fish''s Eye: for 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy is a critical hit and deals double damage on top of that (three times the damage in total). Its Mark is used up, and a hit of 12 damage and 10 stagger lands in a line 2 metres behind it. After Caught Light goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Water to set it up, Sun on the Spray, Seen in the Water, The Gods'' Road.


      <b>Example.</b> A spread-out pack: use Thrown Wide, and pull them into one pile. A captain behind its shield bearer: use The Fish''s Eye, and hit through one into the other.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Enemies who walk onto the net later are Marked, but they are not slowed or pulled in. The Fish''s Eye''s blow doesn''t yet go through with its own damage; for now, a hit of 12 lands behind instead.'
  - Id: caughtlight.brightwater
    Title: Bright Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The blow that ends a chain of blows adds Soaked and a Mark at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The last blow of a chain of light blows, or the heavy blow that ends a chain, while you carry both Water and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Soaked and one Mark.


      <b>Works with.</b> Caught Light (one chain on a fresh enemy adds both), Sun on the Spray.


      <b>Example.</b> Finish each chain, and the net opens under the enemy.'
  - Id: caughtlight.spray
    Title: Sun on the Spray
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge Soaks and Marks every enemy within 3 metres of where the dodge ends.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge, while you carry both Water and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Soaked and one Mark on every enemy within 3 metres, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Caught Light (both afflictions at once, so the net opens), Bright Water, The Gods'' Road.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge through a pack at the last moment, and a net of light opens under each enemy.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The spray happens around you at the moment of the perfect dodge, not where the dodge ends.'
  - Id: caughtlight.seen
    Title: Seen in the Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While an enemy is on the net, your blows on any other enemy on the net are critical hits, as if that enemy were Marked. No Mark is used up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For 4 seconds after each Caught Light, while you carry both Water and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. When the net opens, every enemy within 3 metres of the enemy it started on takes critical hits for 4 seconds. No Mark is used up by these hits.


      <b>Works with.</b> Caught Light (its net), Bright Water, Sun on the Spray.


      <b>Example.</b> Catch a pack in the net and land critical hits on all of them, saving their Marks for later.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It only works on enemies within 3 metres when the net forms, not on enemies who walk onto it later. It also works with any shape, even The Fish''s Eye, which makes no net.'
  - Id: caughtlight.road
    Title: The Gods' Road
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A rainbow of falling spray arches from you to the enemy you face, if it is within 10 metres. You run along it, and nothing can hit you. You come down on the enemy with a falling cut that deals 18 damage and 30 stagger and adds Soaked and a Mark. The road stays for 3 seconds and Soaks and Marks anything standing in it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Water and Light. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The cut deals 18 damage and 30 stagger and adds Soaked and a Mark, so Caught Light opens under the enemy. Your next heavy blow within 1.5 seconds is a critical hit. The road stretches 8 metres ahead of where you cast it and lasts 3 seconds. Once a second, it Soaks and Marks anything standing in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Caught Light and its shapes, Seen in the Water, Sun on the Spray.


      <b>Example.</b> Run the road to the archer, and catch it in the net where it stands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The road doesn''t yet look for a Soaked or Marked enemy first, before the one you face. Pressing light blow as you land doesn''t yet make the cut a critical hit; for now, the heavy blow after it is.'
  - Id: chokedamp.shape
    Title: 'Chokedamp: Blackdamp or Red Ore'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Damp or Ore unlocks two new shapes for Chokedamp. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Chokedamp uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Blackdamp</b>: the poison gas doesn''t spread to the other enemies. It sits still and deep under the one enemy, making a spot you can hold. <b>Red Ore</b>: there is no mist. The poison hardens in the cracks as veins of red ore, and your next heavy blow on the enemy bursts them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Chokedamp goes off while you carry both Earth and Poison. Chokedamp goes off when a dose of poison lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Chokedamp.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Blackdamp: a pool 3.5 metres wide that lasts 7 seconds. Once a second, it gives a dose of poison to everyone standing in it. Enemies in it when it forms move a fifth slower for the 7 seconds. Red Ore: for 4 seconds, your next heavy blow on the enemy bursts the ore within 3 metres. Each enemy it reaches takes 14 damage plus 3 more for each dose it has, 30 stagger, and three doses. After Chokedamp goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Foul Ground to set it up, The Lamp Goes Out, Seam of Ore, The Clay Rank.


      <b>Example.</b> A doorway the enemies must come through: use Blackdamp. A brute with lots of doses in a crowd: use Red Ore, and hit it with a heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Blackdamp''s pool poisons anyone standing in it, but it only slows enemies who were in it when it formed.'
  - Id: chokedamp.foulground
    Title: Foul Ground
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add a Tremor and a dose of poison at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Earth and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Tremor and one dose.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chokedamp (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), The Lamp Goes Out.


      <b>Example.</b> Hit into the pack with a heavy blow, and poison gas breathes out of the ground under them.'
  - Id: chokedamp.lamp
    Title: The Lamp Goes Out
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your weapon breaks the balance of enemies standing in the poison gas a quarter faster.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For 5 seconds after each Chokedamp, on the enemy it went off on and on shaken enemies near it, while you carry both Earth and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A quarter more stagger on the enemy Chokedamp went off on, and on up to three shaken enemies within 7 metres, for 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chokedamp, Foul Ground, Seam of Ore.


      <b>Example.</b> Fill a pack with poison gas, then break their balance one after another.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it keeps its own 5-second timer from each Chokedamp, instead of checking who is standing in the gas. It also raises all the stagger those enemies take, even from spells, not only from your weapon.'
  - Id: chokedamp.seam
    Title: Seam of Ore
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each time poison bites a shaken enemy, the ground shakes under it. Every enemy within 2 metres gets a Tremor.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each bite of poison on an enemy that has Tremor, while you carry both Earth and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. One Tremor on everything within 2 metres of the enemy, at most four times a second. Poisoned enemies it shakes may start Chokedamp.


      <b>Works with.</b> Foul Ground, Chokedamp, The Lamp Goes Out.


      <b>Example.</b> One shaken, poisoned soldier shakes the ground for all the enemies around it with every bite.'
  - Id: chokedamp.clayrank
    Title: The Clay Rank
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Three clay soldiers rise in a line in front of you. They are grey men with cracked faces leaking poison gas. They attack the enemy for 6 seconds. Each hit shakes and poisons the enemy it hits.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Earth and Poison. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 14 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Three soldiers fight for 6 seconds. Each one hits about every 1.2 seconds for 6 damage, 10 stagger, a Tremor and a dose, so Chokedamp starts under the enemies they hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chokedamp and its shapes, Seam of Ore, Foul Ground.


      <b>Example.</b> Send the clay soldiers into the pack, and follow them in.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the soldiers just run at the enemies and hit them. They don''t yet march 7 metres in a line, push enemies, block the enemies'' blows and break after two hits. They don''t yet burst into gas and clay when they break (12 damage, a Tremor and three doses within 2.5 metres, or twice as hard if your heavy blow breaks them).'
  - Id: coldshort.shape
    Title: 'Cold Short: Shed Plate or Snapped Blade'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Plate or Snap unlocks two new shapes for Cold Short. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Cold Short uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Shed Plate</b>: the enemy''s armour turns brittle, and your heavy blow cracks the plates off, like before. The cracked plate falls as a sheet of black ice that knocks down enemies standing on it. <b>Snapped Blade</b>: the armour doesn''t crack. Instead, the enemy''s own weapon turns brittle and snaps, so its blows deal half damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Cold Short goes off while you carry both Ice and Metal. Cold Short goes off when Chill lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on a chilled one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Cold Short.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Shed Plate: the enemy is brittle for 5 seconds, and its notches stay. Your next heavy blow cracks the plates: your weapon deals 25% more to it for 6 seconds, and its notches stay for the rest of the fight. The black ice is 2 metres across and lasts 4 seconds. Every enemy within 1.2 metres slips and falls flat for 0.6 seconds. Snapped Blade: for 5 seconds, the enemy''s notches don''t fade and its blows deal half damage. After Cold Short goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rimed Edge to set it up, Glass Bell, Brittle to the Core, Cold Chisel.


      <b>Example.</b> A wall of shields: use Shed Plate, and the ice under the first enemy knocks down the ones next to it. An enemy that hits hard: use Snapped Blade, and block its blows with your guard.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Shed Plate''s ice knocks down enemies nearby when it falls, but not enemies who step on it later. Snapped Blade doesn''t yet turn a normal guard against the enemy into a perfect guard that breaks its weapon (50 stagger, held for 1 second). For now, its blows deal half damage from the moment it turns brittle.'
  - Id: coldshort.rimededge
    Title: Rimed Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add a Chill and a notch at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Ice and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Chill and one notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Short (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), Glass Bell.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow, and the armour you hit frosts over.'
  - Id: coldshort.glassbell
    Title: Glass Bell
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each blow on a brittle enemy rings like glass. You get 3 mana, and 0.3 seconds come off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each weapon blow on an enemy during the 5 seconds after its Cold Short, until a heavy blow cracks it, while you carry both Ice and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 3 mana and 0.3 seconds off every spell''s wait for each blow, at most ten times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Short, Rimed Edge, Cold Chisel.


      <b>Example.</b> Ring a brittle brute with light blows, then crack it with a heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it keeps its own brittle timer from each Cold Short, instead of checking the enemy''s.'
  - Id: coldshort.core
    Title: Brittle to the Core
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cold Short on a frozen enemy also shatters it. The shatter and the crack land at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each Cold Short on a frozen enemy, while you carry both Ice and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: a hit of 30 damage and 40 stagger on the enemy as it turns brittle.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rimed Edge, Cold Short, Cold Chisel.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze a soldier and notch it, and it breaks right away.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> An effect can''t shatter an enemy yet. For now, it hits as hard as a shatter, and leaves the frozen enemy for your heavy blow to shatter.'
  - Id: coldshort.chisel
    Title: Cold Chisel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A chisel of black ice sticks point-first into the enemy you face, if it is within 8 metres. Each of your next blows on that enemy taps the chisel in, adding a notch and a Chill with a higher ringing sound each time. The tap that finds three notches drives it all the way in and splits the armour: 36 damage, 70 stagger, and Cold Short goes off right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Ice and Metal. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The chisel stays for 6 seconds, for three taps. Each tap deals 6 damage, 8 stagger, a Chill and a notch. The tap that finds three notches drives it in: 36 damage, 70 stagger, and Cold Short goes off even if it would normally have to wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Short and its shapes, Rimed Edge, Glass Bell, Brittle to the Core.


      <b>Example.</b> Stick the chisel in the captain. Tap, tap, and the third blow splits its armour.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Any blow taps it, not only light blows. It is driven in by the tap that finds three notches, instead of by your first heavy blow. The final hit doesn''t yet grow by 12 for each notch.'
  - Id: dark.curse
    Title: Curse
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands puts a Curse on the enemy. A Cursed enemy hits you more weakly, and killing it heals you. Also, one time, a blow that would kill you doesn''t. Instead you rise up again, Risen, with half your health, and that health slowly drains away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every weapon blow that lands, light or heavy, while Dark is your first element. Weapon blows only add your first element''s affliction. If Dark is your second element, you Curse enemies with Grasp, Dark''s other spells, the charged blade and its strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. A Curse is only one stack deep and lasts 8 seconds. Each blow restarts its timer. A Cursed enemy deals 15% less damage, and killing it heals you 6.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grasp and every Dark spell (each one Curses), Dread, Devouring Maw (it uses up the Curse), Collected Twice, Nothing Stays Buried, Never Sated, Hunger (every blow Curses while it is lit), and the charged blade, Shadow Cut Loose.


      <b>The Rise.</b> One time, while you carry Dark, a blow that would kill you doesn''t. You stand up again with half your health, and it drains 4 every second, but it never takes your last point. If you are killed while Risen, you fall. After a fall, you can rise again. Only the god''s gift, A Life for a Life, gives you the Rise back sooner: land a finisher on the enemy that killed you. Dead Already turns the Rise off.


      <b>Example.</b> Hit the brute first. While it is Cursed, each of its swings takes 15% less of your health. When it dies, you get 6 health back.'
  - Id: dark.dread
    Title: Dread
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a Cursed enemy dies, the other Cursed enemies near it lose their nerve. They lose their balance and drop their guard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a Cursed enemy dies, no matter what killed it. At most once a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Every enemy within 8 metres of where it fell takes 30 stagger. Every Cursed one among them can''t raise its guard for 3 seconds. It deals no damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Debts Called and Grasp''s The Pit (they gather Cursed enemies together), Devouring Maw and Gone to Ash, which come after it on the chart, Collected Twice, Nothing Stays Buried (the dead stand up too).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the Cursed enemies should lose their balance; for now, every enemy near where it fell takes the stagger. It should also cancel an attack the enemy was starting.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a Cursed soldier at the front of a pack. The four Cursed soldiers behind it are now 30 closer to being staggered, and open to your blows.'
  - Id: dark.maw
    Title: Devouring Maw
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Devouring Maw, the detonator of the Dark tree. A huge jaw, twice your height, rises out of your shadow and lunges down a path in front of you. On a Cursed enemy, it uses up the Curse to do more damage, and heals you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 10 seconds, and each Cursed enemy bitten heals you 18.) It travels 5 metres along its path and deals 24 damage and 30 stagger. Each Cursed enemy it bites loses its Curse, takes 20 more damage, and heals you 12. The jaw itself doesn''t add a Curse: Curse first, then bite.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Grasp and Debts Called (Curse first), Dread, which it comes after on the chart, Never Sated, Hunger, and the charged blade.


      <b>Example.</b> Curse a line of soldiers in a hallway with Grasp. Then send the jaw down it. Each one bitten takes 44 damage and gives you 12 health.'
  - Id: dark.maw.shape
    Title: 'Devouring Maw: Rahu or From Its Own Shadow'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Devouring Maw. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Rahu</b> (the head with no body that swallows the sun): a normal Cursed enemy is swallowed whole. A moment later, it rolls out of the open throat at your feet, staggered, because there is no body to hold it. A god or an elite is only bitten. <b>From Its Own Shadow</b>: the jaw rises out of the enemy''s own shadow instead, up to 9 metres away, and bites it from below. There is no path; it reaches an enemy far away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Devouring Maw, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain jaw.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Rahu: each normal enemy bitten is held still for 1.5 seconds and can''t attack or guard. Then it is dragged 5 metres toward you and takes 60 stagger. From Its Own Shadow: one jaw bites from below on the nearest enemy within 9 metres, a moment after you cast it. It covers a circle 1.6 metres across and deals 24 damage and 30 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rahu: a heavy weapon (the swallowed enemy comes out staggered at your feet, ready for a finisher), Near the Grave. From Its Own Shadow: archers and spellcasters at the back, Grasp''s Tethered shape.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Rahu should only swallow Cursed enemies; for now, every normal enemy bitten is swallowed, because the bite uses up the Curse first. The enemy should be hidden inside the throat; for now, it is held where it was bitten. From Its Own Shadow should look like it rises out of the enemy''s shadow, and aim at the enemy you face; for now, it rises under the nearest enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Rahu on a Cursed soldier. It disappears into the jaw and drops at your feet, staggered, and your heavy blow finishes it.'
  - Id: dark.maw.master
    Title: 'Devouring Maw: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Bite an enemy while it is winding up an attack, and its attack is swallowed and broken. Devouring Maw is ready to cast again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each bite, while Devouring Maw is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, every enemy bitten has whatever it started broken off (as a flinch, 0.4 seconds). Once every 16 seconds, a bite ends Devouring Maw''s wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Watching for the enemies'' wind-ups, Gone to Ash (another way to answer an attack), Dread.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work during the enemy''s wind-up; for now, every bite breaks whatever the enemy started, and the reset comes once every 16 seconds, no matter what it bit.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute raises its club. The jaw takes it in the middle of its swing, the blow never lands, and the jaw is ready to go again.'
  - Id: dark.ash
    Title: Gone to Ash
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Gone to Ash, Dark''s counter. You stand with your arms crossed as grey ash runs over you. When the first blow lands, you scatter into a cloud of ash and take no damage. A moment later, you stand again behind the attacker. It is Cursed, and if you hit it within a second, that first blow hits like a heavy blow and heals you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then let the enemies hit you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds, and it lasts 1.5 seconds.) It lasts 1.2 seconds. The blow you counter does nothing to you. Then every enemy in front of you within 2.5 metres is Cursed, you step 1.5 metres past the attacker, and your first weapon blow within a second hits twice as hard and heals you 10. If nothing hits you, the wait is half as long: 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Dread, which it comes after on the chart, Devouring Maw (bite the enemy you came out behind), Near the Grave, and a heavy weapon.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Scattering into ash and standing up again a moment later; for now, you step right away. The answering blow should count as a heavy blow; for now, it hits twice as hard.


      <b>Example.</b> A captain swings at you. You scatter, stand up behind it, and your cut lands for double damage and gives you 10 health.'
  - Id: dark.ash.shape
    Title: 'Gone to Ash: Ash on the Brow or Ash Hangs'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Gone to Ash. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Ash on the Brow</b> (the ash on the god Shiva''s forehead): you stand again where you were, and the ash settles on the attacker''s forehead as a grey smear. For 6 seconds it is Cursed, the next blow it lands on you scatters you for no damage again, and your first blow on it counts as a heavy blow. The ash marks the enemy that hit you. <b>Ash Hangs</b>: you stand again where you were, and the ash stays as a cloud that slowly sinks. Enemies inside it are Cursed, and an attack they start inside it goes the way they were already facing.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Gone to Ash, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain counter.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Ash on the Brow: the attacker''s Curse doesn''t fade for 6 seconds, and you get one ward that blocks the next blow that would land. Ash Hangs: a cloud 3 metres across sits on the attacker for 3 seconds, Cursing whatever stands in it each second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ash on the Brow: a one-on-one fight with a strong enemy, Dread, Collected Twice. Ash Hangs: a pack, Dread, Debts Called''s Collected shape.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Both shapes still move you behind the attacker; a shape can''t yet turn off its spell''s step. Ash on the Brow''s ward blocks any blow, not only the attacker''s, and its grey smear isn''t drawn yet. Ash Hangs should stop enemies inside it from turning their attacks; for now, they are only Cursed.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Ash Hangs in a ring of soldiers. The one that hit you and the two next to it come away Cursed.'
  - Id: dark.ash.master
    Title: 'Gone to Ash: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Scatter a red attack, and the attacker is staggered and Gone to Ash is ready to cast again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each blow Gone to Ash scatters, while it is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, any blow you scatter gives the attacker 60 stagger and ends Gone to Ash''s wait, once every 20 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Gods and elites (their red attacks), Devouring Maw and Pawned Heart (a big hit on the staggered enemy).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work on red attacks; for now, any blow does it, once every 20 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> A god''s red overhead swing comes down on you. You scatter, it staggers back, and the counter is ready for its next swing.'
  - Id: dark.lift
    Title: Collected Twice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Collected Twice, Dark''s reaction lift. Every reaction the Curse is part of leaves a black thumbprint on the forehead of the enemy it hit. Your next weapon blow on that enemy repeats the reaction''s damage. Your blade collects the debt.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a reaction with Cursed in it happens, on any enemy. The print lasts 4 seconds, or until your next weapon blow on that enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A weapon blow on a printed enemy within 4 seconds hits it for 15 more damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every element pair with Cursed in it (Dark''s reactions with its neighbours and the rest), the root mark, Dread, which it comes after on the chart, and Never Sated and Nothing Stays Buried, which come after it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should repeat the reaction''s own damage; for now, it is always 15, no matter what the reaction did. The print should go on every enemy the reaction hit; for now, only on the enemy where the reaction happened. The black print isn''t drawn on the head yet.


      <b>Example.</b> A Curse reaction goes off on a soldier. Your next cut on it does 15 more damage.'
  - Id: dark.neversated
    Title: Never Sated
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You carry no flasks at all. Instead, every blow you land heals you a little, every Cursed kill heals you a lot, and every finisher heals you even more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: with every weapon blow that lands, every Cursed enemy you kill, and every finisher.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. You are left with one flask (the game always keeps at least one), and you don''t earn any back. Each blow heals you 6% of the damage it deals. A Cursed kill heals 12, on top of the 6 the Curse already gives. A finisher heals 20.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Devouring Maw (it heals too), Hunger (its healing and this one stack together), Collected Twice, which it comes after on the chart, Dead Already.


      <b>Example.</b> Against a long line of enemies, never stop fighting. Each kill and finisher keeps you on your feet, the way a flask would have.'
  - Id: dark.devotion
    Title: Nothing Stays Buried
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Nothing Stays Buried, Dark''s Devotion, for going all in on Dark only. Every enemy that dies Cursed stands back up as your shade, ash-grey with violet eyes, and fights for you for a while. While you are Risen, every enemy that dies stands back up, Cursed or not. This is the vetala, a spirit that rides the dead. The Dead Stand, which used to be its own spell, lives on here.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Dark twice (all in). Each Cursed death, and each death while you are Risen.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Each shade stands where its body fell for 6 seconds. Every 0.8 seconds, it hits whatever is within 2.5 metres of it for 12 damage, 12 stagger and a Curse. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Yama''s Noose. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Dark alone or twice.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark and every Dark spell (Curse enemies before they die), Dread, Collected Twice, which it comes after on the chart, Dead Already (you are always Risen, so every death stands up), A Life for a Life.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> There should be three shades at most; for now, there is one for every enemy that dies. Each shade should walk to its own enemy; for now, it fights where it fell. A Cursed death while you are Risen should raise two shades, not one.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill three Cursed soldiers in a group. Three grey shades stand up among the rest and keep cutting while you turn to the captain.'
  - Id: dark.bloodprice
    Title: Blood Price
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a spell costs more mana than you have, you pay the rest with health. That health isn''t lost right away. It turns grey, and dealing damage wins it back, like any grey health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you cast a spell that costs mana without enough mana for it: another element''s larger spell, or a duo spell. Dark''s own spells cost no mana. They have waits instead, and Pawned Heart is paid for with health.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each point of mana you are missing costs half a point of health, taken as grey health. It never takes your last point. If you don''t have the health, the spell isn''t cast.


      <b>Works with.</b> Near the Grave and Debts Called, which come after it on the chart (they reward low health), Pawned Heart, duo spells.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The plan is for Blood Price to work on spell waits instead: you could cast a spell that is still waiting, paying 1 health for each second left, as grey health you owe, but never your last point. For now, it pays for the mana a spell is missing.


      <b>Example.</b> You have 10 mana and cast a duo spell that costs 40. It goes off, 15 of your health turns grey, and the blows you land next win it back.'
  - Id: dark.debtscalled
    Title: Debts Called
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Debts Called, the breaker of the Dark tree. Every enemy nearby is dragged in to a spot just in front of you. When they get there, a burst of shadow hits them all at once. The closer you are to death, the harder it hits.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds.) The spot is 1 metre in front of you. For half a second, everything within 6 metres of it is pulled in. Then they all take 18 damage, 35 stagger and a Curse. If you cast it while below half your health, your spells hit 50% harder for the next second.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Blood Price, which it comes after on the chart, Near the Grave and Pawned Heart (low health), Dread, Devouring Maw, and the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The damage should rise smoothly as your health drops, up to 36 at one point of health; for now, it jumps up once you are below half health. The burst should also throw back the enemies it hits.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers are spread around you. One cast pulls them into a tight group at the tip of your sword, all Cursed, ready for a wide swing.'
  - Id: dark.debtscalled.shape
    Title: 'Debts Called: The Black Buffalo or Collected'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Debts Called. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>The Black Buffalo</b> (the buffalo the god Yama rides): there is no pull. A buffalo made of sinking black smoke charges through the spot in front of you. It carries every Cursed enemy on its horns to the far end and throws them down. Follow its path and cut down the enemies it threw. This is Dark''s only push. <b>Collected</b>: only Cursed enemies are called, from farther away, and each one that reaches you gives you health. There is no burst.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Debts Called, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain call.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The Black Buffalo: it charges 8 metres down a path about 2.4 metres wide, dealing 18 damage, 35 stagger and a Curse to everything it runs through. Collected: everything within 10 metres is pulled in and takes 20 stagger and no damage. Each Cursed enemy heals you 4.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Black Buffalo: a heavy weapon to follow its path, Dread, Devouring Maw''s path. Collected: the root mark and Grasp (Curse them first), Never Sated, Near the Grave.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The buffalo should carry only Cursed enemies and throw them down at the far end, and it should look like a buffalo made of smoke; for now, it lunges down the path in the shape of the jaw. Collected should call only Cursed enemies; for now, it pulls in every enemy, and only the Cursed ones heal you.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Collected on a pack of five Cursed soldiers. They come to your feet staggering, and you get 20 health.'
  - Id: dark.debtscalled.master
    Title: 'Debts Called: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it while below a third of your health, and the burst, or the buffalo, hits twice as hard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each cast while below a third of your health, while Debts Called is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For one second after you cast it, your spells deal double damage (two and a half times as much, with the Learn mark''s own boost below half health).


      <b>Works with.</b> Near the Grave, Pawned Heart (you stay at one point of health the whole time), Blood Price, Dead Already.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the burst or the buffalo should get stronger; for now, every spell that lands in that second does.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Pawned Heart, then cast Debts Called. At one point of health, the burst hits four soldiers for 45 each.'
  - Id: dark.nearthegrave
    Title: Near the Grave
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While you are below a third of your health, you hit harder, and each kill wraps you in a shade that blocks the next blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while your health is below a third. You get a shade with each kill you make while that low.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Below a third of your health, your weapon deals 20% more damage. Each kill below a third gives you one ward, up to three.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blood Price, which it comes after on the chart, Pawned Heart and Dead Already, which come after it, Debts Called''s Master mark, Hollow Strength, A Life for a Life.


      <b>Example.</b> You are at 25 health in a pack. Cut harder, and each soldier that falls leaves you a shade to block the next one''s blow.'
  - Id: dark.pawnedheart
    Title: Pawned Heart
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Pawned Heart, Dark''s larger spell. You pay for it with health, not mana. Your health drops to one point, and the rest is set down behind you as a beating heart inside a ring that slowly closes. While the heart beats, your weapon hits much harder and swings cost little stamina. Walk away from the heart and then back onto it, and you get back all the health you gave up, plus a share of the damage you dealt in the meantime. If the ring closes first, the heart stops: you keep one point of health, and only half of what you gave up comes back, as grey health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). You can''t cast it at one point of health.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana; you pay with health. After you cast it, you must wait 40 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower gives 70% more damage and makes the ring last 12 seconds.) The heart sits 1.8 metres behind you and beats for 10 seconds. While it beats, your weapon deals 60% more damage. You must step 2.6 metres away from it, then come back within 1 metre of it. You then get back all the health you gave up, plus 30% of the damage you dealt. A blow that lands on you while it beats can kill you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Near the Grave, which it comes after on the chart (you are below a third of your health the whole time), Debts Called''s Master mark, Dead Already, Hunger, and the wards from Near the Grave and Gone to Ash.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Pawned Heart next to a staggered brute. Cut it down with five strong swings, then step back onto the heart and stand up with more health than you gave up.'
  - Id: dark.pawnedheart.shape
    Title: 'Pawned Heart: Buried Heart or Hung From the Tree'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Pawned Heart. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Buried Heart</b>: the heart sinks into the floor where you stand and beats under it, a dark pulse inside a ring of ash. Your weapon is only strong while you stand near it. When the ring closes, the heart rises whole. Stand your ground and fight. <b>Hung From the Tree</b> (the vetala''s tree): instead, the heart hangs in the air above the enemy you face, and follows it. You must fight your way in under it to touch it. You go in toward the enemy, not away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Pawned Heart, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, the plain heart is set down behind you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, both shapes still set the heart behind you, like the plain one. Buried Heart: a ring of ash 3 metres across appears where you cast it and stays for 10 seconds, Cursing whatever stands in it each second. Hung From the Tree: the enemy you face keeps its Curse for the 10 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Buried Heart: holding your ground against a pack that comes to you, Gone to Ash. Hung From the Tree: a one-on-one fight with a strong enemy, Grasp''s Tethered shape.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Buried Heart''s heart under your feet, its power only working within 3 metres, and the heart rising whole with 30% of the damage dealt when the ring closes. Hung From the Tree''s heart hanging over the enemy and following it. Both are waiting until the heart can be placed somewhere other than behind you.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Buried Heart in a doorway. The soldiers that come through stand in your ring of ash and come away Cursed.'
  - Id: dark.pawnedheart.master
    Title: 'Pawned Heart: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Touch the heart during a perfect dodge, and you get back 60% of the damage you dealt instead of 30%.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, each perfect dodge, while Pawned Heart is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, a perfect dodge heals you 10, once every 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clean dodging, Near the Grave (low health, when a heal matters most), Gone to Ash.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you touch the heart during a perfect dodge, and give back 60%; for now, any perfect dodge heals you 10, whether you touch the heart or not.


      <b>Example.</b> You are at one point of health with the heart beating. Roll through a soldier''s thrust at the last moment, and get 10 health back while you work your way back to the heart.'
  - Id: dark.deadalready
    Title: Dead Already
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You don''t wait to die: from now on, you are always Risen. In every fight, your health drains toward its last point, and your blows heal you. You hit much harder. You don''t get a second Rise.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The drain only runs while an enemy is within 12 metres of you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Your weapon deals 25% more damage, and you get 30% of each blow''s damage back as health. The drain takes 2 health a second, but never your last point. Every mark that needs you to be Risen is always working. A Life for a Life can no longer stand you up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Near the Grave, which it comes after on the chart, Hollow Strength (another 25%, no flinching, and kills pause the drain), Nothing Stays Buried (every death stands up), Hunger (another 30% back), Never Sated.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight without stopping. Every cut gives back much more than the drain takes. The moment you stop and wait, it bleeds you down.'
  - Id: dark.hollowstrength
    Title: Hollow Strength
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While you are Risen, you hit harder and no blow makes you flinch. Each kill pauses the drain on your health for a few seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While you are Risen: after A Life for a Life stands you up, or all the time with Dead Already.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. While Risen, your weapon deals 25% more damage, and blows that land on you don''t stop you. Each kill stops the drain for 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> A Life for a Life, Dead Already, Second Shadow, which comes after it on the chart, Nothing Stays Buried (while Risen, every death stands up), Near the Grave.


      <b>Example.</b> You are Risen with only a sliver of health. Swing straight through the soldiers'' blows. Each one you kill gives you three seconds without the drain.'
  - Id: dark.base
    Title: Grasp
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Grasp, Dark''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A black ball crawls along the ground to the enemy you face, pulling in what it passes. Where it arrives, it hits and Curses, and the enemies it has hold of are dragged to your feet.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Dark, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Dark. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds. It reaches the enemy you face if it is within 6 metres, and deals 10 damage, 12 stagger and a Curse to everything within 1.7 metres of where it arrives.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; the root mark, Dread, Devouring Maw and Debts Called''s Collected shape (they all want Cursed enemies), and the charged blade, which it charges with Dark.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s 14 damage and 8-metre reach. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Grasp the archer at the back. It is dragged to your feet, Cursed, and the jaw finishes it.'
  - Id: dark.base.shape
    Title: 'Grasp: Tethered or The Pit'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Grasp. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Tethered</b> (the god Yama''s noose): instead of the ball, you throw a black rope that ties the enemy you face to you. It can''t get far from you, and your first heavy blow on it yanks it to your feet. You keep it close. <b>The Pit</b>: the ball stops where you aim and spreads into a pool of ink. It slowly drags everything in it to the middle and Curses it. You gather enemies in one spot.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Grasp, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain ball.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Tethered: for 4 seconds after Grasp hits an enemy, your first heavy blow on it drags it 4 metres toward you and gives it 20 stagger. The Pit: a pool 3 metres across, 6 metres ahead of you, that lasts 4 seconds. Each second, it deals 2 damage and a Curse to whatever stands in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tethered: a one-on-one fight, Devouring Maw''s From Its Own Shadow shape, a heavy weapon. The Pit: Dread, Debts Called, Ash Hangs, a pack.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Tethered should throw a rope instead of the ball, and keep the enemy within 5 metres of you; for now, the ball is cast, and only the heavy blow''s yank is built. The Pit should drag everything to its middle; for now, it only deals damage and Curses. If you buy the Empower, The Pit uses its numbers too, and lands 8 metres ahead.


      <b>Example.</b> Put The Pit under a pack in a doorway. In four seconds they are all Cursed, and Dread is ready for the first death.'
  - Id: dark.base.master
    Title: 'Grasp: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it within a second of being hit, and the enemy that hit you is dragged to your feet and Cursed twice. Dark strikes back when it is hurt.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When a blow lands on you, while Grasp is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For one second after a blow lands on you, your first blow on the enemy that hit you, from Grasp or anything else, drags it 4 metres toward you and adds two Curses.


      <b>Works with.</b> Pawned Heart and Near the Grave (getting hurt is the plan), Gone to Ash, Dread.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you answer by casting Grasp; for now, any blow of yours on the attacker within that second does it.


      <b>Example.</b> A spearman pokes you from far away. Answer right away, and it is at your feet and Cursed.'
  - Id: dark.secondshadow
    Title: Second Shadow
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Second Shadow, Dark''s movement spell. You leave your shadow where you stand: a flat black copy of you with violet eyes, joined to you by a thin line on the ground. For a while, it repeats every blow you land, a moment later, for half the damage, and Curses the enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). After that, every weapon blow that lands is repeated.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 10 seconds.) The shadow stays for 8 seconds. Each repeated blow lands a moment after yours, for 50% of its damage, and adds a Curse. You can only have one shadow at a time.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Hollow Strength, which it comes after on the chart, Pawned Heart (half of a much bigger blow), Near the Grave, Hunger.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Pressing the button again to swap places with the shadow, once.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it while a brute kneels, staggered. Every cut of your finisher lands twice.'
  - Id: dark.secondshadow.shape
    Title: 'Second Shadow: Return to the Shadow or Long Shadow'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Second Shadow. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Return to the Shadow</b>: the shadow fights like the plain one, but pressing again doesn''t swap places. Instead, while the shadow lasts, press again to sink into it and rise where it is. Every wound you took since you left it comes back as grey health you can win back. You rewind your health, but you go back to where you were. <b>Long Shadow</b>: your shadow stretches out along the floor in front of you, long like an evening shadow. You sink into its foot and rise at its head a moment later, and every enemy it lay across is Cursed. There is no copy, just the path.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Second Shadow, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain shadow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Return to the Shadow: for the shadow''s 8 seconds, you take a quarter less damage from every blow. Long Shadow: you travel 8 metres ahead in about a third of a second, and every enemy within a metre of the path takes 2 damage and a Curse.


      <b>Works with.</b> Return to the Shadow: Pawned Heart, Near the Grave, a fight where you plan to hold your ground. Long Shadow: Debts Called and Grasp (a path into the pack, then the call), Dread.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Return to the Shadow''s second press, and getting your wounds back as grey health; for now, taking a quarter less damage takes its place. Long Shadow should hide you as you travel under the floor; for now, enemies can see you go.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Long Shadow through a line of three soldiers. You rise behind them, and all three are Cursed.'
  - Id: dark.secondshadow.master
    Title: 'Second Shadow: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Swap places within a second of being hit, and the blow moves to the shadow, and you get back the health it took.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, each time you cast it, while Second Shadow is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, each cast gives you one ward that blocks the next blow that would land on you, up to three.


      <b>Works with.</b> Near the Grave (it gives wards too), Pawned Heart (one point of health, one blow blocked), Gone to Ash.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Swapping places within a second of being hit, the blow moving to the shadow, and getting its health back; for now, the ward on each cast takes its place.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast the shadow before a brute''s string of blows. Its first blow breaks on nothing.'
  - Id: dark.lifeforalife
    Title: A Life for a Life
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When you are Risen (Dark''s root mark stood you up from a blow that would have killed you), a symbol hangs over the enemy that killed you. Land a finisher on it, and you come back to life: your health returns, and you can rise once more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While you are Risen. A finisher on the enemy that killed you ends the Rise. If that enemy is already gone, a finisher on any enemy does.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. The finisher gives back half your full health and makes the Rise ready again. Without the gift, you stay Risen, draining down to one point. If you are killed then, you fall. After a fall, you can rise again.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hollow Strength, Nothing Stays Buried (while Risen, every death stands up), Near the Grave, Never Sated, Hunger.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute''s red overhead swing kills you. The root mark stands you up, Risen. Break the brute''s balance and finish it. You stand up with half your health, and your Rise is ready again.'
  - Id: drownedweight.shape
    Title: 'Drowned Weight: Drawn Under or The Pull of Night'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Bound in Debt unlocks two new shapes for Drowned Weight. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Drowned Weight uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Drawn Under</b>: as the enemy swells, it sinks knee-deep into a pool of black water and is held there, and the other enemies are pulled in toward it. <b>The Pull of Night</b>: there is no burst. Instead, while it is swollen, each of your blows on it pulls the wet enemies nearby toward it, the way the sea at night pulls everything back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Drowned Weight goes off while you carry both Water and Dark. Drowned Weight goes off when a Curse lands on a Soaked enemy, or when water lands on a Cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Drowned Weight.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: the enemy swells for 5 seconds. It moves a fifth slower and takes 15% more damage from weapon blows. Drawn Under: the enemy is held for 1.5 seconds, and everything within 3 metres is pulled 2 metres toward it. It bursts like the plain one when it dies or when a heavy blow lands: 14 damage, 20 stagger, Soaked and Cursed to everything within 4 metres. The Pull of Night: there is no burst. Each weapon blow on the enemy pulls every other Soaked enemy within 5 metres 1 metre toward it, at most once every 0.3 seconds. Enemies that aren''t Soaked stay where they are. After Drowned Weight goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Held Under, Deep Swell, The Debt Passes, Under the Black Water.


      <b>Example.</b> Archers hanging back: use The Pull of Night, and every blow on the swollen enemy drags them in. A tight pack: use Drawn Under, then a heavy blow for the burst.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Drawn Under''s pool isn''t drawn yet, and it pulls the others 2 metres all at once instead of 1.5 metres a second.'
  - Id: drownedweight.heldunder
    Title: Held Under
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a swollen enemy pushes it down knee-deep. It is held for 1 second, and the attack it was making is lost.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard against a swollen enemy, while you carry both Water and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The enemy flinches (whatever it had started is lost) and is held for 1 second (0.4 for elites), at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Drowned Weight, The Debt Passes, Under the Black Water.


      <b>Example.</b> Block the swollen brute''s swing perfectly, and it stands sunk in place for your heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any enemy that is both Soaked and Cursed, whether it is swollen or not.'
  - Id: drownedweight.deepswell
    Title: Deep Swell
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Drowned Weight keeps the enemy swollen 2 seconds longer, and its burst reaches 5 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Drowned Weight goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The enemy stays swollen and slow for 7 seconds instead of 5, and its burst reaches 5 metres instead of 4. It''s the same in both shapes.


      <b>Works with.</b> Drowned Weight and its shapes, Held Under.


      <b>Example.</b> The burst catches the second row of enemies too.'
  - Id: drownedweight.debtpasses
    Title: The Debt Passes
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a swollen enemy bursts, the nearest enemy the burst reaches swells right away. Killing a swollen enemy heals you 6 more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a swollen enemy dies, while you carry both Water and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: when an enemy that is both Soaked and Cursed dies, every enemy within 4 metres is Cursed, at most once a second, and you heal 6. This is on purpose: it may make those enemies swell too.


      <b>Works with.</b> Drowned Weight (its burst soaks them all first), Held Under, Under the Black Water.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill the first swollen soldier, and the debt passes down the line.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works when any Soaked, Cursed enemy dies, and the Curse passes to every enemy within 4 metres, not only the nearest.'
  - Id: drownedweight.blackwater
    Title: Under the Black Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Black water opens under you and under the enemy you face. You both sink, and each of you comes up out of the other''s water: you where it stood, and it where you stood. It comes up Soaked, Cursed, and held knee-deep for 1 second. The water you came up from stays for 3 seconds as a pool that soaks.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Water and Dark. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 9 metres. The enemy takes 8 damage, 15 stagger, Soaked and Cursed (so it swells), and is held for 1 second (0.4 for elites). You and the enemy swap places: it ends up where you stood when you cast, and you end up where it stood. An elite or a god is too heavy to move, so you come up 1.5 metres behind it instead. The pool where you come up is 2.5 metres across and lasts 3 seconds. It Soaks and Curses once a second. Your next weapon blow within 2 seconds hits 50% harder, and your next heavy blow within 2 seconds is a critical hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> Drowned Weight, Held Under, The Debt Passes.


      <b>Example.</b> Drop through the water to the archer and come up swinging.'
  - Id: earth.tremor
    Title: Tremor
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows shake the ground under an enemy and add Tremor. A finisher fills it all the way at once. A shaken enemy is slowed, and its balance doesn''t come back while the shaking lasts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow that lands adds a stack. Every finisher fills it. Like every root mark, your weapon only adds it while Earth is your first element. If Earth is your second element, Tremor comes from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack for each heavy blow, three on a finisher, up to three stacks. Tremor lasts 6 seconds. Each stack slows the enemy by 10%, and while it lasts, the enemy''s stagger meter doesn''t go down.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Hand and The Slow Hand (more heavy blows, or every blow shaking), Cairn (it uses up Tremor to drop stones), Left in Rubble, Bedrock (one more stack with every blow), Buckled on the Forge branch, and The Ground Gives when Soaked meets Tremor.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Earth''s basic bonus from the game plan, which makes afflictions put on you last half as long. Nothing puts an affliction on the player yet.


      <b>Example.</b> A heavy blow on a brute leaves its balance bar stuck where you left it. Step back, guard its swing, and come in again with the bar still waiting.'
  - Id: earth.heavyhand
    Title: Heavy Hand
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Nothing stops your heavy blows. If you are hit while winding one up or swinging it, you still take the blow, but less of it, and your blow still lands. Red attacks can''t stop it either.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: any blow that lands on you while you wind up or swing a heavy blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You take 35% less damage from a blow that lands then, and you don''t flinch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor, The Slow Hand, Under the Mound and Cairn, which come after it on the chart, Hand of the Mountain, Hammer and Anvil on the Forge branch.


      <b>Example.</b> Two soldiers swing at you as you wind up. You take both hits, lightly, and your heavy blow lands on the first one anyway.'
  - Id: earth.mound
    Title: Under the Mound
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Under the Mound, the movement spell of the Earth tree. You sink into the ground and travel in the direction of the stick as a bump of rolling turf, where nothing can reach you. Then you burst out in a ring of flying dirt and stones that shakes everything around you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 8 seconds and the reach 10 metres.) It reaches up to 8 metres. Where you come out, enemies take 12 damage, 25 stagger and one Tremor. For now, it is a step through the enemy you face (or 8 metres ahead if there is none). If it lands, your next heavy blow within 4 seconds is a critical hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Hand, Tremor and Cairn (it shakes what it reaches), The Slow Hand, and the charged blade: it charges your weapon with Earth when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Going under the ground in the direction of the stick, being untouchable on the way, and the ring of dirt 2.5 metres around where you come up. For now, it is a step through the enemy you face that hits it as you pass.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer stands behind a line of shields. Go under them and come up behind it. It is shaken and open to your heavy blow.'
  - Id: earth.mound.shape
    Title: 'Under the Mound: Ditch and Bank or Barrow Road'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Under the Mound. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Ditch and Bank</b> (the old earth walls): the turf you push up stays behind you as a bank a metre high along your whole path. Nothing can walk through it, and enemies standing on the line as it rises are thrown off it, shaken. You draw a wall as you go. <b>Barrow Road</b>: hold the button to stay underground and steer. Come up under an enemy, and it is thrown into the air, as if by Stone Rise, and you stand behind it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Under the Mound, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain spell.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Ditch and Bank: a line of six patches, 8 metres long, starting from where you went under. Each patch is 0.6 metres across and lasts 5 seconds. Once a second, each deals 4 damage, 10 stagger and one Tremor. Barrow Road: you stay under for up to 1.5 seconds. The enemy you come up under is thrown into the air for 0.6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ditch and Bank: Quake Step and Cairn on enemies standing on the bank. Barrow Road: Under the Mound''s own Master mark, and your heavy blows on enemies in the air.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The bank should block enemies and throw them off as it rises; for now, it is ground along your path that deals damage and shakes. Barrow Road''s hold and steer; for now, the spell goes where it goes, and only the throw is built.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Ditch and Bank across a doorway. The pack that follows you through it comes out shaken.'
  - Id: earth.mound.master
    Title: 'Under the Mound: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Go under the ground right as a blow would land on you, like a perfect dodge, and you come up behind the attacker right away. Your next blow counts as a heavy blow, adds two Tremor, and breaks 50% more balance.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time you cast Under the Mound. It must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each cast gives you what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell and strike that is waiting. For 3 seconds, or until your next blow lands, your blows break 50% more balance and each adds one more stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor (the extra stack on the next blow), Heavy Hand and The Slow Hand, Cairn (the mana it gives back).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you go under right as a blow would land, like the other movement spells, and bring you up behind the attacker, with the next blow counting as a heavy blow. For now, every cast gives the dodge''s reward and the stronger next blow.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute''s overhead swing is coming down. Go under it, come up behind it, and your next blow nearly breaks its balance.'
  - Id: earth.cairn
    Title: Cairn
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cairn, Earth''s larger spell and its detonator. You stamp the end of your weapon on the ground, and the Cailleach''s stones fall from her apron onto every shaken enemy near you. Each enemy gets one stone for each stack of Tremor it has. The Tremor is used up, and the stones stay piled on the enemy, slowing it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It costs mana instead of having a wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 40 mana (its Empower makes it 35). It hits every enemy within 10 metres that has Tremor, 0.3 seconds after you cast it. A stone falls for each stack, dealing 12 damage and 15 stagger each (three stones on full Tremor: 36 damage and 45 stagger), plus 10 more damage for the pile. The pile stays for 4 seconds and slows the enemy by 15% for each stone (45% with three stones). An enemy with no Tremor isn''t touched.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor and everything that adds it: heavy blows, finishers, Quake Step, The Slow Hand. Unbroken Cairn and your heavy blows, and Rooted for its Master mark.


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers each have three stacks of Tremor. One stamp drops nine stones, and all three are slowed with their Tremor used up.'
  - Id: earth.cairn.shape
    Title: 'Cairn: Unbroken Cairn or Thrown Stones'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Cairn. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Unbroken Cairn</b>: the stones land softly and wait. Your next heavy blow on an enemy under a pile breaks the pile outward into everything near it. The burst waits for your blow. <b>Thrown Stones</b>: the stones don''t stay. Each enemy''s pile is thrown right away at the nearest other enemy, so one shaken enemy shakes the next.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Cairn, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, the plain stones fall.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Unbroken Cairn: a heavy blow on an enemy under a pile within 4 seconds bursts in a 2.5-metre circle around it, for 36 damage and 20 stagger. Thrown Stones: a stone flies from each enemy under a pile to the nearest other enemy within 5 metres, for 24 damage, 10 stagger and two Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Unbroken Cairn: Heavy Hand and The Slow Hand. Thrown Stones: cast Cairn again on the enemies the stones shook.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Unbroken Cairn''s stones should land softly (for now they still fall when you cast), and its burst should be 18 for each stone (for now it is 36 no matter how many stacks). Thrown Stones'' piles should be thrown instead of staying (for now they still fall and pile up), with a stone for each stack (for now it is two stones'' worth).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Unbroken Cairn on a pack around a brute. One heavy blow on the brute breaks its pile through all of them.'
  - Id: earth.cairn.master
    Title: 'Cairn: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it after standing still for a second, and every stone hits twice and you get half the mana back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, when you cast it while Rooted (you haven''t started a dodge for 3 seconds). Cairn must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You get 20 mana back. A second fall hits each shaken enemy 0.8 seconds after you cast it, for 24 damage and 30 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rooted, Immovable, Heavy Hand, and Mountain Stance, which keeps you standing still.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should work after standing still for one second (for now it needs Rooted, which is three seconds without a dodge), and every stone should hit twice (for now there is a second fall worth two stones).


      <b>Example.</b> Hold your ground through a soldier''s string of blows, then stamp. You get twice the stones, and half the cost back for the next one.'
  - Id: earth.slowhand
    Title: The Slow Hand
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Slow Hand, a keystone. Every swing is slower and costs more stamina, but your light blows hit like heavy ones, and every blow shakes the enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The Tremor on every blow only comes while Earth is your first element.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Swings are 22% slower, and blows cost 20% more stamina. Light blows deal 35% more damage and 60% more stagger. Every weapon blow adds one stack of Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor, Heavy Hand, Cairn (full Tremor on everything, ready to use up), Under the Mound''s Master mark, Bedrock.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Light blows should count as heavy blows. For now, they don''t break through guards, don''t make enemies flinch, and don''t set off anything that needs a heavy blow.


      <b>Example.</b> Three light blows on a soldier leave it with three stacks of Tremor and close to staggered, without a single heavy blow.'
  - Id: earth.rooted
    Title: Rooted
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fight for a few seconds without rolling, and you become Rooted. While Rooted, you take less damage from every blow, and holding your guard costs less stamina. Starting a roll ends it, and the count starts over.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. You become Rooted once you haven''t started a dodge for 3 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. While Rooted, you take 8% less damage, and a blocked blow costs 30% less stamina.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quake Step and Mountain Stance, which come after it on the chart, Immovable, Heavy Hand, the Master marks of Stone Rise and Cairn (both ask you to hold your ground), and Bedrock (20% less damage on top of this while it is lit).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Nothing stops you from being pushed back while Rooted yet.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant your feet in front of a duellist and block its string of blows. Each blocked blow costs less stamina, and the grey health it leaves is smaller.'
  - Id: earth.quakestep
    Title: Quake Step
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Quake Step, a breaker. You lift a foot and stamp it down. The ground heaves all around you and shakes everything it hits. Enemies it hits flinch, which breaks the attack they started, unless they are steadfast.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 8 seconds and the reach 6 metres.) It lands 0.45 seconds after you cast it and reaches 5 metres around you. Each enemy takes 16 damage, 30 stagger and two stacks of Tremor, and flinches for 0.4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor and Cairn (two stacks on everything around you), Rooted, Standing Stones (a pillar on what it hits), Buckled on the Forge branch. When it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Double stagger on an enemy that is starting an attack, and breaking only that enemy''s attack. For now, every enemy it hits flinches, and the stagger is the same for all.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers close in around you. Stamp, and all four have two stacks of Tremor, with their balance bars stuck.'
  - Id: earth.quakestep.shape
    Title: 'Quake Step: Struck From Afar or The Second Wave'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Quake Step. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Struck From Afar</b> (the Cailleach striking the ground with her staff): the stamp doesn''t land around you. Instead it lands on the shadow of an enemy far away, breaking its attack where it stands and holding it for a moment. You can stop an archer drawing its bow without crossing the room. <b>The Second Wave</b>: one stamp sends out two rings, first the quick, thin wave of an earthquake and then the slow, heavy one. Enemies caught by both are knocked off their feet.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Quake Step, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain ring.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Struck From Afar: a ring with a 2.5-metre radius around an enemy up to 8 metres away, 0.45 seconds after you cast it. It deals 16 damage, 30 stagger and two Tremor, and holds the enemy for 0.6 seconds. The Second Wave: first a ring out to 5 metres for 8 damage and one Tremor. Then, 0.7 seconds later, a ring out to 7 metres around where you stamped, for 18 damage and 40 stagger. Enemies the first ring hits are knocked down for 1 second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Struck From Afar: Cairn on the enemy it shook. The Second Wave: Tremor, Unbroken Cairn, and your heavy blows on enemies lying on the ground.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Struck From Afar should land on the enemy you face (for now, the nearest within 8 metres). The Second Wave should only knock down enemies the second ring also catches, at that moment (for now, enemies the first ring catches are knocked down right away).


      <b>Example.</b> Use The Second Wave in a crowd. Step back after the first ring, so the second ring catches them all as they come at you.'
  - Id: earth.quakestep.master
    Title: 'Quake Step: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Break an enemy out of its wind-up, and Quake Step is ready to cast again right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, whenever Quake Step hits an enemy, at most once every 18 seconds. Quake Step must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Its wait ends.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Second Wave (two stamps in a row), Cairn after two stamps.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you break an enemy out of its wind-up. The game can''t tell yet when an enemy starts an attack, so any stamp that lands counts, once every 18 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Stamp a soldier out of its swing, then stamp again as its friend comes in.'
  - Id: earth.stance
    Title: Mountain Stance
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Mountain Stance, a counter (Grey Stone: the Cailleach turning to stone each summer). You turn completely to stone and can''t move. A blow that hits the stone is blocked, red ones too, and it jolts the attacker: it is shaken, and its next attack comes late. The blows the stone blocks power up your next heavy blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then it works on any blow while you stand as stone.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower lets you stand for up to 3 seconds.) For now, it works like this: you stand as stone for up to 2.5 seconds. The first blow to land does nothing to you. You answer with a jolt of stone toward the attacker, 3 metres long and 120 degrees wide, for 6 damage, 10 stagger and two Tremor. For 3 seconds, the attacker''s wind-ups go at 0.7 times their normal speed, and for 3.5 seconds your next blow that lands breaks 50% more balance.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rooted (standing still counts), Immovable, which comes after it on the chart, Heavy Hand, Cairn''s Master mark, Buckled on the Forge branch.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Every blow should be blocked for as long as you stand, not only the first. Each blocked blow should add 20 stagger (25 with the Empower) to your next heavy blow within a second of letting go. The wait should be only 5 seconds if nothing hit the stone. For now, the first blow is blocked, and your next blow of any kind is powered up.


      <b>Example.</b> A duellist starts its string of blows. Turn to stone and take the first cut on the stone. Its next swing comes slowly enough for you to guard.'
  - Id: earth.stance.shape
    Title: 'Mountain Stance: Stone Skin or Fist of Rock'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Mountain Stance. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Stone Skin</b>: the blow is blocked but not answered. Instead, two plates of stone grow on your shoulders. Each one takes most of one blow and stops you from flinching, then breaks in a burst around you. <b>Fist of Rock</b> (the stance as it was first built): you stand as stone for a short while. The first blow to land does nothing to you, and a fist of rock rises under the enemy that hit you. One blow, answered right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Mountain Stance, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast Grey Stone.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Stone Skin: when the stance blocks a blow, you get two wards, up to three. Each one blocks one blow and breaks in a burst 3 metres around you, for 14 damage, 24 stagger and one Tremor. Fist of Rock: you stand for up to 1.5 seconds. The answer is a cone 4 metres long and 90 degrees wide, for 30 damage, 60 stagger and two Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stone Skin: Glaze''s ward if you carry Ice, Heavy Hand, Immovable. Fist of Rock: Tremor and Cairn, and Unbroken Cairn''s heavy blow after it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Stone Skin''s plates should take 60% of one blow (for now, a ward takes all of it), and the stance''s jolt should be turned off while you use this shape. Fist of Rock''s answer should rise under the enemy that hit you; for now, it is a cone thrown at it.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Stone Skin before a pack closes in. The first two blows that reach you each throw stone back at the whole ring.'
  - Id: earth.stance.master
    Title: 'Mountain Stance: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Block a red attack, and the attacker is stunned for a second and a half (less for a god or an elite), and Mountain Stance is ready to cast again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, whenever the stance blocks any blow. Mountain Stance must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The attacker is held for 1.5 seconds (0.6 for a god or an elite; the colossus not at all). The stance''s wait ends, at most once every 20 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fist of Rock (a full answer on a held enemy), Cairn, and your heavy blows on the held enemy.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work on red attacks. The game can''t tell yet whether the blocked blow was red, so any blow counts, and the stance comes back at most once every 20 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute''s red overhead swing lands on the stone. It stands stunned while you break its balance.'
  - Id: earth.immovable
    Title: Immovable
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Immovable, a keystone. You stop rolling away. Your roll barely moves you, but every blow does less damage to you, guarding and dodging cost less stamina, and the timing window for a perfect guard is longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Your roll goes a tenth as far (about half a metre). You take 16% less damage. Dodges and blocked blows cost 30% less stamina. The perfect guard window is 30% longer.


      <b>Works with.</b> Mountain Stance, which it comes after on the chart, Rooted (don''t roll, and both work), Heavy Hand, the Master marks of Cairn and Stone Rise (you are always holding your ground), Bedrock.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The dodge should become a brace that doesn''t move you, whose first moment blocks any blow, red ones too, and Rooted should never end. For now, the roll is still a roll, it lets blows pass through it, and it still ends Rooted.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight a duellist up close. Guard everything, perfect-guard what you can, and roll in place through its one red thrust.'
  - Id: earth.standingstones
    Title: Standing Stones
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When one of your spells hits an enemy, a pillar of stone comes down on it a moment later and shakes it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever one of your spells hits an enemy, Stone Rise or any spell in your slots. At most once every second and a half.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The pillar falls on a warning ring 0.5 seconds later. It has a 1.5-metre radius and deals 20 damage, 35 stagger and one stack of Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stone Rise, which sits next to it on the chart, Left in Rubble and Fissure, Quake Step, Tremor and Cairn.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The plan changes how it works: two of your stones can stand at once, and an enemy knocked into one is hurt and breaks it. Nothing stands on the field to be knocked into yet, so the falling pillar takes its place.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Stone Rise under an archer, and half a second later a pillar lands on it too.'
  - Id: earth.base
    Title: Stone Rise
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Stone Rise, Earth''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A quarter second after you cast it, big stones burst up under the enemy you face and knock it into the air for a moment. Then they sink back into the ground. No wall is left behind.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Earth, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Earth. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds. It hits an enemy within 5 metres, in a 1.3-metre circle around it, for 14 damage, 30 stagger and one Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Tremor and Cairn; Standing Stones (a pillar on what it hits); the charged blade, which it charges with Earth when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s 18 damage and 36 stagger. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Raise the stone under the archer at the back. It is knocked into the air and shaken.'
  - Id: earth.base.shape
    Title: 'Stone Rise: Raised Stone or Dropped From the Apron'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Stone Rise. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Raised Stone</b>: the pillar that lifted the enemy stays next to it, blocking enemies and thrown attacks. A heavy blow of yours on it shatters it into a cone of shards that flies away from you. Raise it, fight around it, then break it through them. <b>Dropped From the Apron</b>: a boulder falls from above onto the spot, its shadow growing, and stays there. A heavy blow on it rolls it in the direction of the blow, crushing a path, until it breaks on a wall or a large enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Stone Rise, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, the plain stone rises.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Raised Stone: for 6 seconds, your next heavy blow on the enemy it lifted throws a cone of shards from you through it. The cone is 5 metres long and 70 degrees wide, and deals 18 damage, 20 stagger and one Tremor. Dropped From the Apron: it lands on the nearest enemy within 5 metres, 0.6 seconds after you cast it, in a 1.5-metre circle around it, for 24 damage, 45 stagger and two Tremor. For 6 seconds, a heavy blow on that enemy crushes a path 8 metres long past it, away from you, dealing 16 damage, 30 stagger and one Tremor to each enemy it hits.


      <b>Works with.</b> Raised Stone: Heavy Hand, The Slow Hand. Dropped From the Apron: a pack in a hallway, Cairn on the path it shook.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Raised Stone''s pillar should stay standing next to the enemy, block things, and be hit itself (for now, the stones sink back like normal, and the heavy blow lands on the enemy). Dropped From the Apron''s boulder should fall from above and lie there as something to hit, breaking on a wall or a large enemy (for now, stone rises under the nearest enemy, and the heavy blow on that enemy sends the path on).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Dropped From the Apron on the first of three soldiers in a hallway. One heavy blow on it crushes the two behind.'
  - Id: earth.base.master
    Title: 'Stone Rise: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it while you hold your ground, Rooted or standing still for a second, and the stone rises under the enemy''s own feet. It throws the enemy three metres away from you with two stacks of Tremor, before the enemy can stand.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, when you cast it while Rooted (you haven''t started a dodge for 3 seconds). Stone Rise must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The enemy it hits is thrown 3 metres straight away from you and takes one more stack of Tremor (two, with the stone''s own).


      <b>Works with.</b> Rooted, Immovable, Mountain Stance, and Cairn on the two stacks it leaves.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should also work after standing still for one second (for now, only Rooted counts), and the throw should happen before the stones sink back.


      <b>Example.</b> Hold your ground, and raise the stone under a brute that got too close. It is thrown off you with two stacks.'
  - Id: earth.lift
    Title: Left in Rubble
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Left in Rubble, Earth''s reaction lift. Every reaction with Tremor in it hits 20% harder and leaves a knee-high pile of rubble where it happened, with a puff of yellow-brown dust and broken stone. The pile shakes whatever stands in it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a reaction with Tremor in it happens, on any enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The reaction''s own damage is 20% higher. A pile 1 metre across that lasts 6 seconds. Once a second, it deals 3 damage, 8 stagger and one Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every element pair with Tremor in it (The Ground Gives with Soaked, Chokedamp with Poison, and the rest), Stone Remembers (it lasts three times as long), and Fissure and Stone Remembers, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The rubble should count as one of your stones, which heavy blows can knock enemies into and which The Circle Speaks can cast from.


      <b>Example.</b> Soak and shake a soldier. The ground gives way under it, and the rubble left behind keeps shaking the next enemy that walks through.'
  - Id: earth.fissure
    Title: Fissure
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fissure, a breaker. You drive your weapon into the ground. A crack runs ahead of you and breaks open into a row of spikes. The spikes hit and shake whatever they catch, and hold normal enemies where they stand.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds and the damage 26.) The crack runs 8 metres at 20 metres a second. There is a spike every 1.5 metres, starting 1.3 metres out, each with a 1-metre radius. Each enemy is hit once, for 20 damage, 35 stagger and one Tremor, and is held for 1.2 seconds (0.5 for a god or an elite).


      <b>Works with.</b> Left in Rubble, which it comes after on the chart, Standing Stones, Tremor and Cairn, and your heavy blows on the enemies it holds. When it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The crack should glow for 0.9 seconds before it breaks open; for now, it breaks open as it runs.


      <b>Example.</b> Three spearmen stand in a line down a staircase. One crack catches all three and holds them for your blade.'
  - Id: earth.fissure.shape
    Title: 'Fissure: Basalt or Fault Line'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Fissure. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Basalt</b>: the spikes come up as a row of six-sided stone columns, like the slow-cooled stone of the giant''s causeway, and stand as a wall. A heavy blow on the row knocks it over away from you, column after column, like dominoes. <b>Fault Line</b>: the crack doesn''t break open on its own. It stays open, and a heavy blow you land next to it makes the whole line erupt. You lay down the line, then fight next to it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Fissure, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain crack.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Basalt: for 4 seconds, a heavy blow on an enemy the spikes hit knocks over a path 6 metres long past it, away from you, dealing 20 damage and 30 stagger to each enemy. Fault Line: the crack itself does no damage. For 6 seconds, a heavy blow on an enemy it crossed erupts in a 2-metre circle around that enemy, for 24 damage, 40 stagger and two Tremor, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Hand and The Slow Hand, and Tremor and Cairn on the enemies the eruption shook.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Basalt''s columns should stand as a wall that blocks, and fall over one after another (for now, the heavy blow on an enemy the spikes hit sends a path on). Fault Line''s open crack should show on the ground and erupt along its whole length when you land a heavy blow within 2 metres of it, at most twice (for now, a heavy blow on an enemy the crack crossed erupts under that enemy, up to twice a second).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Fault Line under a pack, then fight among them. Every heavy blow erupts under the enemy it lands on.'
  - Id: earth.fissure.master
    Title: 'Fissure: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land a heavy blow on the crack''s line while it glows, and it erupts right away, 50% harder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, a heavy blow on an enemy within 0.9 seconds after the spikes hit it. Fissure must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A second eruption in a 1-metre circle around the enemy, for 10 damage, 18 stagger and one Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Hand (nothing can stop the heavy blow as it lands), The Slow Hand, Tremor.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The crack''s glow before it breaks open, and landing the heavy blow on its line during that glow. For now, the timing window opens when the spikes hit, and it adds a second eruption on that enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast the crack at a brute, and step in with a heavy blow as it breaks open. It erupts again under the brute.'
  - Id: earth.devotion
    Title: Stone Remembers
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Stone Remembers, Earth''s Devotion, for going all in on Earth only. It is the rule of the Celtic land, "stone remembers", given to you. Nothing you raise falls on its own: stones, boulders, columns and slabs stay standing until something breaks them. An enemy knocked into one takes a heavy blow of stone, and the stone stays standing. The longer the fight, the more of the floor is yours.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Earth twice (all in). It is always on then.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. For now: whatever you leave on the ground (a spell''s ground, a danger zone from your marks, Left in Rubble''s piles) lasts three times as long. A heavy blow that lands hits its enemy as if it were knocked into stone, in a 1-metre circle around it, for 20 damage, 35 stagger and one Tremor, once every 3 seconds. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Mountain Cleaver. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Earth alone or twice.


      <b>Works with.</b> Left in Rubble, which it comes after on the chart, Fissure, Stone Rise, Standing Stones, Heavy Hand.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Stones that never fall on their own, up to eight, with the oldest crumbling away, and enemies knocked into them by a heavy blow, a finisher or a quake. Nothing stands on the field to be knocked into yet, so every heavy blow takes its place, once every 3 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> A long fight in a narrow hall. The rubble from every reaction stays, and every heavy blow lands as if into stone.'
  - Id: earth.hand
    Title: Hand of the Mountain
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While Bedrock is lit, a hand of stone as tall as a tower hangs over your shoulder. Each time you land a heavy blow or a finisher, the hand rises over that enemy and comes down on it a moment later, hitting everything around it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light Bedrock with the surge button. The hand stays until Bedrock ends.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. The hand falls 0.5 seconds after the blow, in a circle with a 3-metre radius. It deals 70% of the blow''s damage again, 40 stagger, counts as a heavy blow, and adds one stack of Tremor. It answers one blow at a time: a heavy blow you land while it is still falling isn''t answered.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bedrock, Heavy Hand, The Slow Hand, Tremor, finishers.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Bedrock and finish a staggered soldier. The hand comes down on that spot and staggers the two soldiers behind it.'
  - Id: earth.closedring
    Title: The Closed Ring
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Closed Ring, a keystone. Lighting Bedrock raises eight stones in a ring around you. For ten seconds, they hit and shake whatever stands among them, and you take a little less damage. Bedrock costs a quarter more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When you light Bedrock, the surge. Always on once you learn it, while you carry Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Eight stones in a ring 12 metres across, each a metre wide, for 10 seconds. Once a second, each stone hits whatever stands on it for 8 damage, 20 stagger and one stack of Tremor. You take 8% less damage for the same 10 seconds. Bedrock costs 25% more mana (50 instead of 40).


      <b>Works with.</b> Cairn, which it comes after on the chart (from its Master mark), Standing Stones and Left in Rubble (more stone around you), Rooted and Immovable (stand inside the ring and let them come), Hand of the Mountain.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ring should act as a wall: nothing can walk, roll or be thrown across it while it stands, an enemy knocked into a stone is hurt and staggered, and you are Rooted inside it. For now, the stones only hit what stands on them, and anything can pass between them.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Bedrock with two soldiers on you. The ring rises around all three of you, and every second they stand in it they are shaken closer to a stagger.'
  - Id: earthed.shape
    Title: 'Earthed: Thunderstone or Step Current'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Ground Strike unlocks two new shapes for Earthed. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Earthed uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Thunderstone</b>: the charge doesn''t spread. It is held in a stone of glassy rock until your heavy blow throws it out as bolts at everything nearby. <b>Step Current</b>: the fern-shaped burn mark on the ground spreads wider and stays live, so enemies who come at you pay for every step.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Earthed goes off while you carry both Lightning and Earth. Earthed goes off when Static lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Earthed.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Thunderstone: nothing is used up. The enemy''s Static is held for 6 seconds; it doesn''t fade, and blows don''t use it up. The enemy is rooted in place for 1 second. Your first heavy blow on it sets off all of the Static at once, for 7 damage per stack to everything within 6 metres, and puts a Static on each of them. Step Current: the enemy''s Static runs out into everything within 6 metres, for 5 damage and 3 stagger per stack, plus a Tremor. The burn mark stays for 3 seconds and deals 4 damage and 6 stagger every half second. The enemy is rooted for 1 second. After Earthed goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Watch Kept (charge it again with a perfect guard), Deep Earth, The Ground Answers, The Capstone.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack around one big enemy: use Thunderstone, build up lots of charge, and choose your moment. A rush of enemies coming at you: use Step Current.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Thunderstone''s stone doesn''t yet stand on its own where the enemy stood. For now, the enemy holds the charge, only your heavy blow throws it, and a burst of Static nearby doesn''t. Step Current''s burn mark hurts everything on it, standing still or not, but not yet the enemy it started from.'
  - Id: earthed.watchkept
    Title: The Watch Kept
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against an enemy that has Tremor puts two Static on it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard against a shaken enemy, while you carry both Lightning and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two Static, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthed (a shaken enemy that gets charged is Earthed right away), Thunderstone, The Ground Answers.


      <b>Example.</b> Shake the brute and block its swing perfectly. The ground takes its charge.'
  - Id: earthed.deepearth
    Title: Deep Earth
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Earthed''s burn mark is 1 metre wider, and the Earthed enemy stays rooted 0.5 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Earthed goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The burn mark reaches 5 metres instead of 4, and the enemy is rooted for 1.5 seconds instead of 1. With Thunderstone and Step Current, the 6-metre reach becomes 7 metres, and the root lasts 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthed and its shapes, The Watch Kept.


      <b>Example.</b> The fern-shaped mark reaches the archer at the back.'
  - Id: earthed.groundanswers
    Title: The Ground Answers
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every rock you raise holds charge. When Static goes off within 4 metres of one of your rocks, it runs into that rock and out of every other rock you have standing, dealing 7 damage per stack within 2.5 metres of each.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Static goes off, while you carry both Lightning and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: when Static goes off on a shaken enemy, it bursts again around that enemy for 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack within 2.5 metres, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthed, The Watch Kept, The Capstone, Earth''s raised stones.


      <b>Example.</b> Raise stones around the room, then set off Static next to any of them.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your rocks don''t hold charge yet. For now, the second burst comes from the shaken enemy itself, not out of every rock you have standing.'
  - Id: earthed.capstone
    Title: The Capstone
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A round-topped stone falls on the enemy you face. It deals 18 damage and 40 stagger, fills its Tremor all the way, and pins it in place for 2 seconds. While the stone stands, each weapon blow near it calls a bolt of lightning down it into the ground, for 6 damage, 12 stagger and a Static within 2 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Lightning and Earth. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands on an enemy up to 7 metres away, after 0.4 seconds. It deals 18 damage, 40 stagger, a Static, fills Tremor all the way, and holds the enemy for 2 seconds (0.8 for elites and gods). For 5 seconds, it calls a bolt at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthed (the stone charges a shaken enemy, so it is Earthed), The Watch Kept, When the Watch Sleeps.


      <b>Example.</b> Pin the captain and keep hitting it. Every blow calls a bolt down around it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the stone looks like a big sword falling and cracking the ground. The bolts come from your blows on the pinned enemy itself, not from blows on anything within 2 metres of the stone.'
  - Id: earthed.watchsleeps
    Title: When the Watch Sleeps
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, the god looks away and the ground is loose. Every heavy blow shakes the floor within 3 metres of the enemy you hit. Any enemy shaken while it has Static is Earthed right away. Heavy blows also deal 8 more stagger.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Lightning and Earth. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: each heavy blow puts a Tremor on everything within 3 metres and 8 more stagger on the enemy you hit. A charged enemy you hit is Earthed right away, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthed, The Capstone, The Watch Kept.


      <b>Example.</b> Charge up a crowd, light your surge, and every heavy blow sets off Earthed on them.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the enemy you hit is Earthed right away, not every charged enemy the shaking reaches.'
  - Id: earthlight.shape
    Title: 'Earthlight: Down the Passage or Lit Seam'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Lit Stone unlocks two new shapes for Earthlight. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Earthlight uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Down the Passage</b>: the light keeps going through the ground in a straight line from the enemy, away from you. <b>Lit Seam</b>: the cracks of light stay lit, and each heavy blow on the enemy flashes them again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Earthlight goes off while you carry both Earth and Light. Earthlight goes off when a Mark lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on a Marked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Earthlight.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes put a fresh Mark on the enemy. Down the Passage: half a second later, a line 8 metres long and 1 metre wide past the enemy deals 12 damage and a Mark to everything on it. Lit Seam: the light breaks out like the plain one (14 damage, 25 stagger and a Mark within 3 metres). Then for 5 seconds, each heavy blow on the enemy flashes for 10 damage and 15 stagger on everything within 3 metres. After Earthlight goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stone Rise and Glare to set it up, Keeping the Hour, Quartz Vein, Dawn Through the Door.


      <b>Example.</b> A line of archers stands behind a shaken soldier. Mark the soldier from the front, and Down the Passage lights up the whole line.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cracks aren''t drawn as a star shape yet. For now, the light hits everything inside the ring, and Lit Seam flashes around the enemy you hit, not only on enemies standing on the cracks.'
  - Id: earthlight.hour
    Title: Keeping the Hour
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every 6 seconds during a fight, your next heavy blow is right on the hour: it hits harder and shakes the enemy twice.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A heavy blow or finisher that lands, at most once every 6 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two stacks of Tremor and 10 more damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthlight (gives the Tremor to start it), Quartz Vein, The Stone Keeps the Hour.


      <b>Example.</b> Keep your heavy blows six seconds apart, and each one shakes and lights up a Marked soldier.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> There''s no sundial at your feet to show the hour yet. For now, the blow hits for 10 more damage instead of being a critical hit.'
  - Id: earthlight.dawn
    Title: Dawn Through the Door
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked enemy blinds the attacker. It gets Marked again, and its Tremor is filled all the way.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has a Mark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A Mark, and Tremor filled all the way.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthlight (both afflictions at once start it), Glare, Mountain Stance.


      <b>Example.</b> A Marked brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and Earthlight goes off under it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It''s meant to work when you make a perfect guard while standing on the glowing cracks. For now, it works against any Marked attacker.'
  - Id: earthlight.quartz
    Title: Quartz Vein
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Earthlight''s star of light is 0.5 metres wider.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Earthlight goes off, plain or with a shape.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The star reaches 3.5 metres instead of 3, and so does Lit Seam''s. With Down the Passage, the line is half a metre wider.


      <b>Works with.</b> Keeping the Hour, which it comes after on the chart, Earthlight.


      <b>Example.</b> The soldier standing just outside the light gets Marked too.'
  - Id: earthlight.stone
    Title: The Stone Keeps the Hour
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A standing stone rises in front of you, and a line of sunlight sweeps around it like the shadow on a sundial. Every enemy it crosses is hit, shaken and Marked. It never hurts you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Earth and Light. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 16 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The stone stands 3 metres ahead of you for 9 seconds. Once a second, everything within 6 metres takes 10 damage, 15 stagger, a Tremor and a Mark.


      <b>Works with.</b> Earthlight (every enemy it hits gets both afflictions), Keeping the Hour, Hammer and Anvil.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it in a doorway and fight around it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The light doesn''t sweep around the stone as a line yet. The stone doesn''t block the way, and it doesn''t count as stone behind an enemy. A blow timed to the line isn''t made a critical hit yet.'
  - Id: eclipse.shape
    Title: 'Diamond Ring: Shadow Bands or Through the Throat'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Diamond Ring. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Diamond Ring uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Shadow Bands</b>: the bead of light breaks like before, and bands of shadow ripple out across the floor, Cursing every enemy they cross. <b>Through the Throat</b>: the light slips out of the enemy''s far side as a line that cuts and Marks everything on it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Diamond Ring goes off while you carry both Light and Dark. Diamond Ring goes off when a Curse lands on a Marked enemy, or when a Mark lands on a Cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Diamond Ring.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Shadow Bands: half a second later, the bead deals 16 damage and 30 stagger, and puts a Curse on everything within 5 metres. Through the Throat: a line 6 metres long past the enemy deals 12 damage and a Mark to everything on it. With both shapes, your blows don''t use up the enemy''s Mark for 4 seconds. After Diamond Ring goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Dusk and Dawn (its cut sets off Diamond Ring even if it would normally have to wait), Grasp and Glare to set it up, The Dividing Line.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack around a Marked soldier: Shadow Bands Curses them all. A line of enemies: stand at one end, and Through the Throat Marks the whole line.'
  - Id: eclipse.daynight
    Title: Day and Night
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge heals you a little. For a while after a perfect dodge, a perfect guard gives double mana. For a while after a perfect guard, a perfect dodge gives double mana. Switching between the two pays double.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every perfect dodge heals you. Each perfect dodge starts a window when perfect guards give double mana. Each perfect guard starts a window when perfect dodges give double mana.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. You heal 5 for each perfect dodge. Each window lasts 6 seconds. During it, a perfect guard gives 30 mana instead of 15, and a perfect dodge gives 24 instead of 12. For now, the dodge always heals you, not only after a guard.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clarity (perfect timing windows last 50% longer), Glare''s Master mark (a perfect dodge that holds and Marks), Low Sun, and Daybreak and the deep strike to spend the mana.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a duellist''s lunge at the last moment, then perfect-guard its next cut. You get 5 health and 42 mana from two blows.'
  - Id: eclipse.duskdawn
    Title: Dusk and Dawn
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You step through the enemy you face and cut it as you pass, coming out on its far side turned back toward it. If the cut lands, your next heavy blow right after is a critical hit. Nothing can hit you during the step. A wall, or an enemy too big to push past, stops you short.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). For now, it is the step and its critical heavy blow. The saved-up cut of light that should land with that heavy blow is not built yet.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, 5 Shards, 45 mana. It goes up to 5 metres: to 1.8 metres past the enemy you face, or about 4 metres ahead if there is none. The cut deals 10 damage, 10 stagger and a Mark to everything on the line. The heavy blow after it is a critical hit if it lands within 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Diamond Ring (on a Marked and Cursed enemy, its cut sets off Diamond Ring right away, even if it would normally have to wait), Light''s root mark, Reckoning''s Spoken Sentence shape, Weighed, and the charged blade, which it charges with Light.


      <b>Example.</b> Step through a captain''s guard, turn around, and land a critical heavy blow on its back.'
  - Id: eclipse.lowsun
    Title: Low Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your weapon blows give more mana. Each time your health drops below a third, they give much more for a while.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The bigger bonus comes each time your health drops below a third.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Weapon blows give 20% more mana. Dropping below a third adds 60% more for 12 seconds, for 80% in total. For now, it doesn''t yet rise smoothly as your health drops.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Dividing Line, Day and Night, Dark''s marks that you pay for with health, Brimming (gives a use for keeping your mana full), and the mana spells it pays for: Dusk and Dawn, Daybreak.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute''s blow leaves you at a quarter of your health. For twelve seconds, every cut fills your mana almost twice as fast, enough for Dusk and Dawn and a way out.'
  - Id: eclipse.dividingline
    Title: The Dividing Line
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your health decides how you fight. At half your health or more, your critical hits hit harder and your blows give more mana. Below half, every blow Curses and heals you. Each time your health drops below a third, a burst around you Curses everything nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The day side works at half health or more. The night side works below half. The burst comes each time your health drops below a third.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. At half health or more: critical hits deal 1.7 times their damage instead of 1.5, and blows give 20% more mana. Below half: each weapon blow Curses and heals you 8% of its damage. Below a third: a burst with a 3.5-metre radius around you deals 16 damage, 25 stagger and a Curse, at most once every 4 seconds. For now, the burst doesn''t come when your health goes back up past half.


      <b>Works with.</b> Day and Night or Low Sun (it comes after either on the chart), Light''s root mark and Unblemished on the day side, Dark''s Cursed kills on the night side, Totality.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute knocks you below half. Each cut now Curses and heals you. When the next blow drops you below a third, the burst Curses the pack around you.'
  - Id: eclipse.totality
    Title: Totality
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When your surge starts, the sun is covered. While it is dark, every enemy near you stays Marked and Cursed, and every weapon blow you land is a critical hit and heals you. The game remembers how much damage your weapon did to each enemy in the dark. When the surge ends, the light comes back, and a column of light hits every enemy you hurt for a share of that damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light your surge with the surge button (whichever element''s surge you have). The light comes back when it ends.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. Every second, every enemy within 14 metres is Marked and Cursed. Every weapon blow is a critical hit and heals you 1, at most five times a second. The column of light deals half the damage your weapon did to that enemy, up to 80, plus 30 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clarity (eight seconds of dark, and every blow also Marks) or Hunger (it heals you while it drains), Diamond Ring''s shapes, Weighed, Reckoning''s Spoken Sentence shape, The Dividing Line.


      <b>Example.</b> Light the surge among four soldiers. Cut each of them hard while it is dark, and watch the light come back down on all four when it ends.'
  - Id: fire.cinder
    Title: Cinder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands sets the enemy on fire and adds one stack of Burn. Each blow after that adds another stack, up to five. A burning enemy takes damage every second, and more for each stack.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while Fire is your first element: every weapon blow that lands, light or heavy. If Fire is your second element, your weapon adds nothing. Burn then comes from your Fire spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack per blow, up to five. A burn deals 5 damage a second, and each stack adds 30%, so it deals 12.5 a second at five stacks. Each blow resets its timer to 4 seconds. When the timer runs out, all the stacks go away. When another mark sets off a burn, it deals 10 damage plus 10 more for each stack: 60 at five stacks.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heat, Breath of the Lioness (its cone sets off a burn), Catching Fire, Meteor Leap''s Master mark, and Kindle.


      <b>Example.</b> Five quick light blows on a soldier give it five stacks. It burns for 12.5 a second while you step back and raise your guard.'
  - Id: fire.heat
    Title: Heat
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Landing blows without getting hit builds up Heat, one level at a time, up to five. Each level makes your weapon hit harder. A blow that lands on you puts it out, and staying out of the fight too long cools it down one level at a time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. Every weapon blow that lands counts. Lighting Kindle with the surge button fills it right away.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Three blows for each level, up to five levels. Each level adds 6% weapon damage, so 30% at five. Going six seconds without landing a blow loses a level. A blow that lands on you drops it to zero.


      <b>Works with.</b> White Heat, Wildfire Rush (a level for each enemy you pass through), Kindle, and the two spells that come after it on the chart, Uraeus and Meteor Leap.


      <b>Example.</b> Fifteen blows in, every cut does 30% more damage. One careless moment and a brute''s swing puts you back to zero.'
  - Id: fire.base
    Title: Flame Wave
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Flame Wave, Fire''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. Fire runs along the ground in a cone in front of you, hitting everything in it and setting it on fire. Three patches of fire keep burning where it ran.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Fire, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Fire. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 5.5 seconds. The cone is 5.5 metres long and 70 degrees wide. It deals 20 damage, 24 stagger and a stack of Burn. Three patches keep burning for 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Cinder, to add more Burn to what it set on fire; and the charged blade, which it charges with Fire when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s longer-lasting patches (5 seconds). For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight by casting it across a line of soldiers. Every one of them is burning before your first blow.'
  - Id: fire.base.shape
    Title: 'Flame Wave: Cinder Fan or Running Fire'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Flame Wave. You pick one at any rest fire, in the Hand. <b>Cinder Fan</b>: short and wide, the shape for when you are surrounded. Whatever it touches gets two stacks of Burn and is thrown back. <b>Running Fire</b>: one narrow tongue of flame runs far along the ground, and leaves a low burning line behind it. Anything that crosses the line gets two Burn.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Flame Wave, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain wave.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Cinder Fan: 3 metres long and 150 degrees wide. It deals 22 damage, 20 stagger and two Burn, and throws enemies back 1.5 metres. Running Fire: it runs 12 metres in under a second. Whatever the flame touches takes 12 damage, 10 stagger and two Burn. The line burns for 4 seconds, dealing 3 damage and two Burn every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire and Black Pitch in a crowd (Cinder Fan). Breath of the Lioness and Kiln Stance against a pack that closes in. Fuel to It on wet ground (Running Fire across a river makes steam).


      <b>Example.</b> Surrounded by four soldiers, Cinder Fan throws them all back with two stacks of Burn. Facing an archer behind a wall of shields, Running Fire can reach it.'
  - Id: fire.base.master
    Title: 'Flame Wave: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast Flame Wave at the same moment the charged blade''s third blow lands, and its wait doesn''t start.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When the third blow of a charged blade lands, your next Flame Wave within half a second has no wait. Flame Wave must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You have 0.5 seconds after the third charged blow. Then the wave lands, charges the blade again, and the cycle keeps going.


      <b>Works with.</b> The charged blade most of all, plus Cinder and Heat, which reward you for staying in the fight.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The wave should come out of the blade along the swing. For now, it comes out of you, like normal.


      <b>Example.</b> Wave, three blows, and wave again right away: a burning crowd that never gets back on its feet.'
  - Id: fire.uraeus
    Title: Uraeus
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A cobra made of flame rises from your shoulder and stays for a while, spitting burning venom at enemies near you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button. It spits on its own while it is there.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 8). It stays for 8 seconds and spits at enemies within 8 metres, up to six times (its Empower makes it eight). Each spit deals 8 damage, 6 stagger and two Burn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heat, which it comes after on the chart; Cinder; and its Master mark on enemies that are already burning.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The spit should answer an enemy that starts an attack (the game needs a way to notice an enemy''s wind-up), and the venom should burn at the enemy''s feet. For now, the cobra spits on its own, like a guard made of fire.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it before a pack closes in. Every enemy that comes near gets hit with venom while you fight the first one.'
  - Id: fire.uraeus.shape
    Title: 'Uraeus: Coiled or Set Down'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Uraeus. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Coiled</b>: it stays close and only spits at enemies within 3 metres, and each spit adds three Burn. <b>Set Down</b>: it is planted where you cast it and guards a 9-metre circle around itself, whether you stay or not. Leave it at a door.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Uraeus, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Coiled: it reaches 3 metres, and adds three Burn per spit. Set Down: it spits every 1.2 seconds at an enemy within 9 metres of the cobra, for 8 damage and two Burn, for 8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kiln Stance and Breath of the Lioness (Coiled, for fighting up close). Black Pitch and Catching Fire (Set Down next to a crowd).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Coiled''s spit should freeze the enemy''s wind-up for a moment.


      <b>Example.</b> Set it down at a gate, and fight the brute on the far side of the yard while the cobra keeps the soldiers burning.'
  - Id: fire.uraeus.master
    Title: 'Uraeus: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each spit that lands on an enemy that is already burning adds double the Burn. The fire is fed.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When your Uraeus''s spit hits an enemy that is already burning. Uraeus must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Two more stacks of Burn on top of the spit''s own.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder and Flame Wave, which set enemies on fire first.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cobra should stay a second longer for each spit that feeds the fire.


      <b>Example.</b> Set a pack on fire with Flame Wave, then cast Uraeus. Every spit brings them a step closer to five stacks.'
  - Id: fire.meteor
    Title: Meteor Leap
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You leap out of the fight and come down next to the enemy you face like a falling stone. The landing is a heavy blow that leaves a burning crater. Nothing can hit you while you are in the air.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 45 mana (its Empower makes it 35). It finds the enemy you face within 9 metres and lands you 1 metre in front of it. If there is none, you land 5 metres ahead. The leap takes 0.62 seconds. The landing hits everything within 3.2 metres for 46 damage and 55 stagger. The crater burns for 4 seconds and deals 5 damage every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heat, which it comes after on the chart; Catching Fire; and its Master mark on an enemy with five Burn.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s longer-lasting crater (6 seconds).


      <b>Example.</b> An archer stands behind two shield bearers. Leap over them and land on it.'
  - Id: fire.meteor.shape
    Title: 'Meteor Leap: Falling Star or Ash Fall'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Meteor Leap. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Falling Star</b>: you don''t leap. Instead, a burning stone falls where you aim a moment later, and you keep fighting. <b>Ash Fall</b>: the landing throws hot ash around you, and enemies in it keep catching fire.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Meteor Leap, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Falling Star: it falls 0.8 seconds after you cast it, within 9 metres. It deals 46 damage, 60 stagger and a stack of Burn within 3.2 metres, and leaves a burning patch for 4 seconds. Ash Fall: ash covers 3 metres around where you land for 5 seconds. Every second, it deals a Burn and 2 damage to everything in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder and Catching Fire (Ash Fall in a crowd). Kiln Stance and Breath of the Lioness (Falling Star while you hold your ground).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Falling Star''s own stone and growing shadow (for now, it uses the thrown ball). Enemies'' balance not coming back while they stand in Ash Fall.


      <b>Example.</b> Hold the line with your guard, and call the stone down on the brute behind it.'
  - Id: fire.meteor.master
    Title: 'Meteor Leap: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land Meteor Leap on an enemy with five Burn, and its burn goes off with the blow. You also get half the mana back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When the leap''s landing hits an enemy with full Burn. Meteor Leap must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The burn goes off for 10 damage per stack (50 at five). You get 22 mana back, once per landing.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder and Uraeus, to bring an enemy up to five; Heat, for extra damage behind it.


      <b>Example.</b> Five quick blows on the brute, step back, and leap. Its burn goes off under the stone, and your mana is half full again.'
  - Id: fire.lift
    Title: Fuel to It
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fuel to It. Every reaction with Burn in it hits 25% harder and leaves the ground where it happened on fire.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Burn reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Steam with Soaked, Flashover with Static, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The reaction''s own damage is 25% higher. A burning patch with a 1.5-metre radius that lasts 3 seconds. Every half second, it deals 3 damage and a stack of Burn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Fire''s ten reactions.


      <b>Example.</b> Fire and Water on one enemy: Steam bursts, and the ground under it keeps burning.'
  - Id: fire.whiteheat
    Title: White Heat
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heat can go up to eight. Every level above five also makes you swing faster. But if you get hit while above five, you catch fire yourself for a while.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The extra speed comes with each level above five. The burn comes when a blow lands on you while you are above five.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Heat goes up to eight, which gives 48% more weapon damage at the top. Each level above five adds 5% swing speed, so 15% at eight. If you are hit above five, you burn for 4 seconds, taking 4 damage a second, but it never takes your last point. The blow still puts your Heat out, like always.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heat, Wildfire Rush, and Kindle, which now fills Heat up to eight right away.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Kindle at the start of a fight. You are at eight Heat right away, swinging 15% faster for 48% more damage, as long as nothing hits you.'
  - Id: fire.devotion
    Title: Fire Without End
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fire Without End, Fire''s Devotion, for going all in on Fire only. Burns never go out on their own; only death or water ends them. A burn you leave alone grows stronger by itself. Set everything on fire once, then fight the enemy in front of you while the rest of the room cooks.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Fire twice (all in), once you buy it. Then always, on every burning enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A burn''s timer no longer runs down. Every burning enemy that hasn''t had Burn added for 4 seconds gains a stack, and another stack 4 seconds after that, up to five. A burn that is set off still goes away, and Steam (Soaked meeting Burn) still takes half of it. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Burnt Offering. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Fire alone or twice. It costs 20 mana and has its own 8-second wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, Flame Wave, Catching Fire, Breath of the Lioness (which sets off a burn), and the deep strike, whose pillar sets off the burn on the enemy it lands on.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast Flame Wave across a room, then fight one soldier alone. When you turn around, the rest are at five stacks.'
  - Id: fire.backdraft
    Title: Breath of the Lioness
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Breath of the Lioness (it used to be called Backdraft). A perfect guard throws a cone of fire back at the enemy that hit you, the way the goddess Sekhmet''s breath was the desert wind. If the enemy is burning, its burn goes off too.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A cone 4.5 metres long and 100 degrees wide. It deals 16 damage and 18 stagger to everything in it, and each one burns for 3 seconds. The attacker gets two stacks of Burn. A burning attacker''s burn goes off for 10 damage plus 10 more for each stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, to build up the burn it sets off; Kiln Stance (the blows it blocks count as perfect guards); Flame Trail.


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier thrusts at you. Guard just before it lands, and the fire knocks it off balance. At five Burn, its burn goes off at the same moment.'
  - Id: fire.kiln
    Title: Kiln Stance
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You stand ready, wrapped in flame. The first blow that reaches you, from any side, red attack or not, does nothing to you, and you answer it with fire. The blocked blow counts as a perfect guard, so it gives you that mana and sets off every mark that needs a perfect guard. If no blow comes, the stance goes out and the cast is used up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button. Then it works on any blow in the next 1.2 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 7 and adds a fourth Burn). It stays ready for 1.2 seconds. The answer is a pillar of fire with a 1.7-metre radius under the attacker. It deals 30 damage, 50 stagger, counts as a heavy blow, and adds three stacks of Burn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath of the Lioness, which it comes after on the chart, and its Master mark on a burning attacker.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Shut Door, the planned version: you hold it for up to 3 seconds, every blow you block is stored as smoke under a hood (up to three), and when you let go, the door opens in a cone of flame that deals 20 damage plus 15 more for each stored blow. The game needs spells you can hold down on their button first. For now, the Learn mark is the stance as it was first built, The Kiln.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute winds up a red overhead swing you can''t guard. Cast this just before it comes down, and the pillar answers it.'
  - Id: fire.kiln.shape
    Title: 'Kiln Stance: The Kiln or Firewalker'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Kiln Stance. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>The Kiln</b>: the stance as it was first built. A pillar of fire answers the first blow right away; it''s best for single blows. <b>Firewalker</b>: a stance you can walk in, for a shorter time. Walk into the enemy''s swing.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Kiln Stance, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The Kiln: ready for 1.2 seconds. The pillar deals 30 damage, 50 stagger and three Burn. Firewalker: ready for 0.6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Breath of the Lioness; Uraeus''s Coiled shape.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Walking at half speed in Firewalker (for now, it is the stance held for a shorter time).


      <b>Example.</b> Against a quick double cut, The Kiln answers the first one. Against a slow brute, Firewalker meets the swing.'
  - Id: fire.kiln.master
    Title: 'Kiln Stance: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Block a blow from an attacker that is already burning, and a pillar of fire rises under it as your answer. Its burn goes off, and it is knocked down.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When Kiln Stance''s answer hits a burning enemy. Kiln Stance must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Its burn goes off for 10 damage per stack, and it is knocked down for 1.2 seconds (less for elites and gods).


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder and Flame Wave, to set the attacker on fire first.


      <b>Example.</b> Set the brute on fire, wait for its overhead swing, and answer it. It goes up in flames and down at the same time.'
  - Id: fire.trail
    Title: Flame Trail
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your dodge leaves a line of fire on the ground behind you that burns anything walking through it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every dodge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Four patches along the dodge''s path, each with a 0.95-metre radius, left as you roll. Each burns for 2.6 seconds and deals 4 damage every half second, setting whatever it touches on fire for 2 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wildfire Rush, which comes after it on the chart, for a second line; Breath of the Lioness.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll back from a charging brute. It runs through all four patches to reach you, and arrives burning.'
  - Id: fire.rush
    Title: Wildfire Rush
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You turn into a point of fire and charge straight through every enemy in your path, coming out the far side facing back. Each enemy you pass is hit, set on fire, and raises your Heat by one level. Then a second cut lands on everyone on the line, and the line burns. Nothing can hit you while you charge. A wall, or an enemy too big to push past, stops you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 6, and makes the line burn for 4 seconds). It goes up to 7 metres, along a path 2 metres wide. As you pass, each enemy takes 14 damage, 20 stagger and two stacks of Burn. The cut after deals 14 damage, 20 stagger and two more stacks. The line burns for 2.6 seconds, dealing 4 damage every half second. You get one Heat level for each enemy you pass.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flame Trail, which it comes after on the chart; Heat and White Heat; Cinder; Catching Fire.


      <b>Example.</b> Charge through three soldiers in a row. Your Heat goes from zero to three, and each soldier has four stacks of Burn.'
  - Id: fire.rush.shape
    Title: 'Wildfire Rush: Fire Front or Smoke Road'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Wildfire Rush. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Fire Front</b>: you move as the front of a grass fire, a wall of flame. It is slower, and whatever it meets is knocked down at the end. It pushes a crowd instead of passing through it. <b>Smoke Road</b>: the escape. It leaves a line of fire between you and them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Wildfire Rush, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Fire Front: it goes 5 metres, is 4 metres wide, and moves at a third of the speed. Each enemy it hits is knocked down for 0.6 seconds. Smoke Road: it goes 6 metres, and the cut as you pass is weaker (4 damage).


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire and Black Pitch (Fire Front into a crowd); Flame Trail (Smoke Road out of one).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Fire Front should carry the enemies in front of you, and Smoke Road should take you back out of the fight. For now, both charge forward.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Fire Front through a doorway packed with soldiers. They fall down burning in a pile on the far side.'
  - Id: fire.rush.master
    Title: 'Wildfire Rush: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you start Wildfire Rush right as a blow would land on you, it counts as a perfect dodge, and your Heat goes to five.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When you cast Wildfire Rush. Wildfire Rush must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, every cast gives you what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heat and White Heat.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (starting it right as a blow would land) and Heat going to five. Both need the game to know the moment an enemy''s blow lands.


      <b>Example.</b> Charge out of a brute''s swing and through its friends, and every other spell is that much closer to ready.'
  - Id: fire.firebomb
    Title: Firebomb Flask
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Holding the flask button throws the flask instead of drinking it. It doesn''t heal. It bursts into fire where it lands, leaves flames on the ground, and puts three stacks of Burn on everything nearby. A short press still drinks it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Hold the flask button. Each throw uses up a flask.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Hold for 0.3 seconds to throw. It flies at the enemy in front of you within 9 metres, or 5 metres ahead, and lands 0.4 seconds later. The burst has a 2.6-metre radius and deals 36 damage, 30 stagger, three stacks of Burn and 4 seconds of burning. The flames left behind have a 2.2-metre radius and deal 5 damage every half second for 3.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire, Black Pitch, Flame Wave (all on the Ruin bough).


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers bunch up at a gate. One flask gives all three three stacks and leaves them standing in fire.'
  - Id: fire.catch
    Title: Catching Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a burning enemy dies, its burn jumps onto every enemy standing near it, with as many stacks as it had.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The moment a burning enemy dies, no matter what killed it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Every enemy within 4 metres of the one that died gets as many stacks of Burn as it had, up to five. This can happen at most five times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, to build up the stacks first; Black Pitch (which comes after it on the chart); Firebomb Flask; and Breath of the Lioness, to set off the new burns.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a soldier with five Burn in the middle of a pack. The two next to it get five stacks right away, without you hitting them.'
  - Id: fire.pitch
    Title: Black Pitch
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw a jar of embalmer''s pitch (a thick, sticky tar) at the enemy you face. Your next blows on it splash fire over everyone near it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button. Then it works on your next three weapon blows on the coated enemy, within 8 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 9, and makes the coat last 10 seconds). You can throw it up to 7 metres. Each of the three blows on the coated enemy sets off a burst 2 metres around it, for 16 damage, 35 stagger and three Burn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire, which it comes after on the chart; Cinder; Firebomb Flask.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The planned pitch: your blows spread the coat from enemy to enemy without lighting it. Then the first fire that touches any coated enemy (a Burn, a burning patch, or a reaction) sets all of them on fire at once, for 16 damage plus 8 more for each blow that spread it, up to 70, and the floor under each one burns. For now, the coat is a mark that your next blows set off around the coated enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> Coat the soldier in the middle of a pack and cut it three times. The pack burns with three stacks.'
  - Id: fire.pitch.shape
    Title: 'Black Pitch: Pitch Pool or The Torch'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Black Pitch. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Pitch Pool</b>: it is thrown on the floor as a pool of burning pitch. <b>The Torch</b>: one enemy is coated thickly, and nothing splashes. It burns, and every second it sets fire to everyone near it as it walks.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Black Pitch, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Pitch Pool: 3 metres across, lasts 8 seconds, and deals 3 damage and a stack of Burn every half second. The Torch: for 8 seconds, every second, everyone within 2 metres of the torch gets a stack of Burn. Your blows on it set off a burst 0.8 metres across.


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire (send The Torch into a crowd); Flame Wave''s Running Fire across the pool.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The pool should coat anyone who walks through it, and The Torch should only light up when fire touches it (for now, it lights up right away).


      <b>Example.</b> Turn the brute into The Torch and let it walk into its friends.'
  - Id: fire.pitch.master
    Title: 'Black Pitch: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Light it with a finisher, and every other burning enemy nearby goes up in flames along with the coated one, at half strength.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When a finisher lands on a burning enemy. Black Pitch must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The finished enemy''s burn goes off for 5 damage per stack within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, Catching Fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Every other burning enemy within 10 metres should go up at half strength, and only when the finisher lights the coat.


      <b>Example.</b> End a string of blows on a coated soldier with a finisher, and the fire spreads to the next one.'
  - Id: fire.sun
    Title: Falling Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Lighting Kindle starts the sun on its path across the sky. At dawn, it rolls toward the enemy you face, and the ground it crosses burns. At noon, it hangs over that enemy, and burns near you grow stronger on their own. At dusk, it sets on the enemy below it, hitting harder for every burn it finds. If you get hit, the sun goes down, and then it rises again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light Kindle with the surge button. It sets when Kindle ends, or when you press the surge button again.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. Dawn: it rolls up to 9 metres, and the first enemy it meets takes 14 damage, 20 stagger and two Burn. Noon: every second, everything within 6 metres of you gets a stack of Burn. Dusk: 60 damage and 80 stagger within 4 metres, plus 8% more for each Burn stack it finds, up to double. If you call it while it is still rolling, it does half. If you get hit, it goes down and rises again 1.5 seconds later, if Kindle has 3 seconds left.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, Heat.


      <b>Example.</b> Press the surge button again over a crowd with five Burn each.'
  - Id: fire.intothefire
    Title: Into the Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fire never backs off. When you dodge toward an enemy, you pass right through it without being hit, and everything you pass catches fire. Dodging away costs more stamina and can never be a perfect dodge.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every dodge, while you carry Fire. A dodge counts as toward the enemy you face when it goes within 60 degrees of the direct line to it. It counts as away when it goes within 60 degrees of the line directly away from it. A dodge to the side is a normal dodge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. Toward: you can''t be hit for the whole dodge, and you pass through enemies (walls still stop you). Every enemy along your path takes two Burn. A perfect dodge toward the enemy also raises your Heat by one level. Away: the dodge costs twice the stamina, and it is never a perfect dodge, so it gives no mana and nothing that needs a perfect dodge happens.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flame Trail, which it comes after on the chart. Heat, which the perfect dodge builds up. Wildfire Rush. Big attacks and red attacks, which you now meet by going right through them.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute winds up a huge swing. Roll into it. You come out behind it, it is burning, and you are already swinging.'
  - Id: fire.drinks
    Title: The Blade Drinks Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your weapon stops setting enemies on fire. Instead, it drinks the fire that''s already there. Each blow on a burning enemy takes Burn off it and hits harder for each Burn it takes. Burns no longer hurt enemies on their own.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every weapon blow on a burning enemy, while you carry Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. A light blow takes 1 Burn, a heavy blow takes 3, and a finisher takes all of them. Each Burn taken adds 6 damage to that blow. Cinder no longer adds Burn to your blows. A burn left on an enemy does no damage over time, but it still runs out at its normal speed.


      <b>Works with.</b> Catching Fire, which it comes after on the chart. Everything that sets fire without your weapon: Flame Wave, Uraeus, the Firebomb Flask, Fire''s infused strike, and reactions. Fire Without End, which keeps the fuel from running out.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw a Flame Wave over a crowd for two Burn each, then finish the enemy in front of you. The finisher takes both Burn and hits for 12 more damage.'
  - Id: firebloom.shape
    Title: 'Firebloom: Between Two Fires or Ash Bed'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Seeds That Wait unlocks two new shapes for Firebloom. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Firebloom uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Between Two Fires</b>: the fire lilies grow in pairs, facing each other across the enemy. Walking between the two lilies of a pair heals you and charges your blade. <b>Ash Bed</b>: there are no lilies. The burn and the seed become one: the enemy''s Burn stays while it is Seeded, and when it dies, a bed of green shoots springs up from the ash.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Firebloom goes off while you carry both Fire and Life. Firebloom goes off when Burn lands on a Seeded enemy, or when a seed lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Firebloom.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Between Two Fires: the Seeded is used up, and a pair of lilies grows for each seed the enemy had, 3 metres apart on either side of it. Each lily stays for 5 seconds and burns anything within 1 metre, dealing 3 damage and a Burn every half second. Each lily counts as something you grew. Ash Bed: nothing is used up. While the enemy stays Seeded, its Burn doesn''t fade. When it dies, a bed of shoots 3 metres across stays for 6 seconds where it fell, Seeding every enemy in it once a second. After Firebloom goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kindled Guard (make a lily flare with a perfect guard), Dry Pods, The Burning Tree, Need-Fire.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd rushing at you: use Between Two Fires, and fight in the gap. One big burning brute: use Ash Bed, and keep it Seeded so it never stops burning.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Between Two Fires'' pairs don''t heal you or charge your blade yet when you walk between them. With more than one seed, the lilies stand in a ring instead of in facing pairs. Ash Bed''s shoots don''t heal you yet while you stand in them.'
  - Id: firebloom.kindledguard
    Title: Kindled Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard made within 3 metres of a lily makes it flare up. It deals 12 damage and two Burn within 2 metres, and the lily stays 3 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard while something you grew is standing nearby, while you carry both Fire and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 12 damage, 10 stagger and two Burn to everything within 2 metres, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Firebloom (its lilies count as things you grew), Need-Fire, The Burning Tree.


      <b>Example.</b> Stand next to a lily and block the brute''s swing perfectly. Everything around you catches fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the flare bursts around you, not around the lily, whenever anything you grew stands within 4 metres. The lily doesn''t stay longer yet.'
  - Id: firebloom.drypods
    Title: Dry Pods
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> One more seed is thrown, up to four, and the lilies stay 2 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Firebloom goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. One more lily grows than the number of seeds used up, up to four, and the lilies stay for 7 seconds instead of 5. With Between Two Fires, you get one more pair, and they stay for 7 seconds too.


      <b>Works with.</b> Firebloom and its shapes, Kindled Guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Even an enemy with only a few seeds blooms into a full bed.'
  - Id: firebloom.burningtree
    Title: The Burning Tree
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The first lily of each fight grows into a burning tree. It stays for 10 seconds and throws a burning seed at the nearest enemy every 2 seconds, adding Seeded and Burn.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When Firebloom goes off, while you carry both Fire and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: five throws over 10 seconds, each dealing 4 damage, 4 stagger and a Burn, at most once every 30 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Firebloom, Kindled Guard, Need-Fire (which Seeds and burns again).


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight with one bloom, and the tree keeps the pack burning while you work.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> No tree appears yet. For now, the throws go at the enemy that bloomed, not the nearest one, and add a Burn without a seed. It comes once every 30 seconds, not once a fight.'
  - Id: firebloom.needfire
    Title: Need-Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Two fires of green branches spring up on either side of the enemy you face and burn for 6 seconds, joined by a line of sparks. Every enemy between them gets a Burn and a Seed each second.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Fire and Life. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 7 metres away and lasts 6 seconds. Once a second, it deals 2 damage, a Burn and a Seed to every enemy in it, so Firebloom keeps going off on them.


      <b>Works with.</b> Firebloom (everything in it blooms), Kindled Guard, The Burning Tree.


      <b>Example.</b> Light it on the pack''s leader, and fight on the line between the fires.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it is one burning patch 5 metres across on the enemy, not two fires with a line between them. Standing between them doesn''t yet make your blows heal you 2 and cut spell waits twice as much. Shoving an enemy across the line doesn''t yet fill its Burn all the way.'
  - Id: fishpoison.shape
    Title: 'Fish Poison: Rock Pool or The Ebb Takes It'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Bad Water unlocks two new shapes for Fish Poison. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Fish Poison uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Rock Pool</b>: a small slick of poisoned water that floats its enemy up with only two doses. It works on one enemy, often. <b>The Ebb Takes It</b>: the poisoned water flows away from you as a stream, soaking everything along it, and floats up any enemy with four doses.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Fish Poison goes off while you carry both Poison and Water. Fish Poison goes off when poison lands on a Soaked enemy, or when water lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Fish Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Rock Pool: a slick 1.5 metres across that lasts 5 seconds and soaks anything standing in it. An enemy with two or more doses floats up, stunned. The Ebb Takes It: four patches of slick run 6 metres past the enemy, away from you. Each is 1.5 metres across and lasts 5 seconds. An enemy with four doses floats up. The float works like the plain one: the enemy is lifted and held for 1.5 seconds (0.5 for elites; gods take 30 stagger instead). Your next weapon blow on it is a critical hit and uses up two doses for 6 damage each. After Fish Poison goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Tide Brings It, Wide Slick, Fast Water, Poison the Lagoon.


      <b>Example.</b> A one-on-one fight with a brute: use Rock Pool, and float it up again and again. A line of soldiers: use The Ebb Takes It, and soak the whole line.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the enemy where Fish Poison went off can float up. Other enemies that reach enough doses in the slick or along the stream don''t float up yet.'
  - Id: fishpoison.tide
    Title: The Tide Brings It
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When Riptide''s wave crosses a poisoned slick, it carries the poison with it. Everything the wave carries takes two doses.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, each time one of your spells hits an enemy that is both Soaked and poisoned, while you carry both Poison and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two doses, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fish Poison (more doses means more floats), Fast Water, Water''s Riptide.


      <b>Example.</b> Wash a poisoned pack toward you, and each one arrives sicker.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Riptide doesn''t pick up the slick it crosses yet. For now, any spell that hits a Soaked, poisoned enemy adds the two doses.'
  - Id: fishpoison.wideslick
    Title: Wide Slick
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fish Poison''s slick is 4 metres across and lasts 2 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Fish Poison goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The slick is 4 metres across instead of 3, and lasts 7 seconds instead of 5. With Rock Pool, the pool is 2 metres across and lasts 7 seconds. With The Ebb Takes It, the stream lasts 7 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fish Poison and its shapes, The Tide Brings It.


      <b>Example.</b> The slick reaches the soldier standing next to the enemy.'
  - Id: fishpoison.fastwater
    Title: Fast Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Poison on a Soaked enemy bites twice as often and fades twice as fast.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Poison and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Poison normally bites every 2 seconds and lasts 6 seconds. On a Soaked enemy, it bites every second and lasts 3 seconds. Each enemy keeps its own timing, so one enemy getting wet doesn''t change the others.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fish Poison, The Tide Brings It, Poison the Lagoon.


      <b>Example.</b> Soak a poisoned pack, and the bites come fast.'
  - Id: fishpoison.lagoon
    Title: Poison the Lagoon
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You crush a fish-poison seed on the floor in front of you. A slick spreads out in rings until it is 6 metres across, and stays for 6 seconds, soaking everything in it. Each weapon blow on an enemy in it stirs the water and gives a dose to every other enemy in it. Any enemy that reaches four doses floats up, just like Fish Poison.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Poison and Water. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 7 metres away and lasts 6 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage, a dose and a Soaked to every enemy in it. An enemy with four doses has Fish Poison set off on it right away, even if it would normally have to wait, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fish Poison (everything in it gets Fish Poison), Fast Water, The Tide Brings It.


      <b>Example.</b> Drop it under a pack, then work the slick with your blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your weapon blows don''t stir the water to poison the other enemies in it yet. The forced Fish Poison comes at most once a second for all the enemies together, not once a second for each one.'
  - Id: forge.shape
    Title: 'Anvil Stone: Ringing Iron or Hammer Scale'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Anvil Stone. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Anvil Stone uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Ringing Iron</b>: no stone, and no waiting for a heavy blow. The shaken armour rings like struck iron right away. <b>Hammer Scale</b>: no ring. Instead, flakes of glowing metal fly off the enemy into the enemies nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Anvil Stone goes off while you carry both Earth and Metal. Anvil Stone goes off when Sundered lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on a notched one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Anvil Stone. The plain one puts a stone behind the enemy for 5 seconds, and your next heavy blow smashes the enemy into it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Ringing Iron: the enemy''s Sundered fills all the way right away, and a ring of sparks 3 metres around it deals 10 damage, 15 stagger and a notch to each enemy. Hammer Scale: up to four enemies, each within 6 metres of the last one, take 8 damage, a notch and a Tremor. After Anvil Stone goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Buckled (shaken, notched enemies lose their balance sooner), Hammer and Anvil, and Stone Rise and Flung Blade to set it up.


      <b>Example.</b> No time for a heavy blow: Ringing Iron breaks the enemy''s armour the moment the two afflictions meet. A spread-out pack: Hammer Scale notches four at once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Hammer Scale''s flakes should jump from one enemy to the next in an arc, instead of flying at each one.'
  - Id: forge.hammeranvil
    Title: Hammer and Anvil
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on a shaken enemy hits twice: once from your weapon, and once more a moment later as the stone answers. Heavy blows also add Tremor and Sundered together, no matter which of the two elements is your first. For now, the second hit needs Tremor on the enemy, not a stone behind it, and it bursts around you instead of behind the enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow that lands. The second hit needs the enemy to already have Tremor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The second hit lands 0.15 seconds later, in a 2.3-metre circle around you. It deals 60% of the heavy blow''s damage and 20 stagger, at most twice a second. Each heavy blow also adds a stack of Tremor and a notch of Sundered. Your first element''s affliction isn''t added twice if its root mark already adds it with heavy blows.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tremor, Heavy Hand, Buckled, Mountain Stance and Quake Step (Tremor to start it), Metal''s root mark.


      <b>Example.</b> Shake a brute with Quake Step, then land two heavy blows. Each one hits again for 60% as the stone answers.'
  - Id: forge.struckore
    Title: Struck Ore
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard makes a spike of ore shoot up from the ground under the enemy that hit you, shaking it. Three blades also fly from you toward it, notching what they pass.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The spike comes 0.2 seconds later, with a 1.2-metre radius. It deals 10 damage, 20 stagger and one stack of Tremor. The three blades fly in a narrow fan at 10 metres a second for 1.2 seconds. Each one hits the first enemy it meets for 9 damage, 4 stagger and one notch of Sundered. For now, the spike hits and disappears. A pillar that stays standing until it is broken is not built yet.


      <b>Works with.</b> Buckled (both afflictions from one guard), Mountain Stance, Hammer and Anvil, Anvil Stone''s shapes.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a soldier''s swing. It is shaken and notched at once, and the soldier behind it gets hit by a blade too.'
  - Id: forge.buckled
    Title: Buckled
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that is both shaken and notched loses its balance much sooner, from everything that hits it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, on any enemy that has Tremor and Sundered at the same time.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 40% more stagger from everything while it has both. Tremor also stops its stagger meter from going down, so the stagger you add stays.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hammer and Anvil, Struck Ore, World Anvil (adds both), Mountain Stance, Quake Step, the root marks of Earth and Metal, and the charged blade of whichever element you carry second.


      <b>Example.</b> Notch a brute with Metal blows, then shake it with Quake Step. Its balance bar fills almost 50% faster.'
  - Id: forge.drawnfromstone
    Title: Drawn From Stone
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You pull a sword of stone and iron out of the ground and make one huge swing through a half circle in front of you. For now, it is that one swing. Holding the great sword for three swings is not built yet.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 50 mana. A half circle 4 metres deep. It deals 40 damage, 60 stagger and one stack of Tremor to everything in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hammer and Anvil or Struck Ore (it comes after either on the chart), Buckled, Standing Stones. When it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>Example.</b> Soldiers are lined up in front of you. One swing shakes them all and puts each one well on its way to a stagger.'
  - Id: forge.foldedsteel
    Title: Folded Steel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every finisher you land folds your blade one more time. Each fold makes every weapon blow a little stronger, and a little heavier to swing. The count shows on the screen as Folds. The folds belong to this stretch of fighting and last until your next rest fire. They don''t improve the weapon itself (the Way is what improves a weapon). They stay with you from area to area, and every rest fire empties them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each finisher that lands (a heavy blow on a staggered enemy) adds a fold.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. Up to ten folds. Each fold adds 3% weapon damage and 1% stamina cost for each blow. At ten folds: 30% more damage and 10% more stamina.


      <b>Works with.</b> Buckled and Tremor (more staggers means more finishers), World Anvil, Hammer and Anvil.


      <b>Example.</b> After ten finishers in a land, every cut you make does 30% more damage than when you started.'
  - Id: forge.worldanvil
    Title: World Anvil
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your finisher strikes the enemy on an anvil that rises out of the ground. Its ringing sound spreads out and hits every other enemy nearby, shaking it and breaking its armour. A blade is made for each enemy it reaches. The blades hang at your shoulders and fly out with your next heavy blow, each one at a nearby enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every finisher that lands. The blades wait for your next heavy swing.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. The ring reaches 6 metres from the finished enemy. Every other enemy in it takes 15 damage, 30 stagger, three stacks of Tremor and three notches of Sundered. You get one blade for each enemy it reaches, up to six, and keep them for up to 14 seconds. Each blade flies at an enemy within 10 metres for 10 damage, 12 stagger and one notch of Sundered.


      <b>Works with.</b> Buckled (every enemy it reaches is left shaken and notched), Folded Steel, Hammer and Anvil.


      <b>Example.</b> Finish a soldier in a pack of four. The other three are shaken and get three notches each, and your next heavy blow throws three blades.'
  - Id: frostheave.shape
    Title: 'Frost Heave: Sorted Stones or Pingo'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Frost Heave. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Frost Heave uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Sorted Stones</b>: the heave pushes every other enemy away, leaving the heaved enemy alone with you. <b>Pingo</b>: the mound has a core of ice that bursts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Frost Heave goes off while you carry both Ice and Earth. Frost Heave goes off when Tremor lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a shaken one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Frost Heave.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes heave the enemy up 0.4 seconds later. It takes 12 damage and 25 stagger and is thrown into the air for 1 second. Sorted Stones: every other enemy within 3 metres is pushed 3 metres away. Pingo: 2 seconds later, the mound bursts for 24 damage and two Chill within 2.5 metres. After Frost Heave goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hoarfrost and Stone Rise to set it up, Hard Frost, The Hag''s Staff.


      <b>Example.</b> A one-on-one fight in a crowd: Sorted Stones clears space around you. A pack chasing you: use Pingo, and lead them over it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> No mound or ring of stones appears on the floor yet. For now, Sorted Stones just shoves the other enemies away.'
  - Id: frostheave.hardfrost
    Title: Hard Frost
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows on a chilled enemy shake it. Heavy blows on a shaken enemy chill it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Ice and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Tremor on a chilled enemy, or one Chill on a shaken enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Frost Heave (one heavy blow starts it), Permafrost, Winter in the Bones.


      <b>Example.</b> Chill a soldier with Hoarfrost, and one heavy blow heaves it.'
  - Id: frostheave.footing
    Title: Frozen Footing
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard while you stand within 2 metres of a mound heaves the ground under the attacker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has both Chill and Tremor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Frost Heave goes off on the attacker, even if it would normally have to wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Frost Heave, Hard Frost, Mountain Stance.


      <b>Example.</b> A chilled, shaken brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it goes flying up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any attacker that has both afflictions, instead of needing you to stand next to a mound.'
  - Id: frostheave.permafrost
    Title: Permafrost
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Tremor doesn''t fade from a chilled enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Ice and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Once the Chill is gone, the Tremor starts to fade as usual.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hard Frost, which it comes after on the chart, Frost Heave.


      <b>Example.</b> A chilled soldier stays shaken.'
  - Id: frostheave.bones
    Title: Winter in the Bones
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A shaken enemy that is Frozen stays frozen twice as long. Shattering it heaves the ground under every enemy within 4 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a shaken enemy is Frozen or shattered, while you carry both Ice and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A shaken enemy''s freeze lasts twice as long as usual. When it is shattered, everything within 4 metres is thrown into the air for 1 second and takes 12 damage, 25 stagger and a Tremor, at most three times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hard Frost, Hoarfrost, Frost Heave.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter a frozen, shaken soldier in a pack, and the whole pack goes flying up.'
  - Id: frostheave.hagsstaff
    Title: The Hag's Staff
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You strike the ground and hammer frost into it. A circle around you turns hard and white. Each second, every enemy on it is chilled and shaken, so it heaves when the two afflictions meet.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Ice and Earth. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It covers 6 metres around you for 6 seconds. Each second, it deals 2 damage, a Chill and a Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Frost Heave and its shapes, Hard Frost, Winter in the Bones.


      <b>Example.</b> Strike it in the middle of a pack, and fight among the enemies flying up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your heavy blows on the frozen ground don''t push frost under the enemy you hit yet.'
  - Id: girdled.shape
    Title: 'Girdled: Corpse Flowers or Fig in the Crown'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Flowers or Fig unlocks two new shapes for Girdled. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Girdled uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still wrap and crush the enemy like before. <b>Corpse Flowers</b>: when the roots let go, three pale flowers grow where the enemy stood, to fight next to. <b>Fig in the Crown</b>: when an enemy dies in the roots, it drops its seed on the nearest enemy, which starts getting wrapped up too.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Girdled goes off while you carry both Life and Dark. Girdled goes off when a Seed lands on a Cursed enemy, or when a Curse lands on a Seeded one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Girdled.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes work like the plain one: one coil of roots wraps the enemy right away. It moves a quarter slower and takes 6% more damage from your weapon. Each weapon blow heals you 1 and pulls the roots one coil tighter (6% more damage each time). The third blow girdles it: it is held for 1.5 seconds and takes 20 damage and 40 stagger, and you heal 4. The roots hold for 8 seconds. Corpse Flowers: when the roots let go, three flowers grow within 1 metre of where the enemy stood. They count as things you grew and last 8 seconds (up to three). Fig in the Crown: an enemy that dies in the roots Seeds and Curses the nearest enemy within 5 metres. This is on purpose: it may start Girdled on that enemy. After Girdled goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bitter Seed to set it up, Held Down, What the Yew Keeps, Death Turned Back.


      <b>Example.</b> Holding your ground: use Corpse Flowers, and fight on top of the flowers. A pack: use Fig in the Crown, and the roots move from one body to the next.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Corpse Flowers'' flowers only stand there. Your blows near them don''t win back grey health yet. The hollow tree trunk isn''t left standing yet, just like with the plain Girdled.'
  - Id: girdled.bitterseed
    Title: Bitter Seed
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your finishers put a Seed and a Curse on every enemy within 3 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every finisher that lands, while you carry both Life and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Seed and one Curse on everything within 3 metres of the enemy you finished.


      <b>Works with.</b> Girdled (each enemy nearby starts getting wrapped), Held Down.


      <b>Example.</b> End a string of blows in a crowd, and the fig roots take all of them.'
  - Id: girdled.helddown
    Title: Held Down
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Cursed enemy wraps the fig roots around it at two coils, no matter what else it has. Once every 6 seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard against a Cursed enemy, while you carry both Life and Dark, at most once every 6 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. It adds a Seed and sets off Girdled on the enemy right away, even if it would normally have to wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Girdled and its shapes, Bitter Seed.


      <b>Example.</b> Block the Cursed brute''s blow perfectly, and the roots grab its legs.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The roots start at one coil, not two.'
  - Id: girdled.yew
    Title: What the Yew Keeps
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When an enemy with a Seed and a Curse dies, it doesn''t burst into thorns. Instead, its Seed and its Curse move, whole, to the nearest enemy within 6 metres, and the fig roots start on that enemy at two coils.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies with both a Seed and a Curse on it, while you carry both Life and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. All its Seeds and all its Curses are put on the nearest other enemy within 6 metres, so Girdled may start there. At most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bitter Seed, Girdled, Fig in the Crown.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one enemy in the roots, and the next one is wrapped up right away.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The thorns still burst when it dies, and the roots start at one coil, not two.'
  - Id: girdled.deathturnedback
    Title: Death Turned Back
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You drop to one knee, and roots spread out around you for 1.2 seconds. The roots take the first blow that would land. They climb up the attacker and hold it for 2 seconds, give it three Seeds and a Curse, and you heal. Each of your blows on the held enemy heals you 3.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Life and Dark. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, counter, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. When a blow comes, the attacker takes 8 damage, 20 stagger, three Seeds and a Curse, so Girdled starts. It is held for 2 seconds (0.8 for an elite), and you heal 15. For those 2 seconds, each weapon blow on it heals you 3. If no blow comes, the wait is half as long: 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Girdled and its shapes, Held Down, What the Yew Keeps.


      <b>Example.</b> Kneel as the brute swings, then cut it while it is caught in the roots.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You heal 15, instead of the damage the blow would have done (up to 30).'
  - Id: glacier.shape
    Title: 'Supercooled: Sea Ice or Hard Water'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Supercooled. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Supercooled uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Sea Ice</b>: your next weapon blow freezes the enemy solid, like before. Then the cold spreads through the floor in a web of frost that chills everything nearby, so a wet enemy next to it becomes Supercooled too. <b>Hard Water</b>: only a heavy blow freezes it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Supercooled goes off while you carry both Ice and Water. Supercooled goes off when Chill lands on a soaked enemy, or when Soaked lands on a chilled one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Supercooled.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: the water on the enemy stays still for 6 seconds. The blow that ends it freezes a normal enemy solid (Chill filled all the way), gives an elite 3 Chill and a god 2, and uses up the Soaked. Sea Ice: that blow also puts 2 Chill on every Soaked enemy within 4 metres. This is on purpose: it may start another Supercooled. Hard Water: only a heavy blow ends it. After Supercooled goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Icebreaker (mana and a sip of a flask for each shatter), Riptide and Hoarfrost to add the water and the cold, The Wave Stands Still.


      <b>Example.</b> Riptide soaks a pack, and Hoarfrost touches one of them. With Sea Ice, one blow freezes it, and the frost spreads through the wet enemies next to it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Hard Water''s freeze doesn''t last longer yet, and it isn''t harder to shatter yet.'
  - Id: glacier.breaker
    Title: Icebreaker
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Shattering an enemy gives you mana and refills part of a flask you have used.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you shatter a frozen enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 12 mana and a third of a flask for each shatter, as long as you have fewer flasks than your maximum. Three shatters leave it just short; the fourth fills it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Supercooled''s shapes, Flying Shards, All at Once, The Wave Stands Still (one heavy blow shatters everything it holds), The World Holds Still.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter four frozen soldiers in one fight, and a used flask is back on your belt.'
  - Id: glacier.bones
    Title: Cold in the Bones
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hitting a Soaked enemy makes perfect guards easier for a few seconds. The timing window for them is a quarter longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a weapon blow or one of your spells hits a Soaked enemy, at most twice a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The perfect guard window is 25% longer for 4 seconds: about 0.23 seconds instead of 0.18. For now, it makes your own timing window longer. It doesn''t slow down the enemy''s wind-up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide (it soaks), Cold Read, Weight of Ice.


      <b>Example.</b> Cut a Soaked duellist twice, then meet its next attack with a perfect guard you would have missed.'
  - Id: glacier.wave
    Title: The Wave Stands Still
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw a huge wave that rolls forward while you hold the button, soaking and carrying whatever it meets. Let go, and it freezes in place, with everything in it Frozen. One heavy blow next to the wall of ice shatters it and everything inside.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4), and keep holding it. Letting go, or the wave reaching its end, freezes it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 50 mana. The wave starts 1 metre ahead of you and rolls at 6 metres a second. It goes at least 2.5 metres and at most 9, and is 6 metres wide. Whatever it meets takes 10 damage, is Soaked, and is pushed along. Once frozen, it is a wall nothing can walk through, and everything within about 3 metres behind it gets its Chill filled all the way. It stands for 4 seconds. A heavy blow swung next to it shatters it, dealing 45 damage and 60 stagger to everything in it. This counts as a heavy blow, so each frozen enemy also shatters for 45.


      <b>Works with.</b> Icebreaker (mana and flask for every enemy shattered), Flying Shards and Clean Break on the Ice tree, All at Once (each shatter three times as hard), Brittle.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll the wave over a pack and let go as it reaches them. Then walk up and break it, and every one of them shatters at once.'
  - Id: glacier.weight
    Title: Weight of Ice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each blow you block with your guard puts a plate of ice on you, up to five. A perfect guard puts two. Each plate blocks a little of every wound. Your next heavy blow uses them all up in one huge blade of ice along its line, longer and harder for every plate.


      <b>When it happens.</b> You get plates with each blocked blow and each perfect guard. Your next heavy swing uses them up, whether it lands or not. Otherwise they fall off 30 seconds after you got the last one.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. Each plate cuts the damage you take by 6%, so 30% at five. The ice blade reaches 2.5 metres plus 1.2 more for each plate (8.5 at five), and is 1.3 metres wide on each side. For each plate, it deals 12 damage, 12 stagger and one stack of Chill, and it counts as a heavy blow. With five plates, that''s 60 damage, 60 stagger and five Chill, enough to freeze.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Read, Cold in the Bones, Chill.


      <b>Example.</b> Guard a brute''s three-blow string, the last one perfectly, for four plates. Your heavy blow throws an ice blade 7 metres long through the brute and the soldier behind it.'
  - Id: goldenfeathers.shape
    Title: 'Golden Feathers: Eagle''s Sight or Wingbeat'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Feathered unlocks two new shapes for Golden Feathers. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Golden Feathers uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Eagle''s Sight</b>: no feathers fly. Instead, the eagle keeps its eye on the enemy, and your next dodge toward it carries you all the way in for a critical cut. <b>Wingbeat</b>: the feathers gather and beat once, making a gust out from the enemy that throws every other enemy nearby away and Marks them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Golden Feathers goes off while you carry both Light and Wind. Golden Feathers goes off when a Mark lands on an Unsteady enemy, or when Unsteady lands on a Marked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Golden Feathers.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Eagle''s Sight: for 4 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy is a critical hit and deals 10 more damage. Wingbeat: everything within 3 metres of the enemy is thrown 3 metres away from it and Marked. After Golden Feathers goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bane of Serpents, Riding High, Wings Behind, Sweep of Wings.


      <b>Example.</b> One enemy you want to reach: use Eagle''s Sight. Surrounded: use Wingbeat, and the crowd is thrown back and Marked.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Eagle''s Sight doesn''t carry your dodge to the enemy yet. For now, it waits for your next weapon blow on it instead.'
  - Id: goldenfeathers.bane
    Title: Bane of Serpents
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A feather that reaches an enemy that is already Marked hits it for 12 damage and 20 stagger.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Golden Feathers goes off, while you carry both Light and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: three hits in a line past the enemy, 1.4 metres apart, each 0.7 metres across. Each deals 12 damage and 20 stagger, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Golden Feathers, Sweep of Wings (Mark the pack first), Wings Behind.


      <b>Example.</b> Mark a pack, then send the feathers flying through it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The feathers don''t stop at the first enemy they touch yet. For now, they hit everything on their line, Marked or not, and with any shape of Golden Feathers.'
  - Id: goldenfeathers.ridinghigh
    Title: Riding High
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Golden Feathers sends four feathers, and they fly up to 9 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Golden Feathers goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Four feathers fly instead of three, and the farthest one lands 9 metres past the enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Golden Feathers, Bane of Serpents.


      <b>Example.</b> The feathers reach the back of the room.'
  - Id: goldenfeathers.wingsbehind
    Title: Wings Behind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every perfect dodge raises a half ring of golden feathers behind you. It flies out 4 metres, adding a Mark and Unsteady to whatever it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge, while you carry both Light and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A Mark and an Unsteady on every enemy within 4 metres, at most once every half second. Since this mark adds both at once, it sets off Golden Feathers on each enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Golden Feathers, Bane of Serpents, Sweep of Wings.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge through a pack at the last moment, and every one of them throws feathers.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the feathers reach all around you, not just a half ring behind you.'
  - Id: goldenfeathers.sweep
    Title: Sweep of Wings
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A huge wing made of layers of gold feathers sweeps out from your side through 160 degrees, reaching 6 metres. It deals 10 damage, 25 stagger, a Mark and two Unsteady, and everything it hits is thrown 3 metres in the direction of the sweep. For 3 seconds after that, each weapon blow on an enemy it threw adds another Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Light and Wind. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It is a cone 6 metres long and 160 degrees wide. It deals 10 damage, 25 stagger, a Mark and two Unsteady, and throws enemies 3 metres. Then, for 3 seconds, each weapon blow on an enemy it threw adds one Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Golden Feathers (everything it hits gets both afflictions), Bane of Serpents, Wings Behind.


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight with it on a crowd. They are Marked and knocked off balance, and the feathers fly.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the enemies it hits are thrown straight away from you, instead of sideways in the direction of the sweep.'
  - Id: halo.shape
    Title: 'Glint: Mirror to Mirror or Burning Mirror'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Glint. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Glint uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Mirror to Mirror</b>: the ray of light keeps bouncing from enemy to enemy, drawing a zigzag of light across the room. <b>Burning Mirror</b>: there is no ray. Instead, the light gathers into the cracks of the enemy''s armour as one white-hot point, and your next weapon blow on it hits hard and adds two notches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Glint goes off while you carry both Metal and Light. Glint goes off when a Mark lands on a notched enemy, or when Sundered lands on a Marked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Glint. The plain one sends one ray to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Mirror to Mirror: up to four enemies, each within 6 metres of the last one, take 10 damage, 15 stagger and a Mark, and the enemy''s notch is passed on. Burning Mirror: the point lasts 5 seconds. Your next weapon blow on the enemy adds two notches and 10 more damage. After Glint goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Steel (a perfect guard adds both afflictions), Halo of Blades, Counter Cut, Open Line.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd of Marked soldiers: Mirror to Mirror sends one flash through four of them. A one-on-one fight: use Burning Mirror, then a heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Mirror to Mirror''s ray bounces on from every enemy it hits, not only enemies that have Sundered. Burning Mirror''s blow hits for 10 more damage instead of being a critical hit.'
  - Id: halo.brightsteel
    Title: Bright Steel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard makes your blade flash. The attacker is Marked, and its armour gets a notch.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One stack of Marked and one notch of Sundered on the attacker. If your first element''s root mark already adds its own affliction on a perfect guard (Sunder''s notch, or Marked), that one isn''t added twice. The guard leaves one of each.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder, Halo of Blades (the three blows on a Marked enemy that follow are critical hits, and each makes a blade), Counter Cut, Open Line, Edge Guard, Mirror Edge.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a soldier''s swing, then answer with three light blows. All three are critical hits, and they land on notched armour.'
  - Id: halo.haloofblades
    Title: Halo of Blades
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every critical hit you land makes a blade of light in a ring behind your shoulders, up to six. A perfect guard sends them all flying at the enemy that hit you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each critical weapon blow makes one blade. A perfect guard sends off every blade you have. The count shows on the screen as Blades.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Up to six blades. Each flies at 16 metres a second, turning to follow the attacker, and hits for 9 damage, 8 stagger and one notch of Sundered.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Steel and Marked enemies (every blow on them is a critical hit), Counter Cut, Edge Guard, Blinding Guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Land three critical hits on a Marked soldier, then perfect-guard the brute behind it. Three blades fly into the brute.'
  - Id: halo.mirroredge
    Title: Mirror Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard throws a bolt of light straight back at the attacker. It hits hard and Marks what it hits.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every perfect guard. For now, it works on any perfect guard, not only against a thrown attack, and the bolt deals its own damage, not the damage of the blow you blocked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The bolt flies straight at 18 metres a second for 1.5 seconds and passes through enemies. Each enemy it hits takes 20 damage, 30 stagger and one stack of Marked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Steel, Halo of Blades, Blinding Guard, Open Line, Edge Guard.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer shoots, and you perfect-guard the arrow. The bolt flies back down the line and Marks the archer.'
  - Id: halo.blindingguard
    Title: Blinding Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard blinds the enemies in front of you. They are Marked, and they are knocked off balance. Everyone near you, on any side, is frozen in place for a moment. The cost: blocking a blow with a normal guard costs more stamina.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The flash comes with every perfect guard. The cost comes with every blocked blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. You turn to face the attacker. A cone 6 metres long and 120 degrees wide gives 15 stagger and one stack of Marked to everything in it. Everyone within 6 metres is held for 1.5 seconds (less for elites; enemies that can''t be frozen aren''t held at all). A blocked blow costs a fifth more stamina: 1.08 for each point of damage instead of 0.9.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Steel, Halo of Blades, Mirror Edge, Open Line, Edge Guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers close in on you. Perfect-guard the first one. All three stand still and Marked, and your next blows are critical hits.'
  - Id: halo.sixmirrors
    Title: Six Mirrors
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Six steel mirrors stand in a ring around the enemy you face. You vanish and dash across the ring from each mirror in turn, too fast to follow. Then you stand again where you started, and every cut lands at once. Nothing can hit you from the moment you vanish until the cuts land.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. 60 mana. It targets an enemy within 8 metres; the ring has a 3-metre radius. There are six dashes, 0.13 seconds apart, then 0.3 seconds of standing still. Each cut deals 14 damage and 12 stagger to the enemy (84 damage and 72 stagger in total), and half that to anything else its line crosses. Then everything within 1.2 metres of the middle of the ring is Marked and gets three notches.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bright Steel, Halo of Blades (the blows on a Marked enemy after are critical hits), Sunder, Counter Cut, the charged blade (it charges your weapon with Metal).


      <b>Example.</b> Against a lone duellist, cast it. It takes 84 damage, is Marked and gets three notches, and your next three blows are critical hits on broken armour.'
  - Id: ice.chill
    Title: Chill
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands chills the enemy, one stack at a time, up to five. Each stack slows it down. At five stacks it is Frozen: stuck in ice until it thaws, or until a heavy blow shatters it for a burst of damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every weapon blow that lands, light or heavy, while Ice is your first element (or you are all in on Ice). If Ice is your second element, your weapon adds your first element''s affliction, and Chill comes from your Ice spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack per blow, up to five, lasting 6 seconds. Each stack slows the enemy by 7%. At five stacks, it freezes for 2.5 seconds. Elites and gods thaw after 1 second, and some huge enemies can''t be frozen. A heavy blow on a frozen enemy shatters it for 45 more damage, and the Chill ends. A frozen enemy can''t be chilled any more.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glaze (one more Chill per blow), Hoarfrost, Cold Read, Flying Shards, Brittle, All at Once, Cold Settles, Deep Winter, and Soaked, which spreads Chill to everyone within 4 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> Four quick light blows slow a soldier to a crawl. The fifth freezes it, and a heavy blow breaks it for 45 more damage on top of the cut.'
  - Id: ice.shards
    Title: Flying Shards
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A shattered enemy throws its ice all around. Everyone the shards reach is hit and gets two stacks of Chill, including the one that shattered.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a frozen enemy is shattered, by your heavy blow or anything else that shatters.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A burst with a 4.5-metre radius around the shattered enemy. It deals 14 damage, 10 stagger and two stacks of Chill to each one.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill, Brittle, All at Once, Eight Points and Hanging Ice, which come after it on the chart, Cold Settles, Icebreaker on the Glacier branch, and The World Holds Still, whose chain of shatters throws the shards again at each step.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter one frozen soldier in a pack of four. The other three get two stacks right away, three light blows away from freezing.'
  - Id: ice.eight
    Title: Eight Points
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Eight ice shards stand in a ring around the enemy you face for a moment, then fly in together. Each adds a stack of Chill, and as they close in they use up the enemy''s Chill for more damage. This is the detonator of the Ice tree.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, the shards close in on a spot 6 metres ahead, and you still have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds, and the shards close in after 0.9 seconds.) It targets an enemy within 6 metres. The ring has a 3.5-metre radius and closes after 1.2 seconds. Each shard deals 5 damage, 4 stagger and one stack of Chill. Each shard that lands on a chilled enemy then uses up its Chill in a small burst, for 5 damage and 2 stagger per stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flying Shards, which it comes after on the chart; Chill and Cold Read, to add the stacks first; Hanging Ice; All at Once; Cold Settles, when a second element''s affliction is on the enemy too.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The shards shattering a Frozen enemy. A spell''s hit can''t shatter yet, so a frozen enemy''s Chill is used up like any other''s.


      <b>Example.</b> Give a brute four stacks of Chill with your blade, then close the ring on it. The first shard uses up all four for 25 damage on top of its own.'
  - Id: ice.eight.shape
    Title: 'Eight Points: Wolf''s Jaws or Eight on Eight'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Eight Points. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Wolf''s Jaws</b>: two rows of four shards close in from the front and back in half a second, too fast to walk out of. It only hits one target. <b>Eight on Eight</b>: one shard comes down on each of up to eight enemies within 8 metres, each using up that enemy''s Chill. It''s for crowds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Eight Points, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Wolf''s Jaws: the ring has a 2-metre radius around the enemy and closes after 0.5 seconds. Eight on Eight: up to eight enemies within 8 metres are each hit 0.5 seconds after you cast it, for 8 damage, 10 stagger and a stack of Chill. Then their Chill is used up, like above.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hanging Ice (Wolf''s Jaws on its enemy while the icicle falls); Flying Shards and Cold Settles in a crowd (Eight on Eight).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Wolf''s Jaws should be two rows closing from the front and back (for now, it is a tighter, faster ring). Eight on Eight''s shards should fall from above (for now, they rise under each enemy as pillars).


      <b>Example.</b> A duellist that can step out of the plain ring can''t step out of Wolf''s Jaws. Six soldiers with two stacks each: Eight on Eight uses up all of them at once.'
  - Id: ice.eight.master
    Title: 'Eight Points: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land a heavy blow on the enemy as the shards close in, and every shard counts as a shatter.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A heavy blow (a finisher counts) on an enemy within 0.3 seconds of an Eight Points shard landing on it. Eight Points must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The heavy blow hits once more, like a shatter does: 45 damage, 30 stagger and a stack of Chill within a metre.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hanging Ice and Flying Shards; All at Once; the heavy weapons, whose heavy blows are hard to time because they''re slow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Every shard should count as a shatter, so you''d get eight shatters, each with Flying Shards. For now, the timed heavy blow hits once more, as one shatter.


      <b>Example.</b> Start the heavy blow as the ring tightens. It lands with the shards, and the enemy takes a second break on top.'
  - Id: ice.hanging
    Title: Hanging Ice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A huge icicle hangs over the enemy you face and follows it while its shadow grows. Then it falls as a heavy blow that chills what it hits, and leaves cracked ground that hurts for a moment. On a Frozen enemy, it shatters it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, the spell is still used up and you have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 8 seconds, and makes it fall after 2.4 seconds.) It targets an enemy within 7 metres, or 7 metres ahead if there is none. It falls after 3 seconds, following its enemy until a moment before. The fall hits everything within 2 metres for 30 damage, 60 stagger and three stacks of Chill, as a heavy blow. The cracked ground has a 1.5-metre radius. Every half second for 2 seconds, it deals 2 damage and a stack of Chill.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill (freeze the enemy while you wait, and the icicle shatters it), Flying Shards, which it comes after on the chart, Eight Points, Brittle, All at Once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Shattering a Frozen enemy for 90. For now, the fall shatters it like any heavy blow does, for 45.


      <b>Example.</b> Hang it over a brute, and chill it to five with your blade while the icicle waits. The icicle comes down on a frozen body and breaks it.'
  - Id: ice.hanging.shape
    Title: 'Hanging Ice: Icicles or Held by a Thread'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Hanging Ice. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Icicles</b>: three smaller icicles fall a second apart along the enemy''s path. It punishes enemies that run. <b>Held by a Thread</b>: it doesn''t fall on its own. It hangs for up to eight seconds and falls with your next heavy blow on its enemy, landing together with the blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Hanging Ice, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Icicles: three falls, a second apart, each with a 1.5-metre radius. Each deals 12 damage, 25 stagger and a stack of Chill. Held by a Thread: a sign appears on an enemy within 7 metres for 8 seconds. Your next weapon blow on it brings down the full icicle, for 30 damage, 60 stagger and three Chill within 2 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill and the heavy blow (with Held by a Thread, the icicle and the heavy blow land together); Flying Shards.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only a heavy blow should bring Held by a Thread down, and you should see the icicle hanging. For now, any weapon blow on its enemy sets it off, and you only see a sign.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer keeps running from you. Use Icicles, and every step it takes is under falling ice.'
  - Id: ice.hanging.master
    Title: 'Hanging Ice: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Let Hanging Ice land at the very moment its enemy freezes, and it shatters the enemy four times over, and you get its wait back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, when the fall itself freezes what it hits. Hanging Ice must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The spell''s wait ends right away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill and Cold Read, to get the enemy to two stacks before it lands; Eight Points; Rime Guard.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (within 0.3 seconds of the enemy becoming Frozen, from anything), and shattering four times over. For now, a fall that brings its enemy to five stacks freezes it and gives the wait back.


      <b>Example.</b> Give a brute two Chill, then the icicle adds its three. It freezes under the fall, and a second icicle is ready to hang over it.'
  - Id: ice.lift
    Title: Cold Settles
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cold Settles, Ice''s reaction lift. Every reaction with Chill in it leaves frozen ground where it happened, and the enemy it hit is slowed.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Chill reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Steam with Burn, Supercooled with Static, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A patch of frozen ground with a 1.5-metre radius around the reaction, lasting 4 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and a stack of Chill. The enemy where the reaction happened moves at 60% of its speed for 2 seconds (elites are slowed half as much; gods aren''t slowed).


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Ice''s ten reactions; Flying Shards, which it comes after on the chart; All at Once and Deep Winter, above it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Slowing everything the reaction hit. For now, only the enemy where it happened is slowed.


      <b>Example.</b> Ice and Water on a soldier in a crowd. The reaction goes off, the floor freezes around it, and soldiers walking in get Chill.'
  - Id: ice.allatonce
    Title: All at Once
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Shattering hits three times as hard. The cost: Chill no longer slows anyone. It still freezes at five stacks.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A shatter deals 135 instead of 45. Chill no longer slows enemies at any number of stacks.


      <b>Works with.</b> Eight Points, Hanging Ice, Flying Shards, Brittle, Cold Settles, Flash Freeze on the Glacier branch.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze an elite and shatter it with one heavy blow: 135 damage from the ice alone, on top of the blow.'
  - Id: ice.devotion
    Title: Deep Winter
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Deep Winter, Ice''s Devotion, for going all in on Ice only. Chill never fades, and lighting Glaze freezes every enemy within four metres at once. No Chill is ever wasted, so you can spread it over a whole room and come back for each enemy one at a time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Ice twice (all in), once you buy it. The freeze comes each time you light Glaze.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Chill no longer runs out on any enemy. It stays until a freeze thaws it away or a shatter breaks it. Lighting Glaze fills Chill all the way on everything within 4 metres of you, which freezes everything that can be frozen. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Ice Through. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Ice alone or twice. The spike freezes the enemy right away if it has three or more Chill.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every spell in the Ice tree; Splinter Ward and The World Holds Still, which also work with Glaze; Flying Shards, for a whole frozen room.


      <b>Example.</b> Give four soldiers two stacks of Chill each, step into the middle, and light Glaze. All four are Frozen, and every heavy blow breaks one.'
  - Id: ice.coldread
    Title: Cold Read
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard chills the enemy that hit you, two stacks at once.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Two stacks of Chill on the attacker.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill (three blows after the guard freeze it), Rime Guard and Splinter Ward, which come after it on the chart, Flash Freeze on the Glacier branch.


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier that already has three stacks swings at you. Guard it perfectly, and it freezes on the spot, ready for a heavy blow.'
  - Id: ice.rime
    Title: Rime Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You breathe out a low, thin, freezing fog around you. An enemy that attacks you gets two stacks of Chill, frost grows on its weapon arm, and its next wind-up is 50% slower. A perfect guard freezes the attacker right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The answers come from the next blows aimed at you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds, and the fog''s radius 4 metres.) The fog has a 3-metre radius around where you breathed it and lasts 6 seconds. It doesn''t hurt anything itself. Each cast gives three answers. A blow that lands on you or on your guard gives its attacker two Chill. A perfect guard gives five, which freezes it. Every enemy standing in the fog winds up its attacks 50% slower while it stays there.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Read, which it comes after on the chart (a perfect guard in the fog gives Cold Read''s two and the freeze); Hanging Ice; Splinter Ward.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The answers should only work during the fog''s six seconds (for now, the three answers wait for the next blows, fog or not, and those blows use them up). Frost on the attacker''s weapon arm. Only the attacker''s next wind-up should be slowed (for now, everything standing in the fog winds up slowly).


      <b>Example.</b> Breathe the fog at a duellist and let it come. Its first thrust gives it two Chill, its next attack comes slowly enough to guard perfectly, and it freezes.'
  - Id: ice.rime.shape
    Title: 'Rime Guard: Freezing Fog or Breath on the Steel'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Rime Guard. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Freezing Fog</b>: the fog stays where you breathed it, five metres wide, and everything in it gets a Chill every second. Use it to hold a doorway. <b>Breath on the Steel</b>: no fog. Instead, frost goes on your guard. Every blow you guard gives the attacker two Chill, and every perfect guard freezes it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Rime Guard, using the shape you picked. Breath on the Steel''s guard answers work while you have that shape picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Freezing Fog: a 2.5-metre radius, adding a stack of Chill to everything in it each second for 6 seconds. Breath on the Steel: two Chill for each blow you guard, and five (a freeze) for each perfect guard.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Read and Splinter Ward (Breath on the Steel); Winter Wall and Hanging Ice, to keep enemies in the fog (Freezing Fog).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Breath on the Steel should only work for six seconds after you cast it. For now, every guard chills and every perfect guard freezes while you have the shape picked, whether you cast it or not. With the Empower, Freezing Fog reaches a 4-metre radius instead of its own 2.5 (the Empower''s numbers are applied after the shape''s).


      <b>Example.</b> A hallway and a pack: put Freezing Fog in the doorway, and they reach you with three or four stacks.'
  - Id: ice.rime.master
    Title: 'Rime Guard: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard in the fog''s first second freezes everything in the fog, not only the attacker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, any perfect guard while Rime Guard is in a spell slot, once every nine seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Chill filled all the way on everything within 3 metres of you, which freezes everything that can be frozen.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cold Read, Flying Shards (freeze a crowd, then break them one by one), All at Once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work in the fog''s first second, and only on enemies standing in the fog. For now, it works on any perfect guard, fog or not, once every nine seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers swing at once into the fresh fog. Guard the first one perfectly, and all three stand frozen around you.'
  - Id: ice.splinter
    Title: Splinter Ward
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a ward blocks a blow, it bursts around you, hitting and chilling everything nearby. It also gives you what a perfect guard gives: its mana, and time off the wait on every spell.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a ward is used up: Glaze''s ward, or any other.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A burst with a 3.5-metre radius around you. It deals 12 damage, 30 stagger and three stacks of Chill. You get 15 mana, and 2 seconds come off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glaze (one ward when you light it), Cold Read, which it comes after on the chart, Winter Wall and Sent Sliding above it, Deep Winter, Chill.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The blocked blow should count as a perfect guard for other marks too (Cold Read, Rime Guard). For now, it only gives a perfect guard''s mana and time off.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Glaze and let a brute''s red attack land on the ward. Everything around you gets three stacks and is staggered, and your spells are two seconds closer to ready.'
  - Id: ice.wall
    Title: Winter Wall
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You raise a wall of ice in front of you. Nothing can walk through it, and blows from the other side can''t reach you. When its time ends, or when you hit it with a heavy blow, it bursts and chills everything near it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). A heavy blow swung close enough to reach it bursts it early.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 35 mana (its Empower makes it 30, and the burst 36). The wall rises 2 metres ahead of you, is 5 metres wide, and stands for 6 seconds. The burst reaches 4 metres around it and deals 30 damage, 50 stagger and three stacks of Chill. If your heavy blow bursts it, it counts as a heavy blow. Only one wall of ice can stand at a time.


      <b>Works with.</b> Splinter Ward, which it comes after on the chart, Sent Sliding, Rime Guard''s Freezing Fog shape, Chill.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The wall should have 60 health, chill anything that touches it, and throw its burst forward as a 6-metre cone. For now, it only chills when it bursts, all around where it stands.


      <b>Example.</b> Archers shoot at you across a yard. Raise the wall, wait behind it for the soldiers to come around, then cut it as they gather.'
  - Id: ice.wall.shape
    Title: 'Winter Wall: Shield Wall or Cracked Wall'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Winter Wall. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Shield Wall</b>: a shorter wall that moves ahead of you at walking speed. Move forward behind it. <b>Cracked Wall</b>: it bursts on its own a moment after an enemy first hits it. It''s a trap that answers the enemy, not you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Winter Wall, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Shield Wall: 3 metres wide, standing for 4 seconds. Cracked Wall: it bursts on its own 3 seconds after it rises, with the Learn mark''s burst.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rime Guard and Hanging Ice behind a Shield Wall; Flying Shards at a Cracked Wall in a doorway.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Shield Wall should move with you (for now, it stands where it rose, just shorter). Cracked Wall should burst 1.5 seconds after an enemy first hits it (for now, it bursts on a timer).


      <b>Example.</b> A god charges across the arena. Put a Cracked Wall in its path, and it runs into the burst.'
  - Id: ice.wall.master
    Title: 'Winter Wall: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Burst Winter Wall with a heavy blow right after an enemy hits it, and the burst freezes everything it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time the wall bursts. Winter Wall must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Chill filled all the way on everything the burst hits, which freezes everything that can be frozen.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flying Shards and All at Once (a frozen crowd to break), Sent Sliding.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (a heavy blow within 0.3 seconds of an enemy hitting the wall). For now, every burst freezes what it reaches.


      <b>Example.</b> Soldiers are hacking at the wall. Cut it right as one of them hits it, and all of them stand frozen in its shards.'
  - Id: ice.sliding
    Title: Sent Sliding
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blow no longer shatters a Frozen enemy where it stands. Instead, it sends it sliding in the direction of the blow as a block of ice, hitting everything it meets. It shatters at the end, or when it hits a wall. Freeze one, then bowl it into the rest.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: each time a frozen enemy is shattered.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A path of ice 10 metres long and 2 metres wide runs on from the shattered enemy in the direction of the blow. Everything on it takes 30 damage, 30 stagger and two stacks of Chill. At most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flying Shards and Chill (each enemy hit is two stacks closer to freezing), Winter Wall, All at Once, The World Holds Still.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The heavy blow sending the frozen body sliding as a block, knocking aside what it meets, and shattering at the end or on a wall. For now, it shatters where it stands like before, and the path of ice runs on from it.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze the soldier at the front of a line and break it toward the rest. The ice runs through all of them.'
  - Id: ice.brittle
    Title: Brittle
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When an enemy''s ice is gone, from thawing or shattering, it stays brittle for a while. Your weapon cuts deeper into it, and its balance breaks sooner.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, on every enemy that was Frozen and isn''t anymore.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For 5 seconds after its ice is gone, the enemy takes 20% more damage from weapon blows and 20% more stagger from everything. Spells don''t do extra damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chill, Flying Shards, All at Once, Clean Break, which comes after it on the chart (and Ski Run after that), and the finisher: a shatter and a few brittle blows often stagger it.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter a brute, then keep cutting. For five seconds, every blow hits a fifth harder, and its balance bar fills fast.'
  - Id: ice.base
    Title: Hoarfrost
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hoarfrost, Ice''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. Ice crystals grow out along the ground in a fan, and the cold hits as they come. They stay for a while, and one blow of your weapon among them shatters them all.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Ice, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Ice. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 5.5 seconds. Thirteen crystals grow over a fan 4.5 metres long and 110 degrees wide. They deal 10 damage, 15 stagger and two stacks of Chill. They stay for 4 seconds. A weapon blow among them shatters them all, dealing 5 damage, 8 stagger and one more Chill to everything nearby.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Chill; Brittle; and the charged blade, which it charges with Ice when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s third stack of Chill. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it at three soldiers, then cut into the crystals. Every one of them has three stacks before your second blow.'
  - Id: ice.base.shape
    Title: 'Hoarfrost: Black Ice or Frost Line'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Hoarfrost. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Black Ice</b>: no crystals. Instead, a sheet of clear ice covers the ground ahead and chills whatever stands on it. <b>Frost Line</b>: one branching line of frost creeps far along the floor, chilling everything it crosses. It can reach the archer behind the shield.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Hoarfrost, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the crystals.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Black Ice: a sheet with a 2.3-metre radius, 2.5 metres ahead of you, lasting 5 seconds. Each second, it deals 1 damage and a stack of Chill to whatever stands on it. Frost Line: a line 10 metres long and 1.4 metres wide that spreads out in a moment. It deals 8 damage, 10 stagger and two stacks of Chill.


      <b>Works with.</b> Winter Wall and Rime Guard, to keep enemies on the ice (Black Ice); Eight Points and Hanging Ice on the archer it reaches (Frost Line).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Black Ice should make enemies on it get thrown 50% farther, and make a dodge on it slide twice as far.


      <b>Example.</b> Put Black Ice down a hallway. The soldiers who walk in reach you with two or three stacks before you have swung.'
  - Id: ice.base.master
    Title: 'Hoarfrost: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast Hoarfrost within half a second after a shatter, and the crystals grow out from the shattered spot with four stacks of Chill.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, right after any shatter. Hoarfrost must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For 0.8 seconds after a shatter, every enemy within 6 metres of it is marked. The next blow or spell on each one adds two more stacks of Chill.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flying Shards (its burst is the hit that uses up the mark); Chill; All at Once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you cast Hoarfrost in the half second after a shatter, with the crystals growing from the shattered spot. For now, anything that hits the marked enemies in that moment adds the two extra stacks.


      <b>Example.</b> Shatter one soldier and cast right away into the rest. They get the crystals'' Chill plus two more, one blow away from freezing.'
  - Id: ice.ski
    Title: Ski Run
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A path of clear ice shoots out in front of you, and you slide down it right away, throwing up snow, through whatever stands on it. Enemies on the path get two stacks of Chill, and the path stays.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). A wall, or anything too big to push, stops the slide.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds, and the path 12 metres.) You slide 9 metres. Each enemy you pass takes 6 damage, 10 stagger and two stacks of Chill. The path stays for 5 seconds and deals 1 damage every half second to whatever stands on it. Nothing can hit you while you slide.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clean Break, which it comes after on the chart; Brittle; Chill; Hanging Ice and Eight Points on the enemies you leave behind.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You should only be untouchable for the first 0.2 seconds (for now, it''s the whole slide). A dodge along the path should carry you twice as far.


      <b>Example.</b> You''re surrounded. Slide out through the thinnest side. Everyone you pass gets two stacks, and the ice lies between you and them.'
  - Id: ice.ski.shape
    Title: 'Ski Run: Thin Ice or Shooting Run'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Ski Run. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Thin Ice</b>: the path is thin lake ice over black water. Anyone who steps on it breaks through up to the knees and is stuck, and a heavy blow on them then freezes the water around them. <b>Shooting Run</b>: as you slide, you shoot three ice shards at the nearest enemy, the way the huntress shoots from her skis.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Ski Run, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Thin Ice: each enemy the slide passes is held for 1.2 seconds (less for elites) and has three stacks of Chill. A heavy blow on it within those 1.2 seconds fills its Chill all the way, and it is Frozen. Shooting Run: three shards that chase their target, each dealing 8 damage, 6 stagger and a stack of Chill.


      <b>Works with.</b> Brittle and Flying Shards (Thin Ice: a held enemy frozen and broken); Hanging Ice (Shooting Run builds up an archer''s Chill).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Thin Ice should catch anyone who steps on the path within 5 seconds after (for now, only enemies the slide passes), and a god should be slowed by 30% instead of held. Shooting Run''s shards should be shot all through the slide (for now, all three go when it starts).


      <b>Example.</b> Slide past two soldiers on Thin Ice and turn around. Both are stuck to the knees, and one heavy blow freezes the first.'
  - Id: ice.ski.master
    Title: 'Ski Run: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you start Ski Run right as a blow would land on you, it counts as a perfect dodge. Also, slide through an enemy at the moment it shatters, and the path freezes everything standing on it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time you cast Ski Run, and every time you slide through a Frozen enemy. Ski Run must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You get what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting. Sliding through a Frozen enemy sends a path 9 metres long and 2 metres wide on from it, filling Chill all the way on everything on it, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rime Guard''s and Cold Read''s freezes, to slide through; Flying Shards; All at Once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (starting it right as a blow would land), and the moment of a shatter. For now, every cast gives the dodge''s mana and time off, and sliding through any Frozen enemy freezes the path past it.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze the front soldier, then slide through it toward the line behind. The line stands frozen behind you.'
  - Id: ice.stillworld
    Title: The World Holds Still
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Lighting Glaze raises a shell of ice around you. Press the surge button again while Glaze lasts, and the shell breaks outward as a ring that freezes everything it crosses, while the world slows down. Also, whenever you shatter a frozen enemy, a line of breaking ice runs to the nearest enemy that is still Frozen and shatters it too, which sends the line on again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The shell comes when you light the surge and stays while it lasts. The break comes when you press the surge button again, once each time you light it. For now, it works with whichever surge you light. The chain of shatters comes with every shatter, with or without Glaze.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. The ring spreads out to 9 metres in half a second. It deals 10 damage, 30 stagger and fills Chill all the way, so it freezes everything that can be frozen. Enemies slow to a fifth of their speed for 1.5 seconds. Glaze and its ward stay. The chain looks for a Frozen enemy within 7 metres of the one that shattered and hits it 0.2 seconds later for 20 damage and 30 stagger as a heavy blow, which shatters it for 45 more.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glaze, Flying Shards (the shards fly at every step of the chain), All at Once, Brittle, Sent Sliding, Deep Winter.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Glaze in a crowd, break the shell outward, and shatter the nearest enemy. The ice runs from one to the next until every frozen body has broken.'
  - Id: ice.cleanbreak
    Title: Clean Break
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Shatter an enemy that has less than a fifth of its health left, and nothing is left of it. Gods are safe from this; elites are not.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you shatter a frozen enemy, while you carry Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Major, 3 Shards. An enemy below 20% of its health when it is shattered dies a moment later. It never works on a god.


      <b>Works with.</b> Brittle, which it comes after on the chart, and Ski Run, which comes after it. Every way to freeze and shatter: Hoarfrost, Hanging Ice, heavy blows on a frozen enemy, All at Once.


      <b>Example.</b> A knight is frozen and down to a sliver of health. One heavy blow shatters it, and it''s gone.'
  - Id: illwind.shape
    Title: 'Ill Wind: Dust on the Blade or Dust Bank'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Ill Wind. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Ill Wind uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still loosen the enemy''s poison, like before. <b>Dust on the Blade</b>: no trail. Instead, the poisoned grit coats your blade, and your next blows each add a dose and an Unsteady. <b>Dust Bank</b>: the trail doesn''t settle. It keeps going in the direction the enemy was thrown, as a low bank of dust that makes anything it passes Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Ill Wind goes off while you carry both Poison and Wind. Ill Wind goes off when poison lands on an Unsteady enemy, or when Unsteady lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Ill Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: the poison stays loose while the enemy has both afflictions, and your heavy blow on it knocks all of it loose. Dust on the Blade: no cloud. For 6 seconds, your next three weapon blows on the enemy each add a dose and an Unsteady. Dust Bank: a cloud like the plain one (3 seconds, a dose every second), plus a line of dust 8 metres long and 1.2 metres wide, blowing with the wind. It lasts 4 seconds and makes whatever stands in it Unsteady once a second. After Ill Wind goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spring Dust to set it up, Gust at the Heel, No Still Air, The Yellow Gale.


      <b>Example.</b> One brute: use Dust on the Blade, and keep hitting it. A line of soldiers: use Dust Bank, and knock the first one down the line with a heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only a heavy blow knocks the poison loose, not every shove and throw. Dust on the Blade''s coat stays on the enemy instead of on your blade, and any weapon blow counts, not only light blows. Dust Bank''s trail appears all at once instead of drifting 2 metres a second.'
  - Id: illwind.springdust
    Title: Spring Dust
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows poison whatever they shove, and make it Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Poison and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One dose and one Unsteady. For your first element''s affliction, it adds nothing beyond what that element''s root mark already adds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Wind (one heavy blow adds both), Fine Dust, No Still Air.


      <b>Example.</b> Each heavy blow on a fresh enemy loosens its poison.'
  - Id: illwind.gustheel
    Title: Gust at the Heel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect dodge leaves a gust behind you that throws the attacker 3 metres. A poisoned attacker leaves a trail of poison right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect dodge, while you carry both Poison and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Everything within 2.5 metres of you is thrown 3 metres, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Wind, No Still Air, Spring Dust.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a poisoned brute''s swing at the last moment, and it is blown away from you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it throws everything near you, not only the attacker, and the throw doesn''t knock the dust loose yet.'
  - Id: illwind.finedust
    Title: Fine Dust
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Ill Wind''s trails last 2 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Ill Wind knocks poison loose, while you carry both Poison and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The trail of poison lasts 5 seconds instead of 3. With Dust Bank, both trails last 2 seconds longer.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spring Dust, which it comes after on the chart, Ill Wind and its shapes.


      <b>Example.</b> The cloud is still there when the next soldier steps in.'
  - Id: illwind.nostillair
    Title: No Still Air
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A poisoned, Unsteady enemy can''t stand still. Every 2 seconds, the wind shoves it 1 metre away from you, and it leaves a trail of poison each time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> After each Ill Wind, for as long as that enemy has both afflictions, while you carry both Poison and Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Every 2 seconds: a 1-metre shove straight away from you. All its poison is knocked loose into a cloud behind it. The cloud lasts 2 seconds and gives a dose once a second to everyone standing in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Wind, Spring Dust (which keeps it Unsteady and poisoned), The Yellow Gale.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned, Unsteady brute is blown back step by step, spreading poison dust over its own pack.'
  - Id: illwind.yellowgale
    Title: The Yellow Gale
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The wind swirls around you and blows out as a storm of yellow grit, in a cone 9 metres long and 60 degrees wide. Everything in it is thrown 4 metres, gets three stacks of Unsteady and doses of poison, and its poison bites right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Poison and Wind. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It deals 8 damage, 20 stagger, two doses and three Unsteady, throws enemies 4 metres, and makes their poison bite.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ill Wind (everything it hits gets both afflictions), No Still Air, Spring Dust.


      <b>Example.</b> Gather a pack in front of you and blow it away in yellow dust.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You can''t hold the button yet to draw everything in for 1.5 seconds. For now, it doesn''t pull in clouds, trails, and half the doses of each poisoned enemy within 6 metres to share them out. Weapon blows before you cast it don''t add doses yet.'
  - Id: ironthorns.shape
    Title: 'Iron Thorns: Pruning Hook or Iron Harvest'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Graft unlocks two new shapes for Iron Thorns. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Iron Thorns uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Pruning Hook</b>: your blows cut off the thorns instead of throwing them, and each one you cut heals you. <b>Iron Harvest</b>: no thorns are thrown. Instead, they all burst out together at the end.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Iron Thorns goes off while you carry both Life and Metal. Iron Thorns goes off when Sundered lands on a seeded enemy, or when Seeded lands on a notched one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Iron Thorns. The plain one grows three thorns, and each weapon blow throws one at the nearest other enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Pruning Hook: the thorns last 6 seconds. Each of your first three weapon blows on the enemy heals you 4. Iron Harvest: when the enemy dies or the 6 seconds end, a ring of blades bursts out to 3 metres, dealing 8 damage, 8 stagger and a notch to each enemy it reaches. After Iron Thorns goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch and Flung Blade to set it up, Sap of Iron, Grafted Edge, The Sown Men.


      <b>Example.</b> Low on health against one brute: use Pruning Hook, and three blows heal you 12. A pack: use Iron Harvest on the one in the middle.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Iron Harvest''s five thorns burst out as one ring instead of five blades.'
  - Id: ironthorns.sap
    Title: Sap of Iron
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies with thorns in it gives its iron to the ground, and the ground gives it to you. A quarter of a flask fills up right away, the way plants draw metal up through their roots.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies with both Seeded and Sundered on it, while you carry both Life and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A quarter of a flask, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Iron Thorns, Grafted Edge, The Sown Men.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack of seeded, notched soldiers falls, and a flask comes back full.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any enemy that dies seeded and notched, not only one that still has thorns in it.'
  - Id: ironthorns.ore
    Title: Ore in the Root
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Things you grow draw iron out of the ground. Each thing you grew adds a notch of Sundered when it bites.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Life and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: while you stand near something you grew, your blows add a notch, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch, Sapling, Elder Tree, Iron Thorns (a notch on a seeded enemy starts it).


      <b>Example.</b> Fight next to your sapling, and every soldier you hit there is notched and ready for Iron Thorns.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The things you grow don''t add the notch themselves yet. For now, your blows near them do.'
  - Id: ironthorns.grafted
    Title: Grafted Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A notch added to a Seeded enemy adds two instead.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Life and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. For now: each blow on an enemy that has both Seeded and Sundered adds one more notch, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sap of Iron, which it comes after on the chart, Iron Thorns (more notches, more thorns), Flung Blade.


      <b>Example.</b> Two blows on a seeded soldier, and its armour has three notches.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on each blow, not each time a notch is added.'
  - Id: ironthorns.sownmen
    Title: The Sown Men
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw five teeth ahead of you. They sink into the ground, and warriors made of bronze and roots rise from the soil. Each one goes to the nearest enemy and hits it again and again, adding Seeded, and every third hit adds a notch. After 6 seconds, they crumble into rust and leaves.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Life and Metal. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards, 45 mana: the larger spell of this pair. Five warriors fight for 6 seconds. Each one hits about every 0.7 seconds for 8 damage, 4 stagger and a Seeded. About one hit in three also adds a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Iron Thorns (a seeded, notched enemy grows thorns), Sap of Iron, Grafted Edge.


      <b>Example.</b> Sow them into a pack and fight next to them.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The teeth aren''t thrown in a fan yet, and the warriors don''t rise from the floor yet. The notch comes by chance instead of on every third hit. Your heavy blow doesn''t yet call every warrior to hit the same enemy.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.shape
    Title: 'Kindling Point: Burning Glass or The Fire Is Hungry'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Kindling Point. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Kindling Point uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still focus the light on one point, like before: each of your blows adds to its Burn, and the third one sets it on fire. <b>Burning Glass</b>: the point stays after the burst, like a magnifying glass held still, adding Burn every second. <b>The Fire Is Hungry</b>: the flame jumps to the next enemy, so the point moves through a crowd.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Kindling Point goes off while you carry both Light and Fire. Kindling Point goes off when Burn lands on a Marked enemy, or when a Mark lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Kindling Point.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: for up to 8 seconds, each of your weapon blows on the enemy adds a Burn, and the third blow uses up all its Burn. Burning Glass: the third blow bursts for 10 damage per Burn and leaves the enemy with two stacks of Burn. Then, for 4 seconds, it gains one more Burn every second. The Fire Is Hungry: the third blow jumps to the nearest other enemy within 6 metres for 10 damage per Burn, Marking it and giving it two stacks of Burn. This is on purpose: it may start a new point there. After Kindling Point goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glare and Flame Wave to set it up, The Sun Points, Noon Guard, Hands of the Sun.


      <b>Example.</b> One brute: use Burning Glass, and keep it burning. A crowd: use The Fire Is Hungry, and move the point through it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, every weapon blow counts toward the three, not only critical hits.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.sunpoints
    Title: The Sun Points
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The blow that ends a chain of blows on a burning enemy Marks it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The last blow of each chain that lands on a burning enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Mark.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, Kindling Point, Clear Sky.


      <b>Example.</b> End each chain on a burning soldier, and the point starts to gather.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.noonguard
    Title: Noon Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked, burning enemy sets the point on fire right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has both a Mark and Burn.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. All its Burn is used up, for 10 damage each, and it keeps burning with two stacks.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kindling Point, The Sun Points, Kiln Stance.


      <b>Example.</b> A Marked, burning brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it bursts into flame.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.clearsky
    Title: Clear Sky
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A Mark lasts one blow longer on a burning enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Light and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Sun Points, which it comes after on the chart, Kindling Point.


      <b>Example.</b> One more critical hit before the Mark is used up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It does nothing yet.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.feast
    Title: The Feast
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a Kindling Point burst kills an enemy, every burn within 8 metres gains a stack, and you get mana.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy with both a Mark and Burn dies, while you carry both Light and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. One Burn on everything within 8 metres, and 10 mana, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kindling Point, Hands of the Sun, and Daybreak and Meteor Leap to spend the mana.


      <b>Example.</b> Burn down a Marked pack, and every death feeds the fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any Marked, burning enemy that dies, not only one killed by the burst.'
  - Id: kindlingpoint.hands
    Title: Hands of the Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A burning sun rises over you for a moment, and long rays reach down to the four nearest enemies. Each ray ends in a hand of flame that grabs its enemy. Each one is held, gets two stacks of Burn and a Mark, so every one of them has a Kindling Point.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Light and Fire. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to four enemies within 9 metres. Each takes 6 damage, 10 stagger, a Mark and two Burn, and is held for 1 second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kindling Point and its shapes, The Feast, The Sun Points.


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight with it on a pack: four points start gathering at once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the rays jump from one enemy to the next instead of coming down from one sun. The hand doesn''t yet let go at your first blow and make that blow a critical hit.'
  - Id: life.seed
    Title: Seeded
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands plants a seed in the enemy: one stack of Seeded, up to three. Each weapon blow after that on a Seeded enemy heals you a little, and more for each stack. When a Seeded enemy dies, it bursts into thorns that hit everyone near it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every weapon blow that lands, light or heavy, while Life is your first element (or you are all in on Life). If Life is your second element, Seeded comes from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike instead.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack per blow, up to three, lasting 8 seconds. Each weapon blow on a Seeded enemy heals you 1 health for each stack, so 3 at full. When it dies, it deals 12 damage per stack to everyone within 3 metres, so 36 at three stacks.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ripening, Fallen Seeds, Reap What Is Sown (it bursts every seed it reaches), Root and Branch, Regrowth (one more stack per blow), Briar Patch, and Rot Roots when Poison meets the seed.


      <b>Example.</b> With the Twin Knives, three quick cuts give a soldier three stacks. Every cut after that gives you back 3 health while you keep attacking it.'
  - Id: life.fallen
    Title: Fallen Seeds
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Where a Seeded enemy dies, a thornbush grows and stays for a while. It lashes and Seeds whatever stands in it. The bush counts as something you grew.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever an enemy with Seeded dies, no matter what killed it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A bush with a 2-metre radius that lasts 8 seconds. Every half second, it deals 4 damage and one stack of Seeded. Up to four can stand at once; a fifth replaces the oldest.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded (its death burst happens too), Reap What Is Sown and Elder Tree, which come after it on the chart, Root and Branch above them, Drawn From the Wood (it uses up the bushes), Root Walk (a place to come up), Wildwood.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a seeded soldier in a doorway. The bush where it fell Seeds everyone who follows it through.'
  - Id: life.reap
    Title: Reap What Is Sown
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Reap What Is Sown, the detonator of the Life tree. You call in the harvest. Everything you''ve grown bursts where it stands, the seeds in nearby enemies burst right away and are used up, and you heal for each burst.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 10 seconds.) Each thing you grew, wherever it stands, bursts in a 3-metre circle for 16 damage and 25 stagger, then disappears, and heals you 5. A call reaches 6 metres around you 0.2 seconds after you cast it, dealing 16 damage and 25 stagger. Every Seeded enemy it hits has its seed burst right away, for 11 damage and 5 stagger per stack to everyone within 3 metres, and heals you 5.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds and Sapling (the things it bursts), Briar Patch, Seeded and Ripening, Wood Grows Back (the garden comes back), Wildwood (more to burst), and the charged blade: it charges your weapon with Life when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Every Seeded enemy in the fight should burst, not only those within the call''s 6 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> Three saplings stand around you and a pack closes in. Reap, and four bursts go off at once while you heal 20.'
  - Id: life.reap.shape
    Title: 'Reap What Is Sown: Golden Sickle or Second Sowing'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Reap What Is Sown. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Golden Sickle</b> (the druids'' sickle): only the Seeded enemies within eight metres are reaped, each cut once by one sweep of gold, and the things you grew stay standing. <b>Second Sowing</b>: everything bursts like before, and each burst plants a new seed in everything it reaches, so the cycle keeps going.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Reap What Is Sown, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain call.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Golden Sickle: one sweep 8 metres all around you, for 16 damage and 25 stagger. Each Seeded enemy it cuts has its seed burst, like the Learn mark''s. Second Sowing: each seed that goes off (from Reap, Ripening or anything else) puts one Seeded on every enemy within 3 metres of it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ripening (its bursts plant seeds too, while you use Second Sowing); Fallen Seeds and Sapling, for the garden Golden Sickle should keep.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Golden Sickle should keep the garden standing; for now, the things you grew still burst. In Second Sowing, the things you grew should plant seeds as they burst; for now, only seeds that go off plant new ones.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Second Sowing in a crowd. Reap once to Seed the whole pack again, cut, then reap again.'
  - Id: life.reap.master
    Title: 'Reap What Is Sown: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it within half a second of a Seeded enemy dying, and every burst counts twice.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, for half a second after any Seeded enemy dies. Reap What Is Sown must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For half a second, every seed that goes off bursts twice as hard (seed bursts are 100% stronger).


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded''s own death burst (kill one, then reap the rest); Ripening''s heavy blow in the same half second.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The garden''s bursts and the healing should count twice too, and the half second should be tied to casting this spell. For now, any seed that goes off in that half second is doubled.


      <b>Example.</b> Finish the seeded soldier and reap as it falls. The seeds in its friends burst for twice their thorns.'
  - Id: life.tree
    Title: Elder Tree
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Elder Tree, the larger spell of the Life tree. A tree grows where you stand, from shoot to sapling to oak, in three seconds. Once grown, a ring lies under its branches for fifteen seconds. Enemies inside stay Seeded, you heal, and every few seconds a root bursts up under the enemies inside.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 40 mana (its Empower makes it 32, and makes it grow in 2 seconds). The ring has a 3-metre radius and lasts 18 seconds from when you cast it. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and adds Seeded. The roots first hit 3 seconds after planting, then every 3 seconds for 15 seconds, for 16 damage, 45 stagger and one Seeded. Each cast holds 15 seconds of shade: each second you spend in a fight uses one up and heals you 3. The tree doesn''t count as something you grew, so Reap What Is Sown and Drawn From the Wood leave it alone.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded, Ripening (heavy blows on what it Seeded), Fallen Seeds, which it comes after on the chart, Sapling''s Master mark (a small tree of the same kind), and the charged blade: its first hit charges your weapon with Life.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The tree should have 40 health while it grows, and enemies should turn and attack it. Each root should come up under one enemy, staggered ones first (for now, the roots hit everything in the ring). The healing should only happen inside the ring while the tree stands (for now, it is 3 a second for 15 seconds of fighting, anywhere).


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it where a pack has to come to you, and fight at its foot. Every three seconds, the roots stagger and Seed them while you heal.'
  - Id: life.tree.shape
    Title: 'Elder Tree: Walking Tree or Grown at Once'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Elder Tree. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Walking Tree</b>: the tree pulls up its roots and follows you at walking speed, with a smaller ring, like the stag with a forest growing through it. <b>Grown at Once</b>: no growing. It comes up fully grown in an instant but only stays for six seconds, so a slow setup becomes a quick burst.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Elder Tree, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the growing tree.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Walking Tree: the roots hit within 2 metres of it. Grown at Once: the roots hit right away and then every 3 seconds for 6 seconds, within 3 metres, for 16 damage, 45 stagger and Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Root Walk (go to where the tree is); The Garden Holds You and Wildwood near the ring.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Walking Tree following you; for now, it stays where it was planted, with the smaller ring. With either shape, the shade ring and the healing keep the Learn mark''s times.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Grown at Once on a staggered brute. The first root lands before it gets back up.'
  - Id: life.tree.master
    Title: 'Elder Tree: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Plant it on something you grew, and it grows right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, when you cast it within 4 metres of something you grew. Elder Tree must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, a root bursts up right away within 3 metres of you, for 16 damage, 45 stagger and Seeded, before the tree''s own first root.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds and Sapling, for something to plant it on; Briar Patch''s bed; Wildwood.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The whole tree growing at once on the thing you grew. For now, the first root comes right away, and the tree still grows.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast Briar Patch, step into the bed, and plant the tree on it. The roots answer right away.'
  - Id: life.lift
    Title: Root and Branch
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Root and Branch, Life''s reaction lift. Every reaction with Seeded in it heals you and plants a seed in everything it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Seeded reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Rot Roots with Poison, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each of these reactions heals you 4 and puts one Seeded on every enemy within 3 metres of where it happened.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Life''s ten reactions; Seeded; Fallen Seeds, which it comes after on the chart; Wood Grows Back above it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The seed should go into exactly what the reaction hit; for now, it goes into everything within 3 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> Life and Poison on a tight group. Rot Roots goes off on one, you heal 4, and the enemies next to it are Seeded for the next one.'
  - Id: life.devotion
    Title: Wood Grows Back
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Wood Grows Back, Life''s Devotion, for going all in on Life only. It is the land''s rule, "wood forgets". Whatever you grow grows back where it stood after it is used up, burst or cut down, and every spell you cast plants a sapling where it lands. The garden never runs out, so you can use Reap What Is Sown and Root Walk as much as you like, and every fight becomes a forest.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Life twice (all in). A sapling is planted each time a spell hits.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A sapling grows where a spell hits, at most one every 2 seconds. It reaches 3.5 metres and stays for 12 seconds. Every 1.5 seconds, it hits for 6 damage, 8 stagger and Seeded. Up to four can stand at once. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Briar Root. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Life alone or twice. The cutting grows into a briar around the enemy''s feet and roots it in place for a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every spell in the Life tree; Reap What Is Sown and Drawn From the Wood, which use up the garden; Root Walk, which travels to it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Whatever is used up should grow back where it stood 6 seconds later; for now, there are only the saplings each spell plants. Briar Root''s briar should stay as something you grew.


      <b>Example.</b> Briar Patch, Reap What Is Sown, then Briar Patch again. Every cast leaves a sapling, so the reaping always has something to burst.'
  - Id: life.ripen
    Title: Ripening
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on a Seeded enemy bursts its seed right away, without waiting for it to die. The thorns hit everyone near it, and you heal.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on a Seeded enemy, at most once a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The burst has a 3-metre radius and deals 11 damage and 5 stagger for each stack (33 damage and 15 stagger at three). The seed is used up, so it won''t burst again when the enemy dies. You heal 10.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded, Drawn From the Wood and The Garden Holds You, which come after it on the chart, Bitter Rind (a rooted enemy is easy to hit with a heavy blow), Elder Tree (it Seeds for you), Reap What Is Sown''s Second Sowing shape.


      <b>Example.</b> Two light blows and a heavy blow on a soldier in a crowd. The heavy blow bursts its seed through the pack and heals you 10.'
  - Id: life.harvest
    Title: Drawn From the Wood
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Drawn From the Wood. You kneel, and everything you''ve grown withers into you, bursting into thorns as it goes. In return, you get a short window of green blood: every weapon blow is a critical hit and heals you a fifth of its damage. You pay with the garden you grew, not your health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It needs something you grew nearby.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 10 seconds.) Each thing you grew, wherever it stands, bursts in a 2-metre circle for 12 damage and Seeded, then disappears. For now, if you cast it within 4 metres of something you grew, the window lasts 6 seconds: every weapon blow is a critical hit, and you get 20% of its damage back as health.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds, Sapling, Briar Patch and Root Walk, for the garden it uses up; Wood Grows Back, to have the garden again; Ripening, which it comes after on the chart; and Seeded''s healing on top.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The window should last a second and a half for each thing that withered, up to ten seconds, and only count things within 12 metres. The half-second kneel.


      <b>Example.</b> Three bushes stand around a brute. Draw them in, and cut the brute with six seconds of critical, healing blows.'
  - Id: life.harvest.shape
    Title: 'Drawn From the Wood: Blood in the Furrow or Feed the Ground'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Drawn From the Wood. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Blood in the Furrow</b>: the green blood soaks into the ground where you knelt, making a ring that Seeds everything in it each second. You fight in one place, instead of carrying the power around with you. <b>Feed the Ground</b>: the one spell where Life costs you health. Nothing withers. Instead, you give a quarter of your health to your garden, and for a while everything you''ve grown hits twice as hard and twice as often.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Drawn From the Wood, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain withering.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Blood in the Furrow: a ring 8 metres across where you knelt, lasting 6 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and adds Seeded. Feed the Ground: it costs 25 health, which turns grey so you can win it back with blows. You can''t cast it with 25 health or less. For 8 seconds, the things you grow hit twice as hard.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blood in the Furrow: Bitter Rind and Briar Coat, which keep the fight in one place. Feed the Ground: Fallen Seeds, Sapling and Wildwood, for a garden worth feeding; and the Master mark, to pay with grey health.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Blood in the Furrow''s window should only work while you stand in the ring. Feed the Ground should take a quarter of your current health (for now, it takes 25), keep the garden (for now, it still withers like the Learn mark''s), and double the power and speed of what is already standing (for now, only what you grow during the 8 seconds hits harder).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Blood in the Furrow at a narrow spot. Wait in the ring and let them come to you, getting Seeded with every step.'
  - Id: life.harvest.master
    Title: 'Drawn From the Wood: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it within a second of getting hit, and the cost comes out of the grey health that blow left behind. Nothing withers, each thing you grew still gives its time, and Feed the Ground takes none of your real health.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, for one second after any blow lands on you. Drawn From the Wood must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. During that second, costs paid in health cost nothing: Feed the Ground''s 25 isn''t taken.


      <b>Works with.</b> Feed the Ground; Briar Coat (the blow that lands cuts the attacker too); Seeded''s healing, to win the grey health back.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Keeping the garden on that cast while each thing you grew still gives its time, and taking the cost from that blow''s grey health. For now, only the health cost is skipped.


      <b>Example.</b> Take a soldier''s blow, then cast Feed the Ground right away. The garden hits twice as hard, and you pay nothing.'
  - Id: life.garden
    Title: The Garden Holds You
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Garden Holds You. Near anything you grew, you can''t be thrown, pulled or knocked down, and normal blows don''t make you flinch. Roots run from the nearest plant to your heels. Fight in the garden.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while something you grew stands within 4 metres of you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, you take 20% less damage while something you grew stands within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch and Fallen Seeds, for a garden to stand in; Root Walk and Wildwood, which come after it on the chart; Ripening.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The footing itself (never thrown, pulled or knocked down, and no flinching from normal blows), and the roots running to your heels. For now, it only cuts the damage you take in the garden.


      <b>Example.</b> Stand in your own bed of briars against a brute. Its blows land for a fifth less while you trade hits.'
  - Id: life.walk
    Title: Root Walk
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Root Walk, the movement spell of the Life tree. You sink into the ground in a swirl of leaves and come up at something you grew, or ahead of you if there is nothing. Nothing can touch you under the ground. A sapling sprouts where you went down, and roots burst out around you as you come up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds and adds two Seeded.) For now, it is a step: up to 8 metres toward and past the enemy you face, or 6 metres ahead if there is none, and you can''t be hit during it. Whatever it passes takes 10 damage, 15 stagger and Seeded, and your next heavy blow within half a second is a critical hit. The sapling where you went down reaches 3.5 metres and stays for 6 seconds. Every 1.5 seconds, it hits for 6 damage, 8 stagger and Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds, Sapling and Briar Patch, for places to come up; The Garden Holds You, which it comes after on the chart; Wood Grows Back (use up the garden, and it comes back).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Going under the ground to something you grew within 12 metres, the one nearest to where you aim, and coming up half a second later. The roots should burst out around you as you come up, not along the way.


      <b>Example.</b> You''re surrounded. Root Walk out through the soldier in front of you. The sapling you leave keeps the rest busy.'
  - Id: life.walk.shape
    Title: 'Root Walk: Up Through Them or Changeling'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Root Walk. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Up Through Them</b>: you go to a Seeded enemy instead, and come up underneath it, lifting it on roots. <b>Changeling</b>: you swap places with something you grew, so it stands where you stood, in the way of the blow aimed at you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Root Walk, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain walk.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Up Through Them: a Seeded enemy the walk hits is thrown into the air for 0.6 seconds (less for elites and gods). Changeling: a thornbush with a 2-metre radius rises where you stood and lasts 8 seconds. Every half second, it deals 4 damage and Seeded. Enemies within 2.5 metres lose track of you for 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Up Through Them: Seeded, the charged blade, and every mark that works on a lifted enemy. Changeling: Fallen Seeds'' bushes, and Reap What Is Sown to burst what you left behind.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Up Through Them should go to the nearest Seeded enemy, not the one you face. Changeling should swap places with something you grew; for now, a bush rises where you stood, and enemies near it attack it.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Changeling to escape two soldiers closing in from both sides. Both of them swing at the bush you leave behind.'
  - Id: life.walk.master
    Title: 'Root Walk: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you start it right as a blow would land on you, it counts as a perfect dodge, and the sapling you leave behind is a full thornbush.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time you cast it. Root Walk must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each cast gives you a perfect dodge''s mana (12) and takes 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell and strike that is waiting. A thornbush with a 2-metre radius grows where you went down and lasts 8 seconds. Every half second, it deals 4 damage and Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every mark that uses mana; Reap What Is Sown (one more thing to burst); The Garden Holds You.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (starting it right as a blow would land), and the thornbush replacing the sapling; for now, it grows next to it.


      <b>Example.</b> Root Walk out of a brute''s swing, then walk again sooner. Each walk leaves a bush and a sapling behind.'
  - Id: life.wildwood
    Title: Wildwood
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The things you grow last three times as long, and you can have three more of each kind. The cost: far from your garden, your weapon is a fifth weaker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The cost applies whenever nothing you grew stands within 7 metres of you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. The things you grow last three times as long (thornbushes 24 seconds, saplings 36). Three more of each kind can stand at once. Your weapon deals 20% less damage when nothing you grew is within 7 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds, Sapling, Briar Patch, The Garden Holds You, which it comes after on the chart, and Reap What Is Sown and Drawn From the Wood (more to use up).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The things you grow should last forever; for now, they last three times as long.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant a ring of saplings at the top of a staircase and hold it. The garden lasts the whole fight, and you never have to leave it.'
  - Id: life.rind
    Title: Bitter Rind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A normal guard Seeds the attacker. A perfect guard roots it in place for a moment.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each blow you block, and each perfect guard.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One stack of Seeded on the attacker for each blocked blow. A perfect guard holds it still for 1 second (less for elites and gods; a few huge enemies can''t be held).


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Coat, which comes after it on the chart, Sapling (perfect guards plant saplings), Seeded, Ripening (a held enemy is easy to hit with a heavy blow).


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a brute''s swing. It stands rooted for a second, long enough for a heavy blow that bursts its seed.'
  - Id: life.base
    Title: Briar Patch
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Briar Patch, Life''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A bed of thorny vines grows in front of you and stays, lashing, Seeding and slowing whatever stands in it. The bed counts as something you grew.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Life, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Life. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 5.5 seconds. The bed is 2.5 metres across, lands 3 metres ahead of you, and lasts 6 seconds. Twice a second, it lashes for 4 damage and Seeded, and it slows whatever stands in it by a fifth.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Seeded; Reap What Is Sown and Drawn From the Wood, which use up the bed; The Garden Holds You and Root Walk; and the charged blade, which it charges with Life when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s bigger bed, 3.3 metres across. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Put the bed in a doorway and fight at its edge. Everything that comes through is Seeded and slowed.'
  - Id: life.base.shape
    Title: 'Briar Patch: Burrs or Seed Pods'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Briar Patch. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Burrs</b>: no bed. Instead, a spray of hooked burrs covers a fan in front of you. Every enemy hit carries them and drops seeds as it moves, which sprout into tufts of briar. Your blows on an enemy with burrs shake burrs onto the enemies next to it. The enemy plants your garden for you, the way burdock burrs ride on animals. <b>Seed Pods</b>: no bed. Three pods are thrown, swell up, and burst a second later in a spray of seeds, each leaving a small bush.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Briar Patch, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the bed.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Burrs: a cone 4 metres long and 90 degrees wide, dealing 4 damage, 5 stagger and Seeded. An enemy with burrs carries them for 6 seconds and drops a tuft every 1.5 seconds. Each tuft is 0.8 metres across, lasts 4 seconds, and deals 4 damage and Seeded every half second. Tufts count as things you grew, up to six. Your weapon blows on an enemy with burrs give Seeded and burrs to every enemy within 2 metres, at most twice a second. Seed Pods: three pods land under the nearest enemies within 6 metres and burst 1 second later in a 1.5-metre circle, for 12 damage, 10 stagger and two Seeded. Each one that hits leaves a bush 1 metre across for 6 seconds, which deals 4 damage and Seeded every half second. Bushes count as things you grew, up to three.


      <b>Works with.</b> Burrs: Root Walk (places to come up wherever the enemies went), and Reap What Is Sown on a room full of tufts. Seed Pods: Fallen Seeds'' bushes, Drawn From the Wood.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Burrs should drop a seed for every 1.5 metres the enemy moves (for now, every 1.5 seconds, moving or not). Seed Pods should be thrown in a fan, each leaving its bush where it bursts, whether it hits or not (for now, they land under the nearest enemies, with a bush under each one they hit).


      <b>Example.</b> Cover a pack in burrs and back away. They bring your garden to you.'
  - Id: life.base.master
    Title: 'Briar Patch: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it on something you grew, within a metre of it, and the bed grows into a thicket around it, bigger and longer-lasting. Whatever stands in it as it grows is rooted in place.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, when you cast it within 4 metres of something you grew. Briar Patch must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A thicket 4 metres across grows where the bed lands and lasts 10 seconds. Every half second, it deals 4 damage and Seeded. It counts as something you grew, up to two. Every enemy within 2 metres of it as it grows is rooted for 1 second: it can''t walk, but it can still hit whatever comes within reach.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fallen Seeds, Sapling and the bed itself, for something to cast it on; Ripening on what the thicket roots; Wildwood.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The thicket should grow around the thing you cast it on; for now, it grows where the bed lands, as long as you stand near something you grew.


      <b>Example.</b> Two beds in a row: the second one grows into a thicket and roots the soldiers in it.'
  - Id: life.coat
    Title: Briar Coat
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Thorns grow on you. An enemy that hits you, or whose blow you block, is cut and Seeded.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each blow that lands on you, and each blow you block with a normal guard, at most twice a second for each.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A burst within 1.2 metres of the attacker deals 8 damage and one stack of Seeded. For now, any attacker is cut, even one hitting you from far away: the thorns land where the blow came from.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded (your next blows on the enemy heal you), Bitter Rind, which it comes after on the chart, Sapling, which comes after it, Drawn From the Wood''s Master mark (a blow you take).


      <b>Example.</b> Block a soldier''s string of three blows. It is cut and Seeded as it comes in, and your answer heals you.'
  - Id: life.sapling
    Title: Sapling
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Sapling, the counter of the Life tree. For a while after you cast it, each perfect guard or perfect dodge plants a sapling where the attacker stands. Each sapling lashes nearby enemies again and again, Seeding them. They count as things you grew.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then guard or dodge perfectly: press the guard button just before a blow lands, or dodge just before it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds.) Each cast gives three saplings, used up by your next perfect guards and perfect dodges. Each sapling reaches 3.5 metres and stays for 12 seconds. Every 1.5 seconds, it hits everything in reach for 6 damage, 8 stagger and one stack of Seeded. Up to three can stand at once from guards, and three from dodges; one more replaces the oldest. On a dodge, it is planted at the enemy you face.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bitter Rind and Briar Coat (they work on the same guard), Briar Coat, which it comes after on the chart, Reap What Is Sown and Drawn From the Wood (they use up the saplings), Root Walk, Wildwood, Seeded.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The window should last eight seconds after you cast it (for now, the three saplings wait until used). There should be three at most in total between guards and dodges. The Empower''s fourth sapling. Each sapling should only lash the nearest enemy (for now, it hits everything within reach).


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it as a pack closes in and perfect-guard three blows. Three saplings grow among them, Seeding everything that comes near.'
  - Id: life.sapling.shape
    Title: 'Sapling: Strangler or Dodder'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Sapling. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Strangler</b>: instead of a sapling, a plant climbs up the attacker, the way a strangler fig climbs a tree. The attacker is rooted and gets lots of Seeded, and nothing is left standing afterwards. <b>Dodder</b>: the seed grows on the attacker itself, as pale threads that drink from it. Each of your blows on it heals you more, and its balance breaks sooner.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, on every perfect guard and perfect dodge while you have the shape picked, along with the saplings. If you have not bought this, only the saplings are planted.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Strangler: the attacker is rooted for 1 second (it can''t walk, but it can still hit whatever comes within reach) and gets two stacks of Seeded. Dodder: for 6 seconds, each of your weapon blows on it heals you 3, and it takes a quarter more stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Strangler: Ripening on the rooted enemy. Dodder: Seeded''s own healing; Bitter Rind and Briar Coat on the same attacker.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Each should replace the sapling, and only work during the spell''s window. For now, they work on every perfect guard and dodge while picked, and the saplings are still planted.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Dodder on a brute whose swing you perfectly guarded. You get six seconds of cuts that each heal 3 more and break its balance a quarter sooner.'
  - Id: life.sapling.master
    Title: 'Sapling: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Plant all three saplings within three seconds, and the third one comes up as a full Elder Tree for six seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, on a perfect guard, at most once every 15 seconds. Sapling must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A small Elder Tree rises at the attacker. Its roots hit within 3 metres 0.3 seconds later, then every 3 seconds for 6 seconds, dealing 16 damage, 45 stagger and Seeded each time.


      <b>Works with.</b> Elder Tree; Bitter Rind on the same perfect guard; Ripening on what the roots Seed.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need all three saplings planted within three seconds. For now, any perfect guard works, once every fifteen seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a soldier''s swing. A tree rises under it, and the roots break up the crowd behind it.'
  - Id: life.spring
    Title: First Spring
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your surge is lit, press the surge button again to give half your health to the ground. A group of trees grows up through every Seeded enemy nearby, hitting it and holding it. Hit the enemies the forest holds, and you get back the health you gave.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light the surge (Regrowth, or the other element''s surge if you have that one picked), then press the surge button again while it lasts. Once each time you light the surge. If nothing nearby is Seeded, it tells you so, and you can press again.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. It reaches up to eight Seeded enemies within 12 metres. Each takes 30 damage and 60 stagger and is held for 4 seconds (less for elites and gods). Half your current health is taken and turns grey. Each blow on a held enemy wins back half its damage as health, but never more than you gave.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded, Regrowth (4 health a second while it lasts), Briar Patch''s Burrs and Seed Pods shapes to Seed a crowd, Root and Branch.


      <b>Example.</b> Seed a pack with Briar Patch, light Regrowth, and press again. The whole pack is held in place while you cut your health back out of them.'
  - Id: life.sharedroots
    Title: Shared Roots
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Enemies with your seeds in them take part of your damage for you. Thin roots run from your heels to each Seeded enemy near you. But your seeds no longer heal you when you hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every blow that hurts you, while you carry Life, if a Seeded enemy is within 8 metres.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. A third of the damage from each blow is taken off you and dealt to the Seeded enemies within 8 metres instead, split evenly between them (up to eight of them). If no Seeded enemy is nearby, you take all the damage. Blows on Seeded enemies no longer heal you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Coat, which it comes after on the chart, and which Seeds the enemies that hit you. Briar Patch and Seeded, to plant seeds in a crowd.


      <b>Example.</b> Seed a whole pack with Briar Patch, then wade in. Every blow you take is split between them.'
  - Id: life.inseason
    Title: In Its Season
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your seeds ripen if you leave them alone. A ripe seed flowers and stops fading. When a ripe seed bursts, it hits three times as hard and leaves a thornbush behind. A seed that bursts before it is ripe only hits half as hard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A seed ripens once it has stayed in an enemy for six seconds with no new seed planted on it, while you carry Life. A seed bursts when you set it off (Ripening, Reap What Is Sown), when the enemy dies, or in a reaction.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. A seed is ripe after 6 seconds alone. A ripe seed doesn''t fade until it bursts. A ripe burst deals three times the damage, and a thornbush grows there. The bush lasts 8 seconds and deals 4 damage and a seed every half second to whatever stands in it (up to four bushes). An unripe burst deals half the damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Root and Branch, which it comes after on the chart. Ripening, Reap What Is Sown, Fallen Seeds, and the reactions that use Seeded.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant seeds in three enemies, then fight a fourth for six seconds. Now reap: three ripe bursts and three thornbushes.'
  - Id: life.shade
    Title: Shade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While you stand near anything you''ve grown, you heal slowly and your stamina comes back faster.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every second, while something you grew stands within 4 metres of you and you carry Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Major, 3 Shards. 2 health and 5 stamina every second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Root and Branch, which it comes after on the chart. Briar Patch, Elder Tree, Sapling and Fallen Seeds, which grow things to stand next to.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight next to your Elder Tree. Each second, you heal a little, and your stamina refills for the next dodge.'
  - Id: light.mark
    Title: Marked
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The last blow of a chain of blows, and every perfect guard, leave a mark of light on the enemy: it is Marked. Your next three weapon blows on a Marked enemy are critical hits, and each gives more mana. Then the Mark is used up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The last light blow of a chain, or the heavy blow that ends one, Marks the enemy it lands on. A perfect guard Marks the enemy that hit you. Like every root mark, your weapon only adds it while Light is your first element (or your only one). The perfect guard''s Mark works either way. If Light is your second element, Light''s Marks come from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. A Mark lasts 8 seconds or three weapon blows, whichever ends first. Each of those blows is a critical hit, dealing one and a half times its damage, and gives 2 more mana. Marking the enemy again gives you three fresh blows.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glare and every Light spell (each one Marks), Weighed (Marks stack up to three), Just Cut and Unblemished (more critical hits), Condemned, Full Daylight (a Mark that blows don''t use up), Clarity, and the charged blade, whose third Light blow throws a ray that Marks.


      <b>Example.</b> Three light blows on a soldier: the third Marks it. A heavy blow and two more light blows after that all land as critical hits.'
  - Id: light.weighed
    Title: Weighed
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Marks stack up, to three. When an affliction on an enemy fills all the way up (including a Mark at three), your critical hits hit harder for a while.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. Stacking Marks needs Light as your first element; if Light is your second, a Mark stays at one. The stronger critical hits come each time any affliction you add fills up on an enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Marks stack up to three, one more for each Mark added. When an affliction fills up, critical hits deal 1.8 times their damage instead of 1.5, for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Reckoning''s Spoken Sentence shape (12 more for each stack, once built), Ring of Spears and Brimming (they add Marks fast), Condemned, which comes after it on the chart, Unblemished and Just Cut (more critical hits to use it on).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Each stack should make critical hits stronger on its own. For now, the stronger critical hits come when a Mark fills up.


      <b>Example.</b> End three chains on a brute. It has three Marks, and for six seconds every critical hit does 1.8 times damage.'
  - Id: light.reckoning
    Title: Reckoning
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Reckoning, the detonator of the Light tree. You put a seal on the enemy you face: a ring like a clock face on the ground under it, with one hand that sweeps around it in six seconds. On the last tick, it breaks upward in a column of light. It deals 8 damage for each weapon blow you landed on that enemy in those six seconds, along with a big hit to its balance.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, nothing is sealed, and you still have to wait. Then fight that enemy for six seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It targets an enemy within 10 metres. The seal flashes, without hitting anything, each time you land a blow on the enemy. When the six seconds end, it deals 8 damage for each weapon blow that landed on the enemy during that time, plus 40 stagger. Its Empower makes it 10 for each blow, up to 180.


      <b>Works with.</b> Fast weapons (more blows counted), the root mark and Just Cut (critical hits), Weighed, Condemned (the column''s stagger plus a full Mark''s), Ring of Spears (holds the enemy still while you cut), the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The clock face isn''t drawn on the ground yet.


      <b>Example.</b> Seal a captain behind its guard, then cut it eight times in six seconds. As the seal breaks, the column deals 64 more damage and 40 stagger.'
  - Id: light.reckoning.shape
    Title: 'Reckoning: Witnessed or Spoken Sentence'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Reckoning. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Witnessed</b> (the sun god Surya, the eye that sees every deed): the seal counts the enemy''s attacks, not yours. Every blow it lands on you or your guard is scratched in gold on the clock''s edge, and on the last tick a column falls on it. Guard well, and the sun judges for you. <b>Spoken Sentence</b>: no clock. The seal waits for your next heavy blow on the sealed enemy. Then a blade of gold falls on every Marked enemy nearby. It hits many enemies, at the moment you choose.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Reckoning, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain seal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Witnessed: when the six seconds end, a column falls on the sealed enemy within 1.2 metres, for 30 damage, 40 stagger and a Mark. Spoken Sentence: for 8 seconds, your next heavy blow on the sealed enemy makes a blade fall a moment later on every Marked enemy within 8 metres. Each blade deals 32 damage and 40 stagger within 1.2 metres, and uses up their Marks.


      <b>Works with.</b> Witnessed: a build that counters attacks, perfect guards, Standing Light, the root mark (perfect guards Mark the attacker). Spoken Sentence: Weighed (stacked Marks), Ring of Spears and Glare''s Seven Horses shape (lots of Marked enemies at once), Brimming, heavy weapons.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Witnessed should count the enemy''s blows on you, at 30 per blow, plus 15 more for each one you perfectly guarded. For now, the column is worth one blow. Spoken Sentence should do 12 more for each Mark stack; for now, it is 32 no matter how many stacks. Both shapes still keep the plain seal''s count alongside their own, because a shape can''t turn off its spell''s own rule yet.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Spoken Sentence on a captain with three Marked soldiers around it. One heavy blow, and four blades fall.'
  - Id: light.reckoning.master
    Title: 'Reckoning: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A finisher on the sealed enemy pays out the count twice.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While the seal is on its enemy, during the six seconds after you cast it, while Reckoning is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, the first heavy blow on the sealed enemy hits it again for 40 damage and 40 stagger, once per seal.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy weapons, Condemned (the full Mark''s stagger sets up the finisher), Ring of Spears (holds it still to be finished).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need a finisher, not just any heavy blow, and pay out the count a second time. For now, any heavy blow works, for a flat 40.


      <b>Example.</b> Stagger the sealed brute with a full Mark and finish it. The seal pays out right away, and again when it breaks.'
  - Id: light.spears
    Title: Ring of Spears
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Ring of Spears, the breaker. Six spears of gold hang in the air in a ring around the enemy you face. They wait a moment, then drive in together, Marking the enemy and holding it still.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, they close on a spot in front of you, and you still have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 40 stagger in total.) It finds an enemy within 10 metres. The ring has a 3-metre radius and closes after 0.6 seconds. Each spear deals 6 damage, 5 stagger and a Mark: 36 damage and 30 stagger in total. The enemy it hits is held still for 1.5 seconds (a god or an elite for 0.4 of that).


      <b>Works with.</b> Weighed (each spear adds a Mark), Reckoning (holds the enemy still while the seal counts), Condemned, Spoken Sentence, and the charged blade, which it charges with Light.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it on a brute. Land your three critical hits while it is held, then a heavy blow while it is still Marked.'
  - Id: light.spears.shape
    Title: 'Ring of Spears: The Drawn Line or In Turn'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Ring of Spears. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>The Drawn Line</b> (the line drawn around Sita''s door that no one meaning harm could cross): the six spears drive into the floor in a row across the space in front of you and stay standing. An enemy that crosses it is held and Marked, and your blows across the line are critical hits. Fight across it. <b>In Turn</b>: the spears drive in one after another. A spear that lands at the same moment as one of your weapon blows hits for double and is a critical hit. Six beats to match.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Ring of Spears, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain ring.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The Drawn Line: for now, the spears still drive in, then stand in the floor for 5 seconds in their ring 3 metres around the enemy. There are six spears, each 0.7 metres across. Once a second, they Mark and jolt whatever stands at them for 12 stagger. In Turn: six blades fall on the enemy one after another, each for 6 damage, 5 stagger and a Mark. For a second and a half after that, each weapon blow on it hits for one more spear''s worth, 6 damage and 5 stagger, at most four times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Drawn Line: a doorway or a staircase, ranged weapons and spears that reach across it, the root mark (Marked enemies take critical hits). In Turn: fast weapons, Just Cut, the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Drawn Line should be a straight line six metres across in front of you that holds anything crossing it for a second, without the spears driving into the enemy. In Turn should have a quarter second between spears (for now, they fall quickly one after another), and should check for a spear landing within a tenth of a second of your blow.


      <b>Example.</b> Put The Drawn Line across a hallway. The pack comes on, each one is Marked as it reaches the spears, and every cut you make across them is a critical hit.'
  - Id: light.spears.master
    Title: 'Ring of Spears: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land a critical hit on the enemy while the spears hang over it, and they all drive in at once as critical hits, and Ring of Spears is ready again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> During the 0.6 seconds the spears hang over the enemy you face, while Ring of Spears is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, the first weapon blow on that enemy in those 0.6 seconds hits within 1.5 metres of it for 18 damage, 15 stagger and a Mark, and the wait ends right away.


      <b>Works with.</b> A Marked enemy (its blows are critical hits), Just Cut, Unblemished, and fast weapons that can land a blow in that moment.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only critical hits should count, and the spears themselves should drive in at once. For now, any weapon blow counts, and it makes a burst worth 50% more than the spears.


      <b>Example.</b> Mark a duellist with a chain, cast, and cut it before the spears fall. They hit right away, and the ring is ready for the next one.'
  - Id: light.condemned
    Title: Condemned
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When an affliction on an enemy fills all the way up, its balance takes a big hit. The cost: your weapon does less damage to an enemy with no afflictions, though blows and spells on any afflicted enemy hit a little harder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The hit to balance comes each time any affliction you add fills up on an enemy, including a third Mark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A burst 1.2 metres around the enemy deals no damage and 60 stagger, at most once every 4 seconds. Your weapon deals 15% less damage, but damage on an afflicted enemy is 15% higher, so against those the two almost cancel out.


      <b>Works with.</b> Weighed, which it comes after on the chart, Ring of Spears, Glare and Brimming to fill up Marks, Reckoning (the column''s stagger together with it), and a second element''s affliction, which fills up on its own.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> An enemy with three Marks and under a quarter of its health (a tenth for a god or an elite) should be finished off no matter how much balance it has. For now, the full Mark breaks much of its balance.


      <b>Example.</b> Bring a brute to three Marks. The burst nearly staggers it, and a heavy blow finishes the job.'
  - Id: light.justcut
    Title: Just Cut
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A blow that lands may flash. For a moment after a flash, every blow you land is a critical hit. A blow that lands may also give you a little stamina back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Any weapon blow that lands, one time in four, at most once every 1.5 seconds. The flash and the stamina each have their own separate chance.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One chance in four of a flash: every blow is a critical hit for 0.8 seconds. One chance in four of getting 6 stamina back. Each can happen at most once every 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Weighed (stronger critical hits), Step Into Light and Standing Light, which come after it on the chart, Ring of Spears'' Master mark, and fast weapons like the Twin Knives, which fit more blows into the moment.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The flash should come when you press the next blow of a chain at the exact moment the last one lands, making it a critical hit that costs no stamina. For now, it comes by chance.


      <b>Example.</b> In a flurry of blows on a duellist, one cut flashes, and the two after it are critical hits without any Mark.'
  - Id: light.step
    Title: Step Into Light
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Step Into Light, the movement spell. You step through the enemy you face on a line of gold dust and come out on its far side, turned back toward it, cutting and Marking everything on the line. If the cut lands, your next heavy blow right after is a critical hit. Nothing can hit you during the step. A wall, or an enemy too big to push past, stops you short.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, you step forward anyway, and you still have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds and the reach 9 metres.) It goes up to 7 metres: to 1.8 metres past the enemy you face, or about 5 metres ahead if there is none. The cut deals 10 damage, 10 stagger and a Mark to everything on the line. The heavy blow after it is a critical hit if it lands within 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark (the Mark it adds makes the heavy blow and two more critical hits), Just Cut, Weighed, and the charged blade: the cut is a spell, so it charges your weapon with Light for the next three moves.


      <b>Example.</b> Step through an archer behind its shield bearer, turn around, and land a critical heavy blow on its back.'
  - Id: light.step.shape
    Title: 'Step Into Light: Aruna''s Road or Behind the Eye'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Step Into Light. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Aruna''s Road</b> (named for the driver of the sun''s chariot at dawn): the line you stepped along stays for a while as a road of gold dust that Marks whatever crosses it. Pressing again steps you back along it to where you started. Out and back. <b>Behind the Eye</b>: you step to behind the enemy, landing at its back, and your first blow after that deals a finisher''s damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Step Into Light, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain step.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Aruna''s Road: five patches along 7 metres in front of where you cast it, each 0.7 metres across, Mark whatever stands in them once a second for 3 seconds. Behind the Eye: if the cut lands, your next weapon blow within a second deals three times its damage.


      <b>Works with.</b> Aruna''s Road: a pack that follows you, the root mark, Weighed, Spoken Sentence. Behind the Eye: heavy weapons, the Learn mark''s critical heavy blow (the two multiply together), Unblemished.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Aruna''s Road''s step back along the road, with a second press within the three seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Behind the Eye on a captain. Step, turn, and one heavy blow lands as a critical hit, three times as hard.'
  - Id: light.step.master
    Title: 'Step Into Light: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it through two enemies standing in a line. Both are cut and Marked, and Step Into Light is ready again. Light goes straight.


      <b>When it happens.</b> As the step cuts, while Step Into Light is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, a step that cuts anything ends its own wait, once every 16 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hallways and staircases, a line of spearmen, Aruna''s Road (a second road right away).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need one step to cut two enemies. For now, any step that cuts works, at most once every 16 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Two soldiers come up a staircase one behind the other. Step through both, then step again right away.'
  - Id: light.lantern
    Title: Standing Light
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Standing Light, the counter. You plant a brass lamp a little in front of you. A beam of light shines over it, with dust swirling in it, and a glow surrounds it while it stands. Enemies in the glow are hit lightly and Marked again and again, and a perfect guard bursts around you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then guard.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It is planted 2.5 metres in front of you. The glow has a 5-metre radius and lasts 10 seconds. It hits 0.2 seconds after you plant it, then every 2.5 seconds, for 2 damage and a Mark each time. Each lamp gives three perfect-guard bursts, shown next to the weapon as Lamp. Each burst deals 10 damage and 25 stagger within 3.5 metres of you.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark (a fresh Mark, and three fresh critical hits, every 2.5 seconds), Unblemished, which comes after it on the chart, Clarity''s longer perfect timing windows, Reckoning''s Witnessed shape, Condemned.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The burst should only come from a perfect guard made inside the glow. For now, your next three perfect guards after each lamp burst, wherever you stand. The glow doesn''t clear away enemy clouds, flames or frost yet. The Empower''s longer perfect guard and dodge windows inside the glow; for now, the Empower does nothing.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it in a doorway and fight at its edge. Everything that comes through arrives Marked, and each perfect guard throws them back.'
  - Id: light.lantern.shape
    Title: 'Standing Light: Row of Lamps or Circled Lamp'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Standing Light. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Row of Lamps</b> (the rows of lamps at Diwali): three small lamps are set in a line. Every enemy between two of them is Marked and kept clear of clouds, flames and frost. A perfect guard made in line with them shoots a ray down the row. Your guard fires the row. <b>Circled Lamp</b> (the lamp waved in a circle before the god): a smaller lamp circles you, Marking what it passes and burning away any danger zone you walk into. It goes where you go.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Standing Light, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you plant the plain lamp.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Row of Lamps: the lamp still stands, and three patches, each a metre across, along 6 metres in front of you Mark whatever stands in them once a second for 10 seconds. Each perfect guard shoots a ray 6 metres past the attacker, for 12 damage, 25 stagger and a Mark, at most once a second. Circled Lamp: one lamp circles you 2 metres away for 8 seconds, hitting whatever it passes for 2 damage and Marking it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Row of Lamps: a build that counters attacks, Unblemished, perfect guards. Circled Lamp: a fight on the move, fast weapons, Brimming.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Row of Lamps should only affect enemies between two lamps, only fire on a guard made in line with them, and show the three lamps, with nothing placed between them. Circled Lamp should burn away danger zones, and look like a lamp (for now, it is a disc of light).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Row of Lamps across a hall. Stand at one end and perfect-guard the brute that comes down it, and the ray runs back through the pack behind it.'
  - Id: light.lantern.master
    Title: 'Standing Light: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The first perfect guard inside the glow makes every Light spell ready again, once per lamp.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard, while Standing Light is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The waits on Glare, Step Into Light, Ring of Spears, Standing Light and Reckoning end right away, for any that are in a slot. Once every 12 seconds. Daybreak doesn''t have a wait, so nothing changes for it.


      <b>Works with.</b> A Hand full of Light spells, Unblemished, Just Cut, Clarity''s longer perfect timing windows.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work on a perfect guard made inside the glow. For now, any perfect guard works, once every 12 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast everything, plant the lamp, perfect-guard one blow, and cast everything again.'
  - Id: light.unblemished
    Title: Unblemished
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fight for a while without getting hurt, and every weapon blow you land is a critical hit and gives more mana. A blow that lands on you ends it, and until you go a while without getting hurt again, you take more damage from every blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. You count as unhurt once no blow has landed on you for 8 seconds. The bonus lasts until the next blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. While unhurt: every weapon blow is a critical hit, and blows give 25% more mana. After you get hurt, for 8 seconds or longer, you take 15% more damage. A blocked blow, a perfect guard or a perfect dodge doesn''t count as getting hurt.


      <b>Works with.</b> Standing Light, which it comes after on the chart, Weighed (critical hits 30% harder), Clarity''s longer perfect timing windows, Just Cut. Every blow you block keeps you unhurt.


      <b>Example.</b> Guard and dodge a duellist''s whole string of blows without a scratch, and every answer you give it lands as a critical hit.'
  - Id: light.brimming
    Title: Brimming
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your mana is full, every heavy blow sends a wave of light ahead of it that hits and Marks everything in front of you. Mana you save for the big spells is useful while it waits.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each heavy blow or finisher that lands while your mana bar is full.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A cone 5 metres long and 70 degrees wide. It deals 12 damage, 12 stagger and a Mark.


      <b>Works with.</b> Daybreak (the mana it saves up for), Seen Plainly, which comes after it on the chart, Weighed, Condemned, Spoken Sentence, and perfect guards to fill the bar. Spending mana on Daybreak, the surge or a deep strike stops it until the bar is full again.


      <b>Example.</b> You have full mana in front of a pack. Every heavy blow Marks the three enemies in front of you.'
  - Id: light.base
    Title: Glare
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Glare, Light''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A narrow ray shoots from your hand in the direction you face, lighting up the dust along it, and hits and Marks whatever it crosses. The enemies it Marks show gold brackets.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Light, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Light. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 6 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 5 seconds. The ray is 5.5 metres long and about 1.4 metres wide. It deals 8 damage, 22 stagger and a Mark to each enemy on it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; the root mark, Weighed, Condemned, Spoken Sentence (Marked enemies for the blades), and the charged blade, which its ray charges with Light.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s 12 damage and 7-metre reach. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Glare the soldier in front of the captain. It is Marked and staggering, and your next three cuts on it are critical hits.'
  - Id: light.base.shape
    Title: 'Glare: Gathered or Seven Horses'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Glare. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Gathered</b>: hold the button, and the ray narrows, with the dust in it drawn to a point on the enemy. Let go for a ray that breaks balance much harder. It works like a magnifying glass, for breaking one enemy. <b>Seven Horses</b> (the sun god Surya''s seven horses): the ray splits into seven thin rays in a fan. Each hits lightly, and every enemy touched is Marked. It''s for Marking a crowd.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Glare, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain ray.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Gathered: for now, every ray hits as if fully held, adding 38 more stagger on each enemy it crosses (60 in total). Seven Horses: a fan 70 degrees wide and 6 metres long. It deals 5 damage, 6 stagger and a Mark to every enemy in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Gathered: Condemned, Reckoning, and a heavy weapon to finish what it breaks. Seven Horses: Spoken Sentence, Weighed, Brimming, a pack.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Gathered''s hold, for up to a second, with the ray narrowing and the stagger rising as you hold. For now, you get the full stagger right away. Seven Horses'' seven thin rays; for now, it is one cone as wide as the fan. If you buy the Empower, Seven Horses uses its numbers too: 12 damage and 7 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Seven Horses on a pack of five. All five are Marked at once, and a Spoken Sentence drops five blades.'
  - Id: light.base.master
    Title: 'Glare: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it at the moment of a perfect dodge, and the attacker is blinded and can''t turn around for two seconds (one second for a god or an elite). Dazzle, which used to be its own mark, lives on here.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect dodge, while Glare is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, a perfect dodge holds the enemy you face, and every enemy within 2 metres of it, still for 2 seconds (a god or an elite for 0.4 of that), and Marks them, once every 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clarity (perfect timing windows 50% longer), Step Into Light, Unblemished, and the root mark''s three critical hits.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need you to cast Glare during the perfect dodge''s window, and the blinding should only stop the enemy from turning. For now, the dodge alone holds them completely still, whether you cast Glare or not.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll through a brute''s swing at the last moment. It stands still and Marked while you turn and land three critical hits.'
  - Id: light.lift
    Title: Seen Plainly
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Seen Plainly, Light''s reaction lift. Every reaction that Marked is part of is a critical hit, and Marks everything it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a reaction with Marked in it happens, on any enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One Mark on every enemy within 3 metres of where it happened.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every element pair with Marked in it (Light''s reactions with its neighbours and the rest), the root mark (each Mark gives three critical hits), Weighed, and Daybreak and Full Daylight, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reaction itself should be a critical hit; for now, only the Marks spread. The Mark should go on what the reaction reached, not on everything within 3 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> A reaction goes off on one Marked soldier in a pack. The two next to it come away Marked, and your next cuts on all of them are critical hits.'
  - Id: light.daybreak
    Title: Daybreak
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Daybreak, Light''s larger spell, paid for with mana. You brace yourself and fire a beam of light down one long line. It hits everything in it four times and Marks it. You can turn it slowly with the stick or the cursor while it burns.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 45 mana. You brace, then fire a beam 12 metres long and 1.6 metres wide for 0.4 seconds. It hits every 0.1 seconds, four times in all, for 12 damage, 9 stagger and a Mark each time. An enemy that stays in it takes 48 damage and 36 stagger. Its Empower makes each hit 15 damage instead of 12 (60 in total). The two shapes keep their own numbers.


      <b>Works with.</b> Brimming (mana kept full for it), Seen Plainly, which it comes after on the chart, Weighed, Condemned, Spoken Sentence, and the charged blade, which it charges with Light.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The rings should build up on the blade from the blows you land after starting it, up to eight, each worth 8 damage and 6 stagger, and fire with a second press. For now, the beam fires right away, as strong as six rings would be (48 damage).


      <b>Example.</b> A line of spearmen comes up a staircase. One beam down the stairs Marks them all and leaves them staggering.'
  - Id: light.daybreak.shape
    Title: 'Daybreak: Red Before the Sun or High Noon'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Daybreak. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Red Before the Sun</b> (Aruna riding in front of the sun god Surya to shield the world from his heat): the rings stand in front of you as a curved shield of red dawn that stops blows from the front, and then fire as the beam. It is Light''s only shield, and still a beam at the end. <b>High Noon</b>: the light comes straight down on you as a column, burning away every enemy danger zone inside it, clearing every affliction on you, and Marking everything it hits. This is the cleansing version.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Daybreak, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain beam.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards, and the same 45 mana. Red Before the Sun: a wall 3 metres wide stands 1.5 metres in front of you for 4 seconds. Nothing can walk through it or reach you from the other side. When its time is up, or when your heavy blow hits it, it bursts along its width for 48 damage, 36 stagger and a Mark. High Noon: a column 5 metres across lands on you a moment after you cast it, dealing 36 damage, 36 stagger and a Mark to everything inside.


      <b>Works with.</b> Red Before the Sun: archers and charges coming at you, a build that counters attacks, Unblemished. High Noon: being surrounded, Weighed, Spoken Sentence.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Red Before the Sun should add rings for each blow it stops, and fire the beam with a second press; for now, it bursts where it stands. It should also stop thrown attacks. High Noon should burn away enemy clouds, flames, frost and danger zones, and clear the afflictions on you.


      <b>Example.</b> Use High Noon in the middle of a pack. Everything around you is Marked at once, and the cuts that follow are critical hits.'
  - Id: light.daybreak.master
    Title: 'Daybreak: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Fire it within a fifth of a second of a blow landing on you, and the blow is blocked and the beam is a critical hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you cast it, while Daybreak is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, every cast gives you a ward that blocks the next blow that would land on you. A beam fired in the half second after a perfect guard hits 50% harder (spells are 50% stronger for that half second).


      <b>Works with.</b> Standing Light and perfect guards, Unblemished (a blocked blow doesn''t count as getting hurt), Brimming.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should work when a blow lands on you right as you cast it. For now, the ward and the perfect guard bonus take its place.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute''s swing comes in as you fire the beam. It glances off, and the beam runs down the whole pack behind it.'
  - Id: light.devotion
    Title: Full Daylight
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Full Daylight, Light''s Devotion, for going all in on Light only. Blows no longer use up a Mark. Every weapon blow on a Marked enemy is a critical hit until the Mark fades, but after it fades, that enemy can''t be Marked again for a while. The fight gets a rhythm: Mark, use the whole window, then wait for the light to come back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Light twice (all in), once you buy it. It is always on then.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Blows no longer use up a Mark. Every weapon blow on a Marked enemy is a critical hit until the Mark fades, which takes 6 seconds instead of 8. Once it has faded, that enemy can''t be Marked again for 4 seconds. A Mark set off or used up by a spell doesn''t have this wait. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, The Sun on the Blade. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Light alone or twice.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seen Plainly, which it comes after on the chart, the root mark, Just Cut and Unblemished (critical hits even stronger with Weighed), fast weapons, The Scales.


      <b>Example.</b> Glare a captain, then cut it for six seconds. Every one of a dozen blows is a critical hit.'
  - Id: light.scales
    Title: The Scales
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your surge lasts, the damage you deal with your weapon and the damage you take are weighed against each other. If you dealt more when it ends, a sword of light falls on every Marked enemy near you. If not, the light leaves you, and takes your mana with it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light your surge with the surge button (Clarity, or the other element''s surge if you have that one picked). The scales are checked when it ends.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. Each sword deals 60% of how much more damage you dealt than you took, up to 120, plus 80 stagger, as a heavy blow, to each Marked enemy within 12 metres. If you took more damage than you dealt, or the same amount, your mana empties.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clarity (it Marks with every blow and makes perfect timing windows 50% longer, so you take less damage), Unblemished, the root mark, and Standing Light and Brimming, to have enemies Marked when it ends.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Clarity, then guard and cut for eight seconds without getting hit. You dealt 200 and took none, so a sword falls for 120 on every Marked enemy.'
  - Id: light.twosuns
    Title: Two Suns
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Two Suns, a keystone. Every enemy your spell hits is hit again a moment later by a burst of light, and Marked. Your spells have to wait a fifth longer between casts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever one of your spells hits an enemy, while you carry Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. The second burst comes 0.4 seconds later and is 2.5 metres across. It deals 12 damage, 10 stagger and one Mark, at most once every 0.2 seconds. Every spell''s wait is 1.2 times as long.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ring of Spears, which it comes after on the chart (from its Master mark), Reckoning, Weighed and Condemned (more Marks to finish with), and Daybreak''s long line (every enemy on it is hit twice).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> A figure of light should cast the spell again from the far side of the enemy you face. For now, each enemy hit takes a second burst of light where it stands.


      <b>Example.</b> Drive Ring of Spears into a soldier with two others next to it. A moment later the light bursts over it again, and all three are Marked for your next blows.'
  - Id: lightning.static
    Title: Static
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Light blows build up Static on an enemy, one stack at a time, up to five. Static does nothing by itself. A heavy blow or a finisher sets it all off at once, into the enemy you hit and everyone standing near it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. While Lightning is your first element, every light blow that lands adds a stack. If Lightning is your second element, your weapon adds none, and Static comes from your Lightning spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike. Either way, a heavy blow or a finisher on an enemy with Static sets it off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack per light blow, up to five, lasting 6 seconds. Setting it off deals 7 damage and 5 stagger for each stack to everything within 4 metres: 35 damage and 25 stagger at five.


      <b>Works with.</b> Single Stroke, Waiting Thunder, Chain Lance, Seven Flashes, Overdrive''s faster swings, and Ionised Air or Thunder Lift on the mixed branches.


      <b>Example.</b> Four light blows on a soldier, then a heavy blow. 28 damage jumps out of it into the two soldiers next to it as well.'
  - Id: lightning.lift
    Title: Strikes Twice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Strikes Twice. Every reaction with Static in it hits again half a second later where it happened, the way lightning comes back down the same path.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Static reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Flashover with Burn, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Half a second after the reaction, a bolt hits within 2 metres of the enemy, for 10 damage, 10 stagger and a stack of Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Lightning''s ten reactions. Chain Lance and Waiting Thunder, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reaction itself should happen again at half strength. For now, a bolt falls there.


      <b>Example.</b> Burn and Static meet on a soldier. Flashover goes off, and a moment later the bolt follows it down.'
  - Id: lightning.lance
    Title: Chain Lance
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw a bolt forward that jumps through up to five enemies, leaving Static on each one.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 5.5, with six jumps). It reaches 12 metres to the first enemy, then jumps up to 5 metres from one enemy to the next. Each takes 10 damage, 15 stagger and two stacks of Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Strikes Twice, which it comes after on the chart; Static; Waiting Thunder''s heavy blow, which sets off the Static it added.


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight from far away on a pack of five archers. Each one gets two Static before they can draw their bows.'
  - Id: lightning.lance.shape
    Title: 'Chain Lance: Stepped Leader or Return Stroke'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Chain Lance. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Stepped Leader</b>: the lance isn''t thrown. Instead, a bolt steps out along the ground one bright step at a time, adding Static to anything near it. Then the return stroke lights up the whole path at once. <b>Return Stroke</b>: after its last jump, it comes back along the same path, hitting each enemy again for half.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Chain Lance, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Stepped Leader: it goes up to 12 metres, taking a step every 0.15 seconds, with 4 damage and a Static on each step. 1.2 seconds after you cast it, the return stroke hits the whole path for 14 damage, 10 stagger and a Static. Return Stroke: 0.6 seconds later, each enemy it hit takes 5 damage and 5 stagger again.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static and Single Stroke (the return stroke''s Static, then a heavy blow).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Steering Stepped Leader with the stick while you fight, having your heavy blow call the return stroke, and Return Stroke charging the blade for five blows.


      <b>Example.</b> Send Stepped Leader down a hallway of soldiers. They all have Static when the return stroke lights up.'
  - Id: lightning.lance.master
    Title: 'Chain Lance: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Throw Chain Lance within half a second after a finisher, and it jumps to every enemy within twelve metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When a finisher lands. Chain Lance must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, the finisher itself throws the bolt: 10 damage, 10 stagger and two Static, jumping to up to eight enemies within 12 metres, once every 7 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static, Waiting Thunder.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when you throw the lance within half a second of the finisher.


      <b>Example.</b> End a chain of blows on the captain, and every soldier in the yard has Static.'
  - Id: lightning.waiting
    Title: Waiting Thunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Five bolts hang at your shoulder, crackling. Each light blow you land sends one down onto the enemy you hit. A heavy blow sends all the ones that are left. Any not sent when the time runs out fade away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then your blows send the bolts.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 8, with seven bolts). Five bolts, for 10 seconds. Each deals 8 damage, 6 stagger and one stack of Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Strikes Twice, which it comes after on the chart; Static (the bolts build it up as you cut); Single Stroke.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it, land two light blows, then a heavy blow. The last three bolts fall with it, and the heavy blow sets off the Static they added.'
  - Id: lightning.waiting.shape
    Title: 'Waiting Thunder: Hanging Cloud or Hammer Fall'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Waiting Thunder. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Hanging Cloud</b>: the bolts hang in a small black cloud over the enemy you face and fall on it while you fight. <b>Hammer Fall</b>: light blows send nothing. Your heavy blow brings all of it down as one bolt.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Waiting Thunder, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Hanging Cloud: it hangs over the enemy you face, within 8 metres. A bolt falls every 0.9 seconds within 1.5 metres for 10 seconds, each dealing 8 damage, 6 stagger and a Static. Hammer Fall: the enemy you face is marked for 10 seconds, and your next blow on it brings down one bolt within 3 metres, for 40 damage, 60 stagger and two Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static and Single Stroke (Hammer Fall into a heavy blow that sets off the Static).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Hanging Cloud''s bolts should fall as you hit anyone (for now, they fall on their own). Hammer Fall should only wait for a heavy blow.


      <b>Example.</b> Hang the cloud over the archer, and fight the soldier next to you.'
  - Id: lightning.waiting.master
    Title: 'Waiting Thunder: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land the heavy blow within a second of the last bolt falling from a light blow, and all five come back for a second round.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When a heavy blow lands. Waiting Thunder must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, every heavy blow brings five bolts down around the enemy you hit, each dealing 8 damage, 6 stagger and a Static, once every 10 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static, Single Stroke.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work within a second of the last bolt falling from a light blow.


      <b>Example.</b> Light, light, light, light, light, heavy: and the sky goes around again.'
  - Id: lightning.devotion
    Title: In One Breath
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> In One Breath, Lightning''s Devotion, for going all in on Lightning only. After a perfect guard or a perfect dodge, your spells have no wait for a moment. Cast everything you have during that moment. Then all their waits start from the beginning.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Lightning twice (all in), once you buy it. Every perfect guard and perfect dodge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. For 2.5 seconds after the perfect guard or dodge, all four spell slots are ready, no matter how long their waits have left. A spell cast during that moment doesn''t start its wait yet. When the moment ends, its whole wait starts. The waits of spells you didn''t cast keep counting down underneath. The L2 strikes keep their own timers, and spells that cost mana still cost their mana. A perfect guard or dodge during the moment makes it last 2.5 seconds from then. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Down the Channel. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Lightning alone or twice. It costs 20 mana and has its own 8-second wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ring of Drums and Thunderclap''s Master mark, which also work on a perfect guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Wait for the brute''s swing, guard it perfectly, and throw your whole Hand of spells at it.'
  - Id: lightning.charge
    Title: Stored Charge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Perfect guards store up charges, up to three. Each blow you land uses one up for double damage and double stagger.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) stores one. Your next weapon blow that lands uses it up, and the next charge is ready for the blow after that.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Up to three charges, shown on the screen. A charged blow deals twice its damage and twice its stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ring of Drums and Thunderclap''s Master mark on the same perfect guard; a heavy blow that also sets off Static; Single Stroke.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard three blows of a duellist''s string of attacks, then answer with three double-strength blows.'
  - Id: lightning.base
    Title: Thunderclap
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Thunderclap, Lightning''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A bolt falls on the enemy you face and splits off to two more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button. While you carry Lightning, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Lightning. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 7). It reaches up to 10 metres and deals 16 damage and 45 stagger, splitting off to two more enemies. It doesn''t add any Static of its own.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static (a heavy blow right after sets off whatever Static is there); its Master mark on a perfect guard; Stored Charge.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s stronger clap (55 stagger). For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> A shield bearer won''t open up. The clap breaks its balance, and your next blow lands.'
  - Id: lightning.base.shape
    Title: 'Thunderclap: Rolling Thunder or Held Thunder'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Thunderclap. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Rolling Thunder</b>: three claps march out in a line. <b>Held Thunder</b>: one huge clap with no splitting.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Thunderclap, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Rolling Thunder: three claps, 3 metres apart, over 9 metres. Each deals 16 damage, 30 stagger and a Static. Held Thunder: one bolt on the enemy you face within 10 metres, for 20 damage and 90 stagger, with no splitting.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static and Chain Lance (Rolling Thunder down a line); Stored Charge (Held Thunder, then a double-strength blow).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Held Thunder should be held on its button and grow stronger the longer you hold. For now, it is always at full strength.


      <b>Example.</b> Held Thunder on the captain staggers it right away.'
  - Id: lightning.base.master
    Title: 'Thunderclap: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard makes your next Thunderclap instant and ends its wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard. Thunderclap must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Its wait ends right away, and your next cast doesn''t start a new one.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stored Charge and Ring of Drums on the same guard; In One Breath.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cast should also skip its wind-up.


      <b>Example.</b> Guard the thrust perfectly and clap right away: guard, clap, guard, clap.'
  - Id: lightning.drums
    Title: Ring of Drums
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Three drums made of cloud and lightning spin behind your shoulders. Each perfect guard or perfect dodge beats one, and a bolt falls on the attacker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button. Then it works on each perfect guard or perfect dodge until all three drums are beaten.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 8). There are three beats. Each is a bolt within 1.2 metres of the enemy, for 18 damage, 35 stagger and two Static. The screen shows how many drums are left.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stored Charge, which it comes after on the chart; Thunderclap''s Master mark; In One Breath.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ring should last 8 seconds (for now, the drums wait until they are beaten). The third beat should be a huge clap within 4 metres (30 damage, 60 stagger) that sets off all the Static in it.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it before the duellist''s string of attacks. Three perfect guards drop three bolts on it.'
  - Id: lightning.drums.shape
    Title: 'Ring of Drums: Drum Roll or Silent Drum'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Ring of Drums. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Drum Roll</b>: normal guards beat them too, for a small clap. <b>Silent Drum</b>: the beats are saved up, and your next heavy blow carries them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While Ring of Drums is in a spell slot: Drum Roll on every normal guard, Silent Drum on a heavy blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Drum Roll: a clap within 1.5 metres of the attacker, for 8 damage, 15 stagger and a Static, at most once every half second. Silent Drum: a heavy blow hits within 1.2 metres of its target for 18 more damage, 40 stagger and two Static, once every 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stored Charge (Silent Drum on the double-strength heavy blow).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> In Drum Roll, a perfect guard should beat two drums at once. In Silent Drum, nothing should fall on a beat, and the heavy blow should carry exactly the beats you saved.


      <b>Example.</b> Drum Roll is for a player who guards more than they dodge.'
  - Id: lightning.drums.master
    Title: 'Ring of Drums: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Beat all three drums within three seconds, and the ring stays for another round.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now: a perfect dodge beats one extra drum. Ring of Drums must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One more bolt, for 18 damage, 35 stagger and two Static, at most once every 9 seconds.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Beating all three drums within 3 seconds to keep the ring.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge, guard, guard: the ring goes around again.'
  - Id: lightning.stroke
    Title: Single Stroke
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Static builds up to ten. Setting it off no longer jumps to anyone else. All of it lands on the enemy you hit, twice as hard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Static goes up to ten stacks. Setting it off deals 14 damage and 10 stagger for each stack, to the enemy you hit only: 140 damage and 100 stagger at ten.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static, Waiting Thunder, Ring of Drums (which it comes after on the chart), Stored Charge (a charged heavy blow that also sets off Static).


      <b>Example.</b> Against the Gate Captain, keep cutting until it has ten Static, then land one heavy blow for 140.'
  - Id: lightning.blink
    Title: Blink
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each dodge sends a line of lightning along its path that shocks whatever it crosses.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every dodge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The shock has a 1.1-metre radius and travels with you for the length of the dodge. It hits each enemy once for 10 damage and 14 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Afterimage, Lightning Rod, Never There, Storm Step.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll through a gap between two soldiers, and both are shocked as you pass.'
  - Id: lightning.rod
    Title: Lightning Rod
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You plant a sword point-up a little in front of you. For eight seconds, bolts fall again and again inside a circle around it, hitting whatever stands there and adding Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 8, and the circle''s radius 4.5 metres). It is planted 2 metres in front of you; the circle has a 3.5-metre radius. It hits 0.2 seconds after you plant it, then every 0.9 seconds for 8 seconds, for 12 damage, 12 stagger and one stack of Static each time.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blink, which it comes after on the chart; Static (heavy blows on enemies inside set it off).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Pressing again while the rod stands should flash you to it, landing with a shock.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it in a doorway and fight at its edge. Everything that comes through arrives with Static already on it.'
  - Id: lightning.rod.shape
    Title: 'Lightning Rod: Lodestone or Thrown Rod'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Lightning Rod. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Lodestone</b>: the rod pulls in every enemy nearby, farther for each Static they have, then sets all of them off. <b>Thrown Rod</b>: the rod is thrown far, into an enemy far away or into the ground.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Lightning Rod, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Lodestone: everything within 7 metres is pulled in, then a burst deals 14 damage and 30 stagger and sets off all their Static. Thrown Rod: it is planted up to 10 metres away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chain Lance and Waiting Thunder, to add the Static first (Lodestone).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Lodestone should pull toward the rod (for now, it pulls toward you). The flash to the rod, which is what makes Thrown Rod''s reach worth it.


      <b>Example.</b> Lance a pack, then use Lodestone. They come together and go off all at once.'
  - Id: lightning.rod.master
    Title: 'Lightning Rod: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it right as a blow would land on you, and Lightning Rod counts as a perfect dodge, and flashing to the rod doesn''t use it up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When you cast Lightning Rod. Lightning Rod must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, every cast gives you what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (casting it right as a blow would land), and the flash to the rod that would use it up.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant the rod in the brute''s face as it swings.'
  - Id: lightning.afterimage
    Title: Afterimage
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A dodge at the last moment leaves a copy of you behind that strikes where you stood a moment later.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect dodge, which is when a blow passes through you at the very start of your roll.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The strike lands 0.3 seconds later where your dodge started. It has a 2.3-metre radius and deals 26 damage and 28 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blink, and Seven Flashes and Never There, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute swings overhead. Roll through it at the last moment, and the copy left behind hits it as the blow lands.'
  - Id: lightning.flashes
    Title: Seven Flashes
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You vanish and cut from enemy to enemy in one moment, then stand again where you started, with your back to the fight. The cuts all land together once you are standing still, and any enemy left with full Static has it set off. Nothing can hit you while you are gone. If you are hit while standing still afterwards, the cuts land at half strength and no Static is set off.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 45 mana (its Empower makes it 35, with 11 damage per cut). Seven cuts among enemies within 8 metres (against one enemy, seven cuts from all sides), 0.1 seconds apart, then half a second standing still. Each cut deals 9 damage, 8 stagger and one stack of Static. An enemy with full Static has it set off for 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Afterimage, which it comes after on the chart; Static; Never There.


      <b>Example.</b> Against one duellist with three Static: seven cuts fill its Static, and it goes off as you turn around.'
  - Id: lightning.flashes.shape
    Title: 'Seven Flashes: One Flash or Left Behind'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Seven Flashes. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>One Flash</b>: it only hits the enemy you face, setting off its Static again and again. <b>Left Behind</b>: you don''t stand still for as long, and you end up in the middle of the crowd.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Seven Flashes, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One Flash: the cuts only reach 3 metres, and each one that lands on Static sets it off for 4 damage per stack, at most once every half second. Left Behind: you only stand still for a tenth of a second afterwards.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static, Single Stroke (One Flash on the enemy with ten Static).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> One Flash''s seven cuts should be along one line as one blow. Left Behind should end behind the last enemy and leave a shape of light behind to be hit.


      <b>Example.</b> Use One Flash on the duellist with five Static.'
  - Id: lightning.flashes.master
    Title: 'Seven Flashes: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it within a third of a second of a perfect dodge, and you don''t have to stand still afterwards: the cuts land right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Not yet: see below.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> All of it. It needs a timing window for casting after a perfect dodge, and a version of Seven Flashes without the standing still. Buying it now does nothing.'
  - Id: lightning.neverthere
    Title: Never There
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every dodge carries you farther, and a perfect dodge costs no stamina. The cost: blocking a blow with your guard costs twice the stamina.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The free dodge comes with each perfect dodge. The cost comes with each blow you block.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Dodges go 30% farther. A perfect dodge gives back 25 stamina, which is what a dodge normally costs. A blocked blow costs twice the stamina for each point of damage blocked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blink, Afterimage (which it comes after on the chart), Seven Flashes, Lightning Rod.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight a fast duellist by dodging every swing at the last moment and never raising your guard.'
  - Id: lightning.stormstep
    Title: Storm Step
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While Overdrive lasts, every dodge costs no stamina and shocks whatever it passes through, whether you learned Blink or not. A shape of light is left behind where you stood.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every dodge while Overdrive is running. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. Each dodge gives back all the stamina it cost. The shock is the same as Blink''s: 10 damage and 14 stagger, with a 1.1-metre radius along the dodge.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blink, Afterimage, Never There.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Overdrive in a crowd and roll back and forth through them. You spend no stamina, and every pass shocks them.'
  - Id: lightningflower.shape
    Title: 'Lightning Flower: The Rice Wakes or Struck Oak'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Field or Oak unlocks two new shapes for Lightning Flower. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Lightning Flower uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>The Rice Wakes</b>: the fern of lightning throws its bolt like before, and a field of green shoots springs up around the enemy, charging whatever stands in it. <b>Struck Oak</b>: no fern. Instead, the electric current boils the seed inside the enemy''s body, and it bursts like an oak tree hit by lightning. It hits harder for every Static and Seed the enemy had.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Lightning Flower goes off while you carry both Lightning and Life. Lightning Flower goes off when Static lands on a Seeded enemy, or when a Seed lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Lightning Flower.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The Rice Wakes: like the plain one, the fern throws a bolt to the nearest other Seeded enemy within 8 metres, for 8 damage, 10 stagger and a Static. The shoots cover a 3-metre radius around the enemy for 6 seconds. They count as something you grew (up to two fields), and they add a Static once a second to each enemy in them. Struck Oak: a burst within 3 metres deals 16 damage, plus 5 more for each Static and each Seed the enemy had, and 20 stagger. Its Static and Seeds are used up. After Lightning Flower goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Storm Season to set it up, The Favoured Oak, Rain on the Field, Sowing Thunder.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack in a garden you grew: use The Rice Wakes, and the field keeps charging them. One brute with lots of seeds: use Struck Oak.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Rice Wakes'' shoots don''t heal you 2 a second yet while you stand on them. The things you grew nearby don''t throw their own bolts into the flower yet.'
  - Id: lightningflower.stormseason
    Title: Storm Season
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add Static and a Seed at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Lightning and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Static and one Seed.


      <b>Works with.</b> Lightning Flower (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), The Favoured Oak.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow on each soldier in turn, and each one flowers.'
  - Id: lightningflower.favouredoak
    Title: The Favoured Oak
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Seeded enemy hangs a scorched oak leaf on your blade. Your next heavy blow sets off the enemy''s Static at twice the strength.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard against a Seeded enemy, while you carry both Lightning and Life. Then your next heavy blow on that enemy within 8 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The heavy blow uses up all the enemy''s Static in a burst within 4 metres, dealing 14 damage and 10 stagger per stack: twice a normal burst. At most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Storm Season (every heavy blow Seeds and charges), Lightning Flower.


      <b>Example.</b> Block a seeded brute''s swing perfectly, then hit it with a heavy blow for a double-strength burst.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The leaf stays on the enemy you guarded instead of on your blade, so only a heavy blow on that enemy sets off the double burst.'
  - Id: lightningflower.rain
    Title: Rain on the Field
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each Lightning Flower heals you 3 for every bolt thrown into it, and every thing you grew that throws one stays 3 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Lightning Flower goes off, while you carry both Lightning and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. 3 health for each Lightning Flower.


      <b>Works with.</b> Lightning Flower and its shapes, Storm Season, Sowing Thunder.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight a seeded pack among your briars, and every flower heals you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The things you grow don''t throw bolts into the flower yet, so it only heals 3 for the fern''s own bolt, and nothing stays longer.'
  - Id: lightningflower.sowing
    Title: Sowing Thunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw seven seeds in a fan 6 metres ahead, like a farmer planting a field. Each one that hits an enemy Seeds it. For 6 seconds, your heavy blow on a seeded enemy calls lightning down onto its seed, dealing 12 damage and 20 stagger around it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Lightning and Life. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It is a cone 6 metres long and 70 degrees wide. Each enemy in it takes 4 damage, 6 stagger, a Static and a Seed, so each one flowers. For 6 seconds, your first heavy blow on each one calls down a bolt, for 12 damage, 20 stagger and a Static within 1.5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Lightning Flower (every enemy it hits gets both afflictions), Rain on the Field, Storm Season.


      <b>Example.</b> Sow seeds across a line of soldiers, then go down the line with heavy blows.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Seeds that miss don''t stay in the floor yet. One heavy blow or burst of Static doesn''t yet call lightning onto every seed at once. A finisher doesn''t make each bolt hit twice yet.'
  - Id: liveiron.shape
    Title: 'Live Iron: Drawn Iron or Forged Bolt'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Open Plates unlocks two new shapes for Live Iron. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Live Iron uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Drawn Iron</b>: the arcs of lightning turn magnetic, and each enemy they reach is pulled toward the enemy you hit, so a crowd folds in onto your blade. <b>Forged Bolt</b>: no arcs. Instead, the enemy''s charge is hammered into spikes, and your heavy blow tears them out as one thunderbolt thrown in a line.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Live Iron goes off while you carry both Metal and Lightning. Live Iron goes off when Static lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Live Iron.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Drawn Iron: for 6 seconds, each weapon blow on the enemy arcs to the nearest notched enemy within 8 metres for 35% of the blow''s damage and notches it, up to two jumps. It also pulls everything within 8 metres 1.5 metres toward it. At most once every 0.3 seconds. Forged Bolt: for 6 seconds, the enemy''s Static doesn''t fade and blows don''t use it up. Your first heavy blow on it takes up to three Static and throws a bolt 10 metres past it, away from you. Everything on the line takes 12 damage for each Static taken, 20 stagger and two Static. After Live Iron goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Three Smiths, Bare Wire, Iron Calls the Storm, Lightning Seeks Iron.


      <b>Example.</b> A ring of armoured soldiers: use Drawn Iron, and fold them in onto one. A line of them: use Forged Bolt, charge the first one, and send it down the line with a heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Drawn Iron pulls every enemy within 8 metres, not only the ones the arcs reach. Forged Bolt''s spikes don''t show up yet, one for each blow. For now, the heavy blow tears out up to three of the Static the enemy has.'
  - Id: liveiron.threesmiths
    Title: Three Smiths
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every third weapon blow on an enemy with Live Iron is answered from the sky: a bolt that deals 12 damage, 15 stagger and a Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Weapon blows on an enemy that is both notched and charged, while you carry both Metal and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Every third blow on the enemy calls a bolt down on it 0.3 seconds later. Each enemy keeps its own count. The bolt deals 12 damage, 15 stagger and a Static within 1 metre, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Live Iron and its shapes, Bare Wire, Lightning Seeks Iron.


      <b>Example.</b> Keep hitting a notched, charged brute, and the sky joins in.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it counts blows on any enemy that is both notched and charged, not only on an enemy with Live Iron.'
  - Id: liveiron.barewire
    Title: Bare Wire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Live Iron''s arcs carry 45% of the blow''s damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Live Iron''s arcs jump.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Each arc carries 45% of the blow that set it off, instead of 35%. It''s the same with Drawn Iron.


      <b>Works with.</b> Live Iron and Drawn Iron, Three Smiths.


      <b>Example.</b> The wire bites almost as hard as the blade.'
  - Id: liveiron.callsstorm
    Title: Iron Calls the Storm
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every blade you throw (Flung Blade and the rest) turns in the air toward the nearest enemy with Static, and lands with two more Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, each time one of your spells hits a charged enemy, while you carry both Metal and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Two more Static, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Live Iron (more charge, longer wires), Three Smiths, Metal''s Flung Blade.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw your blades into the charged pack, and the storm grows stronger.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Blades don''t turn in the air toward the nearest charged enemy yet. For now, any spell that hits a charged enemy adds the two Static, not only thrown blades.'
  - Id: liveiron.seeksiron
    Title: Lightning Seeks Iron
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You turn into a bolt of lightning. In a quarter of a second, while nothing can touch you, you travel to the farthest notched enemy within 10 metres. Every enemy on the line is cut for 10 damage, 15 stagger and two Static. When you arrive, you deal 16 damage and two Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Metal and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The line is 10 metres long. Each enemy on it takes 10 damage, 15 stagger, a notch and two Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Live Iron (each enemy cut gets both afflictions), Three Smiths, Iron Calls the Storm.


      <b>Example.</b> Shoot through a line of soldiers as a bolt, and every one of them is live.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it goes toward the enemy you face, not the farthest notched one, and it doesn''t hit with its own blow when you arrive. A perfect guard in the 2 seconds before you cast it doesn''t yet send you on to a second notched enemy.'
  - Id: loganstone.shape
    Title: 'Logan Stone: Sandblast or Drifted Over'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Grit or Drift unlocks two new shapes for Logan Stone. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Logan Stone uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still make the enemy rock back and forth like before, and your third blow knocks it down. <b>Sandblast</b>: the dust from the fall doesn''t settle. It blows on, away from you, as stinging grit, which may set the next enemy rocking. <b>Drifted Over</b>: the wind piles sand over the enemy where it falls. It stays down longer, and enemies near it have to wade through the sand.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Logan Stone goes off while you carry both Wind and Earth. Logan Stone goes off when Unsteady lands on a shaken enemy, or when Tremor lands on an Unsteady one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Logan Stone.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: the enemy rocks for 3 seconds. Your first weapon blow breaks its balance 1.25 times as fast, the next 1.5 times, and the third knocks it flat. Its fall deals 20 damage, 30 stagger and a Tremor within 3 metres. An elite only staggers, taking 40 stagger. Sandblast: the enemy is down for 1.8 seconds, and the grit hits in three bursts in a line past it, away from you, for 6 damage, 15 stagger and an Unsteady. This is on purpose: the Unsteady may start a new Logan Stone. Drifted Over: the enemy is down for 2.8 seconds, and every enemy within 2 metres of it moves at 60% speed for those 2.8 seconds. After Logan Stone goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grit in the Wind to set it up, New Face, The Prophecy Broken, Walking Dune.


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soldiers: use Sandblast, and knock them down in a row. A pack around you: use Drifted Over, and finish the buried one while the rest wade through the sand.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Sandblast''s grit comes as three bursts in a line, instead of a cone 5 metres long and 60 degrees wide. Drifted Over slows enemies who are near when it falls, but not enemies who wade in later.'
  - Id: loganstone.grit
    Title: Grit in the Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your light blows on a shaken enemy make it Unsteady. Your heavy blows on an Unsteady enemy shake it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every light blow on an enemy that has Tremor, and every heavy blow or finisher on one that has Unsteady, while you carry both Wind and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Unsteady, or one Tremor.


      <b>Works with.</b> Logan Stone (mixing light and heavy blows adds both), New Face.


      <b>Example.</b> Shake the enemy with a heavy blow, then a light blow sets it rocking.'
  - Id: loganstone.newface
    Title: New Face
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your first blow on an enemy that has been knocked down is a critical hit and gives you 6 mana back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> After Logan Stone knocks an enemy down, while you carry both Wind and Earth: your first weapon blow on it while it is on the ground.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The blow is a critical hit and gives you 6 mana back, if it lands within 1.8 seconds of the fall. Elites, which only stagger, give nothing.


      <b>Works with.</b> Logan Stone and its shapes, Grit in the Wind.


      <b>Example.</b> The third blow knocks it down. Hit it on the ground for a critical hit and the mana for your next spell.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it counts the blows itself from each Logan Stone instead of checking for the fall. So it stays open for 1.8 seconds after the third blow, even when Drifted Over keeps the enemy buried longer.'
  - Id: loganstone.prophecy
    Title: The Prophecy Broken
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Elites and gods can be knocked down. After three Logan Stones have only made one stagger in a fight, the fourth knocks it flat for 1 second.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each Logan Stone on an elite, while you carry both Wind and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Every fourth Logan Stone on an elite knocks it flat for 1 second. Each elite keeps its own count.


      <b>Works with.</b> Logan Stone, Grit in the Wind, Walking Dune.


      <b>Example.</b> Keep an elite rocking, and every fourth stone puts it down.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> No god is knocked flat yet.'
  - Id: loganstone.dune
    Title: Walking Dune
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A ridge of sand piles up in front of you, and the wind drives it 7 metres forward. Each enemy it rolls over takes 16 damage, 35 stagger, two Unsteady and a Tremor, so it rocks as the dune passes. It has to wade through sand for 4 seconds after.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Wind and Earth. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It is a cone 7 metres long and 45 degrees wide. Each enemy in it takes 16 damage, 35 stagger, two Unsteady and a Tremor, then moves at 60% speed for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Logan Stone (everything it hits rocks), New Face, The Prophecy Broken.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll the dune over a pack, then knock them down one by one as they rock.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ridge doesn''t roll forward over them yet and stay as a dune where it stops. A heavy blow into it doesn''t drive it on another 5 metres yet.'
  - Id: metal.sunder
    Title: Sunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows and perfect guards put notches in an enemy''s armour, and a finisher puts in three at once. Each notch is a stack of Sundered, and each one lets your weapon cut deeper.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every heavy blow that lands, every perfect guard (on the enemy that hit you), and every finisher. Light blows don''t notch. Like every root mark, your weapon only adds it while Metal is your first element (or your only one). The perfect guard''s notch works either way. If Metal is your second element, Metal''s notches come from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One notch for each heavy blow or perfect guard, three for a finisher, up to three. Sundered lasts 8 seconds. Each notch adds 8% to your weapon''s damage on that enemy, so 24% at three. It doesn''t make spells stronger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Counter Cut and critical hits (they multiply each other), Poured Bronze (it uses up the notches for bronze), Every Crack Rings, Temper (one more notch with every blow), Flung Blade, and Live Iron when Static meets the notches.


      <b>Example.</b> Two heavy blows on a shield bearer give its armour two notches. Every cut after that does 16% more damage for the next 8 seconds.'
  - Id: metal.countercut
    Title: Counter Cut
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A blow that lands while an enemy is in the middle of its own attack, winding up or swinging, is a critical hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: any weapon blow that lands on an enemy in the middle of its attack, or while it is grabbing you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A critical hit deals one and a half times its damage. The planned extra stagger isn''t in yet.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder (a critical hit and Sundered multiply each other), Poured Bronze, Grave of Swords and Master''s Answer, which come after it on the chart, and Halo of Blades on the Halo branch (each critical hit makes a blade).


      <b>Example.</b> A brute raises its hammer. Step in and land a heavy blow before it comes down, for one and a half times the damage.'
  - Id: metal.bronze
    Title: Poured Bronze
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Poured Bronze, the detonator of the Metal tree. You raise a hand, and over every notched enemy near you, a pot of melted bronze tips over in the air and pours into its cracks. The more notches it had, the harder it is hit. The notches are used up, and the bronze hardens: the enemy is held under a crust that cools from cherry red to black.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Bronze only pours into enemies that have Sundered.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 15 damage for each notch.) It reaches every enemy within 8 metres, 0.4 seconds after you cast it. Each notched enemy takes 10 damage, plus 12 damage and 15 stagger for each notch it had (46 damage and 45 stagger at three). Its notches are used up, and it is held for 0.4 seconds for each notch it had (1.2 seconds at three; a god or an elite for 0.4 of that).


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder, Open Line and Forged Answer (they notch on a perfect guard), Every Crack Rings, the charged blade (its charged blows notch), and Grave of Swords and Returning Edge, which notch a crowd for it to pour into.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> A god or an elite should be held for a quarter of the time. For now, it is held for 0.4 of it. The pot of bronze tipping over each enemy and the crust cooling on it aren''t shown yet.


      <b>Example.</b> Give three soldiers three notches each with heavy blows and Grave of Swords, then pour. Each takes 46 damage and is held while you choose which to finish.'
  - Id: metal.bronze.shape
    Title: 'Poured Bronze: Set or Red-Hot'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Poured Bronze. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Set</b>: the crust stays. A heavy blow on a crusted enemy shatters it, throwing bronze splinters out behind it that notch whatever they hit. Pour, then break. <b>Red-Hot</b> (Talos, the bronze giant, heating himself up): the bronze stays melted in the cracks. There is no hold, but every weapon blow on that enemy spits sparks and deals more damage for each notch that was used up. Pour, then keep attacking.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Poured Bronze, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain pour.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Set: the crust stays for 4 seconds. The next heavy blow on it shatters it for 20 damage and 15 stagger within 1 metre, and sends a path of splinters 3 metres out behind it, away from you. Each enemy the splinters reach takes 8 damage, 8 stagger and a notch. Red-Hot: for 4 seconds, every weapon blow on the enemy spits sparks for 12 more damage (worth two notches).


      <b>Works with.</b> Set: heavy weapons, Counter Cut, a pack standing in a line behind the enemy you poured into. Red-Hot: fast weapons, the charged blade, Open Line (a held enemy to keep attacking).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Both shapes work on every enemy the pour reached, not only the ones it poured into, because the shape checks after the pour has already used up the notches. Red-Hot should do 6 more for each notch used up (for now, it is 12 no matter how many), and it shouldn''t hold the enemy (for now, the hold still happens).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Set on a notched brute with two soldiers behind it. One heavy blow shatters the crust, and the splinters notch the two behind it, ready for the next pour.'
  - Id: metal.bronze.master
    Title: 'Poured Bronze: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it within a second after a perfect guard, and the pour on the attacker is doubled, and Poured Bronze is ready again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, if you cast Poured Bronze within 1 second after a perfect guard, while it is ready, it doesn''t start its wait. Poured Bronze must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A cast in that second doesn''t start a wait, so you can pour again right away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder, Open Line and Forged Answer (the same perfect guard notches, holds and makes a blade), Edge Guard (the longer perfect timing window), Master''s Answer.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Doubling the pour on the attacker. For now, it only skips the wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a duellist''s thrust and pour right away. It is held under the bronze, and the pour is ready again for the next one.'
  - Id: metal.graveofswords
    Title: Grave of Swords
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Grave of Swords, Metal''s larger spell. Twelve blades fall in a ring around you and stand there. Each light blow you land sends the nearest one through that enemy, and a heavy blow sends all that are left. For now, the blades hang behind your shoulders and fly on their own, one after another, at the nearest enemy, and a heavy blow that lands sends all that are left at the enemy it hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It is Metal''s larger spell, paid for with mana.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 50 mana (its Empower makes it 45 mana and sixteen blades). Twelve blades for 12 seconds. For now, the first flies 0.6 seconds after you cast it, then one every 0.55 seconds at the nearest enemy within 6 metres. Each hits for 9 damage, 6 stagger and one notch of Sundered. Any not sent when the time is up disappear.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder, Forged Answer on the heavy blow that sends the rest, Poured Bronze (a notched crowd for it), To the Blade (its blades are blades to fly to), Every Crack Rings.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The twelve should fall in a ring 4.5 metres around you, dealing 8 damage under each, and stand there. A light blow should send the nearest one through its enemy, and only every third blade should notch (for now, every blade notches).


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it in a pack and keep fighting. The blades keep three notches on whoever is nearest, and a heavy blow on the brute sends the last five into it.'
  - Id: metal.graveofswords.shape
    Title: 'Grave of Swords: Clashing Bronze or The Tripods'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Grave of Swords. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Clashing Bronze</b> (the Kouretes clashing bronze around the baby Zeus): the blades don''t fly; they ring. Each light blow you land makes the nearest standing blade ring, sending a sharp ripple along the floor that notches everything it reaches. A heavy blow rings them all at once, and they fall. Every blow rings. <b>The Tripods</b> (the smith''s moving machines): three bronze tripods roll out, with four blades on each. They go to notched enemies first and cut them. A heavy blow you land calls each one home, throwing its blades through whatever it passes.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Grave of Swords, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain blades.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Clashing Bronze: for 12 seconds, every weapon blow on an enemy that was within 8 metres when you cast it rings out 3 metres around it, for 8 damage, 15 stagger and a notch, at most five times a second. A heavy blow on one of them also rings 4.5 metres around you, for 16 damage, 30 stagger and a notch, once a second. The Tripods: three helpers for 6 seconds. Each one cuts the nearest enemy every half second for 9 damage, 6 stagger and a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Clashing Bronze: fast weapons, Counter Cut, Poured Bronze on everything it rang. The Tripods: Poured Bronze, Open Line (the held enemy is cut for you).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Clashing Bronze''s blades should stand in their ring and ring instead of flying (for now, they still fly, and the ringing comes from your blows on enemies that were near when you cast it). The Tripods should go to notched enemies first, notch only every third cut (for now, every cut), and be called home by a heavy blow through what they pass.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Clashing Bronze in a crowd. Every light blow sends a ripple through the three enemies around the one you hit.'
  - Id: metal.graveofswords.master
    Title: 'Grave of Swords: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A finisher sends every blade that''s left, and you get 20 mana back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, the first heavy blow (a finisher counts) that lands on an enemy that was within 8 metres when you cast Grave of Swords, during its 12 seconds; once per cast. Grave of Swords must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. 20 mana back. The heavy blow already sends every blade that''s left.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder (the finisher adds three notches), Counter Cut, Forged Answer, Temper (the mana for it).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need a finisher. For now, any heavy blow on an enemy that was near when you cast it works.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it, stagger the brute, and finish it. The last blades go in, and you get 20 mana back.'
  - Id: metal.mastersanswer
    Title: Master's Answer
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> For two seconds after a perfect guard, your weapon''s skill costs no mana and comes out right away, with no time to charge. At any other time, it costs 50% more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. A perfect guard starts the two seconds; press the skill button during them.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. You get two seconds of a free skill that doesn''t need charging. Outside of those, the skill costs 1.5 times its mana.


      <b>Works with.</b> Counter Cut, which it comes after on the chart, Open Line (the attacker is still held when the skill lands), Edge Guard, Forged Answer, Poured Bronze''s Master mark, and, on the Katana, the Iaido Slash and Answering Draw.


      <b>Example.</b> With the Katana, perfect-guard a thrust, and the Iaido Slash leaves the sheath right away, for free.'
  - Id: metal.openline
    Title: Open Line
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard knocks the attacker''s weapon wide. It is held still for a moment, and for a short time after the guard, your weapon hits harder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The attacker is held for 1.2 seconds (less for elites; enemies that can''t be frozen aren''t held at all). Your weapon deals 15% more damage for 1.5 seconds after the guard, on any enemy. For now, the hold is all it does: the enemy can still raise its guard, and blows on the held enemy don''t count as hitting from behind.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder (a notch from the same guard), Forged Answer, Counter Cut, Master''s Answer (the free skill comes in the same moment), and Returning Edge and Circle of Steel, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a brute''s swing, and it stands frozen with its arm thrown wide. A heavy blow lands before it can move.'
  - Id: metal.returningedge
    Title: Returning Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Returning Edge, a breaker. A spinning blade a metre and a half across flies out in the direction you face and back to your hand, marking its path on the ground, and cuts every enemy it passes, both ways.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 16 damage and 14 stagger, going out and coming back.) It travels 8 metres at 16 metres a second, reaching 0.75 metres to each side of its line. Going out, it deals 12 damage, 10 stagger and a notch to each enemy it passes. A second later, coming back, it deals 12 damage and 10 stagger again to each one it hit going out.


      <b>Works with.</b> Open Line, which it comes after on the chart, Sunder, Poured Bronze on the line it notched, and the charged blade (it charges your weapon with Metal).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The spinning blade itself, flying out and back to your hand. For now, the path breaks open along the ground as it goes, and whatever it hit is cut again a second later where it stands.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers come down a hallway. The blade runs through them and back, and all four are notched, ready for the pour.'
  - Id: metal.returningedge.shape
    Title: 'Returning Edge: Bat It Home or Through the Line'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Returning Edge. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Bat It Home</b>: guard during the perfect timing window as the blade comes back, and it goes out again, faster and harder, up to three times. <b>Through the Line</b>: it doesn''t come back on its own. It stops at the end of its run and spins in place, and your next dodge calls it home along the line, cutting everything in between. Get the enemies between you and the blade.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Returning Edge, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain blade.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Bat It Home: for now, a perfect guard throws a blade out and back at 22 metres a second, dealing 18 damage and 15 stagger to each enemy it passes, both ways, at most once every 2 seconds. Through the Line: the blade spins 8 metres out for 3 seconds, reaching 0.9 metres around it, and deals 8 damage every 0.3 seconds. For now, when the 3 seconds are up, each enemy it hit going out is cut again for 20 damage, 30 stagger and a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bat It Home: Edge Guard and Open Line (the perfect guard), Forged Answer. Through the Line: a fight with lots of dodging, Poured Bronze on the line, Counter Cut.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Bat It Home should work when your guard meets the returning blade, up to three times (for now, any perfect guard throws one, once every 2 seconds). Through the Line''s blade should be called home by your dodge through everything in between, and shouldn''t come back on its own (for now, the normal return still happens, and the final cut lands on the enemies it hit going out when its time is up).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Through the Line down a hallway. Let the soldiers walk into the spinning blade, then the blade comes home through them.'
  - Id: metal.returningedge.master
    Title: 'Returning Edge: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Catch it with a perfect guard as it comes home, and Returning Edge is ready again right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, any perfect guard while its wait is counting down, once every 9 seconds. Returning Edge must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The wait ends right away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Edge Guard and Open Line, Bat It Home, Forged Answer (the same guard makes a blade).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need you to actually catch the blade. For now, any perfect guard works, once every 9 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw it, perfect-guard the brute''s swing, and throw it again right away.'
  - Id: metal.circle
    Title: Circle of Steel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Circle of Steel, the counter. You raise your blade for a moment and can''t move. The first blow that reaches you, from any side, red attack or not, does nothing to you, and you answer with a cut all around you that notches everything nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then it works on any blow in the next 1.2 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower blocks two blows and makes it 16 damage.) The answer hits everything within 3 metres, all the way around, for 14 damage, 20 stagger and one notch, as a heavy blow. For now, the blocked blow doesn''t count as a perfect guard for your marks: it gives no mana and doesn''t set off anything that needs one. If nothing hits you, the wait is half as long: 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Open Line, which it comes after on the chart, Edge Guard, which comes after it, Sunder, Poured Bronze on the ring of enemies it notched, and the charged blade (the answer is a spell, so it charges your weapon with Metal).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s second blocked blow.


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers surround you. Cast it as the first one lunges. The cut blocks its blow and notches all three.'
  - Id: metal.circle.shape
    Title: 'Circle of Steel: The Golden Chair or Ringing Plate'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Circle of Steel. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>The Golden Chair</b> (the smith''s chair that trapped the queen of the gods): a blocked blow closes bands of gold around the attacker, a faint shimmer that turns bright as they lock. It is held, gets two notches, and your blows on it go through any guard. <b>Ringing Plate</b>: you stand like a struck metal plate. Every blow you block rings out in a sharp ripple on the floor, each ring wider than the last. The more enemies hit you, the better.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Circle of Steel, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain circle.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The Golden Chair: the answer is a cut 3 metres long and 90 degrees wide at the enemy that hit you, for 14 damage, 20 stagger and a notch. Whatever it hits is held for 2 seconds (a god or an elite for 0.4 of that), gets one more notch, and can''t raise its guard for 2 seconds. Ringing Plate: ready for 1.5 seconds. The blocked blow rings out 4 metres all around, for 8 damage, 20 stagger and a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Golden Chair: Open Line, Poured Bronze (two notches and a held enemy), Counter Cut. Ringing Plate: being surrounded, Every Crack Rings.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Golden Chair''s bands should go on the attacker alone, while keeping the cut all around (for now, the answer is a cone at the attacker, and everything the cone hits is bound). Ringing Plate should ring for every blow it blocks in its 1.5 seconds, up to four, each ring wider (for now, the first blocked blow rings once).


      <b>Example.</b> Use The Golden Chair on a shield bearer''s charge. It stands trapped in gold with its shield down while you cut.'
  - Id: metal.circle.master
    Title: 'Circle of Steel: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Block a red attack, and you get 15 mana back and Circle of Steel is ready again right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, any blow the circle blocks, once every 20 seconds. Circle of Steel must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The wait ends right away, and you get 15 mana.


      <b>Works with.</b> Edge Guard, Temper (the mana for it), Ringing Plate.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work on red attacks. For now, any blocked blow works, once every 20 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> A god''s red sweep comes. Raise the circle, block it, and raise it again for the next one.'
  - Id: metal.edgeguard
    Title: Edge Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The perfect guard timing window is 50% longer, and every perfect guard cuts the attacker and notches its armour. The cost: dodging and every blow blocked with a normal guard cost 50% more stamina.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The cut comes with every perfect guard. The cost comes with every dodge and every blocked blow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. The perfect timing window grows by 55%, from 0.18 seconds to about 0.28. Each perfect guard hits everything within 1.2 metres of the attacker for 12 damage, 10 stagger and one notch of Sundered. A dodge costs 37.5 stamina instead of 25. A blocked blow costs 1.35 stamina per point of damage instead of 0.9. For now, holding your guard still blocks like normal.


      <b>Works with.</b> Circle of Steel, which it comes after on the chart, Sunder (two notches from one guard), Forged Answer, Open Line, Master''s Answer, every Master mark that works on a perfect guard, Hanging Swords, and Bright Steel and Halo of Blades on the Halo branch.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the perfect guard should block a blow (for now, holding your guard still blocks), and you should have to press guard again after 0.2 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight a fast duellist with just the guard button. Each of its blows you meet at the right moment is blocked, cut and notched.'
  - Id: metal.forgedanswer
    Title: Forged Answer
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each perfect guard makes a blade out of the blow you blocked, and it hangs at your shoulder. Up to three can wait there. Your next heavy blow that lands sends all of them at the enemy it hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard makes one. The next heavy blow that lands sends all of them. The count shows on the screen as Blades.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Up to three blades. Each flies at 16 metres a second, turning to follow the enemy, and hits for 10 damage and 12 stagger. For now, the blades don''t add a notch of their own.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sunder (the heavy blow that sends them adds a notch too), Edge Guard (easier perfect guards), Open Line, Every Crack Rings, which comes after it on the chart, and Flung Blade''s Master mark.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-guard a duellist''s flurry of three blows, then answer with one heavy blow. Three blades follow it in for 30 more damage and 36 stagger.'
  - Id: metal.base
    Title: Flung Blade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Flung Blade, Metal''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A blade flies into the enemy you face and sticks in it. Your next weapon blow on that enemy drives it in with a burst of sparks. Then it tears free and flies back to your hand, cutting along the way.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Metal, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Metal. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 6 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the drive hit for 16. It targets an enemy within 9 metres. It deals 4 damage when it lands, and stays stuck for 6 seconds. When your next weapon blow drives it in, it deals 12 damage, 15 stagger, a notch, and a burst of sparks 1.5 metres around, plus a third as much to anything on its path home.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Sunder, Poured Bronze, To the Blade (it is a blade to fly to), and the charged blade, which it charges with Metal when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s 16 damage when driven, and up to three blades stuck in one enemy with a heavy blow driving them all in (it used to be called Buried Blades). The spell doesn''t read the Empower''s numbers yet, so for now the Empower does nothing.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw the blade into the archer at the back, then close in and cut it. The blade bursts out of it and flies home through the soldier in between.'
  - Id: metal.base.shape
    Title: 'Flung Blade: Cherry Red or The Nail'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Flung Blade. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Cherry Red</b>: three red-hot blades fly in a fan, each sticking in a different enemy. They glow cherry red and cool to black, and one you drive in while it is still red hits double. You throw wide, then hurry. <b>The Nail</b> (the bronze nail in the giant Talos''s ankle): one long nail is driven low into the enemy, and it doesn''t come back. Each heavy blow on that enemy drives it deeper, and on the third, the enemy drops to one knee while golden blood runs out around it. Three heavy blows on one enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Flung Blade, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you throw the plain blade.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Cherry Red: for now, two more blades fly in a fan next to the normal one, dealing 4 damage and a notch to each enemy they pass. The normal blade, if driven in within 2 seconds, hits again for 12 damage and 15 stagger. The Nail: for 10 seconds, every heavy blow on the nailed enemy drives it in for 16 damage, 15 stagger and a notch. If its third weapon blow is a heavy blow, it is knocked down for 1.5 seconds (an elite for half that, a god not at all), and a pool of golden blood 1.5 metres around it stays for 4 seconds, dealing 3 damage and a notch once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cherry Red: fast weapons, a pack spread out wide, Poured Bronze. The Nail: heavy weapons, Counter Cut, Open Line (a held enemy to hammer).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Cherry Red''s three blades should each stick in a different enemy and cool over 6 seconds (for now, the normal blade sticks, and the fan cuts and keeps flying). The Nail should stay in its enemy and be driven deeper, for 12, then 16, then 24 (for now, the normal blade still flies home, and each heavy blow drives in for 16). The third hit should count only heavy blows (for now, it is the third weapon blow, if that one is heavy).


      <b>Example.</b> Put The Nail in a brute. Three heavy blows in a row, and it is down on one knee in a pool of gold.'
  - Id: metal.base.master
    Title: 'Flung Blade: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard drives in every blade stuck in the attacker at once, and Flung Blade is ready again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every perfect guard while Flung Blade is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The wait ends right away. For now, a notched attacker takes one blade''s drive: 12 damage, 15 stagger and a notch within 0.8 metres of it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Edge Guard and Open Line (the perfect guard), Forged Answer, Sunder.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the blades stuck in the attacker should be driven in. For now, a notched attacker takes one blade''s worth, no matter what is stuck in it.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw the blade into a duellist and perfect-guard its lunge. The blade drives in, and the next one is ready in your hand.'
  - Id: metal.lift
    Title: Every Crack Rings
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every Crack Rings, Metal''s reaction lift. Every reaction Sundered is part of rings out like struck metal. It notches everything around it, and hits harder on notched enemies.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a reaction with Sundered in it happens, on any enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The reaction''s own damage is 25% higher. One notch on every enemy within 3 metres of where it happened.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every element pair with Sundered in it (Live Iron with Static, Rust with Soaked, and the rest), Poured Bronze (the notches it spreads), and To the Blade and The Guard Holds, which come after it on the chart.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the 25% counts on every enemy the reaction hits, not only the notched ones. The notch should go on what the reaction reached, not on everything within 3 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> Static meets the notches in one soldier. Live Iron goes off, and the two soldiers next to it come away notched, ready for the pour.'
  - Id: metal.toblade
    Title: To the Blade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> To the Blade, the movement spell of the Metal tree. You fly along a line of sparks to one of your blades, untouchable on the way, cutting whatever is in between, and arrive driving that blade in. If you have no blade out, you throw one ahead into the ground and go to it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds and the reach 14 metres.) For now, it always works like you have no blade out: you rush 6 metres ahead at 24 metres a second, in a quarter of a second. Each enemy on the line, within 1 metre on each side, is hit once for 10 damage, 15 stagger and a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flung Blade and Grave of Swords (blades to fly to), Every Crack Rings, which it comes after on the chart, Sunder, Poured Bronze. When it lands, it charges your blade with Metal.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Flying to one of your blades within 12 metres (a Flung Blade stuck in an enemy or the ground, or a blade from Grave of Swords), being untouchable on the way, and driving that blade in when you arrive. The Empower''s 14 metres. For now, it always throws a blade ahead.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw Flung Blade into the archer across the room, then fly to it through the two soldiers in between.'
  - Id: metal.toblade.shape
    Title: 'To the Blade: Chain or There and Back'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for To the Blade. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Chain</b>: a bronze chain stays stretched along the line you flew. An enemy that crosses it is notched and tripped. Your path becomes a tripwire. <b>There and Back</b>: within two seconds of arriving, press again to fly back along the same line, cutting it a second time. In and out.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast To the Blade, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain flight.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Chain: a line of five patches, 6 metres long from where you started, each 0.6 metres across, lasting 3 seconds. Once a second, it deals 20 stagger and a notch. There and Back: for now, each enemy the flight cut is cut again a second later for 10 damage, 15 stagger and a notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Chain: a doorway, Poured Bronze on enemies that crossed it, Circle of Steel. There and Back: Sunder, Poured Bronze (two notches from one flight).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Chain''s trip should knock enemies down (for now, it is 20 stagger each time). There and Back''s flight back should come from a second press within 2 seconds (for now, the second cut happens on its own where the enemies stand).


      <b>Example.</b> Put Chain across the entrance to a hallway. The soldiers that follow you through it arrive notched and off balance.'
  - Id: metal.toblade.master
    Title: 'To the Blade: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it at the moment of a perfect dodge, and you are untouchable from the moment you press, and To the Blade is ready again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, if you cast To the Blade within 1 second after a perfect dodge, while it is ready, it doesn''t start its wait. To the Blade must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A cast in that second doesn''t start a wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> The perfect dodge, Flung Blade and Grave of Swords (a blade to fly to), Counter Cut on the enemy you arrive at.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing shared by the movement spells: cast it right as a blow would land, and it counts as a perfect dodge and you are untouchable from the moment you press. For now, casting it after a perfect dodge skips its wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Perfect-dodge a brute''s swing and fly right away to the blade in the archer behind it, then on to the next one.'
  - Id: metal.devotion
    Title: The Guard Holds
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Guard Holds, Metal''s Devotion, for going all in on Metal only. It is Metal''s basic bonus, defence, at full strength. Your guard never breaks. A blow your stamina can''t cover is still blocked, and whatever your stamina can''t cover comes through as grey health. Every blow you block notches the attacker and throws sparks into it. You take less damage from everything. Red attacks still need a dodge or a perfect guard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Metal twice (all in), once you buy it. It is always on then.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. You take 15% less damage from everything. A blow your stamina can''t cover is still blocked: all the stamina you have is used up, and the part of the blow it couldn''t cover comes through in full as grey health, on top of the usual chip damage. So with no stamina at all, the whole blow is grey. Each blow you block hits the attacker for 4 damage and a notch, at most five times a second. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Red Iron. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Metal alone or twice.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every Crack Rings, which it comes after on the chart, Sunder, Forged Answer, Hanging Swords (every blocked blow is a perfect guard), Temper.


      <b>Example.</b> Keep your guard up in the middle of a pack. Every blow that lands on it notches its attacker, and the pour waits for all of them.'
  - Id: metal.hangingswords
    Title: Hanging Swords
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While Temper lasts, every blow you block counts as a perfect guard, and a sword is made over the enemy that hit you, up to eight. Each sword hangs over its enemy and follows it. When Temper ends, they fall.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light Temper with the surge button, then guard. The swords fall when Temper ends.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. The perfect timing window lasts all of Temper, as long as you raise your guard fresh. Each guard gives 5 more mana, at most about three times a second. Up to eight swords. They fall 0.3 seconds after Temper ends, 0.11 seconds apart, each hitting 1.5 metres around for 16 damage, 20 stagger and a notch of Sundered.


      <b>Works with.</b> Temper (30% less damage taken, and one more notch with every blow), Sunder, Forged Answer, Open Line, Edge Guard, The Guard Holds, and every Master mark that works on a perfect guard.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Temper and stand in the middle of a pack with your guard up. Every blow is blocked, and when Temper ends, eight swords come down on them.'
  - Id: metal.allbladeshome
    Title: All Blades Home
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> All Blades Home, a keystone. Lighting Temper throws five blades out around you in a fan, and they come back to you, cutting and notching everything they pass, both ways. Temper costs more and lasts a little longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When you light Temper, the surge. Always on once you learn it, while you carry Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Five blades fly at 14 metres a second, out and back over 1.2 seconds. Each deals 12 damage, 8 stagger and one notch of Sundered to whatever it passes. Temper costs 37.5% more mana (55 instead of 40) and lasts 20% longer.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grave of Swords, which it comes after on the chart (from its Master mark), To the Blade, Forged Answer, Every Crack Rings, Hanging Swords (Temper''s god''s gift).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The blades you summon should never fade (up to sixteen), and lighting Temper should call every one of them home in a straight line through whatever stands in between, each adding a moment to Temper. For now, the surge throws its own five blades instead.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast Grave of Swords, fight among the blades, then light Temper. Five more fly out and back through the crowd, and every enemy they cross is notched.'
  - Id: mocksuns.shape
    Title: 'Mock Suns: Prism or Lens of Ice'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Through the Ice unlocks two new shapes for Mock Suns. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Mock Suns uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Prism</b>: no mock suns. Instead, the frost on the enemy clears to glass, your next weapon blow is a critical hit, and its light fans out of the enemy''s far side as three rays. <b>Lens of Ice</b>: the light gathers inside the one enemy. While it is frozen, it keeps its Mark, and when it shatters, the Mark is thrown onto everything nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Mock Suns goes off while you carry both Ice and Light. Mock Suns goes off when a Mark lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a Marked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Mock Suns.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Prism: for 4 seconds, the enemy''s Mark isn''t used up. Your next weapon blow on it, a critical hit thanks to the Mark, sends three hits in a line past it, 1.5 metres apart and 0.8 metres across. Each deals 8 damage, a Mark and a Chill. Lens of Ice: while the enemy stays chilled and Marked, blows don''t use up its Mark. A heavy blow on it at full Chill deals 18 more damage and puts a Mark on every enemy within 4 metres. After Mock Suns goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bent Light, Wide Fan, White Night (freeze a Marked enemy with a perfect guard), Sunstone.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd behind one soldier: use Prism, and hit the soldier so the rays run through the rest. A pack around a big enemy: use Lens of Ice, then freeze and shatter the big one to Mark them all.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Prism''s rays are three hits in a line past the enemy, instead of a fan of thin rays 6 metres long. Lens of Ice keeps the Mark while the enemy is chilled, not only while it is Frozen, and for now its heavy blow at full Chill takes the place of the shatter''s 60%.'
  - Id: mocksuns.bentlight
    Title: Bent Light
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A ray that meets ice you made (Hoarfrost''s crystals, a wall of ice, a frozen enemy) bends toward the nearest enemy and keeps going 5 metres, dealing 8 damage and a Mark.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each spell that hits a frozen enemy, while you carry both Ice and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. An arc from the frozen enemy to the nearest other enemy within 5 metres, dealing 8 damage, 5 stagger and a Mark, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Mock Suns, Sunstone, White Night (freeze the enemy first).


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze one enemy, then cast through it. The light finds the next one.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, only a frozen enemy bends the light, and any spell''s hit counts, not only rays. Hoarfrost''s crystals and walls of ice don''t bend it yet.'
  - Id: mocksuns.widefan
    Title: Wide Fan
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The mock suns hang in the sky 2 seconds longer. With Prism, the light past the enemy reaches farther and has five hits instead of three.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Mock Suns goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The suns hang for 6 seconds instead of 4. With Prism, five hits go out in a line past the enemy instead of three, still 1.5 metres apart. The first is 1 metre past the enemy and the last is 7 metres past it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Mock Suns and its shapes, Bent Light.


      <b>Example.</b> The suns are still hanging there when you''ve circled back around.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Prism''s fan of rays 8 metres long. For now, it is the line of five hits.'
  - Id: mocksuns.whitenight
    Title: White Night
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked enemy that has three or more Chill freezes it right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard against an enemy like that, while you carry both Ice and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Its Chill is filled all the way, which freezes it for as long as Chill normally freezes, at most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Mock Suns, Lens of Ice (freeze, then shatter), Bent Light, Sunstone.


      <b>Example.</b> Mark a brute, give it three stacks of Chill, and turn its next swing into ice.'
  - Id: mocksuns.sunstone
    Title: Sunstone
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You hold up a block of clear ice crystal for 1.2 seconds. The first blow that lands on it does nothing to you and is split: five rays fan out 8 metres toward the attacker, each dealing 12 damage, 20 stagger, a Mark and two Chill.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Ice and Light. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, counter, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The answer is a cone 8 metres long and 80 degrees wide. It deals 12 damage, 20 stagger, two Chill and a Mark to everything in it. If nothing hits it, the wait is half as long: 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Mock Suns (everything it hits gets both afflictions), White Night, Bent Light.


      <b>Example.</b> Raise it as the brute swings, and the pack behind it is Marked and chilled.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the answer is one cone, not five rays. A perfect guard in its last 0.3 seconds doesn''t freeze the attacker solid yet.'
  - Id: nervefire.shape
    Title: 'Nerve Fire: Ozone or Lockjaw'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Struck Nerve unlocks two new shapes for Nerve Fire. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Nerve Fire uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes keep the shocks. <b>Ozone</b>: each shock lets off a puff of sharp blue mist that poisons and charges nearby enemies. <b>Lockjaw</b>: the third shock locks the enemy in place.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Nerve Fire goes off while you carry both Poison and Lightning. Nerve Fire goes off when Static lands on a poisoned enemy, or when poison lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Nerve Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: while both afflictions last, each bite of poison uses up a Static and shocks the enemy, for 7 damage, 5 stagger and a 0.3-second flinch (an elite only flinches once every 4 seconds, and each elite keeps its own wait; a god takes 11 stagger and doesn''t flinch). Ozone: each shock also adds a dose and a Static to everything within 1 metre. Lockjaw: the third shock holds the enemy for 1.2 seconds. After Nerve Fire goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Miasma and Thunderclap to set it up, Strike on the Beat, Five Thunders, Hidden Crimes.


      <b>Example.</b> A tightly packed line: Ozone spreads the shocks down it. A brute winding up its big blow: use Lockjaw.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the shapes'' shocks only show as the burst, not the small shocks across the body that the plain Nerve Fire shows.'
  - Id: nervefire.beat
    Title: Strike on the Beat
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow that lands in time with the beat of the enemy''s poison sets off all its Static and adds just as many doses.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Static and poison, at most twice a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Its Static goes off, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack within 4 metres, and it gets a dose for each stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Nerve Fire, Hidden Crimes, Static.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned soldier has five Static. One heavy blow turns them into five more doses.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any heavy blow on a poisoned, charged enemy, not only one within 0.3 seconds of a bite.'
  - Id: nervefire.fivethunders
    Title: Five Thunders
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every fifth bite of poison on an enemy with Static calls a bolt down on it, which jumps to two more enemies.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When poison bites an enemy that has Static.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Exactly every fifth bite. Each enemy keeps its own count. It deals 14 damage and a Static to the enemy and to up to two more within 5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Nerve Fire, Miasma, Static.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned, charged pack crackles with bolts as the poison works.'
  - Id: nervefire.hiddencrimes
    Title: Hidden Crimes
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Poison counts as charge. When Static goes off on a poisoned enemy, it deals 7 more damage for every two doses the enemy has, and uses them up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Static is set off on a poisoned enemy (by a heavy blow, Strike on the Beat, or Thunderclap).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Every dose is used up, dealing 3.5 damage each to everything within 4 metres, on top of the Static burst. At most three times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Strike on the Beat, Static, Miasma, One by One.


      <b>Example.</b> Eight doses and three Static: the heavy blow bursts for 21, plus 28 more.'
  - Id: nervefire.seal
    Title: Paper Seal
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You slap a seal of yellow paper on the forehead of the enemy you face, and it is held in place. For 6 seconds, each weapon blow on it burns the seal down by a fifth. On the fifth blow, thunder falls on it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Poison and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 13 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches an enemy within 3 metres. A normal enemy is held for 1.2 seconds. The thunder deals 30 damage, 50 stagger and a dose within 3.5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Nerve Fire, Hidden Crimes, Strike on the Beat.


      <b>Example.</b> Seal a captain and land five blows. Thunder falls on it and its guard.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You don''t step to the enemy yet. The thunder doesn''t yet set off the enemy''s Static, add 4 damage per dose, or pass each dose to everything nearby. It doesn''t yet fall at half strength when the 6 seconds run out. Elites aren''t held at all yet.'
  - Id: overgrowth.shape
    Title: 'Split Rock: Taking Root or Root Web'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Split Rock. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Split Rock uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Taking Root</b>: a short hold, and the floor cracks around the enemy right away, shaking everything nearby. <b>Root Web</b>: the roots keep spreading underground and catch every enemy nearby for a moment.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Split Rock goes off while you carry both Life and Earth. Split Rock goes off when Tremor lands on a seeded enemy, or when Seeded lands on a shaken one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Split Rock. The plain one holds the enemy for 2 seconds, and a heavy blow while it is held splits the floor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Taking Root: the enemy is held for 1 second, and every other enemy within 2.5 metres gets one Tremor. Root Web: every Seeded enemy within 6 metres is held for 0.5 seconds and gets one Tremor. After Split Rock goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch and Stone Rise to add the seed and the shaking, Living Rampart, Thorns Take Root, Stone Oak.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack closing in: Root Web holds them all for half a second while you roll away. One brute: use the plain Split Rock, and land a heavy blow while it is held.'
  - Id: overgrowth.moss
    Title: Moss on Stone
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hold your ground, and the ground feeds you. While you fight without rolling, you heal slowly, and thorns grow on you that cut and Seed anyone who hits you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While you haven''t started a dodge for 3 seconds. The healing comes every 3 seconds during a fight (when an enemy is within 12 metres). The thorns come with each blow that lands on you, at most once a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 2 health every 3 seconds. The thorns deal 8 damage and one stack of Seeded within 1 metre of the attacker.


      <b>Works with.</b> Split Rock''s shapes, Living Rampart (guard instead of rolling), Seeded.


      <b>Example.</b> Hold a doorway with your guard and never roll. Any soldier that gets a blow through is Seeded, ready for your answer.'
  - Id: overgrowth.briarpatch
    Title: Thorns Take Root
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The thorns from a Seeded enemy''s death take root where they fall. The patch shakes the ground and Seeds whatever walks in it. It used to be called Briar Patch, until Life''s own spell took that name.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever an enemy with Seeded dies, at most once a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A patch with a 3-metre radius that lasts 6 seconds. Every second, it deals 3 damage and one stack of Seeded. Every 2 seconds, it adds a stack of Tremor, which slows by 10% per stack and stops the stagger meter from going down.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded (its death burst comes first), Fallen Seeds (a thornbush in the same spot), Split Rock (enemies that walk through get both afflictions), Living Rampart.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a seeded soldier in the middle of the pack. The rest have to wade through briars, slowed and Seeded, and their stagger stops going down.'
  - Id: overgrowth.stoneoak
    Title: Stone Oak
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You plant a stone oak a little in front of you. For twenty seconds, its roots hit everything in a wide circle around it, again and again, Seeding what they hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 45 mana. It is planted 2 metres in front of you; the circle has a 5-metre radius. It first hits half a second after planting, then every 2 seconds for 20 seconds, for 10 damage, 15 stagger and one stack of Seeded each time. For now, it hits like a fully grown tree from the start. It doesn''t grow with blows, heal you in its shade, or block the way, and it doesn''t count as something you grew for Shade or Reap What Is Sown.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seeded, Moss on Stone or Thorns Take Root (it comes after either on the chart), Split Rock, and the charged blade: its first hit charges your weapon with Life.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it in the middle of a room and fight inside its circle. Everything there is Seeded every two seconds, and every cut heals you.'
  - Id: overgrowth.livingrampart
    Title: Living Rampart
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your guard is alive. Every blow you block heals you a little and Seeds the enemy that hit you. A perfect guard roots the attacker in place and shakes the ground under it. The cost: guarding costs more stamina.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each blow you block with a normal guard, and each perfect guard.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. A blocked blow: you heal 2, and the attacker gets one stack of Seeded. A perfect guard: the attacker is held still for 1 second (less for elites and gods) and gets two stacks of Tremor (one, if Earth is your first element and you''ve learned its root mark). Blocking costs 15% more stamina.


      <b>Works with.</b> Moss on Stone or Thorns Take Root (it comes after either on the chart), Split Rock (the perfect guard adds the Tremor it needs), Seeded.


      <b>Example.</b> Block a soldier''s string of three blows. It is Seeded, you have 6 more health, and your answer heals you even more.'
  - Id: overgrowth.groundwakes
    Title: The Ground Wakes
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When your surge starts, the ground rises around you in a ring of stone and roots and holds every enemy inside it, shaken and Seeded. Then it flowers: a burst goes off, and you heal for each enemy it held.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you light the surge with the surge button, whichever element''s surge you have.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. Everything within 7 metres of you is held for 2 seconds (less for elites and gods) and gets three stacks of Tremor and one of Seeded. When the hold ends, everything within 7 metres takes 15 damage and 20 stagger, and you heal 5 for each enemy held.


      <b>Works with.</b> Split Rock, Seeded, Living Rampart.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Regrowth with four soldiers around you. All four are held and shaken for two seconds, then you heal 20.'
  - Id: plasma.shape
    Title: 'Flashover: Corona or Fulgurite'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Flashover. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Flashover uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain Flashover. <b>Corona</b>: nothing is used up. Violet flame crawls over the enemy and throws small arcs of lightning at the enemies next to it. <b>Fulgurite</b>: the crack goes down instead of out. The ground under the enemy melts into a scorched, glassy patch that burns and shocks anyone standing in it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Flashover goes off while you carry both Fire and Lightning. Flashover goes off when Static lands on a burning enemy, or when Burn lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Flashover.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Corona: lasts 4 seconds. Every 0.7 seconds, an arc jumps from the enemy to the nearest other enemy within 5 metres for 4 damage, a Burn and a Static (about five arcs in total). Fulgurite: a patch 2.5 metres across that lasts 4 seconds. Once a second, it deals 4 damage, a Burn and a Static. What either shape adds doesn''t start a reaction of its own. After Flashover goes off on an enemy, it still can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ionised Air (an arc jumps on through three more enemies, whatever the shape), Twin Break, Meltdown (Corona and Fulgurite both keep Burn and Static on everyone nearby), Plasma.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack pushing in: use Corona, and one burning soldier you charge keeps arcing into the two next to it for four seconds. Fighting one brute in a doorway: use Fulgurite, and fight on the patch it leaves.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Corona''s arcs jump out from the enemy, instead of pulling other bursts of Static into its flame. Fulgurite''s patch looks like burning, crackling ground until the glassy look is made.'
  - Id: plasma.twin
    Title: Twin Break
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard sets off Burn and Static together on the enemy that hit you. Set off together, they hit 50% harder, and the blow counts as a heavy blow, so it stops the enemy where it stands. If it only has one of the two, that one is set off alone.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has Burn, Static, or both.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Together: 10 damage per Burn stack and 7 per Static stack, times 1.5, plus 5 stagger per stack. At five of each, that''s 127.5 damage and 50 stagger. Alone, within 3 metres: Burn deals 10 plus 15 per stack; Static deals 10.5 per stack; each adds 5 stagger per stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Kiln Stance, Stored Charge, Meltdown, Ball Lightning (it comes after this or Ionised Air on the chart). Breath of the Lioness uses up the burn first, so the two get in each other''s way.


      <b>Example.</b> A burning, charged brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it takes 127 damage and stops dead.'
  - Id: plasma.ionised
    Title: Ionised Air
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Whenever Flashover goes off, an arc jumps from the burning enemy through three more, hitting and charging each one.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Flashover goes off on an enemy, plain or in either shape, while you carry both Fire and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 10 damage, 8 stagger and a Static to each of up to three more enemies, jumping up to 5 metres from one to the next. Flashover itself still can''t go off on the same enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Flashover''s Shape mark (Corona or Fulgurite), Chain Reaction, which comes after it on the chart, and Static or Thunderclap to add the charge.


      <b>Example.</b> Set a soldier on fire in a crowd and hit it with Thunderclap. Flashover goes off, and the arc runs on into three of its friends, leaving each one charged for the next.'
  - Id: plasma.chain
    Title: Chain Reaction
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies with both Burn and Static bursts, and passes both on to everyone nearby, with as many stacks as it had.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The moment an enemy with both dies.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The burst has a 4-metre radius and deals 18 damage plus as many Burn stacks as the dead enemy had. Everyone within 4 metres also gets as many Static stacks as it had. At most a few times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ionised Air, which it comes after on the chart, Catching Fire, Meltdown.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one soldier with five of each, and the three around it start with five of each too, one blow away from Meltdown.'
  - Id: plasma.meltdown
    Title: Meltdown
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy near you with five Burn and five Static starts to shake with light. A few moments later, it bursts, much harder than either one alone, and the burst uses up both. Nothing stops it, and it doesn''t hurt you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Fire and Lightning. A few times a second, it checks for an enemy within 14 metres that has both afflictions full. Only one enemy melts down at a time.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. The enemy shakes for 3 seconds, then bursts. It takes 45 damage and 60 stagger as a heavy blow, and everything else within 5 metres takes half of each. The same enemy can''t melt down again for 10 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Twin Break or Chain Reaction (it comes after either on the chart), Ball Lightning, Corona.


      <b>Example.</b> Fill a brute with both and roll away. Three seconds later it goes up, staggering the pack around it.'
  - Id: plasma.ball
    Title: Ball Lightning
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You release a slow ball of plasma, violet in the middle with real flame on its surface, that floats after the nearest enemy. Hit it with a swing to send it flying fast in the direction of the swing, and it grows one step bigger, up to three. It bursts on the first enemy it meets, adding Burn and Static, and it is stronger for each time you hit it. If you leave it alone, it bursts small when its time is up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Fire and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4), then swing at the ball. The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Fire, this branch''s first element.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, 5 Shards, 40 mana. It appears 1.5 metres in front of you and floats at 2 metres a second for 8 seconds. When you hit it, it flies at 9 metres a second. The burst has a 2.5-metre radius and deals 16 damage, 20 stagger, and two stacks each of Burn and Static. Each hit adds 0.7 metres, 12 damage, 10 stagger and one stack of each. At three hits: 52 damage, 50 stagger, and it counts as a heavy blow.


      <b>Works with.</b> Twin Break or Ionised Air (it comes after either on the chart), Meltdown, and Flashover on whatever it leaves burning and charged.


      <b>Example.</b> Hit it three times into an archer. It deals 52 damage, and everything nearby has five of both.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ball still has its first colours. The violet middle and the flame on its surface will come with its new look.'
  - Id: fire.plasma
    Title: Plasma
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> This branch''s top mark. A heavy blow or a finisher on a burning enemy makes it burst. The burst sets off the burn on everyone it reaches, then sets each of them on fire again. If the enemy has Static as well, Flashover goes off on it right away, even if Flashover would normally have to wait, in the shape you picked.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on a burning enemy, while you carry both Fire and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. The burst has a 3.2-metre radius and deals 18 damage and 16 stagger. Each enemy in it that was burning also has its burn set off, for 10 damage plus 10 more per stack, and every enemy in it burns for 4 seconds afterward. None of the first enemy''s Burn is used up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Cinder, Heat, Catching Fire, Flashover''s shapes, Ionised Air.


      <b>Example.</b> Three soldiers each have five Burn. A heavy blow on one bursts over all three for 78 each, and leaves them burning. If that one was charged too, Flashover goes off on it right away.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> On an enemy with no Static, the burst is still its own thing. The plan is to make it a Flashover that doesn''t use anything up.'
  - Id: poison.venom
    Title: Venom
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your weapon poisons. Every blow that lands adds a dose of poison. A heavy blow adds one more, and a finisher adds even more. The poison doesn''t hurt a little at a time. Instead, on a slow beat, it bites for all its doses at once. You also get a tenth of the damage your poison deals back as health, so poison always heals a Poison player.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. Your blows only add doses while Poison is your first element, because your weapon only adds your first element''s affliction. If Poison is your second element, its doses come from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike instead. The healing works no matter who added the poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One dose per blow; a heavy blow adds two, a finisher four. Up to eight doses, lasting 6 seconds, and each new dose restarts the timer. Every 2 seconds, the poison bites for 2 damage per dose: 16 at eight. When it is set off, it deals 6 damage per dose all at once: 48 at eight. You get back a tenth of the damage the poison really deals, at each bite and each time it is set off (1.6 for a bite of 16, 4.8 for a burst of 48). Stronger or ripened poison heals you more. The health comes back over about 2 seconds, not all at once.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rupture and Volatile to set it off, Catching to spread it, In Time with its beat, every Poison spell, Fever, and Rot Roots when a Seeded enemy is poisoned.


      <b>Example.</b> Four light blows and a heavy blow give a soldier six doses. Roll away, and the next bite takes 12 health from it and gives you a little over 1.'
  - Id: poison.rupture
    Title: Rupture
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on an enemy whose poison is full bursts the poison. All of it lands at once, on that enemy and everyone close to it, and the doses are used up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy with full poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. It is set off for 6 damage per dose (48 at eight) and 4 stagger per dose, to everything within 1 metre of the enemy you hit. At most about three times a second. Full is eight doses, or twenty with Slow Death.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom (fill it up with blows, finish with a heavy blow), Blister and One by One, which come after it on the chart, Volatile, Coiled Strike (four doses toward full), and the root mark''s healing (each burst heals you).


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier has eight doses. Land a heavy blow: 48 damage lands at once with the blow, and the soldier next to it takes the same.'
  - Id: poison.blister
    Title: Blister
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Blister, the detonator of the Poison tree. You raise a blister on the enemy you face. It swells each time its poison bites, through three sizes, and a heavy blow pops it in a burst that poisons everything around it. If you leave it too long, it shrivels up.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, no blister is raised, and you still have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 50 damage.) It targets an enemy within 8 metres. Only one blister can be up at a time, and it lasts 12 seconds. The burst has a 3-metre radius around the enemy and deals 40 damage, 30 stagger and four doses.


      <b>Works with.</b> The root mark, Rupture, which it comes after on the chart (four doses toward full), Coiled Strike (step in and pop it from behind), In Time, and the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Swelling through three sizes: 20, 40 and 70 damage, within 2, 3 and 4 metres, with 2, 4 and 6 doses. For now, it bursts at the middle size. Only a heavy blow should pop it; for now, any weapon blow on it pops it.


      <b>Example.</b> Put a blister on the brute in the middle of a group and cut it once. Everyone around it gets four doses.'
  - Id: poison.blister.shape
    Title: 'Blister: Weeping Sore or The Vessel'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Blister. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Weeping Sore</b>: it never bursts on its own or from a heavy blow. Each time its poison bites, it leaks a bubbling puddle where the enemy stands, so a walking enemy spreads your poison. Your heavy blow bursts every puddle at once. It is slow and sneaky, not a swell and a pop. <b>The Vessel</b> (like the court''s elixir-drinkers): the whole enemy swells up, and when its poison bites while full, it bursts on its own into a cloud that poisons everything in it. You set it and leave it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Blister, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain blister.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Weeping Sore: for 12 seconds, each bite of the enemy''s poison leaves a puddle 1.5 metres across for 3 seconds that gives a dose each second to whatever stands in it. A heavy blow on the sore bursts 2 metres around it for 12 damage and two doses, at most twice a second. The Vessel: for 12 seconds, the first bite while its poison is full puts three doses on everything within 2 metres, and leaves a cloud 4 metres across for 3 seconds that gives a dose each second. Once per cast.


      <b>Works with.</b> Weeping Sore: an enemy that walks around, Catching, Low Mist. The Vessel: Venom''s blows to fill it up, Catching, Slow Death (full is twenty, so it waits longer). Volatile stops the beat, and neither shape leaks or bursts without bites.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Both shapes still raise the plain blister alongside them, which any weapon blow pops, because a shape can''t turn off its spell''s own effect yet. Weeping Sore''s heavy blow bursts around the sore, not at each puddle where it lies.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Weeping Sore on a charging brute. It leaves a trail of puddles as it comes, and the soldiers following it walk through them.'
  - Id: poison.blister.master
    Title: 'Blister: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Pop it with a heavy blow right as its poison bites, within a fifth of a second, and the burst doubles.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The first heavy blow on the blistered enemy, while Blister is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For 12 seconds after you cast it, the first heavy blow on that enemy, if it lands within a fifth of a second of its bite, sets off a second burst just as big around it: 40 damage, 30 stagger and four doses within 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time (the same moment), Coiled Strike''s heavy blow made into a critical hit, a heavy weapon.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The blister''s own burst should double. For now, a second burst lands with it, and only if the heavy blow is the one that pops it (a light blow pops the plain blister first).


      <b>Example.</b> Watch the bubbles on the blistered captain. As they pop, bring the heavy blow down: two bursts, 80 damage to everything around it.'
  - Id: poison.onebyone
    Title: One by One
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> One by One, Poison''s larger spell, paid for with mana. You raise a hand, and a green line runs along the ground from poisoned enemy to poisoned enemy. Then you snap your fingers, and each one''s poison bursts in turn, each one passing its doses to the next before it goes. The last burst is the biggest.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 50 mana (its Empower makes it 7 damage per dose instead of 6). It reaches up to six enemies within 10 metres of you. After half a second''s warning, a column rises under each one in turn, every 0.07 seconds, 1 metre across. The column itself does no damage. Each poisoned enemy it hits has its poison set off: 6 damage and 4 stagger per dose to everything within 1 metre (48 damage and 32 stagger at eight). Whatever a column hits is thrown into the air for 0.8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rupture, which it comes after on the chart, Venom, Blister, Catching and Miasma to add the poison first, the root mark''s healing (every burst heals you), and the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The line should only run between poisoned enemies; for now, the columns come up under the six nearest enemies, poisoned or not. Each enemy should pass its doses to the next, with the last burst the biggest; for now, each bursts with what it has.


      <b>Example.</b> Poison a pack with Miasma and a rush of Share Alike, then snap your fingers. Six bursts go off in a moment, and each one heals you.'
  - Id: poison.onebyone.shape
    Title: 'One by One: Eaten in Turn or All Into One'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for One by One. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Eaten in Turn</b> (the gu jar, where the order of killing matters): the line is drawn, but nothing bursts. For a while, when an enemy on the line dies, all its doses pass to the next one on the line, doubled. One pushed past eight doses bursts right away and passes it on. Your blade chooses the order. <b>All Into One</b> (like the quicksilver of the Metal and Poison reaction): no line. The poison runs out of every poisoned enemy nearby as beads of mercury that join together in the enemy you face. Their doses are added to its own, with no limit. Then that one enemy bursts alone. It''s for the one enemy that matters.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast One by One, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain line.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Eaten in Turn: for 8 seconds, each enemy the spell hit that dies poisoned passes twice its doses to the nearest other enemy within 10 metres. All Into One: only one column, under the nearest enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Eaten in Turn: a pack of weak enemies to kill in order, Slow Death (twenty doses to pass on), Rupture and Volatile on the enemy that gets fed. All Into One: a god or an elite, Catching and Miasma to poison the crowd first.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Eaten in Turn still bursts like the plain spell, so the enemies that die must be poisoned again first. The doses go to the nearest enemy, not the next one on the line, and an enemy pushed past eight doesn''t burst. All Into One doesn''t gather anything yet: the one enemy bursts with what it has.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Eaten in Turn on five soldiers. Poison them again as you cut, kill the front one with six doses, and the next one has twelve.'
  - Id: poison.onebyone.master
    Title: 'One by One: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Snap your fingers within a fifth of a second of the poison''s beat, and every burst is one and a half times as strong.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each poisoned enemy a column hits, while One by One is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, each poisoned enemy hit within a fifth of a second of its own bite takes a second burst: 24 damage and 16 stagger within 1 metre, which is half of an eight-dose burst.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time (the same beat), Catching (the beat spreads across the pack), Venom.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The snap itself should be timed against the beat; for now, each enemy is checked as its column comes up. The bursts should be raised by half; for now, the extra is the same no matter how many doses.


      <b>Example.</b> Watch the pack''s poison bite and raise your hand right then. The columns that land on the bite hit 50% harder.'
  - Id: poison.volatile
    Title: Volatile
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your poison no longer bites on its beat. It builds up until it is full, then bursts on its own, uses itself up, and starts again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. It bursts the moment any affliction fills up on a poisoned enemy. Usually that is the poison reaching full, but another element''s affliction filling up bursts it too, at however many doses it has.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. The burst deals 7 damage per dose (56 at eight) and 4 stagger per dose to everything within 2.5 metres of the enemy. At most twice a second. Full is eight doses, or twenty with Slow Death (140 damage then).


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom, Rupture, which it comes after on the chart, Slow Death, One by One, and the root mark''s healing (each burst heals you). Catching, In Time, Weeping Sore, The Vessel and the beat Master marks all need bites, and there aren''t any.


      <b>Example.</b> Eight light blows on a soldier, and it goes up on the eighth, taking the two next to it with it.'
  - Id: poison.intime
    Title: In Time
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hit right as the poison bites, and your blow adds two doses instead of one. For a moment after that timed blow, every blow you land is a critical hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A weapon blow that lands on a poisoned enemy within a quarter of a second of its poison biting, just before or just after. The extra dose only comes while Poison is your first element; the critical moment comes either way.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One more dose on the timed blow. For 1.2 seconds after it, your blows are critical hits (one and a half times the damage). For now, the timed blow itself isn''t a critical hit, only the ones after it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom, Five Needles and Coiled Strike, which come after it on the chart, every beat Master mark (the same moment), Blister''s Master mark, and a heavy blow or finisher during the critical moment.


      <b>Example.</b> Watch the bubbles. As they pop, land a light blow, then a heavy blow right after, and the heavy blow is a critical hit.'
  - Id: poison.needles
    Title: Five Needles
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Five Needles, the breaker of the Poison tree. Five long needles, dripping with venom, are thrown in a narrow fan at the enemy you face. An enemy hit by all five is pinned in place: held for a moment, with its balance badly shaken.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 14 stagger per needle, 70 in total, and 50 for the pin.) It is a fan 8 metres long and 12 degrees wide. It deals 15 damage, 50 stagger and five doses (all five needles'' worth) to everything in it. The first enemy it hits is pinned: held for 0.8 seconds (0.32 for a god or an elite) and given 40 more stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time, which it comes after on the chart, Rupture (five doses toward full), Coiled Strike (strike through the pinned enemy), and the charged blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Five separate needles, each hitting on its own. For now, the fan hits as one, and the pin goes on the first enemy hit, not the one hit by all five.


      <b>Example.</b> A shield bearer turns to face you. The needles pin it, and you have most of a second to walk around its shield.'
  - Id: poison.needles.shape
    Title: 'Five Needles: Spread or Red Paper'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Five Needles. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Spread</b>: a wide fan, so each needle finds a different enemy. Each enemy hit gets two doses, and its poison bites right away. <b>Red Paper</b> (the five poisons pinned to red paper; the needle grids in the floor are the Poison god''s own weapon): all five needles pin one sheet of red paper to the enemy you face. For six seconds, it can''t guard or block a blow, and your blows on it go through any shield. It''s the breaker for enemies that hide behind their guard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Five Needles, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain fan.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Spread: a fan 60 degrees wide. It deals 3 damage, 10 stagger and two doses to each enemy in it, and each one''s poison bites right away. Red Paper: the first enemy hit can''t raise its guard for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spread: a crowd, Catching (every bite throws a dose onward), In Time. Red Paper: enemies that guard and captains, a heavy weapon, Blister.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Spread is one fan, not five needles, and it still pins the first enemy it hits. Red Paper should stop the enemy from blocking blows and let your blows go through shields.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Red Paper on a captain who guards everything. For six seconds, your whole chain of blows lands.'
  - Id: poison.needles.master
    Title: 'Five Needles: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Throw them on a poisoned enemy''s beat, within a fifth of a second of its bite, and every needle that hits it makes its poison bite one more time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each poisoned enemy the needles hit, while Five Needles is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, an enemy hit within a fifth of a second of its bite bites once more right away, for all its doses: 16 damage at eight.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time (the same beat), Catching (every bite throws a dose onward), the root mark''s healing (every bite heals you), Spread.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> One extra bite for every needle. For now, there is one extra bite for the whole fan.


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier has eight doses. Throw on its bite, and it is bitten twice in a moment, for 32 damage.'
  - Id: poison.coiled
    Title: Coiled Strike
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Coiled Strike, Poison''s counter. You coil up, then strike through the enemy you face in an instant and come out on its far side, facing back, cutting everything on the line and poisoning it heavily. If the cut lands, your next heavy blow soon after is a critical hit. Nothing can hit you during the strike. A wall, or an enemy too big to push past, stops you short.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it 30 damage.) It goes up to 6 metres, ending 1.8 metres past the enemy you face (or 4.5 metres ahead if there is none). The cut reaches 1.1 metres to each side of the line. It deals 24 damage, 25 stagger and four doses, the strength of two fangs. A heavy blow within 3 seconds is a critical hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time, which it comes after on the chart, Slow Death, which comes after it, Rupture (four doses toward full), Blister, and the charged blade: if it lands, it charges your weapon with Poison.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Holding the coil for up to 2.4 seconds while three fangs light up (14, 24 or 38 damage and 2, 4 or 8 doses, depending on the fangs). For now, it strikes right away as two fangs. Letting go right as a blow is about to land should make the blow pass through you, for three fangs.


      <b>Example.</b> Strike through a shield bearer to its back, turn around, and the heavy blow you owe it is a critical hit.'
  - Id: poison.coiled.shape
    Title: 'Coiled Strike: Centipede''s Run or Shed Skin'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Coiled Strike. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Centipede''s Run</b> (the centipede, one of the five poisons): let go, and you strike through up to three enemies in a zigzag of short rushes, biting each one, with the fangs'' doses shared between them. You end up behind the last one. The snake strikes one; the centipede strikes many. <b>Shed Skin</b> (the snake that sheds its skin): let go during the perfect timing window, and you leave your skin standing where you were, pale and papery. The enemy keeps attacking it while your strike lands from behind.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Coiled Strike, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Centipede''s Run: a straight rush 12 metres long through everything on a line 2 metres wide, dealing 14 damage, 15 stagger and two doses to each (30 damage each once you buy the Empower, which sets its number on the rush too). Shed Skin: every enemy within 3 metres of where you stood loses sight of you for 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Centipede''s Run: a row of enemies, Catching, Rupture. Shed Skin: a pack that crowds you, Blister (pop it from behind), a heavy weapon.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Centipede''s Run''s zigzag of three short rushes, ending behind the last enemy; for now, it is one straight run. Shed Skin should only work during the perfect timing window, and should show a skin standing there; for now, every cast blinds the enemies near where you stood.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Centipede''s Run down a line of archers. Each one gets two doses, and you end up past the last.'
  - Id: poison.coiled.master
    Title: 'Coiled Strike: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Let go during the perfect timing window, and Coiled Strike is ready again.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While Coiled Strike is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, a perfect dodge ends Coiled Strike''s wait, once every 10 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Watching for the enemies'' wind-ups, Shed Skin, In Time.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need you to let go during the perfect timing window. For now, any perfect dodge does it.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a captain''s swing perfectly, and Coiled Strike is ready to put you at its back.'
  - Id: poison.slowdeath
    Title: Slow Death
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your weapon does much less damage. But your poison goes much deeper, and it never fades while the enemy carrying it is alive.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The extra doses only work while Poison is your first element; the poison stops fading either way.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Your weapon deals 30% less damage. Poison can hold twelve more doses, up to twenty. At twenty, a bite deals 40 damage every 2 seconds, and setting it off deals 120. Poison you add doesn''t run out.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom and its healing (a deeper bite heals you more), Coiled Strike, which it comes after on the chart, Catching, Volatile (it fills up at twenty), Rupture (full is now twenty), One by One''s Eaten in Turn shape, Clean Blade.


      <b>Example.</b> Give a Gate Captain twenty doses and spend the rest of the fight guarding. It takes 40 damage every two seconds, and the poison never wears off.'
  - Id: poison.catching
    Title: Catching
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each time poison bites, one dose jumps from that enemy to the nearest other enemy in reach.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every bite of poison on any enemy, no matter how it was added.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. One dose to the nearest enemy within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom, and Miasma and the reaction lift The Delayed Touch, which come after it on the chart, Five Needles'' Spread shape and Master mark (more bites, more jumps), Share Alike, The Vessel.


      <b>Example.</b> Poison one soldier heavily and keep it near its friends. Every two seconds, one of them gets a dose without you hitting it.'
  - Id: poison.base
    Title: Miasma
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Miasma, Poison''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A low cloud rolls out in front of you, hangs there, creeps after the nearest enemy, and gives a dose of poison to whatever stands in it once a second. Bubbles rise through it, and it leaves a stain. You can stand in it without getting hurt.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Poison, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Poison. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 7 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds. The cloud lands 4 metres ahead of you, is 2.5 metres across, lasts 4 seconds, and gives a dose once a second to whatever stands in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; the root mark, Catching, which it comes after on the chart, Rupture and One by One (doses for them to burst), and the charged blade, which it charges with Poison.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s bigger cloud, 3 metres across. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Fill a doorway with the cloud where a pack must come through, then guard. Each one that comes through is poisoned.'
  - Id: poison.base.shape
    Title: 'Miasma: Low Mist or Censer'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Miasma. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Low Mist</b> (from this land''s hours of the miasma, when mist pools low and drives people uphill): the cloud is heavier than air and pours out from your feet along the floor in the direction you face. It is a low sheet with a hard top edge, and it stops at walls. Enemies in it get a dose once a second, and a dodge or roll in it costs them a dose. It is a long path, where the plain cloud is a circle. <b>Censer</b> (it used to be Swinging Censer): you carry the cloud. A censer swings around you on its chain, and each pass deals damage and a dose and leaves smoke hanging in the air. The fight comes with you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Miasma, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain cloud.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Low Mist: a strip of mist 8 metres long and 2.5 metres wide from your feet, lasting 5 seconds, giving a dose each second to whatever stands in it. Censer: one censer swings around you 3 metres away for 8 seconds, going around once every 1.2 seconds. It hits whatever is within 1 metre of it for 4 damage, 4 stagger and a dose.


      <b>Works with.</b> Low Mist: a hallway, Weeping Sore, Tasted Every Herb. Censer: a pack that crowds you, Catching, holding your guard while it swings.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Low Mist still casts the plain cloud alongside the strip, and it doesn''t stop at walls or take a dose when an enemy dodges. Censer''s smoke should hang in the air for 2 seconds after each pass.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Censer and hold your ground in a doorway. Anything that crowds you gets poisoned with every swing while you guard.'
  - Id: poison.base.master
    Title: 'Miasma: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast it on the poison''s beat, within a fifth of a second of a bite on any nearby enemy, and the cloud gives two doses a second.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each enemy the cloud poisons, while Miasma is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, an enemy the cloud poisons within a fifth of a second of its own bite gets one more dose each second for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time (the same beat), Catching, every other beat Master mark.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cast itself should be timed against a bite on any enemy within 8 metres. For now, each enemy is checked as the cloud poisons it.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it as a poisoned soldier''s poison bites, and the cloud gives it two doses a second while it stands there.'
  - Id: poison.lift
    Title: The Delayed Touch
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Delayed Touch, Poison''s reaction lift, from the legend of the touch that kills later. Every reaction that poison is part of is held inside the body. A thin ring of mist closes in on it, and the reaction lands again later at double strength, poisoning everything it reaches. A heavy blow on that enemy makes it happen right away. Poison makes them pay later, and your heavy blow chooses the moment.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a reaction with Poisoned in it happens, on any enemy. It lands two and a half seconds later, or at your first heavy blow on that enemy before then.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A burst with a 2.5-metre radius around the enemy. It deals 20 damage, 20 stagger and two doses to everything it reaches.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every reaction with Poisoned in it (Fire Medicine, Nerve Fire, Quicksilver, Venom Rime, Rot Roots, and the rest), Catching, which it comes after on the chart, Share Alike and Tasted Every Herb, which come after it, and a heavy weapon.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reaction itself should be held and land again at double strength. For now, a burst of its own lands when the hold ends. Burning Sap, which already waits, should only get the double strength. The closing ring of mist.


      <b>Example.</b> Quicksilver goes off on a soldier. Two and a half seconds later, or when your heavy blow lands, it bursts again and poisons the two soldiers next to it.'
  - Id: poison.sharealike
    Title: Share Alike
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Share Alike, the movement spell. You rush straight through everything in front of you, untouchable, and come out the far side facing back. Every enemy you pass is hit and poisoned, and then every one of them is raised to the same number of doses as the sickest one.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes it reach 9 metres.) It goes up to 7 metres along a path 2 metres wide. A wall, or an enemy too big to push past, stops you. Each enemy you pass takes 10 damage, 10 stagger and two doses.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Delayed Touch, which it comes after on the chart, Catching, One by One (a line of poisoned enemies), Rupture, and the charged blade: if it lands, it charges your weapon with Poison.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Raising every enemy you pass to the sickest one''s number of doses. For now, each one gets its two doses.


      <b>Example.</b> Rush through a line of three archers. Each one is poisoned, and you end up behind them.'
  - Id: poison.sharealike.shape
    Title: 'Share Alike: Carried on the Blade or Shed Tail'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Share Alike. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Carried on the Blade</b>: you rush through like the plain spell, but you carry the sickest enemy''s number of doses on your blade, and your next five weapon blows each set whatever they hit to that number. Venom drips heavily from the edge, five drops to count down. The rush loads your weapon. <b>Shed Tail</b> (like a gecko): you rush backward, away, and a thrashing tail is left where you stood, lashing everything near it. You get away, and whatever follows you pays.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Share Alike, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain rush.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Carried on the Blade: for 5 seconds after the rush, every blow adds two more doses. Shed Tail: where you stood, for 2 seconds, everything within 2 metres gets three doses every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carried on the Blade: a fast weapon, Rupture, In Time. Shed Tail: enemies that chase you, Catching, Low Mist.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Carried on the Blade should carry the sickest enemy''s number of doses for five blows; for now, it adds two more doses per blow for 5 seconds. Shed Tail should rush you backward; for now, you rush forward like the plain spell, and the tail is left where you started.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Carried on the Blade through a pack, then turn on the captain. Every blow of your chain adds three doses.'
  - Id: poison.sharealike.master
    Title: 'Share Alike: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Rush on the poison''s beat, within a fifth of a second of a bite on any enemy you pass, and the doses double.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each enemy the rush passes, while Share Alike is in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, each enemy you pass within a fifth of a second of its own bite gets two more doses.


      <b>Works with.</b> In Time and every beat Master mark, Catching (the beat spreads across the pack), One by One afterward.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should work on a bite from any enemy you pass. For now, each enemy is checked as you pass it.


      <b>Example.</b> Rush through a poisoned pack as their poison bites, and each one comes out with four more doses.'
  - Id: poison.devotion
    Title: Tasted Every Herb
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Tasted Every Herb, Poison''s Devotion, for going all in on Poison only. No poison, cloud or mist can hurt you, whether it is yours or the enemy''s, including the court''s miasma. Standing in any cloud heals you, and your blows add two doses while you stand in one. Where everyone else leaves the mist, you fight in it. It is named for Shennong, who tasted every poison, and for realgar wine, poison used against poison.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Poison twice (all in).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. You heal 2 a second while an enemy is nearby, and every time you add doses, you add one more. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Nine Times Refined. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Poison alone or twice.


      <b>Works with.</b> Miasma and its Low Mist shape, Venom, The Delayed Touch, which it comes after on the chart, Fever, Clean Blade.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Poison, clouds and mist should not hurt you; for now, the enemy''s clouds hurt you like before. The healing and the extra dose should only work while you stand in a cloud; for now, they work in every fight.


      <b>Example.</b> A god fills the floor with its own mist. You stay in it, healing, and every blow you land there adds two doses.'
  - Id: poison.cleanblade
    Title: Clean Blade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your surge lasts, the poison waits: it doesn''t bite and it doesn''t fade, and every bite it would have made is saved up. Your blows also add one more dose. When the surge ends, time slows down, you flick your blade clean, and every saved-up bite on every poisoned enemy nearby lands at once.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light the surge (tap L2, or press F); it starts with whichever surge you have picked. The flick comes when the surge ends. The extra dose only works while Poison is your first element.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. One more dose per blow, on top of Fever''s. When the surge ends, every enemy within 14 metres takes one bite for every full 2 seconds it waited, at however many doses it has then, plus 20 stagger. Fever''s 10 seconds save up five bites: 80 damage on an enemy with eight doses.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom, Fever, Slow Death (deeper bites), and Share Alike and Miasma to poison a pack while it waits. In Time, Catching and the beat Master marks need bites, and there aren''t any while the poison waits.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Fever, give a whole pack eight doses while the poison waits, and let the surge run out. Each enemy takes 80 at once.'
  - Id: poison.plaguebearer
    Title: Plague Bearer
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Plague Bearer, a keystone. You carry the sickness. Every enemy near you is poisoned without you hitting it, and more the longer it stays. Your flasks heal less.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry Poison, every second and a half.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. One dose of poison every 1.5 seconds on every enemy within 4 metres of you. Flasks heal 30% less.


      <b>Works with.</b> One by One, which it comes after on the chart (from its Master mark), Venom, Catching (each bite jumps onward), Volatile and Slow Death (the doses never stop coming), Rupture, Tasted Every Herb.


      <b>Example.</b> Stand in the middle of three soldiers and guard. Without landing a blow, each one has five doses within eight seconds.'
  - Id: pyre.shape
    Title: 'Fire Medicine: Fire Lance or Flying Fire'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Fire Medicine. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Fire Medicine uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Fire Lance</b>: the bang is a jet of fire out of the enemy''s far side, away from you. <b>Flying Fire</b>: the bang throws five burning pellets that land around the enemy as small fires.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Fire Medicine goes off while you carry both Poison and Fire. Fire Medicine goes off when Burn lands on a poisoned enemy, or when poison lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Fire Medicine. The plain one makes a bang 2.5 metres around the enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Fire Lance: after a 0.6-second fuse, a path 5 metres long and 2 metres wide past the enemy takes 4 damage per dose and 6 per Burn stack, plus 30 stagger, a dose and a Burn. Flying Fire: five patches, each 1 metre across, land in a ring 3 metres out and last 3 seconds. Each one burns for 3 damage and a Burn every half second, and poisons once a second. Both shapes use up half of the enemy''s doses. After Fire Medicine goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Goes Up (with full poison, the bang is twice as hard and uses nothing up), Funeral Fire, Miasma and Flame Wave to set it up, Slow Burn.


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soldiers stands behind a poisoned one. Stand in front, and Fire Lance burns all the way down the line. Holding a doorway: Flying Fire surrounds the poisoned enemy with fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Fire Lance''s jet is a straight path instead of a cone 40 degrees wide.'
  - Id: pyre.flashpoint
    Title: Goes Up
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When Fire Medicine goes off on an enemy whose poison is full, it hits twice as hard and uses nothing up. A second bang of the same size follows the first, and the doses it used are put back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Fire Medicine goes off on an enemy with full poison (eight doses), while you carry both Poison and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A second bang 2.5 metres around the enemy, 0.65 seconds after the reaction. It deals 32 damage, 30 stagger and a Burn to everything it reaches. Four doses are put back on the enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Miasma and Venom (eight doses fast), Fire Medicine''s shapes, Funeral Fire, Slow Burn.


      <b>Example.</b> Give a soldier eight doses, then set it on fire. Two bangs go off, and it still has eight doses.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reaction itself isn''t doubled yet. For now, a second bang takes its place, and the doses are put back after half are used up.'
  - Id: pyre.funeralfire
    Title: Funeral Fire
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies while poisoned and burning explodes, and leaves the ground burning where it fell.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The moment an enemy dies with both poison and Burn on it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A burst with a 3.5-metre radius deals 20 damage, 20 stagger, two doses of poison and one stack of Burn to everything in it. The ground burns 1.5 metres around for 4 seconds, dealing 5 damage and a stack of Burn every half second. At most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Goes Up, Catching Fire, Fire Medicine.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one poisoned, burning soldier in a crowd, and the rest catch both.'
  - Id: pyre.slowburn
    Title: Slow Burn
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every affliction you add works harder, including Burn and poison: every bite, and every time one is set off.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards. Afflictions are 30% stronger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Goes Up or Funeral Fire (it comes after either on the chart), Fire Medicine, Light the Pyres.


      <b>Example.</b> A five-stack burn that dealt 12.5 damage a second now deals over 16.'
  - Id: pyre.lightthepyres
    Title: Light the Pyres
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You kneel and drive your weapon into the ground. A line of fire runs to every poisoned or burning enemy nearby and follows it. Stay still through three breaths, and a pillar of flame rises under each one, setting off its burn and its poison. A dodge or getting hit breaks it. If no enemy is poisoned or burning, you get the mana back.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from its spell slot (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4) and stay still.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. 60 mana. It reaches up to six enemies within 9 metres. The three breaths take 2.2 seconds. Each pillar has a 2-metre radius and deals 40 damage and 40 stagger, counts as a heavy blow, and adds two doses of poison. On top of that, the poison is set off for 6 damage per dose, and the Burn for 10 damage plus 10 per stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Goes Up, Fire Medicine, Cinder, Slow Burn.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight until five enemies are burning, roll away from them, and light the pyres.'
  - Id: quicksilver.shape
    Title: 'Quicksilver: Heavy Silver or Running Silver'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Quicksilver. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Quicksilver uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes keep the beads of mercury: while the enemy has both afflictions, each of your weapon blows makes its poison bite right away. <b>Heavy Silver</b>: the mercury pools under the enemy, slowing it and poisoning anything that steps in. <b>Running Silver</b>: when the enemy dies, its mercury runs along the floor to the nearest enemy and gives it doses and notches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Quicksilver goes off while you carry both Metal and Poison. Quicksilver goes off when poison lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Quicksilver.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: each weapon blow makes the poison bite right away, at most once every half second on each enemy (each enemy keeps its own wait), and nothing is used up. Heavy Silver: the enemy moves 30% slower while it has both afflictions, and a puddle 1.5 metres across stays for 6 seconds where Quicksilver went off. It gives a dose every second and a notch every 2 seconds to whatever stands in it. Running Silver: when the enemy dies, every dose and every notch it had passes to the nearest enemy within 6 metres. After Quicksilver goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Miasma and Flung Blade to set it up, Envenomed Edge, The Nail Pulled, Ichor.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute that runs: use Heavy Silver. A pack: Running Silver moves the poison from one enemy to the next.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The puddle stays where Quicksilver went off, instead of following the enemy, and the enemy is slowed everywhere, not only while it stands in the puddle.'
  - Id: quicksilver.edge
    Title: Envenomed Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows add one notch and one dose of poison.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Metal and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One notch and one dose. For your first element''s affliction, it adds nothing beyond what that element''s root mark already adds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quicksilver (one heavy blow starts it), Cinnabar, The Nail Pulled.


      <b>Example.</b> Each heavy blow on a fresh enemy sets the mercury running.'
  - Id: quicksilver.nail
    Title: The Nail Pulled
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A finisher on an enemy with Quicksilver pulls out the nail: all its poison bites at once, twice.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Sundered and poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two bites at once, 0.3 seconds apart.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quicksilver, Envenomed Edge, Miasma.


      <b>Example.</b> Eight doses and a finisher: the poison lands twice.'
  - Id: quicksilver.cinnabar
    Title: Cinnabar
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Notches last as long as the poison does. While an enemy is poisoned, its notches don''t fade.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Metal and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Once the poison is gone, the notches start to fade as usual.


      <b>Works with.</b> Envenomed Edge, which it comes after on the chart, Quicksilver.


      <b>Example.</b> A poisoned soldier stays notched.'
  - Id: quicksilver.drink
    Title: The Undying Drink
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your flasks are mixed with quicksilver. Drinking one throws a ring of silver drops around you that gives three doses of poison and a notch to everything nearby. But each flask heals a fifth less.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you drink a flask, while you carry both Metal and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Three doses and a notch on everything within 4 metres. Flasks heal 20% less.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quicksilver (everything nearby now has both afflictions), The Nail Pulled, Wellspring.


      <b>Example.</b> Drink in the middle of a pack, and the pack starts getting eaten by its own poison.'
  - Id: quicksilver.ichor
    Title: Ichor
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You raise your guard. The first blow that lands on it is blocked, and your answer pulls out the nail: the attacker''s notches are filled all the way, it gets three doses of poison, and it bleeds for 4 seconds. While it bleeds, its poison bites every half second, and each of your weapon blows adds a dose. At the end, every dose goes off at once.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Metal and Poison. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, counter, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The guard stays up for 1.2 seconds. The answer is a cone 3 metres long that deals 10 damage and 20 stagger, fills the notches all the way, and adds three doses. The bleeding lasts 4 seconds. If nothing hits you, the wait is half as long: 5.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Quicksilver, The Nail Pulled, Envenomed Edge.


      <b>Example.</b> Block a brute''s swing, and it bleeds silver.'
  - Id: redhot.shape
    Title: 'Red Hot: Slag or The Burning Shirt'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Slag or Shirt unlocks two new shapes for Red Hot. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Red Hot uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Slag</b>: the enemy''s armour melts, dripping hot liquid metal that burns where it falls, so drive the enemy through its friends. <b>The Burning Shirt</b>: no sparks. Instead, the hot plates of armour cling to the enemy and burn it, and it can''t raise its guard.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Red Hot goes off while you carry both Metal and Fire. Red Hot goes off when a Burn lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Red Hot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Slag: for 5 seconds, the enemy drips a pool of melted metal each second. Each pool is 0.8 metres across and burns for 3 seconds, dealing 3 damage and a Burn each second to everything in it except the enemy dripping it. The Burning Shirt: for 5 seconds, the enemy can''t guard, and every 1.5 seconds it gets a Burn. In both shapes, its Burn bites twice as hard for those 5 seconds, like the plain Red Hot''s. After Red Hot goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heated Edge to set it up, Strike While Hot, Red-Short, Tipped Crucible, Heated Bronze.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute in a crowd: use Slag, and drive it among them. A shield bearer: use The Burning Shirt, and its guard is gone.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Slag drips each second where the enemy stands, instead of once for each metre it moves. The Burning Shirt doesn''t yet answer each attack the enemy starts (with two more Burns and a flinch). For now, it burns on a timer, and the enemy can''t guard for the whole 5 seconds, not only while it has five Burns.'
  - Id: redhot.heatededge
    Title: Heated Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add a Burn and a notch at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Metal and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Burn and one notch.


      <b>Works with.</b> Red Hot (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), Strike While Hot.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow, and the armour you hit glows.'
  - Id: redhot.strikewhilehot
    Title: Strike While Hot
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each heavy blow on a red-hot enemy gives you 4 mana and takes 0.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>When it happens.</b> During the 5 seconds after each Red Hot, on each heavy blow on that enemy, while you carry both Metal and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 4 mana and 0.5 seconds off every spell''s wait, for each heavy blow.


      <b>Works with.</b> Red Hot, Heated Edge, Tipped Crucible.


      <b>Example.</b> Hammer the glowing brute, and your spells come back sooner.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it keeps its own 5-second timer from each Red Hot, instead of checking whether the enemy is glowing.'
  - Id: redhot.redshort
    Title: Red-Short
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A finisher on a red-hot enemy crumbles its armour. Every notch is used up for 15 damage each, and for 10 seconds it takes 30% more damage from your weapon.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The first heavy blow on an enemy during the 5 seconds after its Red Hot, while you carry both Metal and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. 15 damage for each notch used up, and 30% more damage from your weapon for 10 seconds. Once for each Red Hot.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heated Edge, Red Hot, Strike While Hot.


      <b>Example.</b> Give it lots of notches, make it glow, then crack it open.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, any heavy blow crumbles it, not only a finisher, and it keeps its own 5-second timer from each Red Hot.'
  - Id: redhot.crucible
    Title: Tipped Crucible
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A pot of melted bronze hangs at your shoulder for 8 seconds, with four pours in it. Each pour that lands deals 14 damage, 25 stagger, a Burn and a notch, and the ground under it burns while the bronze is red. A heavy blow tips out all the pours that are left.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Metal and Fire. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards, 35 mana: the larger spell of this pair. Four pours, each dealing 14 damage, 25 stagger, a notch and a Burn, so Red Hot starts. The ground under each enemy it hits burns for 3 seconds, dealing 3 damage and a Burn twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Red Hot and its shapes, Heated Edge, Strike While Hot, Heated Bronze.


      <b>Example.</b> Raise the pot, close in, and land a heavy blow. The bronze pours over the pack.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The pours aren''t yet tipped out one by one along each heavy swing, as streams 4 metres long that cool from white-gold to black. For now, they fly at the nearest enemy on their own, or all at once on a heavy blow.'
  - Id: redhot.heatedbronze
    Title: Heated Bronze
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, you glow red all the way through, like the bronze guardian. Every blow that lands on you or your guard burns and notches the enemy that hit you. Your guard costs 30% less stamina. A perfect guard grabs the attacker, gives it three Burns, and throws it 2 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Metal and Fire. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: a Burn and a notch on whoever hits you or your guard; your guard costs 30% less stamina; a perfect guard adds three Burns and throws the attacker 2 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Red Hot, Tipped Crucible, Heated Edge.


      <b>Example.</b> Light your surge and let the pack break itself on your guard.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the perfect guard throws the attacker right away, instead of holding it for 1 second first.'
  - Id: rotroots.shape
    Title: 'Rot Roots: Gathered In or Summer Grass'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Bad Ground unlocks two new shapes for Rot Roots. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Rot Roots uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Gathered In</b>: the roots drink poison instead of giving it, pulling the crowd''s poison into the one enemy they grow from, ready for you to set off. <b>Summer Grass</b>: no roots. Instead, the fungus waits inside the enemy, and when it dies, a stalk grows from its head and bursts in a cloud of spores.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Rot Roots goes off while you carry both Life and Poison. Rot Roots goes off when poison lands on a Seeded enemy, or when a seed lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Rot Roots.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Gathered In: roots reach three enemies within 5 metres for 6 seconds, and each one is Seeded once. Each time poison bites one of them, one of its doses is taken and passed to the nearest other enemy within 5 metres. Summer Grass: lasts 20 seconds. If the enemy dies, 1.5 seconds later a puff of spores 4 metres across gives three doses and a Seed to everything in it, and you heal 6. After Rot Roots goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tasting, Deep Threads, The Host, The Stalks Rise (use up what Gathered In pulled together).


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd: use Gathered In on the toughest one, then set it off. A pack you will cut down one at a time: use Summer Grass, so each death Seeds the rest.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Gathered In''s doses go to the nearest other enemy (usually the one the roots grow from) instead of straight to it, and they aren''t capped at eight. Summer Grass heals you wherever you stand, not only inside the spores (at most once every 2 seconds).'
  - Id: rotroots.tasting
    Title: Tasting
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While you stand on a root, each poison bite on an enemy joined by the roots heals you 1.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each poison bite, while you carry both Life and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: 1 health for each bite on a Seeded enemy, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rot Roots (every bite runs down the roots), Deep Threads, The Host.


      <b>Example.</b> Spread roots through a pack and let the poison heal you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it heals you wherever you stand, and for any Seeded enemy bitten, not only one joined by roots.'
  - Id: rotroots.deepthreads
    Title: Deep Threads
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Rot Roots'' roots reach 7 metres and join one more enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Rot Roots goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The roots reach 7 metres instead of 5, and join four enemies instead of three. With Gathered In, the dose is passed up to 7 metres too.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rot Roots and its shapes, Tasting.


      <b>Example.</b> The roots find the archers at the back.'
  - Id: rotroots.host
    Title: The Host
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Whatever you grow feeds on poisoned enemies. When a poisoned enemy dies within 3 metres of something you grew, that thing stays 3 seconds longer and adds a dose of poison with every hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each poisoned enemy that dies, while you carry both Life and Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: while something you grew stands within 4 metres of you, a poisoned enemy that dies gives a dose to everything within 3 metres of where it fell, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rot Roots, the things Life grows (Briar Patch and the rest), The Stalks Rise.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight next to your briars, and each poisoned death spreads its rot.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The grown thing near the death doesn''t stay longer or start poisoning with its hits yet. For now, the dose goes to the enemies near where it fell, and only while something you grew stands near you.'
  - Id: rotroots.stalks
    Title: The Stalks Rise
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every enemy within 9 metres that is both Seeded and Poisoned grows a dark stalk from its head. After 1.2 seconds, each stalk bursts, using up both: it deals 5 damage per dose and 8 per seed within 2.5 metres, and the spores give two doses to everything nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Life and Poison. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 14 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. On each enemy that has both: 5 damage for each dose and 8 for each seed to everything within 2.5 metres, plus 15 stagger, and two doses on each.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rot Roots and Gathered In (gather the doses first), Tasting, The Host.


      <b>Example.</b> Spread the roots through the pack, let the poison gather, then raise the stalks.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the seeds are used up, but the doses are only counted and stay on the enemy. A weapon blow on an enemy while its stalk stands doesn''t double its spores yet.'
  - Id: rust.shape
    Title: 'Rust: Seized Joints or Flaking'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Rust. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Rust uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still rust the enemy like the plain one. <b>Seized Joints</b>: while the enemy rusts, its joints get stiff, so it walks and turns more slowly. Go around it. <b>Flaking</b>: each heavy blow knocks the rust off in a shower of flakes that notch and soak nearby enemies.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Rust goes off while you carry both Water and Metal. Rust goes off when Soaked lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on a soaked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Rust.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: while the enemy is Soaked, its notches stay and spread, one more every 2 seconds. While it rusts, notches can go past three, up to five. Once it is rusted all the way through (five notches), a plate of armour falls off, and weapon blows hit it 16% harder for 6 seconds. Seized Joints: the enemy moves a fifth slower while it rusts. Flaking: a heavy blow uses up its notches, dealing 6 damage for each one to everything within 3 metres, plus a notch and a Soaked. After Rust goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Flung Blade to set it up, Salt Edge, Salt Guard, The Sea Takes It.


      <b>Example.</b> An armoured brute: use Seized Joints, and walk around to its back. A pack: use Flaking, and land heavy blows on the rusted one.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Seized Joints slows by a fifth, instead of a little more for each notch, including turning.'
  - Id: rust.saltedge
    Title: Salt Edge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows add one notch and Soak the enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Water and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One notch and one Soaked. For your first element''s affliction, it adds nothing beyond what that element''s root mark already adds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rust (one heavy blow starts it), Brine, The Wreck.


      <b>Example.</b> Each heavy blow on a fresh enemy starts it rusting.'
  - Id: rust.saltguard
    Title: Salt Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard adds one notch and Soaks the attacker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One notch and one Soaked on the attacker.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rust, Salt Edge, Counter Cut.


      <b>Example.</b> Guard a brute perfectly, and it starts to rust.'
  - Id: rust.brine
    Title: Brine
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Soaked lasts twice as long on a notched enemy.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Water and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. While the enemy has a notch, its Soaked fades at half the usual speed.


      <b>Works with.</b> Salt Edge, which it comes after on the chart, Rust.


      <b>Example.</b> A notched soldier stays wet, so it keeps rusting.'
  - Id: rust.wreck
    Title: The Wreck
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A rusted enemy that dies leaves its armour on the floor as a wreck that Soaks and notches anything standing in it. Your heavy blows from inside it can''t be interrupted by blows you take.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy with both Soaked and Sundered dies, while you carry both Water and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A wreck 1.5 metres across that lasts 8 seconds. It adds a Soaked every second and a notch every 2 seconds to whatever stands in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rust, Salt Edge, The Sea Takes It.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight from inside the wreck of the last soldier you killed.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your heavy blows from inside it can still be interrupted for now.'
  - Id: rust.seatakesit
    Title: The Sea Takes It
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A wave of salt water rolls ahead of you. Everything it crosses is Soaked, and every notched enemy it crosses rusts all the way through right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Water and Metal. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 13 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches 7 metres ahead and is 50 degrees wide. It deals 8 damage, 10 stagger and a Soaked. Notched enemies get their notches filled all the way.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rust and its shapes, Salt Edge, The Wreck.


      <b>Example.</b> Notch a pack with Flung Blade, then roll the sea through it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The enemies'' joints don''t get stiff yet (turning 40% slower), and a heavy blow on a stiff one doesn''t knock a plate of armour off yet.'
  - Id: seized.shape
    Title: 'Seized: Can''t Let Go or Running Water'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Seized. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Seized uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Can''t Let Go</b>: no film of water. Instead, your blade keeps the one enemy locked in place. <b>Running Water</b>: no hold. Instead, the electrified water runs outward and locks up whatever it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Seized goes off while you carry both Lightning and Water. Seized goes off when a Soaked enemy has two or more Static. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Seized.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Can''t Let Go: the enemy is held for 1 second, and for 3 seconds, each of your weapon blows on it holds it 0.3 seconds longer. Running Water: everything within 7 metres is held for 0.5 seconds and gets a Static. After Seized goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Thunderclap to set it up, Wet Thread, Grounded, The Flashing.


      <b>Example.</b> One brute: use Can''t Let Go, and keep cutting. A wet pack: Running Water locks them all up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Running Water reaches everything within 7 metres at once, instead of spreading out as a ring. Can''t Let Go doesn''t have its 3-second limit on the hold yet.'
  - Id: seized.wetthread
    Title: Wet Thread
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Light blows on a Soaked enemy add one Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every light blow that lands on a Soaked enemy, while you carry both Lightning and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide, Seized (two Static on a wet enemy lock it up), Salt Water.


      <b>Example.</b> Two light blows on a soaked soldier, and it locks up.'
  - Id: seized.grounded
    Title: Grounded
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on a locked-up enemy sends its Static into the floor. Everything standing in electrified water takes the full burst.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Soaked and Static.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Its Static goes off, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack to everything within 4 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seized, Wet Thread, The Sea Finds Land.


      <b>Example.</b> A soaked pack surrounds a locked-up enemy. One heavy blow, and the floor bursts with lightning.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it reaches everything within 4 metres, instead of everything standing in electrified water.'
  - Id: seized.saltwater
    Title: Salt Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The film of electrified water stays 2 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Seized goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The film of electrified water lasts 5 seconds instead of 3.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wet Thread, which it comes after on the chart, Seized.


      <b>Example.</b> The film keeps charging enemies that wade into it.'
  - Id: seized.seafindsland
    Title: The Sea Finds Land
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Static on a Soaked enemy doesn''t need a heavy blow. When it fills up, it goes off on its own, through the water, to every Soaked enemy within 8 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Static fills up on a Soaked enemy, while you carry both Lightning and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack within 8 metres, at most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wet Thread, The Flashing, Riptide.


      <b>Example.</b> Fill a wet soldier with Static, and the whole wet floor goes off.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it reaches every enemy within 8 metres, not only the soaked ones.'
  - Id: seized.flashing
    Title: The Flashing
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You slap the floor, and a flash runs across the ground to an enemy, then darts on to the next, up to five. Each one it reaches is hit, Soaked, and charged twice, so it locks up right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Lightning and Water. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 10 metres to the first enemy, and up to 6 metres between jumps. Each enemy takes 12 damage, 20 stagger, two Static and a Soaked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seized and its shapes, Grounded, The Sea Finds Land.


      <b>Example.</b> Start a fight with it on a pack: five enemies lock up at once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It doesn''t look for the nearest soaked enemy or run along the floor yet, and its path doesn''t stay wet. Your next blow on the last enemy it reached doesn''t set off everything it touched yet.'
  - Id: shiningherb.shape
    Title: 'Shining Herb: Bitter Leaf or Seed of Light'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Shining Herb. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Shining Herb uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Bitter Leaf</b>: the herb grows on the enemy, and your next blow on it picks the herb. It''s for players who stay on one target. <b>Seed of Light</b>: no herb. Instead, the seed bursts on your next blow as a flash of light that Marks and Seeds nearby enemies.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Shining Herb goes off while you carry both Life and Light. Shining Herb goes off when a Mark lands on a seeded enemy, or when Seeded lands on a Marked one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Shining Herb.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Bitter Leaf: for 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy heals you 8. Seed of Light: for 6 seconds, your next weapon blow on the enemy Marks and Seeds everything within 4 metres. After Shining Herb goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch and Glare to set it up, Sixth Moon, Open to the Sun, Turned to the Sun.


      <b>Example.</b> A long one-on-one fight: Bitter Leaf heals you as you fight. A pack: Seed of Light spreads both afflictions through it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, any weapon blow picks or bursts it, not only a critical hit. The next blow after picking the herb isn''t a critical hit yet.'
  - Id: shiningherb.sixthmoon
    Title: Sixth Moon
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The blow that ends a chain of blows on a Seeded enemy Marks it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The last blow of each chain that lands on a Seeded enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Mark.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch, Shining Herb, Long Day.


      <b>Example.</b> End each chain on a seeded soldier, and a herb springs up.'
  - Id: shiningherb.opentosun
    Title: Open to the Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked enemy gives it three stacks of Seeded.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has a Mark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Three Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glare, Shining Herb, Sixth Moon.


      <b>Example.</b> A Marked brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and a herb springs up from it.'
  - Id: shiningherb.longday
    Title: Long Day
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Herbs stay 3 seconds longer before they flower.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Shining Herb goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Herbs stay for 9 seconds before they flower, instead of 6. It''s the same in both shapes.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sixth Moon, which it comes after on the chart, Shining Herb.


      <b>Example.</b> More time to step in and pick the herb.'
  - Id: shiningherb.wholemountain
    Title: The Whole Mountain
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Picking a herb pulls every other herb across the floor to you, and each one picked after the first heals you twice as much.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Shining Herb goes off, while you carry both Life and Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: you heal 4 each time a herb springs up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Shining Herb, Sixth Moon, Open to the Sun.


      <b>Example.</b> A fight full of herbs keeps you on your feet.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Herbs aren''t things you can pick up from the floor yet, so none are pulled to you. For now, you heal each time one springs up instead.'
  - Id: shiningherb.turnedtosun
    Title: Turned to the Sun
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You plant a glowing herb where you aim. Every 2 seconds, it flashes, and every Seeded or Marked enemy nearby turns to face it, so their backs are open to you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Life and Light. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands up to 7 metres ahead of you, covers a circle with a 3-metre radius, and lasts 6 seconds. Every 2 seconds, it deals 2 damage, a Seeded and a Mark to everything in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Shining Herb, Sixth Moon, The Whole Mountain.


      <b>Example.</b> Plant it in a pack, and every enemy in it gets both afflictions.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Enemies don''t turn to face it yet, so blows on their backs aren''t critical hits yet.'
  - Id: sicklewind.shape
    Title: 'Sickle Wind: Sent Downwind or Cut Unfelt'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Loose Plates unlocks two new shapes for Sickle Wind. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Sickle Wind uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Sent Downwind</b>: the whirlwind of torn armour plates leaves its enemy and drifts along with the last wind, cutting and notching everything it passes. Aim it with Gust and your shoves. <b>Cut Unfelt</b>: at first, the shards don''t draw blood. When the whirlwind ends, every cut opens at once for 50% more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Sickle Wind goes off while you carry both Wind and Metal. Sickle Wind goes off when Unsteady lands on a notched enemy, or when a notch lands on an Unsteady one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Sickle Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Sent Downwind: three patches of whirling grit land in a line past the enemy, 2 metres apart and 1 metre across, for 3 seconds. They cut for 3 damage and 4 stagger every 0.4 seconds. Half a second in, each enemy there loses a notch of armour. Cut Unfelt: nothing happens for 3 seconds. Then it deals 32 damage and 28 stagger within 1.5 metres, plus 6 more for each weapon blow you landed on the enemy during those 3 seconds. After Sickle Wind goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bellows, Grit, Wind Sickle, Three Weasels.


      <b>Example.</b> A line of soldiers: use Sent Downwind, and shove the first one into the rest. One armoured brute: use Cut Unfelt, and pile on blows before the bill comes due.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Sent Downwind''s whirlwind doesn''t drift yet at 2 metres a second in the direction the enemy was last shoved. For now, it lands all at once in a line past the enemy. Cut Unfelt''s final hit is a set amount plus your blows, instead of the cuts the shards made, added up and paid back at 50% more.'
  - Id: sicklewind.bellows
    Title: Bellows
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A dodge that ends within 3 metres of a whirlwind feeds it: it gets one more shard, and its next cut adds Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each dodge that ends, while you carry both Wind and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: one Unsteady on everything within 3 metres of where you stop, at most once every 2 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sickle Wind (an Unsteady added to a notched enemy starts it), Grit, Wind Sickle.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge in next to a notched brute, and the wind takes its armour plates.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It doesn''t feed a shard to a whirlwind yet. For now, it makes everything near where your dodge ends Unsteady, whirlwind or not.'
  - Id: sicklewind.grit
    Title: Grit
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Sickle Wind''s whirlwind has one more shard and lasts 1 second longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Sickle Wind goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The whirlwind cuts for 6 damage each time instead of 3, and lasts about 4 seconds instead of 3. With Sent Downwind, it adds one more patch, and the patches last 4 seconds. With Cut Unfelt, you have 4 seconds to add blows to the final hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sickle Wind and its shapes, Bellows.


      <b>Example.</b> Even a fresh enemy throws off a full whirlwind.'
  - Id: sicklewind.windsickle
    Title: Wind Sickle
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The last blow of a chain of light blows throws a sickle of wind 5 metres in the direction of the swing. It deals 6 damage and an Unsteady, and a notch torn from a Sundered enemy becomes a shard that joins the nearest whirlwind.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The last blow of each chain of light blows that lands, while you carry both Wind and Metal.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A cone 5 metres long and 30 degrees wide. It deals 6 damage, 6 stagger and one Unsteady to everything in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sickle Wind (a notched enemy it reaches starts its whirlwind), Bellows, Three Weasels.


      <b>Example.</b> End each chain facing the pack, and the sickles keep them off balance.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Turning the torn notch into a shard for the nearest whirlwind. For now, it is only the sickle''s cut.'
  - Id: sicklewind.weasels
    Title: Three Weasels
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Three presses within 4 seconds, like the three sickle weasels working together. First, a rush of wind to the enemy you face that throws it. Second, a pass straight through it that notches its armour. Third, a step back out of reach that patches up your wounds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Wind and Metal. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Wind. Press the same button again within 4 seconds for the second weasel, and again for the third.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After the first press, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. First press: a step of up to 5 metres to the enemy, dealing 6 damage, 20 stagger and two Unsteady, and throwing it 2 metres. Your next heavy blow within 2 seconds is a critical hit. Second press: you pass straight through the enemy you face to 3 metres past it, and nothing can hit you for 0.4 seconds. It deals 12 damage, 10 stagger and a notch. Third press: you step 4 metres back from the enemy and heal 2 for each weapon blow you landed since the first press, up to 16.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sickle Wind (it adds both afflictions at once), Bellows, Wind Sickle.


      <b>Example.</b> Rush the archer and send its armour plates whirling.'
  - Id: sleetcharge.shape
    Title: 'Sleet Charge: Thundersnow or Glass Spear'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Sleet Charge. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Sleet Charge uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes keep the charging: the frost adds a Static to the enemy every half second for 3 seconds. They change how it ends. <b>Thundersnow</b>: the bolt that ends it falls on every chilled enemy nearby. <b>Glass Spear</b>: the bolt freezes as it lands, and the enemy is Frozen right away, no matter how much Chill it has.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Sleet Charge goes off while you carry both Ice and Lightning. Sleet Charge goes off when Static lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Sleet Charge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: a Static every 0.5 seconds for 3 seconds, then the Static goes off, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack to everything within 4 metres. Thundersnow: then a bolt jumps on to every chilled enemy within 6 metres of the last one, up to six, dealing 7 damage, 5 stagger and a Static to each. Glass Spear: then a normal enemy is Frozen for 1.5 seconds, however much Chill it had. An elite gets 3 Chill and a god 2. A heavy blow still sets off the Static early. After Sleet Charge goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hoarfrost or Glass Storm to add the Chill, Thunderclap or Static to add the charge, Charged Hail (Glass Spear''s frozen enemy is ready to shatter), Nothing Resists.


      <b>Example.</b> A frozen line of soldiers: use Thundersnow and charge one of them. Three seconds later, the bolt runs through every chilled enemy near it. Against one brute: use Glass Spear, and when the charge breaks, it stands frozen for your heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Thundersnow''s bolt jumps from one chilled enemy to the next instead of falling on each one at once, and each takes 7 damage instead of setting off the Static it has. The spear of clear ice isn''t drawn yet.'
  - Id: sleetcharge.hail
    Title: Charged Hail
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Shattering an enemy that had Static throws its charge into the shards. Everything the shards hit gets a Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever a frozen enemy with Static shatters (from a heavy blow or a finisher on a frozen enemy).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A Static on everything within 3 metres of the shattered enemy, at most five times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Glass Spear (it freezes a charged enemy for you to shatter), Glass Storm, Nothing Resists, and Sleet Charge on whatever the shards chilled.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze a charged soldier and shatter it in a pack. The three around it are charged, and the chilled ones start their own Sleet Charge.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the charge goes in a ring around the shattered enemy, instead of from each shard that hits.'
  - Id: sleetcharge.snap
    Title: Cold Snap
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a chilled enemy makes its Sleet Charge go off right away, with full Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has Chill.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The enemy''s Static is filled all the way (five), and it goes off right away, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack (35 damage and 25 stagger at full) to everything within 4 metres. At most once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hoarfrost, Glass Storm, Thundersnow, Nothing Resists, and the time a perfect guard takes off your spell waits.


      <b>Example.</b> A chilled brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it takes 35 damage, and its pack takes the same.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It doesn''t end a Sleet Charge that is already running. For now, the full charge goes off alongside it, and a new Sleet Charge may start from the Static it added.'
  - Id: sleetcharge.resists
    Title: Nothing Resists
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cold carries lightning without losing any of it. When Static goes off on a chilled enemy, it keeps going at full strength through the chilled enemies nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Static is set off on an enemy that has Chill (by a heavy blow, the end of a Sleet Charge, or Cold Snap).


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. An arc runs from the enemy through up to four chilled enemies, each within 5 metres of the last one, dealing 7 damage, 5 stagger and a Static to each, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thundersnow, Cold Snap, Charged Hail, and Hoarfrost and Glass Storm to keep the pack chilled.


      <b>Example.</b> Glass Storm has chilled a pack of five. One heavy blow on a charged one, and the arc runs through the other four.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it only works when Static goes off, not on every arc that leaves a chilled enemy. Each arc after the first deals its own 7 damage, instead of carrying the full hit along.'
  - Id: sleetcharge.glassstorm
    Title: Glass Storm
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Six shards of clear ice spin around you, 2 metres away, for 8 seconds, with threads of lightning jumping between them. Each shard that passes through an enemy cuts it and adds a Chill, and every other pass adds a Static too, so the ring sets up Sleet Charge on any enemy that pushes in.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Ice and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Ice, this branch''s first element.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 14 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Six shards spin for 8 seconds. Each one that passes through an enemy hits it at most once every half second, for 4 damage, 4 stagger and a Chill. Every other pass on an enemy adds a Static as well. Each enemy keeps its own count.


      <b>Works with.</b> Sleet Charge and its shapes, Charged Hail, Nothing Resists, Cold Snap, and any heavy blow on a chilled, charged enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it as a pack closes in. Every enemy the ring cuts is chilled, the ones it cuts twice are charged, and Sleet Charge starts going off all around you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your weapon blows don''t throw the shards yet (each blow that lands should throw the nearest shard into the enemy you hit, for 8 damage, a Chill and a Static, and a heavy blow should throw all that are left). The shards look like toothed discs until their glassy look is made.'
  - Id: smoulder.shape
    Title: 'Smoulder: Night Sun or Coal Seam'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Banked Coals unlocks two new shapes for Smoulder. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Smoulder uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Night Sun</b>: the fire sinks into the enemy like before, and while it smoulders, each of your blows on it heals you. <b>Coal Seam</b>: the fire goes down into the floor where Smoulder went off, leaving a patch of smouldering ground. Any burning enemy that stands on it is Cursed, so it smoulders too.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Smoulder goes off while you carry both Dark and Fire. Smoulder goes off when a Curse lands on a burning enemy, or when Burn lands on a Cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Smoulder.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Night Sun: works like the plain Smoulder (its Burn grows by one stack every 2 seconds while the Curse lasts, and flares up for 10 damage per stack within 3 metres when the Curse ends or the enemy dies). On top of that, each of your blows on it heals you 1 for each Burn on it (3 at three Burns), at most five times a second. Coal Seam: a patch 2.5 metres across that lasts 8 seconds and Curses whatever stands on it every 2 seconds. This is on purpose: that Curse may start Smoulder. After Smoulder goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grasp and Cinder to set it up, Third Eye, The Sun Goes Under, The Burning Lake.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Night Sun on a brute you are trading blows with. Use Coal Seam at a doorway, then fight the burning enemies across it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Coal Seam''s patch doesn''t yet flare up at the end and set off every smoulder on it.'
  - Id: smoulder.thirdeye
    Title: Third Eye
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a burning, Cursed enemy opens a line of black-edged fire from you through it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has both Burn and a Curse.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A line 6 metres long in front of you. It deals 20 damage, 2 Burns and a Curse to everything on it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Smoulder, Deep Coal, Breath of the Lioness.


      <b>Example.</b> A smouldering soldier swings. Guard it perfectly, and burn a line through it and the two soldiers behind it.'
  - Id: smoulder.deepcoal
    Title: Deep Coal
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Burn on a smouldering enemy can stack two higher.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Dark and Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards.


      <b>Works with.</b> Smoulder (it grows on its own while the Curse lasts), Fire Without End.


      <b>Example.</b> A smouldering brute reaches seven Burns instead of five before it flares up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It does nothing yet. The game can''t yet let an affliction stack higher on one enemy.'
  - Id: smoulder.sungoesunder
    Title: The Sun Goes Under
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your health is below half, every Burn you add smoulders, Curse or no Curse.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Dark and Fire and your health is below half.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: below half health, each blow that lands on a burning enemy Curses it, at most once a second, so Smoulder starts.


      <b>Works with.</b> Pawned Heart and the Dark marks you pay for with health, Smoulder and its shapes, Third Eye.


      <b>Example.</b> When you''re hurt, every enemy you set on fire starts to smoulder.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it Curses the burning enemy, instead of making the Burn itself smoulder.'
  - Id: smoulder.lake
    Title: The Burning Lake
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A lake opens where you aim: a still black surface, with flames licking at its edge and embers rising. Every enemy on it is Cursed and gets a Burn each second, so all of them smoulder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Dark and Fire. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 16 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It lands up to 8 metres ahead of you, is 4 metres across, and lasts 7 seconds. Once a second, it deals 2 damage, a Curse and a Burn to everything on it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Smoulder and its shapes, The Sun Goes Under, Third Eye.


      <b>Example.</b> Open it under a pack and let them smoulder while you hold the edge.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It doesn''t slow enemies standing on it yet, grow with each enemy killed on it, or make every smoulder flare up at once on a finisher.'
  - Id: steam.shape
    Title: 'Steam: Blind Steam or Scald'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Two Sisters unlocks two new shapes for Steam. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Steam uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Blind Steam</b>: the cloud of steam doesn''t rise. It stays low around the enemy, a white cloud that enemies inside can''t see out of. <b>Scald</b>: no cloud. Instead, the water flashes into steam in a harder burst that throws everything nearby, and the Burn isn''t used up but keeps boiling.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Steam goes off while you carry both Fire and Water. Steam goes off when Fire lands on a Soaked enemy, or when Water lands on a burning one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Steam.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Blind Steam: like the plain one, the Soaked is used up, along with half the Burn. The cloud covers 3 metres around the enemy for 4 seconds and stings for 3 damage a second. Enemies in it when it forms can only see 2 metres for those 4 seconds, and their swings come a fifth slower. Scald: the Soaked is used up, but the Burn stays. A burst within 2.5 metres deals 14 damage, plus 6 more for each Burn the enemy has, and 30 stagger, and throws everything in it 1.5 metres. After Steam goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hissing Blade to set it up, Strike Through Steam, Kettle, The Sisters'' Quarrel.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd closing in on you: use Blind Steam, and they lose you in the cloud. One brute with lots of Burn: use Scald, again and again, since its Burn is never used up.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> An enemy that walks into Blind Steam''s cloud after it forms gets stung, but not blinded.'
  - Id: steam.hissingblade
    Title: Hissing Blade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add Burn and Soaked at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Fire and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Burn and one Soaked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Steam (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), Strike Through Steam.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow, and the enemy you hit steams right away.'
  - Id: steam.strikethrough
    Title: Strike Through Steam
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A weapon blow on an enemy within a second of its Steam is a scald: it does 50% more damage and stagger.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For one second after Steam goes off on an enemy, while you carry both Fire and Water. It works on your first weapon blow on that enemy in that second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. The blow deals 1.5 times its damage and stagger. During that second, every bit of stagger the enemy takes is raised the same way, even from spells.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hissing Blade (the heavy blow that makes it steam, then a quick light blow), Steam.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow to make it steam, then cut through the cloud right away.'
  - Id: steam.kettle
    Title: Kettle
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A Soaked enemy that dies while burning goes up as steam at full strength, with no wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a Soaked, burning enemy dies, while you carry both Fire and Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. A burst deals 8 damage, plus 4 more for each Burn the enemy had, and 15 stagger to everything within 2.5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hissing Blade, Steam, The Sisters'' Quarrel.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a soaked, burning soldier in the middle of its pack, and the pack is scalded.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it bursts like the plain Steam, whatever shape you picked, instead of in your chosen shape.'
  - Id: steam.quarrel
    Title: The Sisters' Quarrel
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Flame from one hand and a wave from the other close in like pincers on the enemy you face, up to 7 metres away. Where they meet, steam bursts, dealing 30 damage and 55 stagger within 3.5 metres, and Steam goes off at full strength. For 3 seconds after, your weapon blows on the enemies caught in it hit harder and burn.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Fire and Water. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The two hits close in over 0.45 seconds, each dealing 8 damage and 10 stagger, with one adding Burn and the other Soaked. The blast deals 30 damage and 55 stagger within 3.5 metres. Steam goes off on the enemy even if it would normally have to wait. For 3 seconds, your weapon blows on the enemy deal a quarter more damage, and each one adds a Burn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Steam and its shapes, Strike Through Steam, Kettle.


      <b>Example.</b> Trap the captain between flame and wave, then cut it while it is scalded.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The flame and the wave don''t run along two curved paths yet, hitting what they cross. For now, two hits close in on the enemy from a ring around it.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.shape
    Title: 'Stolen Warmth: Cold Shade or The Long Night'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Let the Cold In unlocks two new shapes for Stolen Warmth. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Stolen Warmth uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Cold Shade</b>: the enemy''s warmth is drawn into you like before, and a shattered enemy leaves its shadow behind as a figure of black ice that chills everything nearby. <b>The Long Night</b>: you don''t draw any health. Instead, the warmth goes into the dark, and while the enemy is frozen, the cold spreads to everything near it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Stolen Warmth goes off while you carry both Dark and Ice. Stolen Warmth goes off when a Curse lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a Cursed one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Stolen Warmth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: while the enemy has both afflictions, its Chill doesn''t fade, and each of your weapon blows on it adds a Chill. Cold Shade: each blow also heals you 2 (1 against a god), at most four times a second. When the enemy dies, a patch 5 metres across stays for 4 seconds where it fell, chilling whatever stands in it once a second. The Long Night: no healing. While the enemy is frozen, everything within 3 metres gets a Chill once a second. After Stolen Warmth goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hel''s Half, Deep Veins, No Summer, Ice in the Blood, Three Winters.


      <b>Example.</b> Fighting alone and hurt: use Cold Shade, and heal as you hit. A pack around a big enemy: use The Long Night, freeze the big one, and let the cold take the rest.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Cold Shade''s shadow is left when the enemy dies instead of when it is shattered, and it is just a chilling patch. You can''t hit it with a heavy blow to shatter it a second time yet.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.helshalf
    Title: Hel's Half
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Killing an enemy whose warmth is being stolen Curses every enemy within 4 metres and gives each one two Chill.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each kill, while you carry both Dark and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: any enemy that dies while both Cursed and chilled puts a Curse and two Chill on everything within 4 metres, at most once every half second. Since this mark adds both at once, it starts Stolen Warmth on each of them.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stolen Warmth (it spreads from body to body), Three Winters, No Summer.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one enemy in the pack, and the cold spreads into the rest.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any enemy that dies Cursed and chilled, whether its warmth was being stolen or not.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.deepveins
    Title: Deep Veins
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each blow on an enemy whose warmth is being stolen draws 3 health instead of 2, up to 12 a second.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each weapon blow on an enemy like that.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Each blow draws 3 health instead of 2 (1.5 from a god), at most four times a second: 12 a second. It''s the same with Cold Shade.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stolen Warmth and Cold Shade, Hel''s Half.


      <b>Example.</b> A long fight with one brute keeps you at full health.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.nosummer
    Title: No Summer
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hunger costs no health while any enemy within 8 metres is Frozen.


      <b>When it happens.</b> While you have Dark''s Hunger as your surge, and carry both Dark and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: for 4 seconds after each shatter, costs paid with health are skipped.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stolen Warmth (its Chill doesn''t fade, so freezing comes easily), The Long Night, Three Winters, Dark''s Hunger.


      <b>Example.</b> Keep one enemy frozen near you, and feed Hunger for free.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It doesn''t check yet whether an enemy near you is Frozen. For now, the cost is skipped for 4 seconds after each shatter, and for every cost paid with health, not only Hunger''s.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.iceinblood
    Title: Ice in the Blood
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A sign of black frost is put on the enemy you face for 6 seconds. Each of your next four weapon blows on it freezes one of its veins, for 8 damage and two Chill. The fourth breaks it from the inside: 35 damage, 50 stagger, and a Curse and two Chill on everything within 3 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Dark and Ice. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 8 metres. Your weapon blows set it off for 35 damage, 50 stagger, a Curse and two Chill to everything within 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stolen Warmth (everything it hits gets both afflictions), Hel''s Half, Three Winters.


      <b>Example.</b> Put the sign on the captain, land four blows, and its guard breaks in frost.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The veins aren''t frozen one at a time yet. For now, your blows set off the sign directly, and it doesn''t wait for the fourth blow.'
  - Id: stolenwarmth.threewinters
    Title: Three Winters
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, frost creeps over the floor around you, and every enemy that dies freezes where it falls as a statue of black ice for 5 seconds. Each statue adds a stack of Chill every second within 2.5 metres. A heavy blow on a statue shatters it, dealing 25 damage, two Chill and a Curse within 3 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Dark and Ice. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: each enemy that dies leaves a patch 5 metres across for 5 seconds, chilling whatever stands in it once a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Stolen Warmth, Hel''s Half, No Summer, Ice in the Blood.


      <b>Example.</b> Light your surge in a crowd, and each death freezes the floor around it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The frost on the floor isn''t drawn yet, and no statue appears for you to shatter. For now, each death leaves a chilling patch.'
  - Id: tempest.shape
    Title: 'Thunder Gust: Thunderhead or Bolt from the Blue'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Thunder Gust. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Thunder Gust uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Thunderhead</b>: the enemy is thrown up, not away, and a bolt meets it at the top and sets off its Static over the heads of the rest. <b>Bolt from the Blue</b>: nothing is thrown. Instead, its Static goes off where it stands and keeps going as an arc through the enemies behind it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Thunder Gust goes off while you carry both Lightning and Wind. Thunder Gust goes off when Unsteady lands on a charged enemy, or when Static lands on an Unsteady one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Thunder Gust.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Thunderhead: the enemy is thrown up for 1 second. Half a second in, its Static goes off, dealing 7 damage and 5 stagger per stack to everything within 4 metres. Bolt from the Blue: its Static goes off within 1.5 metres, then an arc runs through up to three enemies, each up to 5 metres from the last, dealing 14 damage, 10 stagger and a Static to each. After Thunder Gust goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Sky Finds Them (Thunderhead''s throw calls its bolt), Slip Current, Static, Gust.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack around one charged soldier: Thunderhead lifts it out of the middle, and the bolt bursts over all of them. A line of archers: Bolt from the Blue sends the charge down the line.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Bolt from the Blue''s arc jumps to the nearest enemies, instead of along the line behind the first one, and each takes 14 damage instead of the whole burst it would have had.'
  - Id: tempest.slip
    Title: Slip Current
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Where a dodge ends, every enemy close to you gets one more stack of Static and one more of Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At the end of every dodge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One stack of Static and one of Unsteady on every enemy within 2.2 metres of where you stop.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thunder Gust''s shapes, Blink, Storm Step, Tailwind.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll into the middle of three soldiers. All three get a stack of each before you''ve even swung.'
  - Id: tempest.sky
    Title: The Sky Finds Them
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy you throw into the air is hit by a bolt, which jumps on to two more enemies near it and leaves Static on each.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time one of your marks throws an enemy into the air, like Thunder Gust''s Thunderhead. For now, the bolt falls as soon as the enemy is thrown up, not when it reaches the top.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 14 damage and two stacks of Static to the enemy you threw, then the same to up to two more, each within 4 metres of the last.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thunder Gust''s Thunderhead shape, Static, Updraft.


      <b>Example.</b> Thunderhead throws a soldier up. The bolt hits it and the two soldiers next to it, leaving each with two stacks of Static.'
  - Id: tempest.eye
    Title: Eye of the Storm
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You become the storm: a spinning column of wind and lightning around you that drags enemies in and bites them, and carries the light ones around. While it spins, you walk more slowly, take half damage, and can''t be made to flinch. Let go, and it throws them all out, and a bolt meets each one where it lands.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4), and keep holding it. Letting go ends it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 5 Shards, 50 mana. It lasts up to 2.5 seconds, and at least half a second. The column has a 3.5-metre radius and can hold up to eight enemies. It bites every 0.4 seconds for 6 damage and one stack of Static (this branch''s spells are made of Lightning, whichever element is first). Enemies are thrown out up to 5 metres, and each bolt deals 12 damage and 20 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Slip Current or The Sky Finds Them (it comes after either on the chart), Thunder Gust''s shapes, Static.


      <b>Example.</b> Swarmed by small enemies: hold it for the full time, then let go, and the bolts fall on every enemy thrown.'
  - Id: tempest.falling
    Title: Falling Thunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> After your finisher lands, you throw the enemy into the sky, vanish after it in a streak of light, and come down on it as a bolt. Where you land, everything nearby is shocked, knocked off balance, and thrown back. Nothing can hit you from the throw until you land.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every finisher that lands (a heavy blow on a staggered enemy).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 5 Shards. You can buy it once you carry both elements; no god gives it. You land 0.34 seconds after the throw. Everything within 4.5 metres takes 22 damage, 40 stagger, three stacks of Static and three of Unsteady, and is pushed 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Static, Thunder Gust''s Thunderhead shape, Slip Current, Stored Charge.


      <b>Example.</b> Finish a staggered brute in a crowd, and land among its friends, leaving each with three stacks of both.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.shape
    Title: 'The Bolt Runs True: Laid Flat or Struck Again'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Flat or Again unlocks two new shapes for The Bolt Runs True. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your The Bolt Runs True uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Laid Flat</b>: the thread of light lies along the ground to the next Marked enemy, and the bolt runs along that line. <b>Struck Again</b>: no big bolt. Instead, the thread stays on the enemy while its Mark lasts, and each critical hit calls a smaller bolt down it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time The Bolt Runs True goes off while you carry both Light and Lightning. The Bolt Runs True goes off when Static lands on a Marked enemy, or when a Mark lands on a charged one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain The Bolt Runs True.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Laid Flat: 0.5 seconds after it goes off, it deals 18 damage, 20 stagger and two Static to the enemy and to the nearest other Marked enemy within 10 metres. Then the enemy''s Static goes off, dealing 7 damage per stack within 4 metres. Struck Again: while the enemy''s Mark lasts, each weapon blow on it (blows on a Marked enemy are your critical hits) calls down a bolt for 9 damage, 15 stagger and a Static. After The Bolt Runs True goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Charged Light to set it up, Diamond Guard, The Diamond Falls, Diamond Thunder, The Net of Jewels.


      <b>Example.</b> Two Marked captains far apart: use Laid Flat, and the bolt runs between them. One Marked brute: use Struck Again, and every critical hit calls down the sky.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Laid Flat''s bolt jumps to the enemy at the far end, instead of hitting everything on the line in between, and it only looks for Marked enemies, not charged ones.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.chargedlight
    Title: Charged Light
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your light blows add Static to a Marked enemy, and a burst of Static Marks every enemy it reaches.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every light blow on a Marked enemy, and every time Static goes off, while you carry both Light and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Static per light blow. A Mark on every enemy within 4 metres of a Static burst, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Bolt Runs True (a light blow on a Marked enemy adds both), Diamond Guard, The Net of Jewels.


      <b>Example.</b> Mark an enemy, cut it with a light blow, and the bolt comes down.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.diamondguard
    Title: Diamond Guard
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked enemy calls the bolt down on it right away, no matter what else it has. Once every 5 seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect guard against a Marked enemy, while you carry both Light and Lightning, at most once every 5 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. It adds a Static, and The Bolt Runs True goes off right away, even if it would normally have to wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Bolt Runs True and its shapes, Charged Light.


      <b>Example.</b> Block the Marked brute''s swing perfectly, and the bolt answers.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.diamondfalls
    Title: The Diamond Falls
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Bolt Runs True no longer needs Static. The third critical hit on a Marked enemy calls it down, once every 5 seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Critical hits on a Marked enemy, while you carry both Light and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Every third critical hit on a Marked enemy adds a Static to it, so the bolt comes down. Each enemy keeps its own count. At most once every 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Charged Light, The Bolt Runs True, Diamond Thunder.


      <b>Example.</b> A Light player with Lightning as a second element calls down the bolt with the blade alone.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.diamondthunder
    Title: Diamond Thunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A straight line of light lies on the floor from your hand, 14 metres long, and lightning runs the whole line in one strike. Every enemy on it takes 22 damage and 40 stagger and is Marked, and every Static it meets goes off. Your first blow on each one within 2 seconds is a critical hit.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Light and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Light.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It deals 22 damage, 40 stagger and a Mark to each enemy on the line. Their Static goes off, dealing 7 damage per stack within 4 metres. For 2 seconds, your first weapon blow on each one is a critical hit.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Bolt Runs True (a charged enemy it hits gets both afflictions), Charged Light, The Net of Jewels.


      <b>Example.</b> Charge up a line of soldiers, then run the strike down them.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You can''t hold the line on the button yet for up to 1.5 seconds, turning with you, and release it when you let go. A perfect guard while you hold it doesn''t yet release it right away for double damage.'
  - Id: boltrunstrue.net
    Title: The Net of Jewels
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, every Marked enemy within 12 metres is linked to every other one by threads of light. When Static goes off on one, it runs along the threads to all the others for half. Each critical hit on one gives every linked enemy a Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Light and Lightning. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 45 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: when Static goes off on a Marked enemy, it arcs through up to five Marked enemies within 12 metres, for 3.5 damage per stack each, at most once every half second. A critical hit on a Marked enemy adds a Static to every enemy within 12 metres, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Charged Light, Diamond Thunder, The Bolt Runs True.


      <b>Example.</b> Mark a pack, light your surge, and one burst of Static runs through all of them.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> There are no threads yet, so the Static arcs from one enemy to the next, and a critical hit charges every enemy nearby, Marked or not.'
  - Id: thegroundgives.shape
    Title: 'The Ground Gives: Quicksand or Sand Boil'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Wet Ground unlocks two new shapes for The Ground Gives. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your The Ground Gives uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Quicksand</b>: the sand pulls whatever stands near it into its middle, so enemies end up bunched together. <b>Sand Boil</b>: the pool only lasts a moment, then erupts in a fountain of mud and water.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time The Ground Gives goes off while you carry both Water and Earth. The Ground Gives goes off when Tremor lands on a soaked enemy, or when Soaked lands on a shaken one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain The Ground Gives. The plain one sinks the enemy to its knees for 4 seconds and slows it by half.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Quicksand: the enemy sinks for 4 seconds and is slowed by half. Once a second, everything within 1.5 metres is pulled 1 metre toward it. A heavy blow holds it for 0.5 seconds. Sand Boil: the enemy sinks for 1.2 seconds, then is lifted up for 1 second, and everything within 3 metres takes 12 damage, a Soaked and a Tremor. After The Ground Gives goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide and Stone Rise to set it up, Driven Down, Given to the Bog, The Island Breathes.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Quicksand in a pack. The soldiers around the sunken one are pulled into a tight group for one big sweep. Use Sand Boil before a string of heavy blows: the enemy comes up right into your blows.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The pool itself isn''t shown as a spot on the ground yet. For now, Quicksand pulls in toward the sunken enemy, from 1.5 metres away.'
  - Id: thegroundgives.bog
    Title: Given to the Bog
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies while sunk is swallowed up, and the bog gives something back: a quarter of a flask.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies with both Soaked and Tremor on it, while you carry both Water and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. A quarter of a flask, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Ground Gives and its shapes, Drawn From the Drowned.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill three sunken soldiers, and a flask is almost full again.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any enemy that dies soaked and shaken, not only one that was sunk.'
  - Id: thegroundgives.drivendown
    Title: Driven Down
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows on a sunken enemy drive it deeper and hold it in place.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Soaked and Tremor.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Held still for 0.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Ground Gives, Quicksand, Mountain Stance.


      <b>Example.</b> A sunken brute can''t fight back. Each heavy blow pins it again.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The heavy blow doesn''t go through the enemy''s guard yet, and for now it works on any soaked, shaken enemy, not only a sunken one.'
  - Id: thegroundgives.landwassea
    Title: The Land Was Sea
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your dodges become slides. Each one leaves a line of wet sand behind it and gives back its stamina.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At the end of every dodge, while you carry both Water and Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Three patches, each 0.8 metres across, along 3 metres, lasting 3 seconds. They soak whatever stands in them every half second. You get 15 stamina back.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Ground Gives (shake the enemies you soak), Quicksand, Wake.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll around a pack and leave it standing in wet sand, ready for your Stone Rise.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It is meant to work only on a dodge through wet sand. For now, every dodge slides.'
  - Id: thegroundgives.island
    Title: The Island Breathes
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The ground where you aim breathes in: it heaves up like the back of a huge animal, and every enemy on it is lifted up, shaken twice and soaked, so it sinks with The Ground Gives when it comes down.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Water and Earth. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, breaker, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 15 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Up to five pillars of ground rise under the enemies near where you aim (up to 8 metres away), 0.9 seconds after you cast it. Each deals 10 damage, 30 stagger, a Soaked and two Tremor, and throws its enemy up.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Ground Gives and its shapes, Driven Down, Given to the Bog.


      <b>Example.</b> Make the ground breathe in under a pack, then fight them while they are sunk.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The ground breathing out (the spot slumping into wet sand for 4 seconds) is The Ground Gives on each enemy for now, not a spot of its own. Blows on lifted enemies don''t keep them up in the air yet.'
  - Id: thegrudge.shape
    Title: 'The Grudge: First Strike or Ash Plume'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for The Grudge. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Grudge uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>First Strike</b>: the storm cloud doesn''t wait for the enemy to attack you. Your first heavy blow on it calls the cloud down. It''s for players who would rather not get hit. <b>Ash Plume</b>: the cloud grows with each of your blows and comes down as a storm of lightning bolts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time The Grudge goes off while you carry both Dark and Lightning. The Grudge goes off when a second Static lands on a Cursed enemy, or when a Curse lands on an enemy with two Static. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Grudge.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. First Strike: the cloud waits for 8 seconds. Your first heavy blow on the enemy sets off all its Static at double strength, for 14 damage each within 4 metres, and holds it still for 0.6 seconds. Ash Plume: the cloud waits for up to 6 seconds. When the enemy next attacks you (whether the blow lands, is guarded, or is blocked by a ward), or when the time runs out, one bolt falls around it for each weapon blow you landed on it, up to five bolts, each dealing 10 damage and a Static. Then all its Static goes off within 4 metres. After The Grudge goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grievance to add the Static, The Exile Returns, Given Bones, The Exile''s Grudge.


      <b>Example.</b> A lone brute: use First Strike, and end the cloud with a heavy blow. A pack around one Cursed captain: use Ash Plume, and keep hitting the captain for six seconds.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cloud doesn''t move to the next enemy yet when the enemy under it dies.'
  - Id: thegrudge.grievance
    Title: Grievance
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Blocking a blow from a Cursed enemy puts two Static on it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each blow you block from a Cursed enemy.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two Static, at most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Grudge (two Static on a Cursed enemy is all it needs), Long Memory, Given Bones.


      <b>Example.</b> Curse a brute, then block its first swing. The cloud gathers over its head.'
  - Id: thegrudge.exilereturns
    Title: The Exile Returns
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Grudge''s black bolt heals you 1 for each Static it sets off, and Curses everything within 3 metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time the Grudge''s black bolt falls, in any of its shapes: when the enemy under the cloud attacks you, when your heavy blow calls it down (First Strike), or when Ash Plume''s storm falls. Other ways of setting off Static don''t count. You must carry both Dark and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 1 health for each Static the bolt sets off, and one Curse on everything within 3 metres. At most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Grudge and its shapes, Given Bones, Ash and Thunder.


      <b>Example.</b> Bring the cloud down on a charged captain. You heal, and the soldiers next to it are Cursed, ready for the next cloud.'
  - Id: thegrudge.longmemory
    Title: Long Memory
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A Curse on a charged enemy lasts as long as its Static does. While an enemy has Static, its Curse doesn''t fade.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Dark and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Once the Static is gone, the Curse starts to fade as usual.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grievance, which it comes after on the chart, The Grudge.


      <b>Example.</b> The Curse doesn''t run out before the cloud gathers.'
  - Id: thegrudge.givenbones
    Title: Given Bones
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Grudge''s black bolt costs you 2 health and hits twice as hard. The health you pay turns grey, and you win it back by landing blows.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time the Grudge''s bolt falls, in any of its shapes. Other ways of setting off Static don''t count. You must carry both Dark and Lightning.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. 2 health turns grey (it never takes your last point). The bolt hits a second time for 14 damage for each Static it sets off, within 4 metres. At most once every 0.3 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Grudge, The Exile Returns (which heals back what it costs), Ash and Thunder.


      <b>Example.</b> A Cursed brute has five Static. The bolt lands, then lands again.'
  - Id: thegrudge.exilesgrudge
    Title: The Exile's Grudge
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A black cloud hangs over you for 3 seconds. Whoever hits you or your guard during that time gets answered when the cloud breaks: a bolt that Curses it and gives it three Static.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Dark and Lightning. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, counter, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. The cloud stays for 3 seconds. The answer is a cone 4 metres long that deals 12 damage, 15 stagger, a Curse and three Static.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Grudge (the answer leaves every attacker ready for it), Grievance, Given Bones.


      <b>Example.</b> You''re surrounded. Cast it, guard whatever comes, and every attacker is Cursed and charged.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Each attacker isn''t tied to you by a thread yet and paid back twice the damage it did to you (up to 60). A blow on your guard doesn''t count double yet.'
  - Id: thegrudge.ashthunder
    Title: Ash and Thunder
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, a cloud of ash streams up off your shoulders. Every blow you land adds a Static, every third one calls a bolt down on a Cursed enemy, and each Cursed enemy you kill heals you 6 more.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Dark and Lightning. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. Each kill while it is lit adds 1 second. Each blow adds one Static. Every third blow on a Cursed enemy sets off its Static. Each enemy keeps its own count. Each Cursed enemy you kill heals you 6.


      <b>Works with.</b> The Grudge, The Exile Returns, Given Bones.


      <b>Example.</b> Curse a pack, light your surge, and the ash cloud keeps the storm clouds gathering.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the bolt falls on the enemy you hit, on every third blow that enemy takes. It should fall on every third blow you land, on the nearest Cursed enemy within 8 metres.'
  - Id: thermalshock.shape
    Title: 'Thermal Shock: Hot Shards or Crazing'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Thermal Shock. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Thermal Shock uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Hot Shards</b>: when the enemy splits, it throws shards of hot stone and ice that make the crowd crack too. <b>Crazing</b>: the crack doesn''t wait for a heavy blow. While the enemy has both afflictions, it splits again on its own.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Thermal Shock goes off while you carry both Fire and Ice. Thermal Shock goes off when Chill lands on a burning enemy, or when Burn lands on a chilled one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Thermal Shock.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes crack the enemy''s body for 14 damage. Hot Shards: your next heavy blow within 5 seconds splits it for 30 damage and 40 stagger, and shards hit everything within 3 metres for 8 damage, a Burn and a Chill. This is on purpose: the shards may crack those enemies too. Crazing: while the enemy has both afflictions, it splits every 1.5 seconds for 10 damage and 12 stagger. After Thermal Shock goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spark and Rime, Fire-Setting, and Flame Wave and Hoarfrost to set it up.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd: use Hot Shards, and one heavy blow cracks the pack. A brute: use Crazing, and keep both afflictions on it.'
  - Id: thermalshock.sparkrime
    Title: Spark and Rime
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows add one Burn and one Chill.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Fire and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One stack of each. For your first element''s affliction, it adds nothing beyond what that element''s root mark already adds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thermal Shock (one heavy blow starts it), Neither Wins, Fire Under the Ice.


      <b>Example.</b> Every heavy blow on a fresh enemy cracks it.'
  - Id: thermalshock.hotstone
    Title: Hot Stone, Cold Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a cracked enemy splits it right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has both Burn and Chill.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. 30 damage and 40 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thermal Shock, Kiln Stance, Spark and Rime.


      <b>Example.</b> A cracked brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it splits without your heavy blow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any attacker that has both afflictions, not only a cracked one.'
  - Id: thermalshock.neitherwins
    Title: Neither Wins
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> On an enemy that has both, neither the Burn nor the Chill fades.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Fire and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. Each one is kept while the other is there. Once one of them is gone, the other starts to fade as usual.


      <b>Works with.</b> Crazing (both afflictions must last), Spark and Rime, which it comes after on the chart.


      <b>Example.</b> A burning, chilled enemy stays both for the whole fight.'
  - Id: thermalshock.underice
    Title: Fire Under the Ice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A Frozen enemy that is burning shatters on any blow, not only a heavy one, and its shatter sets everything within 3 metres on fire with two Burns.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a burning enemy shatters, while you carry both Fire and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. Two Burns on everything within 3 metres, at most five times a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hoarfrost and Winter Wall (to freeze enemies), Spark and Rime, Thermal Shock.


      <b>Example.</b> Freeze a burning soldier in a pack and shatter it. The pack catches fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The shatter still needs a heavy blow for now. The spreading fire is built.'
  - Id: thermalshock.firesetting
    Title: Fire-Setting
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A fire is lit against the enemy you face. When it is put out with a splash of frost, the heated body splits: a hard hit and three Chill, and the enemies next to it take half.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Fire and Ice. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Fire.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 12 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches an enemy within 6 metres. Putting it out deals 20 damage, 60 stagger, two Burns and three Chill within 2.5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thermal Shock and its shapes, Spark and Rime.


      <b>Example.</b> Set it on a brute, hit the brute, and putting out the fire splits it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> A second press doesn''t put it out yet; for now, your weapon blow sets it off, like a sign on the enemy. Its Burn doesn''t grow while the fire waits, and blows before you put it out don''t make it stronger yet.'
  - Id: thermalshock.gap
    Title: The Yawning Gap
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A duo surge. While it is lit, your weapon blows take turns burning and chilling, so Thermal Shock keeps going off, and each split drops melted water that heals you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At a rest fire, pick it for your surge slot in the Hand while you carry both Fire and Ice. Light it with the surge button.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo surge, 5 Shards. 40 mana, lasts 10 seconds. For now: each blow adds a Chill, and each Thermal Shock heals you 2.


      <b>Works with.</b> Thermal Shock, Spark and Rime, Cinder.


      <b>Example.</b> Light your surge in a crowd, and every second blow cracks something.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Your blows don''t really take turns yet; for now, each blow adds a Chill. Thermal Shock still has to wait 6 seconds on each enemy, instead of 2.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.shape
    Title: 'Turned to Stone: The King''s Men or Grave Marker'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Turned to Stone. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Turned to Stone uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still turn the enemy to stone like the plain one. <b>The King''s Men</b>: when your heavy blow breaks it out, the curse moves on to the nearest enemy, so a crowd falls one at a time. <b>Grave Marker</b>: an enemy that dies while turned to stone leaves its stone standing, Cursing enemies nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Turned to Stone goes off while you carry both Earth and Dark. Turned to Stone goes off when Tremor lands on a Cursed enemy, or when a Curse lands on a shaken one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Turned to Stone.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes: over 2 seconds, the enemy slows to a stop (for 1 second it moves 30% slower, then for 1 second 60% slower), then stands as stone for 1.5 seconds. While it is stone, it is held in place, takes double stagger from weapon blows, and takes half damage from spells. A heavy blow breaks it out for 50 stagger. The King''s Men: then a Curse and a Tremor jump to the nearest enemy within 5 metres. This is on purpose: that enemy may start turning to stone. Grave Marker: if the enemy dies while it is stone, a patch 3 metres across stays for 10 seconds and Curses whatever stands in it every 2 seconds. After Turned to Stone goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grasp and Stone Rise to set it up, Grave Dirt, Broken Idol, Stone Underfoot.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack: use The King''s Men, and break them out one by one. A doorway: use Grave Marker, and Curse everyone who comes through.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The grave stone doesn''t block the way yet.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.gravedirt
    Title: Grave Dirt
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Heavy blows on a Cursed enemy shake it. The blow that ends a chain of blows on a shaken enemy Curses it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow on a Cursed enemy, and the last blow of each chain on a shaken one.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Tremor, or one Curse.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned to Stone, Heavy Curse, Broken Idol.


      <b>Example.</b> Curse a soldier, and one heavy blow starts the stone climbing up it.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.brokenidol
    Title: Broken Idol
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A finisher on an enemy that was just broken out of stone heals you and Curses every enemy nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Tremor and a Curse.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. You heal 10, and everything within 5 metres is Cursed.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned to Stone (breaking out often leaves the enemy on its knees), Grave Dirt.


      <b>Example.</b> Break a brute out of stone, finish it, and the pack around it is Cursed.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it works on any finisher on an enemy that has both, not only one just broken out of stone.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.heavycurse
    Title: Heavy Curse
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The stone climbs up the enemy half a second faster.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Turned to Stone goes off.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The climb has two parts. The first part, when the enemy moves 30% slower, takes half a second instead of one. So the stone closes over it after 1.5 seconds instead of 2. It''s the same in both shapes.


      <b>Works with.</b> Grave Dirt, which it comes after on the chart, Turned to Stone.


      <b>Example.</b> The stone closes over the enemy before it can attack.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.forgive
    Title: Stone Does Not Forgive
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that has been turned to stone once keeps its Tremor and its Curse until it dies.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time the stone closes over an enemy (in the plain Turned to Stone or either shape), while you carry both Earth and Dark.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. From then on, its Tremor and its Curse never fade. They can still be used up or set off.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned to Stone, Grave Dirt.


      <b>Example.</b> Turn a captain to stone once. It stays shaken and Cursed, so the next Tremor or Curse turns it again once the 6-second wait is over.'
  - Id: turnedtostone.underfoot
    Title: Stone Underfoot
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You stride to the enemy you face, leaving grey dusty footprints, and step on its shadow. It is Cursed and gets two stacks of Tremor, and stone starts climbing up it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Earth and Dark. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Earth.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It reaches up to 8 metres. It deals 6 damage, 10 stagger, two Tremor and two Curses.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned to Stone and its shapes, Broken Idol.


      <b>Example.</b> Step to the captain at the back, and leave it turning to stone.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The enemy isn''t turned to stone right away yet, skipping the climb. Enemies you pass aren''t slowed yet. The heavy blow that breaks it out doesn''t deal double stagger yet.'
  - Id: venomrime.shape
    Title: 'Venom Rime: Frost Over Frost or Cold Damage'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Floor or Bone unlocks two new shapes for Venom Rime. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Venom Rime uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes still keep the enemy''s poison from fading, with each bite adding a Chill, like before. <b>Frost Over Frost</b>: a frosted enemy that freezes spreads its poisoned frost over the floor around it. <b>Cold Damage</b>: the cold drives the poison deeper inside. While the enemy is frozen, its poison bites harder, and when it is shattered, every dose goes off at once.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Venom Rime goes off while you carry both Poison and Ice. Venom Rime goes off when a dose lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a poisoned one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Venom Rime.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Both shapes work like the plain one: while the enemy has both afflictions, its poison doesn''t fade, and each bite adds a Chill. Frost Over Frost: at the first bite after the enemy freezes, frost covers 3 metres around it for 5 seconds, adding a Chill and a dose each second to whatever stands there. At most once every 5 seconds. Cold Damage: while the enemy is frozen, each bite deals twice as much. When it is shattered, every dose goes off at once, for 6 damage each, on top of the shatter. After Venom Rime goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bitter Frost to set it up, Six Levels Down, The Drops Quicken, Rime Over Rime.


      <b>Example.</b> A crowd around one freezing brute: use Frost Over Frost. One captain with lots of doses: use Cold Damage, and shatter it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The game can''t tell yet the exact moment an enemy freezes, so Frost Over Frost spreads at the first bite after the freeze.'
  - Id: venomrime.bitterfrost
    Title: Bitter Frost
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your heavy blows add a Chill and a dose of poison at the same time.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, while you carry both Poison and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Chill and one dose.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom Rime (one heavy blow on a fresh enemy adds both), Six Levels Down.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow, and the poisoned frost starts to creep.'
  - Id: venomrime.sixlevels
    Title: Six Levels Down
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each poison bite on a frozen enemy keeps it frozen 0.3 seconds longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each bite of poison on a frozen enemy, while you carry both Poison and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Each bite adds 0.3 seconds to the freeze, at most once every 0.3 seconds. Each enemy keeps its own wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom Rime and its shapes, Bitter Frost, The Drops Quicken.


      <b>Example.</b> A frozen, poisoned soldier stays frozen while the poison works.'
  - Id: venomrime.drops
    Title: The Drops Quicken
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A frosted enemy that dies leaves behind a drop of poisoned frost. It rises as a small frost creature, hits the nearest enemy three times for 6 damage, a dose and a Chill each, then melts.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies with both poison and Chill on it, while you carry both Poison and Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: three hits 0.6 seconds apart, each dealing 6 damage, 6 stagger and a dose. The frost it rises from adds a Chill to everything within 3 metres. At most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bitter Frost, Six Levels Down, Venom Rime.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill one enemy in the frost, and its drop goes after the enemy next to it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the creature''s hits only add the dose. The Chill comes from the frost around where the enemy fell.'
  - Id: venomrime.rimeoverrime
    Title: Rime Over Rime
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A drop of venom falls on the ground where you aim, within 8 metres, and frost grows out from it, layer over layer, out to 4.5 metres. Each time it grows, it gives every enemy it reaches a dose and a Chill, three times in all.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Poison and Ice. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Poison.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, opener, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. It bites three times, 0.6 seconds apart, over 4.5 metres. Each bite deals 3 damage, a dose and a Chill, so Venom Rime starts on everything in it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Venom Rime and its shapes, Bitter Frost, Six Levels Down.


      <b>Example.</b> Drop it in the middle of a pack, and the frost takes them all.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The frost doesn''t grow outward in rings yet (1.5, 3 and 4.5 metres), and it doesn''t stay for 4 seconds afterward. A heavy blow on it doesn''t yet shatter every ring at once (for 14 damage, a Chill and two doses to each enemy standing on it).'
  - Id: water.soak
    Title: Soaked
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands leaves the enemy Soaked. A Soaked enemy takes more damage from your spells. Also, any Static or Chill put on it is put on everyone standing near it too.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every weapon blow that lands, while Water is your first element (or you are all in on Water). If Water is your second element, Soaked comes from your spells, the charged blade and the R2 strike instead.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. Soaked is either on or off, and lasts 8 seconds. Each blow restarts it. Spells deal 25% more damage to a Soaked enemy. Static or Chill put on it is also put on every other enemy within 4 metres, whether they are Soaked or not.


      <b>Works with.</b> Riptide, Wellspring, Heavy Water, Deep Crack (which uses it up), Drawn From the Drowned, Spreading Water, Lightning''s Static and Ice''s Chill as your second element, and the reactions Steam (with Fire) and The Ground Gives (with Earth).


      <b>Example.</b> Carrying Water and Lightning, cut three soldiers standing close together, then hit one with Thunderclap. The Static spreads to all three.'
  - Id: water.ebbflood
    Title: Ebb and Flood
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard brings the Flood: a splash soaks the attacker and the enemies next to it, and for a while your blows hit harder. A heavy blow that lands brings the Ebb: for a while, your guard and dodge cost half the stamina, and the timing window for a perfect guard is longer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> The Flood comes with every perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands). The Ebb comes with every heavy blow or finisher that lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Flood: a splash 2.5 metres around the attacker deals 6 damage, 8 stagger and Soaked. Then your weapon deals 15% more damage for 5 seconds. Ebb: dodges and blocked blows cost half the stamina for 4 seconds, and the perfect guard window is 22% longer for that time.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blowhole and Deep Crack, which come after it on the chart; Spreading Water above them; Heavy Water; Undertow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Flood should reach farther and splash with each blow. For now, it gives more damage and one splash on the attacker.


      <b>Example.</b> Land a heavy blow, then perfectly guard the enemy''s answer, using the Ebb''s wider window. The Flood soaks it, and your next cuts hit 15% harder.'
  - Id: water.crack
    Title: Deep Crack
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A fist of water snaps shut on the enemy you face. A bubble as big as a body forms on it, then collapses with a crack. On a Soaked enemy, the water boils out of it: its Soaked is used up, and every other Soaked enemy nearby cracks with it. This is the detonator of the Water tree.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It closes on the nearest enemy in reach.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds and the damage 22.) It targets an enemy within 7 metres. The bubble collapses 0.4 seconds after you cast it, within 1 metre, dealing 18 damage and 40 stagger. On a Soaked enemy, its Soaked is used up, and the crack spreads to up to six other Soaked enemies, each within 5 metres of the last, dealing 18 damage and 30 stagger to each.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, Riptide and Whirlpool to soak a crowd first, Ebb and Flood, which it comes after on the chart, Spreading Water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Each of the other enemies should crack where it stands and have its own Soaked used up. For now, the crack spreads to them as an arc of water, and they keep their Soaked.


      <b>Example.</b> Pull three soldiers to your feet with Riptide, Soaked, and snap the bubble on the middle one. All three take the crack.'
  - Id: water.crack.shape
    Title: 'Deep Crack: Ink or String of Bubbles'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Deep Crack. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Ink</b>: the bubble is black water from the deep sea, and it bursts into a pool of ink. Enemies in it lose track of you if you are more than three metres away, and they stay Soaked, while you can still see clearly. <b>String of Bubbles</b>: three bubbles in a row along the direction you face, each cracking a moment after the last. Aim it down a hallway.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Deep Crack, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the single bubble.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Ink: a pool with a 1.5-metre radius that lasts 5 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and adds Soaked. Whatever it reaches when it bursts can''t see farther than 3 metres for 5 seconds. String of Bubbles: a line of water 7 metres long and 2 metres wide, moving at 13 metres a second. It deals 18 damage and 40 stagger to everything it crosses, and the crack spreads from each Soaked enemy, like above.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blowhole and Whirlpool, to hold enemies in the ink; Riptide''s Breaking Swell shape, to line them up for the string.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Ink''s blindness should only last while an enemy stands in the ink (for now, enemies the ink reaches first stay blinded for 5 seconds). String of Bubbles should be three bubbles 2 metres apart (for now, it is one moving line).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Ink in a doorway. The archers behind it lose track of you, and the soldiers in it stay Soaked for the next crack.'
  - Id: water.crack.master
    Title: 'Deep Crack: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Snap it as your heavy blow lands on the same enemy, and every Soaked enemy in the fight cracks.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, a heavy blow (a finisher counts) on the enemy within 0.3 seconds after the bubble cracks on it. Deep Crack must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The crack spreads to up to twelve Soaked enemies, each within 20 metres of the last, dealing 18 damage and 30 stagger to each.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, and every mark that soaks a crowd; Heavy Water; Spreading Water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The snap should be timed to your heavy blow landing, not to the crack. Each enemy should crack where it stands.


      <b>Example.</b> Soak the room with Riptide and Whirlpool. Then cast it on the brute in front of you, and bring the heavy blow down as it cracks. Every soaked enemy in the room cracks with it.'
  - Id: water.blowhole
    Title: Blowhole
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You stand on a hole in black rock that the sea blows up through. Each blow that comes is blocked and drives the sea down the hole, and its spray rises one step. Let go, and the sea shoots up as a spout under the attacker, harder for every step, and throws it up, open to your blade.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then it works on any blow in the next 3 seconds. If no blow comes, the cast is used up.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 8 seconds and 18 damage per step.) It is ready for 3 seconds. For now, the first blow is blocked and answered right away by a spout worth three steps: a cone 3 metres long and 140 degrees wide, dealing 42 damage (54 with the Empower), 66 stagger and Soaked. Whatever it hits can''t start an attack or guard for 0.8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ebb and Flood (the blocked blow counts as guard work), Deep Crack on the Soaked attacker, Heavy Water, Undertow.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The blowhole itself: standing on it for 3 seconds and blocking every blow, red ones too (15 stamina each), with each blow raising the spray one step, up to five. Letting go should release the spout, at 14 damage per step, throwing enemies up for 0.8 seconds at three steps and 1.2 at five. For now, the first blow is blocked and answered as three steps, and the enemy it hits is left open instead of thrown up.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it as a brute winds up. The blow is blocked, the spout answers, and the brute stands open for your next cut.'
  - Id: water.blowhole.shape
    Title: 'Blowhole: Still Water or Blown Out'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Blowhole. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Still Water</b>: only the first blow is blocked, and you answer right away with a thrust of water six metres straight through the attacker. It''s a quick parry, not a build-up. <b>Blown Out</b>: the spout comes up under you instead and throws everything within four metres out three metres. It''s for making room, not hitting one target.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Blowhole, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain blowhole.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Still Water: the answer is a thrust 6 metres long and 25 degrees wide, with Blowhole''s damage and stagger. Blown Out: the answer is a ring 4 metres around you with Blowhole''s damage and stagger, and everything within 4 metres is thrown 3 metres out.


      <b>Works with.</b> Deep Crack''s String of Bubbles shape down the line Still Water opens; Undertow, to pull back what Blown Out threw.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Blown Out should build up before the spout (like the Learn mark should). For now, the first blocked blow brings it up right away.


      <b>Example.</b> Surrounded, use Blown Out. The first blocked blow throws the whole ring of enemies back.'
  - Id: water.blowhole.master
    Title: 'Blowhole: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Let go at the same moment a blow lands on the blowhole, and the spout is doubled, and the blowhole stays for a second round.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every answer, since for now the answer always comes the moment the blow lands. Blowhole must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The answer hits again where it lands, for 42 damage, 66 stagger and Soaked within 1.5 metres. Blowhole''s wait also ends right away, once every 20 seconds, so you can cast it again for a second round.


      <b>Works with.</b> Ebb and Flood; Deep Crack; Heavy Water on the attacker it soaks.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (letting go within 0.2 seconds of a blow landing on the blowhole). For now, every answer is doubled.


      <b>Example.</b> Block a soldier''s blow, and cast it again right away as the next one swings.'
  - Id: water.lift
    Title: Spreading Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Spreading Water, Water''s reaction lift. Every reaction with Soaked in it spreads at half strength to every other Soaked enemy nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Soaked reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Steam with Burn, The Ground Gives with Tremor, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, an arc of water runs from where the reaction happened to up to four other Soaked enemies, each within 4 metres of the last, dealing 10 damage and 15 stagger to each.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Water''s ten reactions; Soaked, to soak a crowd; Ebb and Flood, which it comes after on the chart; The Sea Comes In above it.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reaction itself should spread at half strength. For now, it is a plain arc of water.


      <b>Example.</b> Water and Fire on a group of soaked soldiers. Steam goes off on one, and the water carries it on to the rest.'
  - Id: water.devotion
    Title: The Sea Comes In
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The Sea Comes In, Water''s Devotion, for going all in on Water only. Every spell leaves standing water where it lands. Enemies in the water are always Soaked and slower, and in the water, your dodge slides farther and your spell waits count down faster. Pull the fight onto your own water and stay there, the way the tide takes over the beach.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Water twice (all in). A pool is left each time a spell hits.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A pool 4 metres across forms where a spell hits, at most one every 2.5 seconds, and lasts 10 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and adds Soaked. For now, these work everywhere, not only in water: your dodge goes 50% farther, and every spell''s wait counts down 30% faster. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Thrown on the Reef. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Water alone or twice. The lash wraps around the enemy, and a wave throws it 5 metres away from you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every spell in the Water tree; Deep Crack and Spreading Water on the soaked crowd; Undertow, to pull enemies onto the water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Four pools at most, enemies in them moving 15% slower, and the faster dodge and spell waits only while you stand in your own water. Thrown on the Reef should deal 30 damage, 50 stagger, and knock the enemy down for 0.8 seconds when it hits a wall, a rock or another enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> Riptide and Whirlpool leave two pools in the middle of the room. Stand between them and cast again and again, a third faster.'
  - Id: water.undertow
    Title: Undertow
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow that lands pulls every enemy near you a step closer.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands, at most twice a second. For now, it pulls everyone nearby, Soaked or not.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Every enemy within 6 metres of you is pulled 1.5 metres in. At most once every half second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy weapons with wide swings, The Tide Turns, which comes after it on the chart, Heavy Water, Whirlpool, Blowhole''s Blown Out shape (which throws them back out).


      <b>Example.</b> Two archers hang back while you fight a brute. Each heavy blow on the brute pulls them a step closer to your blade.'
  - Id: water.tide
    Title: The Tide Turns
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The tide in your hand. Press once, and the tide going out pulls you straight back out of reach, leaving a line of wet sand. Press again within three seconds, and the tide coming in brings you back along the line and past where you started, as a breaking wave that soaks everything on the line and carries it ahead of you.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 8 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 6 seconds and the wave 10 metres long.) For now, one press is just the incoming wave: you rush 8 metres ahead at 20 metres a second, and nothing can touch you on the way. Everything on the line, 2 metres wide, takes 10 damage, 10 stagger and Soaked, and is carried 2 metres ahead of you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Undertow, which it comes after on the chart; Riptide, to pull enemies in before the wave; Deep Crack on the line it soaks; Heavy Water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The tide going out (pulling you 5 metres straight back in 0.3 seconds, untouchable, leaving wet sand behind) and the incoming wave as the second press back along that line. For now, one press is the wave, rushing ahead.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack closes in on you. Rush through it as a wave and out the far side. Every one of them is Soaked and stumbling.'
  - Id: water.tide.shape
    Title: 'The Tide Turns: King Tide or Out to Sea'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for The Tide Turns. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>King Tide</b>: the wave comes in four metres wide and knocks down what it carries for a moment, but it stops where you started. It''s the shape for a crowd. <b>Out to Sea</b>: there''s no incoming wave. The outgoing tide takes the enemy you face along with you, pulling it 5 metres, Soaked, and dropping it at your feet, away from its friends. It''s a pull, not a return.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast The Tide Turns, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain tide.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. King Tide: the wave is 4 metres wide and 5 metres long. Whatever it hits is knocked down for 0.6 seconds (half as long for elites; gods not at all). Out to Sea: the enemy you face is pulled 5 metres toward you and Soaked.


      <b>Works with.</b> Deep Crack and Heavy Water on the crowd King Tide knocks down; Blowhole and your blade on the one Out to Sea brings to you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> King Tide should keep its own 5 metres when you buy the Empower (for now, the Empower''s 10 metres wins). Out to Sea should pull you back along with the enemy.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer stands behind three shields. Out to Sea brings it to your feet, alone.'
  - Id: water.tide.master
    Title: 'The Tide Turns: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you start The Tide Turns right as a blow would land on you, it counts as a perfect dodge. A wave started right as a blow would land doesn''t start a wait.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time you cast The Tide Turns, and when you cast it within a second after a perfect dodge. The Tide Turns must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You get what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting. If you cast it within 1 second after a perfect dodge, while the spell is ready, it doesn''t start a wait.


      <b>Works with.</b> The dodge; Ebb and Flood; every spell whose wait this shortens.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (starting it right as a blow would land), and the wave''s own press. For now, every cast gives the dodge''s mana and time off, and a perfect dodge makes your next cast skip its wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a brute''s swing perfectly, and rush through it right away. The spell is ready again before the brute turns around.'
  - Id: water.heavy
    Title: Heavy Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Hitting a Soaked enemy makes your weapon break balance faster for a few seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time a weapon blow or one of your spells hits an enemy that is already Soaked. Each new hit restarts the timer.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Your weapon''s blows deal 20% more stagger for 3 seconds. It can restart at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, Whirlpool, which comes after it on the chart, Ebb and Flood, Blowhole, heavy blows and finishers, and Riptide to soak first.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The planned version, where Soaked enemies are slow to recover after they attack. The game can''t change how fast an enemy recovers yet, so your weapon breaks their balance faster instead.


      <b>Example.</b> Soak a brute, then hit it with light blows. Its balance bar fills a fifth faster, and the finisher comes sooner.'
  - Id: water.whirlpool
    Title: Whirlpool
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You open a whirlpool under the enemy you face. Enemies in it stay Soaked and get bitten, and your blows on them break their balance harder. This is the breaker of the Water tree.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If you aren''t facing an enemy, it opens 6 metres in front of you.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 8 seconds, and makes it last 7 seconds.) It opens on the enemy you face within 6 metres. The whirlpool has a 4-metre radius and lasts 5 seconds. It bites every half second for 3 damage, with no stagger, and adds Soaked with each bite. Each bite makes its enemy take 30% more stagger for 0.7 seconds, so while it stands in the water, every blow breaks its balance harder.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, Heavy Water, which it comes after on the chart, Undertow, Deep Crack, a second element''s Static or Chill, and Steam if you carry Fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Enemies should be pulled to the middle at 1.2 metres a second, and their balance shouldn''t recover inside it.


      <b>Example.</b> Open it under a pack. For five seconds, every one of them stays Soaked and breaks a third faster under your blade.'
  - Id: water.whirlpool.shape
    Title: 'Whirlpool: Maelstrom or Rip Current'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Whirlpool. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Maelstrom</b>: smaller, with a strong pull. At the end, it throws everything inside up in a spout and down again. <b>Rip Current</b>: no pull to the middle. Instead, a path of churning foam runs from the whirlpool, away from you. Enemies in it are carried down it and can''t dodge against it. Cut them as the current drags them past.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Whirlpool, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain whirlpool.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Maelstrom: a 2.5-metre radius. When the bites on an enemy stop (the whirlpool ends, or the enemy leaves the water), it is thrown up for 0.8 seconds and takes 50 stagger. Rip Current: each bite carries its enemy 1.25 metres away from you, two and a half metres a second, and the bites still churn.


      <b>Works with.</b> Deep Crack on the bodies Maelstrom throws up; Undertow and your blade on the enemies Rip Current carries past.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Maelstrom''s strong pull (3 metres a second). Rip Current''s path of foam, 2 metres wide and 9 long, lasting 6 seconds, and its rule against dodging. For now, the whirlpool itself carries the enemies it bites away from you.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Maelstrom on two soldiers. They churn around for five seconds, then go up together for your heavy blow.'
  - Id: water.whirlpool.master
    Title: 'Whirlpool: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Open it under an enemy right as its attack warning shows, and the attack is lost as it sinks in up to its knees. Its wait is cut in half.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, the first enemy it bites on each cast. Whirlpool must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The first enemy bitten flinches and loses whatever it had started, once every 5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Blowhole and Ebb and Flood, for the moment an attack starts; Heavy Water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (right as its attack warning shows) and the wait cut in half. For now, its first bite breaks whatever its enemy had started.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute raises its club. Open the whirlpool under it, and the swing never comes.'
  - Id: water.gourd
    Title: Broken Gourd
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Holding the flask button throws the flask instead of drinking it. Where it breaks, it hits and soaks everyone nearby and leaves a pool that keeps whatever stands in it Soaked. The throw also heals you a little right away. A short press still drinks it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Hold the flask button. Each throw uses up a flask.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Hold for 0.3 seconds to throw. It flies at the enemy in front of you within 9 metres, or 5 metres ahead. The burst has a 2.6-metre radius and deals 18 damage, 20 stagger and Soaked. The pool has a 1.5-metre radius and lasts 6 seconds. Once a second, it deals 1 damage and adds Soaked. The throw heals you 20.


      <b>Works with.</b> Drawn From the Drowned, which comes after it on the chart, to win the flask back; Deep Well; Heavy Water; Deep Crack on the soaked crowd; Steam if you carry Fire.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The pool should heal you while you stand in it. For now, the throw heals you part of that right away.


      <b>Example.</b> A pack gathers at a door. Break a gourd on the first one, and every one of them is Soaked before you reach them.'
  - Id: water.base
    Title: Riptide
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Riptide, Water''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A wave rises in front of you and rolls back toward you, soaking everything in its path and carrying it to your feet, within reach of your blade.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Water, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Water. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 6 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 4.5 seconds. The wave rises 5 metres in front of you and is 90 degrees wide. It deals 8 damage, 18 stagger and Soaked, and whatever it hits is carried to your feet.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Soaked; Deep Crack and Heavy Water on what it brings; and the charged blade, which it charges with Water when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s wave rising 6.5 metres away. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> Three archers stand far away. Riptide brings all three to your feet, Soaked, for one wide swing.'
  - Id: water.base.shape
    Title: 'Riptide: River Serpent or Breaking Swell'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Riptide. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>River Serpent</b>: a serpent made of water goes out along a path and back, and only brings back the first enemy it grabs, dropping it in front of you with its guard down. It hits one target, from farther away. <b>Breaking Swell</b>: the wave doesn''t come back. It rolls away from you and breaks, knocking down everything in it. It''s the shape for making room.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Riptide, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain wave.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. River Serpent: a path 8 metres long and 2.4 metres wide, moving at 16 metres a second. It deals 12 damage, 30 stagger and Soaked. The first enemy it hits is pulled 4 metres back toward you and can''t guard for 1 second. Breaking Swell: a wave 6 metres long and 90 degrees wide. It deals 8 damage, 18 stagger and Soaked. Whatever it hits is thrown 3 metres away and knocked down for 0.6 seconds (half as long for elites; gods not at all).


      <b>Works with.</b> Blowhole and your blade on the enemy River Serpent brings; Deep Crack''s String of Bubbles shape down the line Breaking Swell clears.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> River Serpent should go out and come back, and drop its enemy 1.5 metres in front of you. For now, the serpent runs its path, and its first enemy is pulled back 4 metres.


      <b>Example.</b> A shaman stands behind its guards. River Serpent grabs it alone, guard down, and brings it to your blade.'
  - Id: water.base.master
    Title: 'Riptide: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Press again within a second of the wave reaching you, and it goes back out the way it came, carrying what it brought past you and on another four metres, with their backs turned. It''s the tide going back out, in your hand.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, on everything the wave hits. Riptide must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For now, whatever the wave hits stands with its back to you for 1.2 seconds. It can''t start anything, and your first weapon blow on it is a critical hit and ends the effect.


      <b>Works with.</b> The charged blade; Heavy Water; every mark that works on a critical hit.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The second press, sending the wave back out with what it brought. For now, what it brings is left turned away.


      <b>Example.</b> Pull in two soldiers with Riptide and cut the nearer one right away. The first blow lands on its back as a critical hit.'
  - Id: water.drowned
    Title: Drawn From the Drowned
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When a Soaked enemy dies, its water runs across the floor to you as a line of foam and refills part of an empty flask. Soak, then kill.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies while Soaked, from any blow or spell, as long as you have fewer flasks than your maximum.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each Soaked death fills a fifth of a flask. Five fill one flask.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, Broken Gourd, which it comes after on the chart, Poured Blade and Deep Well above it, and every spell that soaks a crowd.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The line of foam running to you isn''t shown yet.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Whirlpool on a pack and cut it down. By the last body, a flask is back on your belt.'
  - Id: water.poured
    Title: Poured Blade
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You call up a copy of yourself made of water to stand next to you. It repeats each of your weapon blows a moment later, with part of the strength, and soaks whatever it hits.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 30 mana: Water''s larger spell. (Its Empower makes it 25 mana, 35% damage, and 15 seconds.) The copy lasts 12 seconds. Each weapon blow that lands is repeated 0.2 seconds later for 30% of its damage, and adds Soaked. You can only have one copy at a time.


      <b>Works with.</b> Soaked, Heavy Water, Wellspring, Drawn From the Drowned, which it comes after on the chart.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The copy''s blows should carry all of the blow''s stagger. For now, they carry none.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it while a brute is staggered. The finisher lands, then lands again for almost a third.'
  - Id: water.poured.shape
    Title: 'Poured Blade: Poured on the Edge or Brother at Your Back'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Poured Blade. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Poured on the Edge</b> (the spell''s first design): no copy. Instead, a flask is poured along your weapon. For a while, it reaches farther, every blow heals you and soaks the enemy, and the last blow of a chain throws a wave. It costs a flask instead of mana. <b>Brother at Your Back</b> (the fisher''s brothers): the copy stands behind you, facing the other way, and repeats your blows on whatever is behind you. It''s for when you''re surrounded.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Poured Blade, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the copy at your side.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Poured on the Edge: for 12 seconds, you get a tenth of your weapon damage back as health. While you have this shape picked, the last blow of every chain throws a wave 5 metres long and 60 degrees wide, dealing 10 damage, 15 stagger and Soaked. Brother at Your Back: the copy works like the plain one.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Water and every mark that works on the end of a chain (Poured on the Edge); Undertow and Blowhole''s Blown Out shape when you''re surrounded (Brother at Your Back).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Poured on the Edge should cost a flask instead of mana, make your weapon reach 40% farther, and only throw its wave during its 12 seconds. Brother at Your Back''s copy should stand behind you, facing the other way.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers surround you. Use Brother at Your Back, and whatever comes at your back gets cut as you cut what''s in front.'
  - Id: water.poured.master
    Title: 'Poured Blade: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Make a perfect dodge while the copy is there, and the copy steps into the blow you avoided and answers the attacker at full strength.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every perfect dodge while Poured Blade is in a spell slot, once every 2 seconds.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. A copy left where you stood hits everything within 2.3 metres of it 0.3 seconds later, for all of your last blow''s damage plus 30 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> The dodge; The Tide Turns'' Master mark; Heavy Water.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work while the copy is there, and the answer should aim at the attacker. For now, any perfect dodge leaves the answering copy.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute swings at you and your copy. You roll away, and the copy answers it with your heavy blow.'
  - Id: water.well
    Title: Deep Well
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You carry one more flask, but a flask no longer heals you right away. Instead, it fills a well, and the well only heals you while you are landing blows.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Drinking fills the well. The well heals you while one of your weapon blows has landed in the last 3 seconds and you are not at full health. The well shows on the screen as Well.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Four flasks. Each flask adds 70 to the well (more with Wellspring), and the well holds up to 140. It heals you 6 health a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wellspring (flasks hold 50% more), Drawn From the Drowned, which it comes after on the chart, Broken Gourd (the throw still heals you 20 right away).


      <b>Example.</b> Drink early in a fight, then keep cutting. The health comes back as you fight, but if you stop, it waits.'
  - Id: water.sea
    Title: The Sea Rises
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While Wellspring is lit, press the surge button again to flood the ground around you, using up a flask. For six seconds, everything in the flood stays Soaked, and every weapon blow you land sends a wave out across the water. Then the sea pulls back to you, dragging everything with it, and breaks in the direction you face.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light Wellspring with the surge button, then press the surge button again before it ends. If you have no flask left, it tells you so and waits.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. The flood has a 9-metre radius around where you stood. Each wave hits everything within 3.6 metres of the enemy you hit, for 40% of the blow''s damage, 8 stagger and Soaked. At the end, everything within 9 metres of you is pulled 4 metres in. 0.55 seconds later, the sea breaks in a cone 9 metres long and 120 degrees wide, dealing 55 damage and 90 stagger as a heavy blow.


      <b>Works with.</b> Wellspring, Soaked, Heavy Water, Drawn From the Drowned (for the flask), Deep Crack after the sea breaks, a second element''s Static or Chill.


      <b>Example.</b> Flood a crowd, cut into the middle of it for six seconds, then roll away and face them as the sea breaks.'
  - Id: water.highlow
    Title: High Water, Low Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Your own tide goes back and forth. At high water, your spells hit harder and your weapon hits softer. At low water, it''s the other way around. A perfect guard or a perfect dodge turns the tide right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry Water. The tide turns on its own every six seconds. "High water" or "Low water" shows on the screen when it turns.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. High water: spells hit 33% harder, and your weapon hits 20% softer. Low water: your weapon hits 33% harder, and spells hit 20% softer. The tide starts high. A perfect guard or a perfect dodge turns it and restarts the six seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spreading Water, which it comes after on the chart. Ebb and Flood. Every spell in your Hand, and a weapon you like to swing.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast your spells at high water. When it turns low, close in and use the blade. Need spells right now? Make a perfect dodge to turn the tide back.'
  - Id: water.drawnback
    Title: Drawn Back to Break
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Each time you dodge away from the enemy you face, the sea comes back with you as a wave. Your next weapon blow that lands breaks all your waves at once in front of you. If you have no waves, your blows are weaker.


      <b>When it happens.</b> You get a wave with each dodge away from the enemy you face, while you carry Water. The waves break on your next weapon blow that lands. "Waves" shows how many you have, next to the weapon.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Keystone, 6 Shards. Up to three waves. For each wave, the break deals 12 damage and 20 stagger in front of you, out to 4 metres. Everything it hits is Soaked and thrown 2 metres back. With no waves, your weapon hits 20% softer.


      <b>Works with.</b> Heavy Water, which it comes after on the chart. Undertow and The Tide Turns, which pull enemies back in after you. Anything that uses Soaked.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge back three times from a pack, then step in with one blow. Three waves break for 36 damage and 60 stagger, and they are all thrown back and soaked.'
  - Id: water.spilled
    Title: Spilled Water
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> When you drink a flask, a ring of water bursts out from you. It soaks everyone nearby and pushes them back, so you get room to breathe.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time you drink a flask, while you carry Water.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Major, 3 Shards. The ring reaches 3.5 metres. Everything in it takes 1 damage and 15 stagger, is Soaked, and is pushed 2.5 metres away.


      <b>Works with.</b> Spreading Water, which it comes after on the chart. Everything that uses Soaked. Deep Well and Broken Gourd, the other flask marks.


      <b>Example.</b> You''re surrounded and hurt, so you drink. The ring throws the pack back and soaks them, ready for your next reaction.'
  - Id: wind.unsteady
    Title: Unsteady
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Every weapon blow that lands makes the enemy Unsteady, one stack at a time, up to five. An Unsteady enemy loses its balance sooner, is thrown farther, and turns more slowly, so you can circle behind it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it: every weapon blow that lands, light or heavy, while Wind is your first element. If Wind is your second element, your weapon adds your first element''s affliction, and Unsteady comes from Gust, the charged blade and your R2 strike.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 2 Shards. One stack per blow, up to five, lasting 6 seconds. Each stack means the enemy takes 6% more stagger, is shoved 15% farther, and turns 8% slower: 30%, 75% and 40% at five. During Tailwind, every blow adds one more stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut (five stacks throws it into the air), Indrawn Breath (its burst uses up the stacks), Against the Wall, Nothing Beneath, Carried Far, and the Carried Embers reaction when a burning enemy is made Unsteady.


      <b>Example.</b> Four light blows and a heavy blow on a soldier. With five stacks, it takes 30% more stagger from the heavy blow, which often breaks its balance.'
  - Id: wind.rising
    Title: Rising Cut
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on an enemy with five stacks of Unsteady throws it into the air. It hangs there helpless, and each blow you land on it up there keeps it up a little longer, up to three blows. The throw uses up the Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy with five Unsteady, at most twice a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. It stays in the air for 1 second (elites and gods for 0.6 of that). Each of the first three blows on it up there adds 0.45 seconds. An enemy too heavy to lift staggers for a second instead and takes 40 stagger.


      <b>Works with.</b> Unsteady, Lifting Wind and Sky Dance, which come after it on the chart, Carried Far, Nothing Beneath, the deep strike Whirl Cut, and Thunder Lift and The Sky Finds Them on the Tempest branch.


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier with five stacks takes your heavy blow and goes up. Two quick light blows keep it there while you get ready for the third.'
  - Id: wind.lifting
    Title: Lifting Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A circle appears under the enemy you face and follows it for a moment. Then a column of wind bursts up from it, hitting as a heavy blow and throwing the enemy into the air, no matter how steady it is. An enemy too heavy to lift staggers instead.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). If no enemy is in reach, the column comes up 5 metres in front of you, and you still have to wait.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds.) It targets the nearest enemy within 5 metres. The column rises 0.35 seconds after you cast it, with a 2-metre radius. It deals 8 damage, 40 stagger, counts as a heavy blow, adds one stack of Unsteady to everything in it, and keeps them in the air for 0.8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut, which it comes after on the chart; Nothing Beneath; Sky Dance; Against the Wall (it counts as a spell hitting); and the charged blade: when it lands, your next three blows carry Wind.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Hanging in the air for a full second (1.3 seconds with its Empower). For now, the column lifts for its own 0.8 seconds.


      <b>Example.</b> An archer stands behind a shield. Lift the archer, step in, and keep it up with your blows.'
  - Id: wind.lifting.shape
    Title: 'Lifting Wind: Dust Devil or Turned About'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Lifting Wind. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Dust Devil</b>: the column wanders after enemies for a while, lifting the first one it reaches, then the next. <b>Turned About</b>: the column spins its enemy around to face away from you, and your first blow on its back is a critical hit. It''s the shape for enemies that can''t be lifted.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Lifting Wind, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Dust Devil: two columns rise under the two nearest enemies, 0.6 seconds after you cast it. Turned About: for 1.2 seconds, the enemy can''t start an attack, and the first weapon blow on it is a critical hit, which ends the effect.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut and Nothing Beneath (Dust Devil in a pack); the heavy weapons and the colossus fights (Turned About).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Dust Devil should be one column wandering for 3 seconds (for now, two rise at once). Turned About shouldn''t lift the enemy, should spin it around in the column, and should give half the time against an elite and make a god''s next attack swing wide (for now, it lifts the enemy too).


      <b>Example.</b> A brute too heavy to lift: use Turned About, and your next cut lands as a critical hit on its back.'
  - Id: wind.lifting.master
    Title: 'Lifting Wind: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast Lifting Wind on the enemy a perfect dodge just took you past, and it hangs in the air twice as long.


      <b>When it happens.</b> A perfect dodge, then Lifting Wind within a second. Lifting Wind must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Within a second of a perfect dodge, casting Lifting Wind while it is ready doesn''t start its wait. Each enemy the column lifts is held up for 1.6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Passing Cut and Leaf on the Wind, which keep you dodging; Nothing Beneath.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Only the enemy you dodged past should hang twice as long. For now, every enemy the column lifts hangs longer, whether you dodged or not.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll through a duellist''s lunge and cast it as you come out behind it. It goes up for twice as long, and the next cast is free.'
  - Id: wind.skydance
    Title: Sky Dance
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You throw the enemy you face into the air and leap up next to it. Five rings close in on it, one after another. Press light attack as each ring closes for a perfectly timed cut that hits 50% harder. Then a path follows the stick (or the cursor) for a moment, and you throw the enemy down along it. It bursts where it lands, harder for each perfectly timed cut. Nothing can hit you from the leap until you land.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It needs an enemy you face within 6 metres that isn''t still staggering from the last dance. Otherwise, it isn''t cast and no mana is spent. Pressing heavy attack throws the enemy right away.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards, 45 mana (its Empower makes it 35, with six rings). A ring closes every 0.34 seconds. A press in the last part of its closing counts as perfectly timed. Each cut deals 10 damage, or 15 if perfectly timed. The first cut adds a stack of Unsteady. The throw goes 6 metres. The landing deals 20 damage, plus 6 more for each perfectly timed cut (50 with all five), and 60 stagger as a heavy blow, to the enemy you threw and everything within 3 metres. Those enemies are pushed 3 metres away. An enemy that can''t be lifted is danced with on the ground.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut, which it comes after on the chart; Nothing Beneath; Against the Wall (each cut counts as a spell hitting); Unsteady.


      <b>Example.</b> Dance a brute up into the air, land all five cuts perfectly, and throw it down into the soldiers behind it, for 50 damage to each.'
  - Id: wind.skydance.shape
    Title: 'Sky Dance: Carried Off or Two Rise'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Sky Dance. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Carried Off</b>: there''s no throw. Instead, you carry the enemy down the path and drop it on another enemy, and both take the burst. <b>Two Rise</b>: the enemy you face and the nearest other enemy both go up, and the rings take turns between them.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Sky Dance, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Carried Off: the path is 8 metres long. Two Rise: the first cut throws everything within 3 metres of the enemy into the air for 1.5 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Against the Wall and Indrawn Breath (Carried Off into a crowd); Nothing Beneath (Two Rise keeps two enemies up).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Carried Off should carry the enemy and drop it on another one (for now, it is thrown farther). Two Rise''s rings should take turns between the two (for now, the second enemy is only lifted).


      <b>Example.</b> Two soldiers stand side by side. Two Rise takes both of them up, and the landing bursts on both.'
  - Id: wind.skydance.master
    Title: 'Sky Dance: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Land all five cuts perfectly, and you come down with Tailwind lit for four seconds, for free.


      <b>When it happens.</b> When Sky Dance hits. Sky Dance must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. For 4 seconds, your dodge carries you 30% farther and keeps you safe 25% longer, and you move 15% faster. These are Tailwind''s own bonuses, once per dance.


      <b>Works with.</b> Passing Cut, Leaf on the Wind, Closing Circle: the fight keeps moving after you land.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work when all five cuts were perfectly timed, and should light Tailwind itself (with its hanging blows and its Unsteady). For now, any dance that hits gives the dodge and speed bonuses.


      <b>Example.</b> Land five perfect cuts and throw the enemy into a pack. You land and roll right through them, farther and safer.'
  - Id: wind.lift
    Title: Carried Far
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Carried Far. Every reaction with Unsteady in it throws whatever it hits away from where it happened.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever Unsteady reacts on an enemy with another affliction it has (Carried Embers with Burn, Windthrow, and the rest).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Every enemy within 2.5 metres of the reaction is thrown 1.5 metres away from it.


      <b>Works with.</b> Carrying a second element: every one of Wind''s ten reactions; Against the Wall, for throwing enemies into things.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The reactions should reach 50% farther (the game needs a number for how far a reaction reaches first).


      <b>Example.</b> Wind and Fire on a soldier in a crowd. Carried Embers goes off, and the soldiers around it are thrown back.'
  - Id: wind.nothing
    Title: Nothing Beneath
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy in the air stays up as long as you keep hitting it, each blow up there gives double mana, and your blows don''t stop holding it up after three. The cost: your weapon is weaker against an enemy that isn''t Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. The longer hang time and the mana come with each blow that lands on an enemy in the air.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Up to 23 blows can hold an enemy in the air, instead of three. The first adds 0.45 seconds, and each one after adds a little less, about 3 seconds more in total. Blows on an enemy in the air give twice their mana. Your weapon deals 15% less damage, but full damage against an Unsteady enemy.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut, Closing Circle, Lifting Wind, Sky Dance, Unsteady.


      <b>Example.</b> Lift a soldier and keep cutting. It hangs in the air for four seconds while your mana bar fills twice as fast.'
  - Id: wind.devotion
    Title: On the Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> On the Wind, Wind''s Devotion, for going all in on Wind only. Your spells have no wind-up, and you can cast them in the middle of a dodge or a swing without stopping it. A spell cast during a dodge carries you farther along it. Almost the whole fight happens on the move.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Only while you carry Wind twice (all in). It is always on then.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. A spell cast during a dodge carries you 2 metres farther. Buying this also gives you the deep strike, Whirl Cut. Use it with L2 and R2 while you carry Wind alone or twice. The crescent becomes a small whirlwind that holds the first enemy it reaches in the air for a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every spell in the Wind tree; Passing Cut and Leaf on the Wind; the deep strike.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Spells with no wind-up, cast during a dodge or a swing without stopping it, and the 2 metres added to a dodge you cast in. For now, every dodge carries you 30% farther.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll at a pack, cast Indrawn Breath during the roll, and come out of it with the pack piled up in front of you.'
  - Id: wind.passing
    Title: Passing Cut
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Where a dodge ends, a cut of wind hits everything close to you and leaves it Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At the end of every dodge, at most twice a second.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Everything within 2.2 metres of where you stop takes 10 damage, 8 stagger and one stack of Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Closing Circle and Turned Aside, which come after it on the chart; Leaf on the Wind; Tailwind''s longer dodge.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The cut should follow the path you passed through, and each enemy you passed shouldn''t be able to turn for a moment. For now, the cut comes where you stop.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll into the middle of three soldiers. All three are cut and Unsteady before you swing.'
  - Id: wind.circle
    Title: Closing Circle
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You run a full circle around the enemy you face. When the circle closes, the wind inside it rises. Everything inside is hit, given five stacks of Unsteady, and thrown into the air. You end where you started, facing in, and nothing can hit you while you run.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). It needs an enemy you face within 5 metres. If there is none, it isn''t cast and nothing is spent.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 7 seconds and the time in the air 1.5 seconds.) The circle is as wide as the gap between you and the enemy, with a radius of 2 to 3 metres. The run takes 0.7 seconds, and the circle snaps shut 0.2 seconds later. Everything inside takes 6 damage, 10 stagger and five stacks of Unsteady, and hangs in the air for 1.2 seconds. Enemies that can''t be lifted stagger instead.


      <b>Works with.</b> Passing Cut, which it comes after on the chart; Rising Cut and Nothing Beneath; Indrawn Breath, whose burst uses up the stacks it added.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, nothing can hit you for the whole run. The plan is for that to last only the first half.


      <b>Example.</b> Two soldiers stand on either side of a brute. Run the circle around the brute, and all three go up with five stacks.'
  - Id: wind.circle.shape
    Title: 'Closing Circle: Half Circle or Wide Circle'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Closing Circle. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Half Circle</b>: you end on the far side of the enemy, behind it. Nothing is lifted, and your first blow on its back is a critical hit. <b>Wide Circle</b>: you run around a group, and the column takes everyone inside, but only lifts each one for a moment.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Closing Circle, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Half Circle: a step through an enemy within 5 metres, dealing 6 damage and 10 stagger, and for 1.5 seconds your next blow is a critical hit. Wide Circle: it targets an enemy you face within 7 metres, the circle has a radius of up to 4 metres, and enemies stay in the air for 0.6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned About (another way to get behind an enemy); Indrawn Breath and Against the Wall (Wide Circle around a crowd).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Half Circle should run half the circle (for now, it steps through the enemy). The Empower''s longer hang time also applies to Wide Circle''s short one (the Empower''s numbers are applied after the shape''s, so with the Empower, enemies hang for 1.5 seconds).


      <b>Example.</b> A shield bearer is facing you. Use Half Circle, and the first cut lands as a critical hit on its unguarded back.'
  - Id: wind.circle.master
    Title: 'Closing Circle: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> If you start Closing Circle right as a blow would land on you, it counts as a perfect dodge, and you run the circle around the attacker, wherever it stands within eight metres.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time you cast Closing Circle. It must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. You get what a perfect dodge gives: 12 mana, and 1.5 seconds off the wait on every spell that is waiting.


      <b>Works with.</b> Lifting Wind''s Master mark and the other marks a perfect dodge sets off; Leaf on the Wind.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The timing (starting it right as a blow would land), and running the circle around the attacker within 8 metres. For now, every cast gives the dodge''s mana and time off.


      <b>Example.</b> A spearman thrusts at you. Start the circle into it, and you run around it as the spear passes, with your mana and spell waits paid back.'
  - Id: wind.turned
    Title: Turned Aside
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard throws back everyone near you and gives the attacker three more stacks of Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> On a perfect guard: press the guard button just before a blow lands.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Every enemy within 4 metres is pushed 3 metres away. The attacker gets three stacks of Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Bag of Winds and Leaf on the Wind, which come after it on the chart; Rising Cut; Against the Wall.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Sending a thrown attack back at whoever threw it.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers surround you. Perfect-guard the first swing, and the ring is thrown back, and the one who swung has three stacks.'
  - Id: wind.bag
    Title: Bag of Winds
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You untie a bag of wind at your hip. Each blow you perfectly guard or perfectly dodge is caught in it, up to three, and thrown things that come near are caught too. Press again, and it opens: a cone that hits harder for each wind inside, and every thrown thing flies back at whoever threw it.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). Then it works on each perfect guard or perfect dodge while the bag holds a wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 10 seconds to cast it again (its Empower makes it 8). The bag holds three winds, shown on the screen as Winds. Each perfect guard or perfect dodge lets one out as a cone 7 metres long and 60 degrees wide, dealing 12 damage, 25 stagger and a stack of Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned Aside, which it comes after on the chart; the Master marks of Lifting Wind and Closing Circle (more perfect dodges); Indrawn Breath.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The planned bag: it stays open for 8 seconds, each caught blow throws its attacker back 2 metres and is kept, thrown things are caught, and a second press (or the end of the time) opens it as one cone dealing 12 damage and 25 stagger for each wind inside, with the thrown things flying back. For now, casting it fills the bag, and each perfect guard or dodge lets a wind out right away. The Empower''s fourth wind.


      <b>Example.</b> Cast it as a brute starts its string of blows. Guard three blows perfectly, and three cones throw it back down the hall.'
  - Id: wind.bag.shape
    Title: 'Bag of Winds: Kept Wind or Loose Neck'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Bag of Winds. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Kept Wind</b>: the bag doesn''t open on its own. It keeps its winds for a long time, so you can open it when you choose, like on a staggered enemy. <b>Loose Neck</b>: nothing is kept. Each wind leaves right away as a free Gust toward the enemy you face next.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Bag of Winds, using the shape you picked. Loose Neck works on every perfect guard and perfect dodge while it is in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Kept Wind: 20 seconds. Loose Neck: each perfect guard or perfect dodge throws a Gust, a cone 5 metres long and 70 degrees wide, dealing 8 damage, 20 stagger and two Unsteady. An attacker you guarded against is also thrown 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Turned Aside and Rising Cut (Loose Neck''s Unsteady); a staggered enemy (Kept Wind).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Kept Wind does nothing beyond the bag yet. It is waiting for a bag that keeps its winds. Loose Neck''s gust should aim at the enemy you face next (for now, it aims at the one that was caught).


      <b>Example.</b> Use Loose Neck against a pack of duellists. Every perfect guard throws a gust into them.'
  - Id: wind.bag.master
    Title: 'Bag of Winds: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Catch three winds within three seconds, and the bag bursts on its own as a whirlwind. Everything within six metres goes into the air.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, on a perfect dodge, at most once every 10 seconds. Bag of Winds must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Everything within 6 metres of you goes into the air for a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut and Nothing Beneath, for the enemies in the air; Lifting Wind.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need three winds caught within 3 seconds to burst the bag. For now, a perfect dodge raises the whirlwind.


      <b>Example.</b> Dodge a brute''s swing perfectly in a crowd. Everything around you goes up, ready for your cuts.'
  - Id: wind.leaf
    Title: Leaf on the Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Dodging costs no stamina. Instead, it uses a breath of wind. You can hold three, and you get them back over time and by landing blows. With no breath left, you can''t dodge. The cost: you take more damage.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it. Every dodge uses a breath. The breaths come back as you fight.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 6 Shards. Up to three breaths, shown on the screen as Breath. You get one back every 2 seconds while you have fewer than three, and one for every three blows you land. You take 20% more damage from every blow.


      <b>Works with.</b> Passing Cut, Closing Circle, Bag of Winds, Tailwind''s longer, safer dodge.


      <b>Example.</b> Against a fast duellist, dodge three times in a row, then land three blows. A breath is back before the next swing comes.'
  - Id: wind.wall
    Title: Against the Wall
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Any enemy you throw that hits a wall, a pillar or another enemy is hit hard, harder for each Unsteady it has. When two enemies crash together, both are hit. This is the payoff every throw in the Wind tree is waiting for.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every time one of your spells hits an enemy: Gust, any spell in your slots, and the spells your marks throw.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. The plan: 15 damage, plus 6 more for each Unsteady, and 30 stagger. For now: a burst with a 1.6-metre radius on each enemy a spell hits, dealing 6 damage, 15 stagger and one stack of Unsteady.


      <b>Works with.</b> Gust and its Long Gust shape, Indrawn Breath''s All One Way shape, Sky Dance, Turned Aside, and the charged blade''s Gale Edge.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The thrown enemy hitting something (a wall, a pillar, another enemy). For now, the burst comes wherever a spell hits.


      <b>Example.</b> Gust three soldiers in a row. Each one takes the gust and a burst, and they shake each other with their bursts.'
  - Id: wind.base
    Title: Gust
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Gust, Wind''s own spell. It comes with the element; you never buy it. A rush of air shoots out in front of you, hitting everything in it, making it Unsteady and throwing it straight back with the wind.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). While you carry Wind, you can always put it in a slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Comes with Wind. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 6 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Its Empower makes the wait 4.5 seconds. It is a cone 5 metres long and 70 degrees wide. It deals 8 damage, 20 stagger and two stacks of Unsteady, and everything in it is thrown 3 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Its Empower, its two shapes and its Master mark; Against the Wall; and the charged blade, which it charges with Wind when it lands.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The Empower''s third stack of Unsteady. For now, the spell only gets the Empower''s shorter wait.


      <b>Example.</b> A soldier stands at the edge of a drop. Gust it, and it''s gone.'
  - Id: wind.base.shape
    Title: 'Gust: Downburst or Long Gust'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Gust. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Downburst</b>: the wind comes down from above and spreads out in a ring, throwing everything nearby outward. Aim it at your feet to clear a ring around you. <b>Long Gust</b>: a narrow path that throws what it hits far away, for throwing one enemy into a wall, a pit or its friends.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Gust, using the shape you picked. If you have not bought this, you cast the plain gust.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Downburst: everything within 3 metres of you takes 8 damage, 20 stagger and two Unsteady, and is thrown 2.5 metres outward. Long Gust: a path 2 metres wide and 10 metres long that deals 8 damage, 20 stagger and two Unsteady, and throws enemies 5 metres.


      <b>Works with.</b> Against the Wall (Long Gust into a wall); Passing Cut and Turned Aside when you''re surrounded (Downburst).


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Downburst should be aimed at a spot up to 6 metres away. For now, it always comes down on you.


      <b>Example.</b> Four soldiers surround you. Downburst throws them all back with two stacks. An archer stands behind a line of soldiers: Long Gust knocks it off its ledge.'
  - Id: wind.base.master
    Title: 'Gust: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast Gust right as an enemy''s attack warning shows, and the gust pushes its blow aside into empty air.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every enemy Gust hits. Gust must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each enemy it hits flinches, which breaks whatever it had started, unless it is steadfast. The plan is for elites'' blows to come 0.4 seconds late instead, and for gods not to be pushed aside.


      <b>Works with.</b> Every shape of Gust; Against the Wall.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should only work right as the attack warning shows (the game needs a way to notice an enemy''s wind-up first), and elites'' blows should come late. For now, any gust that lands makes its enemies flinch.


      <b>Example.</b> A brute starts its red overhead swing. Gust it, and the blow falls on nothing.'
  - Id: wind.indrawn
    Title: Indrawn Breath
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You breathe in, and everything nearby is dragged into one spot in front of you. Then the breath bursts out of that spot, hitting all of them as a heavy blow and using up each enemy''s Unsteady, harder for every stack.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4).


      <b>The numbers.</b> 4 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 11 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. (Its Empower makes the wait 9 seconds and pulls from 8.5 metres.) The spot is 2 metres in front of you, or on an enemy that close. For 0.5 seconds, everything within 7 metres of it is dragged most of the way in. Then the burst deals 10 damage, 25 stagger and two stacks of Unsteady to each, as a heavy blow, and each enemy''s Unsteady goes off for 6 damage and 8 stagger per stack.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hammer Down, which it comes after on the chart; Against the Wall; Closing Circle (five stacks on everything inside the circle); Rising Cut; and Catching Fire if you carry Fire, since it bunches up a crowd.


      <b>Example.</b> Archers are spread around a room, each with a few stacks. Pull them all to one spot, and every stack they have bursts at once.'
  - Id: wind.indrawn.shape
    Title: 'Indrawn Breath: Held Breath or All One Way'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two shapes for Indrawn Breath. You pick one at any rest fire. <b>Held Breath</b>: the pull lasts as long as you hold the button, and the spot moves with you. Let go to burst. <b>All One Way</b>: the burst doesn''t scatter them. All of them are thrown together in the direction you face. Gather a crowd, then throw it into a wall.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Whenever you cast Indrawn Breath, using the shape you picked.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Held Breath: up to 2 seconds. All One Way: each enemy the burst hits is thrown 5 metres straight away from you.


      <b>Works with.</b> Against the Wall (All One Way), Sky Dance, Bag of Winds.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Held Breath should be held on its button, with the spot moving with you (for now, the pull always lasts the full 2 seconds). All One Way''s burst shouldn''t scatter them first.


      <b>Example.</b> Pull a crowd into a pile, then All One Way throws the pile into the wall behind it.'
  - Id: wind.indrawn.master
    Title: 'Indrawn Breath: Mastered'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Cast Indrawn Breath in the middle of a dodge. The spot travels with you to where the dodge ends, and the burst doesn''t use up any Unsteady, so every stack stays for the next blow.


      <b>When it happens.</b> For now, every enemy the burst hits. Indrawn Breath must be in a spell slot.


      <b>The numbers.</b> 3 Shards. Each enemy it hits gets two stacks of Unsteady back after the burst.


      <b>Works with.</b> Rising Cut (back toward five stacks), Closing Circle.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> It should need you to cast it in the middle of a dodge, with the spot traveling with you and the burst using nothing up. For now, each enemy gets two stacks back.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll through a pack and pull it in behind you. The burst lands, and the pack stays Unsteady for your next heavy blow.'
  - Id: wind.remembers
    Title: The Wind Remembers
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> While your surge lasts, every weapon blow you land leaves its cut hanging in the air where it landed, up to sixteen. When the surge ends, time slows down, and all of them fall again, one right after another, each at half the strength of the blow it remembers.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Light the surge with the surge button (tap L2, or press F). The cuts fall the moment it ends.


      <b>The numbers.</b> A god''s gift; it is never sold, but in the playtest you can buy it. Up to sixteen cuts. After that, each new blow is added to the weakest cut hanging there. Each falls within 1.4 metres of where it landed for half the blow''s damage, 10 stagger and one stack of Unsteady. Tailwind lasts 10 seconds and costs 35 mana.


      <b>Works with.</b> Tailwind, Against the Wall (each falling cut counts as a spell hitting), Nothing Beneath, Rising Cut.


      <b>Example.</b> Light Tailwind and cut a pack sixteen times. As it ends, sixteen cuts fall on them again at half strength.'
  - Id: wind.hammer
    Title: Hammer Down
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on an enemy in the air slams it into the ground. The blow hits 50% harder, and where the enemy lands, the ground bursts and throws back everyone nearby.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each heavy blow (finishers too) that lands on an enemy in the air or staggering, while you carry Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Major, 3 Shards. The heavy blow deals 50% more damage. The enemy drops right away. The burst deals 14 damage and 30 stagger to everything within 3 metres and throws them 2 metres back.


      <b>Works with.</b> Against the Wall, which it comes after on the chart, and the enemies it throws. Indrawn Breath, which comes after it. Lifting Wind, Sky Dance, Rising Cut, and anything that throws enemies up.


      <b>Example.</b> Throw a soldier up with Lifting Wind, then bring a heavy blow down on it. It slams into the ground and knocks its friends back.'
  - Id: windthrow.shape
    Title: 'Windthrow: Blown Seed or Deep Roots'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Unlocks two new shapes for Windthrow. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Windthrow uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. <b>Blown Seed</b>: the shove doesn''t tear the enemy out of the ground. Instead, its seeds blow away with the wind and root whatever they reach. <b>Deep Roots</b>: the enemy is held longer, your blows on it heal you, and tearing it out staggers it harder.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Windthrow goes off while you carry both Wind and Life. Windthrow goes off when Seeded lands on an Unsteady enemy, or when Unsteady lands on a seeded one. If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Windthrow.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Blown Seed: the enemy is rooted for 2 seconds. Your heavy blow throws its seeds up to 6 metres past it, away from you, giving two Seeded to everything they reach. This is on purpose: it may root them too. Deep Roots: the enemy is rooted for 4 seconds, and each blow on it heals you 2. The heavy blow tears it out for 14 damage and 60 stagger, throws it 6 metres, and leaves a torn-up pit. After Windthrow goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 6 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Gust and Briar Patch to set it up, Seed on the Wind, The Fairy Wind, Thistledown.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Blown Seed on the front soldier of a line, and the line behind it gets rooted.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, only a heavy blow tears the enemy out or blows its seeds, not any shove. Deep Roots heals a flat 2 per blow.'
  - Id: windthrow.seedonwind
    Title: Seed on the Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A dodge that passes within 2 metres of an enemy leaves it Seeded and Unsteady.


      <b>When it happens.</b> At the end of each dodge, while you carry both Wind and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. One Seeded and one Unsteady on everything within 2 metres of where you stop.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windthrow (both at once root the enemy), Thistledown, Green Wood.


      <b>Example.</b> Roll past a soldier, and it is rooted to the floor behind you.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, it reaches enemies near where the dodge ends, not the ones it passed on the way.'
  - Id: windthrow.tornup
    Title: Torn Up
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A heavy blow on a rooted enemy counts as a shove: it tears the enemy out, whatever weapon you use.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every heavy blow or finisher that lands on an enemy that has both Seeded and Unsteady.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. For now: the enemy is thrown 3 metres away from you, at most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windthrow, The Fairy Wind, any weapon.


      <b>Example.</b> The Hammer''s heavy blow throws a rooted soldier across the room.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, Windthrow already tears enemies out on any heavy blow, so this only adds a throw.'
  - Id: windthrow.greenwood
    Title: Green Wood
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Torn-up pits stay longer and add one more stack of Seeded.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy is torn out.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, a minor mark, 1 Shard. The torn-up pit stays for 8 seconds instead of 5, and adds two Seeded instead of one. It''s the same with Deep Roots.


      <b>Works with.</b> Seed on the Wind, which it comes after on the chart, Windthrow.


      <b>Example.</b> The pits left behind keep Seeding the fight.'
  - Id: windthrow.fairywind
    Title: The Fairy Wind
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Where an enemy is torn out, a small whirlwind rises and wanders for 4 seconds, making whatever it passes Unsteady and Seeded. So tearing one enemy out roots the next.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time Windthrow goes off, while you carry both Wind and Life.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. For now: a patch 2.4 metres across appears where Windthrow went off and lasts 4 seconds. It adds an Unsteady every half second and a Seeded every second to whatever stands in it. This is on purpose: these may start Windthrow again.


      <b>Works with.</b> Torn Up, Seed on the Wind, Thistledown.


      <b>Example.</b> One Windthrow in a pack starts the next.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the whirlwind stays still instead of wandering around.'
  - Id: windthrow.thistledown
    Title: Thistledown
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You break apart into a cloud of seed fluff and blow in the direction of the stick, passing through enemies. Each enemy you pass gets two stacks of Seeded and Unsteady, so it gets rooted as you go by.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Wind and Life. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Wind.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, movement, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 9 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. You travel 7 metres. Everything within 1.5 metres of your path takes 4 damage, two Unsteady and two Seeded.


      <b>Works with.</b> Windthrow, Torn Up, The Fairy Wind.


      <b>Example.</b> Drift through a pack, turn around, and find them rooted.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> You can still be hit while you drift. No ring of flowers forms where you land yet. A light blow right after doesn''t count as a shove yet.'
  - Id: winterseed.shape
    Title: 'Winter Seed: Hard Spring or Heart Under Ice'
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> Thaw unlocks two new shapes for Winter Seed. At a rest fire, you pick which shape your Winter Seed uses. From then on, it uses that shape instead of the plain one. Both shapes keep the sleep: the seed stops fading while the Chill lasts. They change how it wakes up. <b>Hard Spring</b>: it wakes up twice as hard and fills the Seeded of everything it reaches all the way. <b>Heart Under Ice</b>: waking up heals you instead of hurting enemies, and gives you a ward.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Every time Winter Seed goes off while you carry both Ice and Life. Winter Seed goes off when Seeded lands on a chilled enemy, or when Chill lands on a seeded one. The seed wakes up when its Chill ends (a thaw or a shatter also ends it). If you have not bought this mark, or you carry only one of the two elements, you get the plain Winter Seed.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Hard Spring: thorns deal 24 damage per Seeded stack within 2.5 metres, and fill the Seeded of everything they reach all the way. You heal 3 per stack. Heart Under Ice: thorns deal 6 damage per stack within 2.5 metres. You heal 5 per stack and get one ward. After Winter Seed goes off on an enemy, it can''t go off on that enemy again for 4 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Hoarfrost and Briar Patch to set it up, a heavy blow on a frozen enemy (a shatter is the fastest way to wake it), Green Under the Ice, Seed Under Snow.


      <b>Example.</b> Use Hard Spring on a frozen, seeded soldier in a pack. Shatter it, and the thorns fill the whole pack''s Seeded all the way.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> Hard Spring is meant to wake up only on a shatter. For now, it wakes up whenever the cold ends.'
  - Id: winterseed.undersnow
    Title: Seed Under Snow
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> An enemy that dies chilled and Seeded leaves a frozen seed that cracks open. You heal, and everything nearby is chilled and Seeded.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each time an enemy dies with both Chill and Seeded on it.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. You heal 8, and everything within 2.5 metres of where it fell gets a Chill and a Seeded. At most twice a second.


      <b>Works with.</b> Winter Seed, Late Frost, Hidden in the Tree.


      <b>Example.</b> Kill a frosted, seeded soldier, and the soldiers next to it start their own Winter Seed.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The seed is meant to lie on the floor for 8 seconds until you step on it. For now, it cracks open as the enemy dies.'
  - Id: winterseed.latefrost
    Title: Late Frost
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> A perfect guard against a Seeded enemy freezes its feet to the ground.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Each perfect guard (press the guard button just before a blow lands) against an enemy that has Seeded.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, 3 Shards. Two stacks of Chill, and the enemy is held still for 0.8 seconds.


      <b>Works with.</b> Winter Seed (the Chill puts the seed to sleep), Briar Patch, Seed Under Snow.


      <b>Example.</b> A seeded brute swings. Guard it perfectly, and it stands frozen to the ankles with its seed asleep.'
  - Id: winterseed.hidden
    Title: Hidden in the Tree
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> The things you grow are covered in frost. While you stand within 4 metres of one, its frozen bark blocks the first blow that would land on you every 6 seconds.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Always on once you learn it, while you carry both Ice and Life and stand near something you grew.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, keystone, 5 Shards. You get a ward every 6 seconds while you stand within 4 metres of something you grew.


      <b>Works with.</b> Briar Patch, Sapling and Elder Tree (things to grow), Late Frost.


      <b>Example.</b> Fight next to your sapling, and the first blow of each exchange glances off its bark.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> The things you grow don''t chill what they hit yet.'
  - Id: winterseed.green
    Title: Green Under the Ice
    Group: 
    Body: '<b>What it does.</b> You strike the floor, and lines of green run through the frost to every enemy nearby. On each one that has both Chill and Seeded, the frost breaks and its sleeping seed wakes up right away.


      <b>When it happens.</b> Cast it from a spell slot while you carry both Ice and Life. Press that slot''s button (hold L2 and a face button, or press 1 to 4). The first time it lands, it charges your blade with Ice.


      <b>The numbers.</b> Mix, duo spell, detonator, 5 Shards. It costs no mana. After you cast it, you must wait 14 seconds to cast it again. Fighting with your weapon makes the wait shorter. Everything within 9 metres takes 4 damage and 4 stagger. On enemies that have both afflictions, the Chill is used up, so Winter Seed (plain or with a shape) wakes up.


      <b>Works with.</b> Winter Seed and its shapes, Seed Under Snow, Late Frost.


      <b>Example.</b> Put five soldiers to sleep under the frost, then wake them all up at once.


      <b>Not built yet.</b> For now, the lines reach every enemy within 9 metres, not only those with both afflictions. A frozen enemy isn''t shattered yet. If no one is reached, you don''t get half the wait back yet. An enemy your weapon hit in the last 2 seconds doesn''t wake up harder yet.'
